(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqEs=[{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"A point charge +q is placed at the origin. A second point charge +9q is placed at (d, 0, 0) in Cartesian coordinate system. The point in between them where the electric field vanishes is",o:["(d/4, 0, 0)","(4d/3, 0, 0)","(3d/4, 0, 0)","(d/3, 0, 0)"],c:0,s:`Let the point be at x. Both charges are positive, so the fields cancel between them.
q/x\xB2 = 9q/(d \u2212 x)\xB2
Take the square root: (d \u2212 x) = 3x, so x = d/4.
The point is (d/4, 0, 0).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecn",st:"Charge sharing and earthing",q:"Two metal spheres of radius R and 3R have same surface charge density \u03C3. If they are brought in contact and then separated, surface charge density on smaller and bigger sphere becomes \u03C3\u2081 and \u03C3\u2082, respectively. The ratio {\u03C3\u2081;\u03C3\u2082} is",o:["{1;9}","{1;3}","3","9"],c:2,s:`Initial charges: q\u2081 = \u03C3\xB74\u03C0R\xB2, q\u2082 = \u03C3\xB74\u03C0(3R)\xB2 = 9q\u2081. Total = 10q\u2081.
After contact the potentials are equal, so charge is shared in the ratio of radii, 1 : 3.
Small sphere: 2.5q\u2081, big sphere: 7.5q\u2081.
\u03C3\u2081 = 2.5q\u2081/(4\u03C0R\xB2), \u03C3\u2082 = 7.5q\u2081/(4\u03C0\xB79R\xB2).
\u03C3\u2081/\u03C3\u2082 = (2.5 \xD7 9)/7.5 = 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"A small uncharged conducting sphere is placed in contact with an identical sphere but having 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 C charge and then removed to a distance such that the force of repulsion between them is 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 N. The distance between them is (Take {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} as 9 \xD7 10\u2079 in SI units)",o:["3 cm","1 cm","4 cm","2 cm"],c:3,s:`On contact the charge is shared equally: each sphere has 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 C.
F = kq\xB2/r\xB2, so r\xB2 = kq\xB2/F.
r\xB2 = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 (2\xD710\u207B\u2078)\xB2 / (9\xD710\u207B\xB3) = 3.6\xD710\u207B\u2076/(9\xD710\u207B\xB3) = 4\xD710\u207B\u2074 m\xB2.
r = 2\xD710\u207B\xB2 m = 2 cm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"In hydrogen like system, the ratio of coulombian force and gravitational force between an electron and a proton is in the order of",o:["10\xB9\u2079","10\xB2\u2079","10\xB3\u2079","10\xB3\u2076"],c:2,s:`Coulomb force: ke\xB2/r\xB2. Gravitational force: Gm_e m_p/r\xB2. The r\xB2 cancels.
Ratio = ke\xB2/(G m_e m_p) = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 (1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079)\xB2 / (6.67\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB9 \xD7 9.1\xD710\u207B\xB3\xB9 \xD7 1.67\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2077).
= 2.3\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2078 / 1.0\xD710\u207B\u2076\u2077 \u2248 2\xD710\xB3\u2079.
So the order is 10\xB3\u2079.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecn",st:"Charge sharing and earthing",q:"The vehicles carrying inflammable fluids usually have metallic chains touching the ground,",o:["to alert other vehicles","it is a custom","to protect tyres from catching dirt from ground","to conduct excess charge due to air friction to ground and prevent sparking"],c:3,s:`Friction with air charges the vehicle body.
A metal chain touching the ground gives this extra charge a path to earth.
This stops charge from building up, so no spark can set fire to the fluid.
Answer: to conduct excess charge due to air friction to ground and prevent sparking.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"\u03C3 is the uniform surface charge density of a thin spherical shell of radius R. The electric field at any point on the surface of the spherical shell is",o:["\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080","\u03C3/4\u03B5\u2080","\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080R","\u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080"],c:0,s:`Apply Gauss's law to a sphere just outside the shell: E\xB74\u03C0R\xB2 = \u03C3\xB74\u03C0R\xB2/\u03B5\u2080, so E = \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080.
Inside the shell the field is zero.
The book takes the field on the surface as the value just outside, \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080.
Answer: \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"Two identical conducting spheres P and S with charge Q on each, repel each other with a force 16 N. A third identical uncharged conducting sphere R is successively brought in contact with the two spheres. The new force of repulsion between P and S is",o:["4 N","1 N","12 N","6 N"],c:3,s:`Original force: kQ\xB2/r\xB2 = 16 N.
R touches P: each has Q/2. R (Q/2) then touches S (Q): each has 3Q/4.
Now P has Q/2 and S has 3Q/4.
New force = k(Q/2)(3Q/4)/r\xB2 = (3/8) \xD7 16 = 6 N.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecn",st:"Charge sharing and earthing",q:"Two charged conducting spheres of radii a and b are connected to each other by a conducting wire. The ratio of charges of the two spheres respectively is",o:["\u221A(ab)","{b;a}","{a;b}","ab"],c:2,s:`Connected spheres reach the same potential: kq_a/a = kq_b/b.
So q_a/q_b = a/b.
The charge is shared in the ratio of the radii.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"Force between two point charges q\u2081 and q\u2082 placed in vacuum at 'r' cm apart is F. Force between them when placed in a medium having dielectric constant k = 5 at 'r/5' cm apart will be",o:["25F","F/5","5F","F/25"],c:2,s:`In vacuum: F = kq\u2081q\u2082/r\xB2.
In the medium: F' = kq\u2081q\u2082/(K\xB7(r/5)\xB2) = 25kq\u2081q\u2082/(5r\xB2) = 5 \xD7 kq\u2081q\u2082/r\xB2.
So F' = 5F.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:`Two beads, each with charge q and mass m, are on a horizontal, frictionless, non-conducting, circular hoop of radius R. One of the beads is glued to the hoop at some point, while the other one performs small oscillations about its equilibrium position along the hoop. The square of the angular frequency of the small oscillations is given by
[\u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of free space.]`,o:["q\xB2/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB3m)","q\xB2/(32\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB3m)","q\xB2/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB3m)","q\xB2/(16\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB3m)"],c:1,s:`Equilibrium: the free bead is diametrically opposite the fixed one.
Let it move through a small angle \u03C6. The chord between the beads is 2R cos(\u03C6/2) \u2248 2R(1 \u2212 \u03C6\xB2/8).
U = kq\xB2/chord \u2248 (kq\xB2/2R)(1 + \u03C6\xB2/8). Kinetic energy = \xBDmR\xB2\u03C6\u0307\xB2.
This is SHM with \xBDC\u03C6\xB2, C = kq\xB2/(8R), and moment of inertia mR\xB2.
\u03C9\xB2 = C/(mR\xB2) = kq\xB2/(8mR\xB3) = q\xB2/(32\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB3m).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"A 10 \u03BCC charge is divided into two parts and placed at 1 cm distance so that the repulsive force between them is maximum. The charges of the two parts are",o:["9 \u03BCC, 1 \u03BCC","7 \u03BCC, 3 \u03BCC","8 \u03BCC, 2 \u03BCC","5 \u03BCC, 5 \u03BCC"],c:3,s:`Let the parts be q and (10 \u2212 q) \u03BCC. Force \u221D q(10 \u2212 q).
The product is maximum when q = 10 \u2212 q, that is q = 5 \u03BCC.
Both parts are 5 \u03BCC.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"If two charges q\u2081 and q\u2082 are separated with distance 'd' and placed in a medium of dielectric constant k. What will be the equivalent distance between charges in air for the same electrostatic force?",o:["k\u221Ad","1.5d\u221Ak","2d\u221Ak","d\u221Ak"],c:3,s:`In the medium: F = q\u2081q\u2082/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080 k d\xB2).
In air at distance d': F = q\u2081q\u2082/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080 d'\xB2).
Equal forces give d'\xB2 = k d\xB2, so d' = d\u221Ak.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"A point charge 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 C is moved from P to S in a uniform electric field of 30 N C\u207B\xB9 directed along positive x-axis. If co-ordinates of P and S are (1, 2, 0) m and (0, 0, 0), respectively, the work done by electric field will be",o:["1200 mJ","600 mJ","\u2212600 mJ","\u22121200 mJ"],c:2,s:`Force on the charge: F = qE = 2\xD710\u207B\xB2 \xD7 30 = 0.6 N along +x.
Displacement along x: from x = 1 to x = 0, that is \u22121 m.
W = F\xB7\u0394x = 0.6 \xD7 (\u22121) = \u22120.6 J = \u2212600 mJ.
The y displacement does no work because it is perpendicular to the field.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecn",st:"Charge sharing and earthing",q:"Two isolated metallic solid spheres of radii R and 2R are charged such that both have same charge density \u03C3. The spheres are then connected by a thin conducting wire. If the new charge density of the bigger sphere is \u03C3'. The ratio {\u03C3';\u03C3} is",o:["{5;3}","{9;4}","{5;6}","{4;3}"],c:2,s:`Charges: q\u2081 = \u03C3\xB74\u03C0R\xB2, q\u2082 = \u03C3\xB74\u03C0(2R)\xB2 = 4q\u2081. Total = 5q\u2081.
After connection the charge divides in the ratio of the radii, 1 : 2.
Bigger sphere: q\u2082' = (2/3) \xD7 5q\u2081 = 10q\u2081/3.
\u03C3' = (10q\u2081/3)/(4\u03C0\xB74R\xB2) = (10/3)(\u03C3\xB74\u03C0R\xB2)/(16\u03C0R\xB2) = (10/12)\u03C3 = 5\u03C3/6.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two identical positive charge Q each are fixed at a distance of '2a' apart from each other. Another point charge q\u2080 with mass 'm' is placed at midpoint between two fixed charges. For a small displacement along the line joining the fixed charges, the charge q\u2080 executes SHM. The time period of oscillation of charge q\u2080 will be",o:["\u221A{4\u03C0\xB3\u03B5\u2080ma\xB3;q\u2080Q}","\u221A{q\u2080Q;4\u03C0\xB3\u03B5\u2080ma\xB3}","\u221A{2\u03C0\xB3\u03B5\u2080ma\xB3;q\u2080Q}","\u221A{8\u03C0\xB3\u03B5\u2080ma\xB3;q\u2080Q}"],c:0,s:`Displace q\u2080 by x towards one charge. Net force towards the middle:
F = kQq\u2080[1/(a \u2212 x)\xB2 \u2212 1/(a + x)\xB2] \u2248 kQq\u2080 \xB7 4x/a\xB3 (for x << a).
So m\u03C9\xB2 = 4kQq\u2080/a\xB3, \u03C9\xB2 = Qq\u2080/(\u03C0\u03B5\u2080 m a\xB3).
T = 2\u03C0/\u03C9 = 2\u03C0\u221A(\u03C0\u03B5\u2080ma\xB3/(Qq\u2080)) = \u221A(4\u03C0\xB3\u03B5\u2080ma\xB3/(q\u2080Q)).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"A charge of 4 \u03BCC is to be divided into two. The distance between the two divided charges is constant. The magnitude of the divided charges so that the force between them is maximum, will be",o:["1 \u03BCC and 3 \u03BCC","2 \u03BCC and 2 \u03BCC","0 and 4 \u03BCC","1.5 \u03BCC and 2.5 \u03BCC"],c:1,s:`Parts q and (4 \u2212 q) \u03BCC. Force \u221D q(4 \u2212 q).
The product is greatest when q = 4 \u2212 q, so q = 2 \u03BCC.
Both charges are 2 \u03BCC.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"Two identical metallic spheres A and B when placed at certain distance in air repel each other with a force of F. Another identical uncharged sphere C is first placed in contact with A and then in contact with B and finally placed at the midpoint between spheres A and B. The force experienced by sphere C will be",o:["3F/2","3F/4","F","2F"],c:1,s:`Let A and B have charge q and be r apart: F = kq\xB2/r\xB2.
C touches A: A and C have q/2. C touches B: C and B have 3q/4.
Now A = q/2, B = 3q/4, C = 3q/4, with C at distance r/2 from each.
Force on C from A: k(3q/4)(q/2)/(r/2)\xB2 = (3/2)F, towards B (repulsion).
Force on C from B: k(3q/4)(3q/4)/(r/2)\xB2 = (9/4)F, towards A.
Net = 9F/4 \u2212 3F/2 = 3F/4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two identical charged particles each having a mass 10 g and charge 2.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 C are placed on a horizontal table with a separation of L between them such that they stay in limited equilibrium. If the coefficient of friction between each particle and the table is 0.25, find the value of L. [Use g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2]",o:["12 cm","10 cm","8 cm","5 cm"],c:0,s:`Limiting equilibrium: electric repulsion = maximum friction.
kq\xB2/L\xB2 = \u03BCmg = 0.25 \xD7 0.01 \xD7 10 = 0.025 N.
L\xB2 = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 4\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2074/0.025 = 3.6\xD710\u207B\u2074/0.025 = 0.0144 m\xB2.
L = 0.12 m = 12 cm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Three identical charged balls each of charge 2C are suspended from a common point P by silk threads of 2 m each (as shown in figure). They form an equilateral triangle of side 1 m. The ratio of net force on a charged ball to the force between any two charged balls will be",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Three charged balls B1, B2, B3 hang by 2 m threads from a point P and form an equilateral triangle of side 1 m",d:[["l",140,22,200,22,"B"],["t",170,14,"P","m"],["l",170,22,110,150],["l",170,22,230,150],["l",170,22,170,116,"d"],["c",110,150,6,"F"],["c",230,150,6,"F"],["c",170,122,6,"F"],["l",116,150,224,150],["l",114,146,166,126],["l",226,146,174,126],["t",98,156,"B\u2081","e"],["t",240,156,"B\u2082"],["t",180,118,"B\u2083"],["t",128,78,"2 m","e"],["t",212,78,"2 m"],["t",170,172,"1 m","m"]]},o:["1 : 1","1 : 4","\u221A3 : 2","\u221A3 : 1"],c:3,s:`Let the force between any two balls be F.
On one ball, two forces of size F act, along the two sides of the equilateral triangle. The angle between them is 60\xB0.
Resultant = 2F cos30\xB0 = \u221A3 F.
Ratio = \u221A3F : F = \u221A3 : 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two particles A and B having charges 20 \u03BCC and \u22125 \u03BCC respectively are held fixed with a separation of 5 cm. At what position a third charged particle should be placed so that it does not experience a net electric force?",fig:{w:340,h:110,alt:"Charge 20 microcoulomb at A and charge minus 5 microcoulomb at B, 5 cm apart",d:[["c",70,55,6,"F"],["c",250,55,6,"F"],["t",70,35,"20 \u03BCC","m"],["t",250,35,"\u22125 \u03BCC","m"],["t",70,100,"A","m"],["t",250,100,"B","m"],["l",76,80,244,80,"b"],["t",160,74,"5 cm","m"]]},o:["At 5 cm from \u22125 \u03BCC on the right side.","At 5 cm from 20 \u03BCC on the left side of system.","At 1.25 cm from a \u22125 \u03BCC between two charges.","At midpoint between two charges."],c:0,s:`The third charge must be outside the pair, next to the smaller charge (\u22125 \u03BCC), so that the forces can cancel.
Let it be x cm beyond B, on the side away from A.
k\xB720/(5 + x)\xB2 = k\xB75/x\xB2, so (5 + x)/x = 2.
x = 5 cm.
It is at 5 cm from \u22125 \u03BCC, on the right side.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"A certain charge Q is divided into two parts q and (Q \u2212 q). How should the charge Q and q be divided so that q and (Q \u2212 q) placed at a certain distance experience maximum electrostatic repulsion?",o:["Q = 2q","Q = {q;2}","Q = 4q","Q = 3q"],c:0,s:`Force \u221D q(Q \u2212 q). Its maximum is where d/dq[q(Q \u2212 q)] = Q \u2212 2q = 0.
So q = Q/2, that is Q = 2q.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two identical tennis balls each having mass m and charge q are suspended from a fixed point by threads of length l. What is the equilibrium separation when each thread makes a small angle \u03B8 with the vertical?",o:["x = ({q\xB2l\xB2;2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080m\xB2g\xB2})^(1/3)","x = ({q\xB2l;2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mg})^(1/2)","x = ({q\xB2l\xB2;2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080m\xB2g})^(1/3)","x = ({q\xB2l;2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mg})^(1/3)"],c:3,s:`For one ball: T cos\u03B8 = mg and T sin\u03B8 = kq\xB2/x\xB2, so kq\xB2/x\xB2 = mg tan\u03B8.
For small \u03B8, tan\u03B8 \u2248 sin\u03B8 = (x/2)/l = x/(2l).
kq\xB2/x\xB2 = mg x/(2l), so x\xB3 = 2lkq\xB2/(mg) = q\xB2l/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mg).
x = (q\xB2l/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mg))^(1/3).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two electrons each are fixed at a distance '2d'. A third charge proton placed at the midpoint is displaced slightly by a distance x (x << d) perpendicular to the line joining the two fixed charges. Proton will execute simple harmonic motion having angular frequency (m = mass of charged particle) (q = magnitude of the charge of each particle)",o:["({\u03C0\u03B5\u2080md\xB3;2q\xB2})^(1/2)","({2q\xB2;\u03C0\u03B5\u2080md\xB3})^(1/2)","({q\xB2;2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080md\xB3})^(1/2)","({2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080md\xB3;q\xB2})^(1/2)"],c:2,s:`Each electron attracts the proton with force kq\xB2/(d\xB2 + x\xB2).
The components along the line cancel. The components towards the midpoint add up:
F = 2 \xD7 kq\xB2/(d\xB2 + x\xB2) \xD7 x/\u221A(d\xB2 + x\xB2) \u2248 2kq\xB2x/d\xB3.
So m\u03C9\xB2 = 2kq\xB2/d\xB3, \u03C9\xB2 = q\xB2/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080md\xB3).
\u03C9 = (q\xB2/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080md\xB3))^(1/2).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Three charges +Q, q, +Q are placed respectively, at distance, 0, d/2 and d from the origin, on the x-axis. If the net force experienced by +Q, placed at x = 0, is zero, then value of q is",o:["+Q/4","\u2212Q/2","+Q/2","\u2212Q/4"],c:3,s:`Force on the +Q at x = 0 from the other +Q (distance d) is repulsive, along \u2212x: kQ\xB2/d\xB2.
The force from q (distance d/2) must be attractive: kQq/(d/2)\xB2 = 4kQq/d\xB2 in magnitude.
So q is negative and 4|q| = Q.
q = \u2212Q/4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:"Charge is distributed within a sphere of radius R with a volume charge density \u03C1(r) = {A;r\xB2} e^(\u22122r/a), where A and a are constants. If Q is the total charge of this charge distribution, the radius R is",o:["a log( {1;1 \u2212 Q/(2\u03C0aA)} )","a log(1 \u2212 Q/(2\u03C0aA))","{a;2} log(1 \u2212 Q/(2\u03C0aA))","{a;2} log( {1;1 \u2212 Q/(2\u03C0aA)} )"],c:3,s:`Q = \u222B\u2080\u1D3F \u03C1\xB74\u03C0r\xB2 dr = 4\u03C0A \u222B\u2080\u1D3F e^(\u22122r/a) dr = 4\u03C0A\xB7(a/2)(1 \u2212 e^(\u22122R/a)).
So Q = 2\u03C0aA(1 \u2212 e^(\u22122R/a)).
e^(\u22122R/a) = 1 \u2212 Q/(2\u03C0aA).
Take logs: R = \u2212(a/2) log(1 \u2212 Q/(2\u03C0aA)) = (a/2) log(1/(1 \u2212 Q/(2\u03C0aA))).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"There is a uniform spherically symmetric surface charge density at a distance R\u2080 from the origin. The charge distribution is initially at rest and starts expanding because of mutual repulsion. The figure that represents best the speed V(R(t)) of the distribution as a function of its instantaneous radius R(t) is",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"Four graphs of V against R starting at R0: (a) rises then falls, (b) S-shaped rise, (c) accelerating rise, (d) rise that flattens to a constant V0",d:[["t",20,20,"(a)"],["l",45,95,45,28,"a"],["l",45,95,160,95,"a"],["t",51,30,"V","s"],["t",163,98,"R","s"],["t",45,109,"R\u2080","ms"],["t",180,20,"(b)"],["l",205,95,205,28,"a"],["l",205,95,320,95,"a"],["t",211,30,"V","s"],["t",323,98,"R","s"],["t",205,109,"R\u2080","ms"],["t",20,135,"(c)"],["l",45,210,45,143,"a"],["l",45,210,160,210,"a"],["t",51,145,"V","s"],["t",163,213,"R","s"],["t",45,224,"R\u2080","ms"],["t",180,135,"(d)"],["l",205,210,205,143,"a"],["l",205,210,320,210,"a"],["t",211,145,"V","s"],["t",323,213,"R","s"],["t",205,224,"R\u2080","ms"],["p","M45,95 C70,35 95,35 115,65 S140,90 158,93"],["p","M205,95 C225,92 240,38 275,36 S300,35 318,35"],["p","M45,210 C85,205 125,185 150,140"],["p","M205,210 C207,160 240,148 305,142"],["l",205,141,305,141,"d"],["t",310,139,"V\u2080","s"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:3,s:`Energy of the charged shell of radius R is Q\xB2/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R). It is released at rest at R\u2080.
Energy conservation: \xBDMv\xB2 = (Q\xB2/8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(1/R\u2080 \u2212 1/R).
So v \u221D \u221A(1 \u2212 R\u2080/R).
v is zero at R = R\u2080, rises quickly, then flattens out and approaches a constant V\u2080 as R becomes very large (1/R \u2192 0). That is graph (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"Two identical conducting spheres A and B, carry equal charge. They are separated by a distance much larger than their diameters, and the force between them is F. A third identical conducting sphere, C, is uncharged. Sphere C is first touched to A, then to B, and then removed. As a result, the force between A and B would be equal to",o:["{3F;8}","{F;2}","{3F;4}","F"],c:0,s:`Let each of A and B have charge q: F = kq\xB2/r\xB2.
C touches A: A = q/2. C (q/2) touches B: B = 3q/4.
New force = k(q/2)(3q/4)/r\xB2 = (3/8)F.`},{y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"Shown in the figure are two point charges +Q and \u2212Q inside the cavity of a spherical shell. The charges are kept near the surface of the cavity on opposite sides of the centre of the shell. If \u03C3\u2081 is the surface charge on the inner surface and Q\u2081 net charge on it and \u03C3\u2082 the surface charge on the outer surface and Q\u2082 net charge on it then",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A thick spherical shell with a cavity; +Q near the upper right and -Q near the lower left of the cavity wall",d:[["c",170,100,90],["c",170,100,58],["c",205,66,3,"F"],["t",212,62,"+Q"],["c",135,134,3,"F"],["t",100,140,"\u2212Q"]]},o:["\u03C3\u2081 \u2260 0, Q\u2081 \u2260 0 ; \u03C3\u2082 \u2260 0, Q\u2082 \u2260 0","\u03C3\u2081 \u2260 0, Q\u2081 \u2260 0 ; \u03C3\u2082 = 0, Q\u2082 = 0","\u03C3\u2081 \u2260 0, Q\u2081 = 0 ; \u03C3\u2082 = 0, Q\u2082 = 0","\u03C3\u2081 = 0, Q\u2081 = 0 ; \u03C3\u2082 = 0, Q\u2082 = 0"],c:2,s:`The two point charges have zero total charge, so the inner surface carries a total induced charge Q\u2081 = 0 (shell neutral, Q\u2082 = 0 as well).
The charges sit near the surface of the cavity, so the induced charge on the inner surface is non-uniform and non-zero: \u03C3\u2081 \u2260 0.
The outer surface has no net charge and the field outside is zero, so the outer surface has no charge at all: \u03C3\u2082 = 0.
So \u03C3\u2081 \u2260 0, Q\u2081 = 0; \u03C3\u2082 = 0, Q\u2082 = 0.`},{y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two charges, each equal to q, are kept at x = \u2212a and x = a on the x-axis. A particle of mass m and charge q\u2080 = {q;2} is placed at the origin. If charge q\u2080 is given a small displacement (y << a) along the y-axis, the net force acting on the particle is proportional to",o:["\u22121/y","y","\u2212y","1/y"],c:1,s:`At height y, each fixed charge is at distance \u221A(a\xB2 + y\xB2) from q\u2080.
The x-components cancel. The y-components add, and since all charges are positive they push q\u2080 away from the axis:
F_y = 2\xB7k q q\u2080 y/(a\xB2 + y\xB2)^(3/2) \u2248 2kqq\u2080 y/a\xB3 for y << a.
So the force is proportional to y.`},{y:"AIEEE 2011",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two identical charged spheres suspended from a common point by two massless strings of length l are initially a distance d (d << l) apart because of their mutual repulsion. The charge begins to leak from both the spheres at a constant rate. As a result the charges approach each other with a velocity v. Then as a function of distance x between them",o:["v \u221D x^(\u22121/2)","v \u221D x^(\u22121)","v \u221D x^(1/2)","v \u221D x"],c:0,s:`For small angles, equilibrium gives kq\xB2/x\xB2 = mg\xB7x/(2l), so x\xB3 \u221D q\xB2 and q \u221D x^(3/2).
Charge leaks at a constant rate: dq/dt = constant.
dq/dt \u221D x^(1/2) dx/dt = constant, so v = dx/dt \u221D x^(\u22121/2).`},{y:"AIEEE 2010",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two identical charged spheres are suspended by strings of equal lengths. The strings make an angle of 30\xB0 with each other. When suspended in a liquid of density 0.8 g cm\u207B\xB3, the angle remains the same. If density of the material of the sphere is 1.6 g cm\u207B\xB3, the dielectric constant of the liquid is",o:["1","4","3","2"],c:3,s:`In air: tan\u03B8 = F/(mg). In the liquid: tan\u03B8 = (F/K)/(mg \u2212 upthrust).
Upthrust/weight = 0.8/1.6 = 1/2, so the effective weight is mg/2.
Same angle: F/(mg) = (F/K)/(mg/2), so 1 = 2/K.
K = 2.`},{y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"A charge Q is placed at each of the opposite corners of a square. A charge q is placed at each of the other two corners. If the net electrical force on Q is zero, then {Q;q} equals",o:["\u22122\u221A2","\u22121","1","\u2212{1;\u221A2}"],c:0,s:`Let the side be a. On one Q: the other Q (diagonal, distance a\u221A2) pushes along the diagonal with kQ\xB2/(2a\xB2).
Each q (distance a) gives kQq/a\xB2; the two are at right angles, so the resultant is \u221A2 kQq/a\xB2 along the diagonal.
For zero net force, the q's must attract: Q\xB2/2 = \u2212\u221A2 Qq.
Q/q = \u22122\u221A2.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2009",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"Three concentric metallic spherical shells of radii R, 2R, 3R, are given charges Q\u2081, Q\u2082, Q\u2083, respectively. It is found that the surface charge densities on the outer surfaces of the shells are equal. Then, the ratio of the charges given to the shells, Q\u2081 : Q\u2082 : Q\u2083, is",o:["1 : 2 : 3","1 : 3 : 5","1 : 4 : 9","1 : 8 : 18"],c:1,s:`Equal surface density on outer surfaces means outer-surface charges are in the ratio of areas, 1 : 4 : 9.
Outer charge of shell 1 = Q\u2081. Of shell 2 = Q\u2081 + Q\u2082. Of shell 3 = Q\u2081 + Q\u2082 + Q\u2083.
So Q\u2081 = k, Q\u2081 + Q\u2082 = 4k, Q\u2081 + Q\u2082 + Q\u2083 = 9k.
Q\u2082 = 3k, Q\u2083 = 5k.
Q\u2081 : Q\u2082 : Q\u2083 = 1 : 3 : 5.`},{y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"ecl",st:"Charge, quantisation and conservation",q:"If g_E and g_M are the accelerations due to gravity on the surfaces of the earth and the moon respectively and if Millikan's oil drop experiment could be performed on the two surfaces, one will find the ratio {electronic charge on the moon;electronic charge on the earth} to be",o:["g_M/g_E","1","0","g_E/g_M"],c:1,s:`The charge on an electron is a fundamental constant of nature.
It does not depend on gravity or on the place where it is measured.
So the ratio is 1.`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Four charges equal to \u2212Q are placed at the four corners of a square and a charge q is at its centre. If the system is in equilibrium the value of q is",o:["\u2212{Q;4}(1 + 2\u221A2)","{Q;4}(1 + 2\u221A2)","\u2212{Q;2}(1 + 2\u221A2)","{Q;2}(1 + 2\u221A2)"],c:1,s:`Consider one corner charge \u2212Q, square side a. Forces away from the square's centre:
Two neighbours: 2 \xD7 (kQ\xB2/a\xB2) cos45\xB0 = \u221A2 kQ\xB2/a\xB2.
Opposite corner: kQ\xB2/(2a\xB2).
The centre charge is at a/\u221A2, so it must attract with kQq/(a\xB2/2) = 2kQq/a\xB2 (q positive).
2Qq = (\u221A2 + 1/2)Q\xB2, so q = (Q/4)(1 + 2\u221A2).`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"Three charges \u2212q\u2081, +q\u2082 and \u2212q\u2083 are placed as shown in the figure. The x-component of the force on \u2212q\u2081 is proportional to",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Charge minus q1 at the origin, plus q2 at distance b along the x-axis, minus q3 at distance a up and to the left, making angle theta with the y-axis",d:[["l",140,160,140,20,"a"],["l",140,160,325,160,"a"],["t",147,24,"y"],["t",326,164,"x"],["l",140,160,80,56],["c",140,160,4,"F"],["c",80,56,4,"F"],["c",280,160,4,"F"],["t",96,178,"\u2212q\u2081"],["t",50,52,"\u2212q\u2083"],["t",272,150,"+q\u2082"],["t",100,112,"a","e"],["t",146,128,"\u03B8"],["l",140,176,280,176,"b"],["t",210,192,"b","m"]]},o:["{q\u2082;b\xB2} \u2212 {q\u2083;a\xB2} cos\u03B8","{q\u2082;b\xB2} + {q\u2083;a\xB2} sin\u03B8","{q\u2082;b\xB2} + {q\u2083;a\xB2} cos\u03B8","{q\u2082;b\xB2} \u2212 {q\u2083;a\xB2} sin\u03B8"],c:1,s:`Take \u2212q\u2081 at the origin, +q\u2082 at distance b on the x-axis, and \u2212q\u2083 at distance a along a line at angle \u03B8 to the y-axis.
+q\u2082 attracts \u2212q\u2081 along +x: kq\u2081q\u2082/b\xB2.
\u2212q\u2083 repels \u2212q\u2081, pushing it away from \u2212q\u2083, which has x-component +kq\u2081q\u2083 sin\u03B8/a\xB2.
Both act along +x, so F\u2093 \u221D q\u2082/b\xB2 + (q\u2083/a\xB2) sin\u03B8.`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"If a charge q is placed at the centre of the line joining two equal charges Q such that the system is in equilibrium then the value of q is",o:["Q/2","\u2212Q/2","Q/4","\u2212Q/4"],c:3,s:`Let each Q be a distance r from the centre. The Q's are 2r apart.
For one Q: repulsion from the other Q is kQ\xB2/(2r)\xB2 = kQ\xB2/(4r\xB2).
The force from q must cancel it: kQq/r\xB2 = \u2212kQ\xB2/(4r\xB2).
q = \u2212Q/4.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two identical non-conducting solid spheres of same mass and charge are suspended in air from a common point by two non-conducting, massless strings of same length. At equilibrium, the angle between the strings is \u03B1. The spheres are now immersed in a dielectric liquid of density 800 kg m\u207B\xB3 and dielectric constant 21. If the angle between the strings remains the same after the immersion, then",o:["electric force between the spheres remains unchanged","electric force between the spheres reduces","mass density of the spheres is 840 kg m\u207B\xB3","the tension in the strings holding the spheres remains same"],c:[1,2],s:`In the liquid the electric force becomes F/21, so it reduces: (b) is true and (a) is false.
Same angle means F/W is unchanged, where W is the effective weight (W = mg in air, W' = mg(1 \u2212 800/\u03C1) in the liquid).
So (F/21)/W' = F/(mg), giving 1/21 = 1 \u2212 800/\u03C1.
800/\u03C1 = 20/21, so \u03C1 = 840 kg m\u207B\xB3: (c) is true.
Both F and the weight fall, so the tension T = \u221A(F\xB2 + W\xB2) falls: (d) is false.
Correct: (b) and (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A uniform electric field, E\u20D7 = \u2212400\u221A3 \u0135 N C\u207B\xB9 is applied in a region. A charged particle of mass m carrying positive charge q is projected in this region with an initial speed of 2\u221A10 \xD7 10\u2076 m s\u207B\xB9. This particle is aimed to hit a target T, which is 5 m away from its entry point into the field as shown schematically in the figure. Take {q;m} = 10\xB9\u2070 C kg\u207B\xB9. Then",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Downward uniform field in a region; a particle enters at the left edge with speed u at angle theta to the horizontal dashed line and the target T is 5 m away on that line",d:[["l",90,18,90,172],["l",290,18,290,172],["l",115,24,115,170,"a"],["l",150,24,150,170,"a"],["l",185,24,185,170,"a"],["l",220,24,220,170,"a"],["l",255,24,255,170,"a"],["l",30,120,335,120,"d"],["l",90,120,112,78,"a"],["t",118,76,"u"],["p","M110,120 A20,20 0 0 0 100,103"],["t",114,114,"\u03B8","s"],["c",262,120,3,"F"],["t",262,110,"T","m"],["l",90,140,262,140,"b"],["t",176,156,"5 m","m"],["t",300,60,"E\u20D7"]]},o:["the particle will hit T if projected at an angle 45\xB0 from the horizontal","the particle will hit T if projected either at an angle 30\xB0 or 60\xB0 from the horizontal","time taken by the particle to hit T could be \u221A{5;6} \u03BCs and \u221A{5;2} \u03BCs","time taken by the particle to hit T is \u221A{5;3} \u03BCs"],c:[1,2],s:`Acceleration a = qE/m = 10\xB9\u2070 \xD7 400\u221A3 = 4\u221A3 \xD7 10\xB9\xB2 m/s\xB2, downward. The particle moves like a projectile.
Range R = u\xB2 sin2\u03B8/a = (4\xD710\xB9\xB3) sin2\u03B8/(4\u221A3\xD710\xB9\xB2) = (10/\u221A3) sin2\u03B8.
R = 5 gives sin2\u03B8 = \u221A3/2, so \u03B8 = 30\xB0 or 60\xB0: (a) false, (b) true.
Time t = 2u sin\u03B8/a = (\u221A10/\u221A3) sin\u03B8 \u03BCs. For 30\xB0: \u221A(5/6) \u03BCs. For 60\xB0: \u221A(5/2) \u03BCs. So (c) true, (d) false.
Correct: (b) and (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2009",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Under the influence of the Coulomb field of charge +Q, a charge \u2212q is moving around it in an elliptical orbit. Find out the correct statement(s).",o:["The angular momentum of the charge \u2212q is constant.","The linear momentum of the charge \u2212q is constant.","The angular velocity of the charge \u2212q is constant.","The linear speed of the charge \u2212q is constant."],c:0,s:`The Coulomb force on \u2212q always points along the line to +Q, so its torque about +Q is zero.
Hence the angular momentum of \u2212q is constant: (a) is correct.
The direction of velocity changes, so the linear momentum is not constant.
In an ellipse the speed and the angular velocity change (faster near +Q).
Only (a) is correct.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two equal negative charge \u2212q are fixed at points (0, \u2212a) and (0, a) on y-axis. A positive charge Q is released from rest at the point (2a, 0) on the x-axis. The charge Q will",o:["execute simple harmonic motion about the origin","move to the origin remain at rest","move to infinity","execute oscillatory but not simple harmonic motion"],c:3,s:`At (x, 0) the y-components of the two forces cancel. The net force is along \u2212x (attraction towards the origin):
F = 2kqQ x/(x\xB2 + a\xB2)^(3/2).
The charge is pulled to the origin, passes through it, and goes to the other side, so it oscillates.
The force is not proportional to x, because x = 2a is not small compared with a. So the motion is oscillatory but not simple harmonic.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:`A positive ion A and a negative ion B has charges 6.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C and 9.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 C, and masses 19.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg and 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg respectively. At an instant, the ions are separated by a certain distance r. At that instant the ratio of the magnitudes of electrostatic force to gravitational force is P \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3, where the value of P is ______.
(Take {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\xB2 C\u207B\xB9 and universal gravitational constant as 6.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 N m\xB2 kg\u207B\xB2)`,n:5,s:`The distance r cancels in the ratio.
Ratio = k q_A q_B/(G m_A m_B).
The 6.67 cancels between q_A and G. Numerically 9 \xD7 9.6/(19.2 \xD7 9) = 0.5, and the powers of ten combine to give 5 \xD7 10\u2074\u2074.
So the coefficient is 5 and the official answer is P = 5. (With the powers of ten exactly as printed the ratio works out to 5 \xD7 10\u2074\u2074; the printed 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 appears to be a misprint, but the coefficient matches the key.)`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two identical charged spheres are suspended by strings of equal lengths. The strings make an angle \u03B8 with each other. When suspended in water, the angle remains the same. If density of the material of the sphere is 1.5 g/cc, the dielectric constant of water will be ______. (Take density of water = 1 g/cc)",n:3,s:`In air: tan(\u03B8/2) = F/(mg). In water: tan(\u03B8/2) = (F/K)/(mg \u2212 upthrust).
Upthrust/weight = \u03C1_water/\u03C1_sphere = 1/1.5 = 2/3, so effective weight = mg/3.
Same angle: F/mg = (F/K)/(mg/3), so 1 = 3/K.
K = 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:`A thin metallic wire having cross sectional area of 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2 is used to make a ring of radius 30 cm. A positive charge of 2\u03C0 C is uniformly distributed over the ring, while another positive charge of 30 pC is kept at the centre of the ring. The tension in the ring is ______ N; provided that the ring does not get deformed (neglect the influence of gravity).
(given, {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 SI units)`,n:3,s:`Take a small arc of the ring subtending angle d\u03B8. Its charge is dq = (2\u03C0/2\u03C0)d\u03B8 = d\u03B8 coulomb.
Centre charge q = 30 pC repels it: dF = k q dq/R\xB2 (outwards).
Tension balance for the small arc: 2T sin(d\u03B8/2) \u2248 T d\u03B8 = dF.
T = k q /R\xB2 = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 30\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 / (0.3)\xB2 = 0.27/0.09 = 3 N.
(As in the official answer, only the force from the central charge is used.)`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two identical charged spheres are suspended by strings of equal lengths. The strings make an angle if 37\xB0 with each other. When suspended in a liquid of density 0.7 g cm\u207B\xB3, the angle remains same. If density of material of the sphere is 1.4 g cm\u207B\xB3, the dielectric constant of the liquid is ______. (tan37\xB0 = {3;4})",n:2,s:`Upthrust/weight = 0.7/1.4 = 1/2, so the effective weight in the liquid is mg/2.
In air: tan(\u03B8/2) = F/(mg). In liquid: tan(\u03B8/2) = (F/K)/(mg/2).
Same angle gives 1 = 2/K.
K = 2. (The angle 37\xB0 is not needed.)`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:`As shown in the figure, a configuration of two equal point charges (q\u2080 = +2 \u03BCC) is placed on an inclined plane. Mass of each point charge is 20 g. Assume that there is no friction between charge and plane. For the system of two point charges to be in equilibrium (at rest) the height h = x \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m. The value of x is ______.
(Take {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\xB2 C\u207B\xB2, g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)`,fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Right-angled triangle with a 30 degree angle at the lower right; a charge q0 on the slope at height h and another q0 at the foot of the slope",d:[["l",60,20,285,150,"B"],["l",60,20,60,150,"d"],["l",60,150,300,150,"d"],["c",110,49,5,"F"],["c",285,150,5,"F"],["t",118,42,"q\u2080"],["t",292,140,"q\u2080"],["l",110,52,110,148,"bd"],["t",118,106,"h"],["t",222,144,"30\xB0","s"]]},n:300,s:`The lower charge is at the foot of the incline (height 0); the upper charge is at height h along the slope, distance r from it.
For the upper charge, along the slope: kq\u2080\xB2/r\xB2 = mg sin30\xB0.
r\xB2 = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 4\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 / (0.02 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.5) = 0.036/0.1 = 0.36, so r = 0.6 m.
h = r sin30\xB0 = 0.3 m = 300 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m.
x = 300.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"Three point charges q, \u22122q and 2q are placed on x-axis at a distance x = 0, x = {3;4}R and x = R respectively from origin as shown. If q = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C and R = 2 cm, the magnitude of net force experienced by the charge \u22122q is ______ N.",fig:{w:340,h:120,alt:"Charges q at x = 0, minus 2q at 3R/4 and 2q at R along the x-axis",d:[["l",20,50,325,50,"a"],["c",40,50,4,"F"],["c",205,50,4,"F"],["c",260,50,4,"F"],["t",40,38,"q","m"],["t",205,38,"\u22122q","m"],["t",260,38,"2q","m"],["t",328,54,"x","s"],["t",40,66,"x = 0","ms"],["l",205,50,205,100,"d"],["l",260,50,260,100,"d"],["l",40,80,205,80,"b"],["t",122,76,"3R/4","ms"],["l",40,100,260,100,"b"],["t",150,114,"R","m"]]},n:5440,s:`On \u22122q: q is at distance 3R/4 and attracts it towards the left: F\u2081 = k(2q)(q)/(3R/4)\xB2 = 32kq\xB2/(9R\xB2).
2q is at distance R/4 and attracts it towards the right: F\u2082 = k(2q)(2q)/(R/4)\xB2 = 64kq\xB2/R\xB2.
Net = (64 \u2212 32/9)kq\xB2/R\xB2 = (544/9)kq\xB2/R\xB2.
kq\xB2/R\xB2 = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 4\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2/(4\xD710\u207B\u2074) = 90 N.
Net = (544/9) \xD7 90 = 5440 N.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Two equal positive point charges are separated by a distance 2a. The distance of a point from the centre of the line joining two charges on the equatorial line (perpendicular bisector) at which force experienced by a test charge q\u2080 become maximum is {a;\u221Ax}. The value of x is ______.",n:2,s:`At distance r on the bisector: E = 2kq r/(a\xB2 + r\xB2)^(3/2).
For maximum E set dE/dr = 0: (a\xB2 + r\xB2) \u2212 3r\xB2 = 0, so r = a/\u221A2.
Comparing with a/\u221Ax gives x = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:`A cubical volume is bounded by the surfaces x = 0, x = a, y = 0, y = a, z = 0, z = a. The electric field in the region is given by E\u20D7 = E\u2080x \xEE. Where E\u2080 = 4 \xD7 10\u2074 NC\u207B\xB9m\u207B\xB9. If a = 2 cm, the charge contained in the cubical volume is Q \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074 C. The value of Q is ______.
(Take \u03B5\u2080 = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2/Nm\xB2)`,n:288,s:`The field has only an x-component, so only the faces at x = 0 and x = a carry flux.
At x = 0, E = 0: no flux. At x = a, E = E\u2080a over area a\xB2: flux = E\u2080a\xB3.
Gauss's law: q = \u03B5\u2080E\u2080a\xB3 = 9\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 4\xD710\u2074 \xD7 (0.02)\xB3 = 9\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 4\xD710\u2074 \xD7 8\xD710\u207B\u2076.
q = 2.88\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 C = 288 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074 C.
Q = 288.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"A point charge q\u2081 = 4q\u2080 is placed at origin. Another point charge q\u2082 = \u2212q\u2080 is placed at x = 12 cm. Charge of proton is q\u2080. The proton is placed on x axis so that the electrostatic force on the proton is zero. In this situation, the position of the proton from the origin is ______ cm.",n:24,s:`The net force on the proton can vanish only outside the two charges, on the side of the smaller charge (q\u2082), at distance d beyond x = 12 cm.
k\xB74q\u2080/(12 + d)\xB2 = k\xB7q\u2080/d\xB2, so (12 + d) = 2d.
d = 12 cm, so the position is 12 + 12 = 24 cm from the origin.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"Three point charges of magnitude 5 \u03BCC, 0.16 \u03BCC and 0.3 \u03BCC are located at the vertices A, B, C of a right angled triangle whose sides are AB = 3 cm, BC = 3\u221A2 cm and CA = 3 cm and point A is the right angle corner. Charge at point A experiences ______ N of electrostatic force due to the other two charges.",n:17,s:`The charge 5 \u03BCC at A is acted on by forces along AB and AC, which are perpendicular.
F_B = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 5\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 0.16\xD710\u207B\u2076/(0.03)\xB2 = 8 N.
F_C = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 5\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 0.3\xD710\u207B\u2076/(0.03)\xB2 = 15 N.
Net = \u221A(8\xB2 + 15\xB2) = 17 N.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"A particle of mass 1 mg and charge q is lying at the mid-point of two stationary particles kept at a distance 2 m when each is carrying same charge q. If the free charged particle is displaced from its equilibrium position through distance 'x' (x << 1 m). The particle executes SHM. Its angular frequency of oscillation will be ______ \xD7 10\u2075 rad/s if q\xB2 = 10 C\xB2.",n:6e3,s:`Displace by x: net restoring force = kq\xB2[1/(1 \u2212 x)\xB2 \u2212 1/(1 + x)\xB2] \u2248 4kq\xB2x (distances 1 m from the middle).
m\u03C9\xB2 = 4kq\xB2, so \u03C9\xB2 = 4 \xD7 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 10/10\u207B\u2076 = 3.6\xD710\xB9\u2077.
\u03C9 = 6\xD710\u2078 rad/s = 6000 \xD7 10\u2075 rad/s.
Answer: 6000.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:`An infinite number of point charges, each carrying 1 \u03BCC charge, are placed along the y-axis at y = 1 m, 2 m, 4 m, 8 m ......... The total force on a 1 C point charge, placed at the origin, is x \xD7 10\xB3 N. The value of x, to the nearest integer, is ______.
[Take {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\xB2/C\xB2]`,n:12,s:`All forces on the 1 C charge point the same way, along the y-axis, so they add.
F = k(1)(10\u207B\u2076)[1/1\xB2 + 1/2\xB2 + 1/4\xB2 + 1/8\xB2 + ...] = 9\xD710\xB3 \xD7 (1 + 1/4 + 1/16 + ...).
The series is geometric with ratio 1/4: sum = 1/(1 \u2212 1/4) = 4/3.
F = 9\xD710\xB3 \xD7 4/3 = 12 \xD7 10\xB3 N, so x = 12.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two small spheres each of mass 10 mg are suspended from a point by threads 0.5 m long. They are equally charged and repel each other to a distance of 0.20 m. The charge on each of the sphere is {a;21} \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 C. The value of 'a' will be ______. [Given g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2]",n:20,s:`Each sphere is 0.1 m from the vertical, so sin\u03B8 = 0.1/0.5 and tan\u03B8 = 0.1/\u221A(0.25 \u2212 0.01) = 1/\u221A24.
Balance: kq\xB2/(0.2)\xB2 = mg tan\u03B8 = 10\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10 /\u221A24 = 2.04\xD710\u207B\u2075 N.
q\xB2 = 2.04\xD710\u207B\u2075 \xD7 0.04/(9\xD710\u2079) = 9.07\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2077, so q = 9.52\xD710\u207B\u2079 C = (20/21) \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 C.
So a = 20.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecn",st:"Charge sharing and earthing",q:`Two identical conducting spheres with negligible volume have 2.1 nC and \u22120.1 nC charges, respectively. They are brought into contact and then separated by a distance of 0.5 m. The electrostatic force acting between the spheres is ______ \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 N.
[Given: 4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080 = {1;9\xD710\u2079} SI unit]`,n:36,s:`On contact the total charge 2.1 \u2212 0.1 = 2.0 nC is shared equally by the two identical spheres.
Each sphere now carries 1.0 nC = 10\u207B\u2079 C.
F = kq\xB2/r\xB2 = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 (10\u207B\u2079)\xB2 / (0.5)\xB2 = 9\xD710\u207B\u2079 / 0.25 = 36\xD710\u207B\u2079 N.
The force is 36 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 N, so the answer is 36.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Four point charges, each of +q, are rigidly fixed at the four corners of a square planar soap film of side a. The surface tension of the soap film is \u03B3. The system of charges and planar film are in equilibrium, and a = k[{q\xB2;\u03B3}]^(1/N), where k is a constant. Then N is ______.",n:3,s:`Use dimensions. Surface tension \u03B3 is force per unit length, so the film pulls with a force of order \u03B3a.
The electric force on a corner charge is of order q\xB2/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2).
In equilibrium q\xB2/(\u03B5\u2080a\xB2) ~ \u03B3a, so a\xB3 ~ q\xB2/(\u03B5\u2080\u03B3).
Thus a = k (q\xB2/\u03B3)^(1/3), where the constant k also contains \u03B5\u2080.
So N = 3.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"ecl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Four charges Q\u2081, Q\u2082, Q\u2083 and Q\u2084 of same magnitude are fixed along the x-axis at x = \u22122a, \u2212a, +a and +2a respectively. A positive charge q is placed on the positive y-axis at a distance b > 0. Four options of the signs of these charges are given in List I. The direction of the forces on the charge q is given in List II. Match List I with List II and select the correct answer using the code given below the lists.
List I
P. Q\u2081, Q\u2082, Q\u2083, Q\u2084 all positive
Q. Q\u2081, Q\u2082 positive; Q\u2083, Q\u2084 negative
R. Q\u2081, Q\u2084 positive; Q\u2082, Q\u2083 negative
S. Q\u2081, Q\u2083 positive; Q\u2082, Q\u2084 negative
List II
1. +x
2. \u2212x
3. +y
4. \u2212y`,fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Charges Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 on the x-axis at -2a, -a, +a and +2a, and the positive charge q on the y-axis at height b above the origin",d:[["l",20,150,325,150,"a"],["t",328,154,"x","s"],["l",190,150,190,50,"d"],["c",90,150,5,"F"],["c",140,150,5,"F"],["c",240,150,5,"F"],["c",290,150,5,"F"],["t",90,172,"Q\u2081","ms"],["t",140,172,"Q\u2082","ms"],["t",240,172,"Q\u2083","ms"],["t",290,172,"Q\u2084","ms"],["t",90,138,"\u22122a","ms"],["t",140,138,"\u2212a","ms"],["t",240,138,"+a","ms"],["t",290,138,"+2a","ms"],["c",190,50,6,"F"],["t",200,46,"q (0, b)"],["l",190,50,190,20,"a"],["t",196,22,"y","s"],["t",180,104,"b","es"]]},o:["P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2","P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1","P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4","P-4, Q-2, R-1, S-3"],c:0,s:`Like charges repel, unlike charges attract. Take b not much larger than a, so the nearer charges Q\u2082, Q\u2083 matter more than Q\u2081, Q\u2084.
P: all repel q; the x-parts cancel by symmetry, so the force is along +y. P \u2192 3.
Q: Q\u2081, Q\u2082 (positive, on the left) push q to the right; Q\u2083, Q\u2084 (negative, on the right) pull q to the right. Force is +x. Q \u2192 1.
R: the set-up is symmetric about the y-axis, so the force is along y. The near negative charges Q\u2082, Q\u2083 pull q down and win over the far positive ones. R \u2192 4 (\u2212y).
S: Q\u2082 (negative, at \u2212a) pulls q to the left and Q\u2083 (positive, at +a) pushes q to the left; these near charges win. S \u2192 2 (\u2212x).
Code P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2 is option (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:`Comprehension: A dense collection of equal number of electrons and positive ions is called neutral plasma. Certain solids containing fixed positive ions surrounded by free electrons can be treated as neutral plasma. Let N be the number density of free electrons, each of mass m. When the electrons are subjected to an electric field, they are displaced relatively away from the heavy positive ions. If the electric field becomes zero, the electrons begin to oscillate about the positive ions with a natural angular frequency \u03C9\u209A, which is called the plasma frequency. To sustain the oscillations, a time varying electric field needs to be applied that has an angular frequency \u03C9, where a part of the energy is absorbed and a part of the energy is reflected. As \u03C9 approaches \u03C9\u209A, all the free electrons are set to resonance together and all the energy is reflected. This is the explanation of high reflectivity of metals.
Taking the electronic charge as e and the permittivity as \u03B5\u2080, use dimensional analysis to determine the correct expression for \u03C9\u209A.`,o:["\u221A{Ne;m\u03B5\u2080}","\u221A{m\u03B5\u2080;Ne}","\u221A{Ne\xB2;m\u03B5\u2080}","\u221A{m\u03B5\u2080;Ne\xB2}"],c:2,s:`\u03C9\u209A has the dimension of frequency, so \u03C9\u209A\xB2 has dimension T\u207B\xB2.
Coulomb's law gives e\xB2/\u03B5\u2080 = force \xD7 length\xB2, which is kg m\xB3 s\u207B\xB2.
Divide by m (kg): e\xB2/(m\u03B5\u2080) has dimension m\xB3 s\u207B\xB2.
Multiply by N (m\u207B\xB3): Ne\xB2/(m\u03B5\u2080) has dimension s\u207B\xB2. This matches \u03C9\u209A\xB2.
The other three expressions do not reduce to s\u207B\xB2. So \u03C9\u209A = \u221A(Ne\xB2/(m\u03B5\u2080)), option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:`(Same plasma passage as the previous question: all the energy is reflected when the wave frequency equals the plasma frequency \u03C9\u209A.)
Estimate the wavelength at which plasma reflection will occur for a metal having the density of electrons N \u2248 4 \xD7 10\xB2\u2077 m\u207B\xB3. Take \u03B5\u2080 \u2248 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 and m \u2248 10\u207B\xB3\u2070, where these quantities are in proper SI units.`,o:["800 nm","600 nm","300 nm","200 nm"],c:1,s:`Reflection occurs when \u03C9 = \u03C9\u209A = \u221A(Ne\xB2/(m\u03B5\u2080)), with e = 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 C.
Ne\xB2 = 4\xD710\xB2\u2077 \xD7 2.56\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2078 = 1.02\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 and m\u03B5\u2080 = 10\u207B\u2074\xB9.
\u03C9\u209A = \u221A(1.02\xD710\xB3\xB9) \u2248 3.2\xD710\xB9\u2075 rad/s.
\u03BB = 2\u03C0c/\u03C9\u209A = 2\u03C0 \xD7 3\xD710\u2078 / 3.2\xD710\xB9\u2075 \u2248 5.9\xD710\u207B\u2077 m.
This is about 600 nm, option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"A small ball of mass 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 kg having a charge of 1 \u03BCC is suspended by a string of length 0.8 m. Another identical ball having the same charge is kept at the point of suspension. Determine the minimum horizontal velocity (in m/s) which should be imparted to the lower ball so that it can make complete revolution. (Take g = 10 m/s\xB2)",n:5.86,s:`The fixed charge at the point of suspension repels the ball with a constant radial force F = kq\xB2/L\xB2 = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 (10\u207B\u2076)\xB2 / 0.8\xB2 = 1.41\xD710\u207B\xB2 N, directed outward along the string. It does no work on the circular path.
Weight mg = 2\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 10 = 2\xD710\u207B\xB2 N.
Minimum speed at the top (tension zero): mv\xB2/L = mg \u2212 F, so v_top\xB2 = L(g \u2212 F/m) = 0.8 \xD7 (10 \u2212 7.03) = 2.375 m\xB2/s\xB2.
Energy from bottom to top: v\xB2 = v_top\xB2 + 4gL = 2.375 + 32 = 34.375.
v = \u221A34.375 \u2248 5.86 m/s.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"ecn",st:"Charge sharing and earthing",q:"Two isolated metallic solid spheres of radii R and 2R are charged such that both of these have same charge density \u03C3. The spheres are located far away from each other and connected by a thin conducting wire. Find the new charge density on the bigger sphere.",a:"5\u03C3/6",s:`Initial charges: q\u2081 = \u03C3 \xD7 4\u03C0R\xB2 and q\u2082 = \u03C3 \xD7 4\u03C0(2R)\xB2 = 4q\u2081. Total charge = 5q\u2081 = 20\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3.
The wire makes the potentials equal: kq\u2081'/R = kq\u2082'/(2R), so q\u2082' = 2q\u2081'.
Hence the bigger sphere gets 2/3 of the total: q\u2082' = (2/3)(20\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3) = 40\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3/3.
New density \u03C3' = q\u2082'/(4\u03C0(2R)\xB2) = (40\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3/3)/(16\u03C0R\xB2) = 5\u03C3/6.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Three particles, each of mass 1 g and carrying a charge q, are suspended from a common point by insulated massless strings, each 100 cm long. If the particles are in equilibrium and are located at the corners of an equilateral triangle of side length 3 cm, calculate the charge q on each particle. (Take g = 10 m/s\xB2.)",a:"3.16\xD710\u207B\u2079 C",s:`The three particles hang symmetrically. Each is at distance r = 3/\u221A3 = \u221A3 cm = 1.73\xD710\u207B\xB2 m from the vertical axis.
For each string, tan\u03B8 = r/\u221A(L\xB2 \u2212 r\xB2) \u2248 1.73\xD710\u207B\xB2 (L = 1 m).
One particle is pushed outward by the other two: each exerts F = kq\xB2/s\xB2 (s = 3 cm), and the outward resultant is 2F cos30\xB0 = \u221A3 F.
Horizontal balance: \u221A3 kq\xB2/s\xB2 = mg tan\u03B8.
q\xB2 = mg tan\u03B8 \xD7 s\xB2 / (\u221A3 k) = (10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 10 \xD7 1.73\xD710\u207B\xB2 \xD7 9\xD710\u207B\u2074) / (1.73 \xD7 9\xD710\u2079) = 10\u207B\xB9\u2077.
q = 3.16\xD710\u207B\u2079 C.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1978",tp:"ecl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A rigid insulated wire frame in the form of a right angled triangle ABC, is set in a vertical plane as shown in the figure. Two beads of equal masses m each and carrying charges q\u2081 and q\u2082 are connected by a cord of length l and can slide without friction on the wires. Considering the case when the beads are stationary, determine
(a) (i) the angle \u03B1
(ii) the tension in the cord
(iii) the normal reaction on the beads.
(b) If the cord is now cut, what are the values of the charges for which the beads continue to remain stationary?`,fig:{w:340,h:230,alt:"Right angled triangle ABC with BC horizontal, angle B 30 degrees, angle C 60 degrees and the right angle at A. Bead P with charge q1 on AB and bead Q with charge q2 on AC are joined by a cord of length l. Angle alpha is at P between PA and the cord.",d:[["l",40,200,300,200],["l",40,200,235,87],["l",235,87,300,200],["l",183,117,287,177],["c",183,117,6,"F"],["c",287,177,6,"F"],["t",240,80,"A"],["t",24,212,"B"],["t",304,212,"C"],["t",172,112,"P, q\u2081","e"],["t",296,181,"Q, q\u2082"],["t",70,196,"30\xB0"],["t",262,196,"60\xB0"],["t",194,112,"\u03B1"],["t",238,144,"l"]]},a:"(a) (i) \u03B1 = 60\xB0  (ii) T = q\u2081q\u2082/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080l\xB2) + mg  (iii) R\u2081 = \u221A3 mg, R\u2082 = mg  (b) Unlike charges",s:`Let N be the net pull along the line PQ on each bead (tension minus Coulomb repulsion). It is the same for both beads. Let \u03B1 = angle APQ; the angle at A is 90\xB0, so angle AQP = 90\xB0 \u2212 \u03B1.
Bead P (wire AB at 30\xB0 to the horizontal): along the wire, N cos\u03B1 = mg sin30\xB0.
Bead Q (wire AC at 60\xB0 to the horizontal): along the wire, N sin\u03B1 = mg sin60\xB0.
Dividing: tan\u03B1 = sin60\xB0/sin30\xB0 = \u221A3, so \u03B1 = 60\xB0. Then N = mg (sin30\xB0/cos60\xB0 = 1).
Tension: T \u2212 q\u2081q\u2082/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080l\xB2) = mg, so T = q\u2081q\u2082/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080l\xB2) + mg.
Normal reactions (perpendicular to the wires): R\u2081 = mg cos30\xB0 + N sin60\xB0 = \u221A3 mg. R\u2082 = mg cos60\xB0 + N sin30\xB0 = mg.
(b) Without the cord the pull N = mg must come from the Coulomb force alone, so it must be attraction: the charges are unlike, with q\u2081q\u2082/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080l\xB2) = mg in magnitude.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"ecl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Two identical metallic spheres of exactly equal masses are taken. One is given a positive charge Q coulomb and the other an equal negative charge. Their masses after charging are different.",o:["True","False"],c:0,s:`Charging a metal sphere changes the number of electrons on it.
A positive charge Q means electrons were removed, so that sphere loses mass (Q/e) \xD7 m\u2091.
The negatively charged sphere gains the same number of electrons, so its mass increases by the same amount.
So the masses become different. The statement is True.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"ecl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Five point charges, each of value +q coulomb, are placed on five vertices of a regular hexagon of side L metre. The magnitude of the force on the point charge of value \u2212q coulomb placed at the centre of the hexagon is ______ newton.",a:"(1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(q\xB2/L\xB2) N",s:`The centre of a regular hexagon is at distance L from each vertex.
If all six vertices held +q, the forces on the centre charge would cancel in opposite pairs, giving zero.
So the force from the five charges is equal and opposite to the force the missing sixth charge would give.
That force has magnitude (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) q\xB2/L\xB2.
So the force is (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(q\xB2/L\xB2) newton, pointing away from the empty vertex.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1986",tp:"ecl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Two small balls having equal positive charge Q (coulomb) on each are suspended by two insulating strings of equal length L (metre) from a hook fixed to a stand. The whole set-up is taken in a satellite into space where there is no gravity (state of weightlessness). The angle between the two strings is ______ and the tension in each string is ______ newton.",a:"180\xB0, (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(Q\xB2/4L\xB2) N",s:`With no weight, each ball is acted on only by the string tension and the Coulomb repulsion.
For equilibrium these must be along the same line, so the strings are in one straight line with the balls on opposite sides of the hook: the angle is 180\xB0.
The distance between the balls is 2L.
T = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) Q\xB2/(2L)\xB2 = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(Q\xB2/4L\xB2) newton.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:`A small bob of mass 100 mg and charge +10 \u03BCC is connected to an insulating string of length 1 m. It is brought near an infinitely long non-conducting sheet of charge density '\u03C3' as shown in the figure. If the string subtends an angle of 45\xB0 with the sheet at equilibrium, the charge density of the sheet will be
(Given, \u03B5\u2080 = 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 F/m and acceleration due to gravity, g = 10 m/s\xB2)`,fig:{w:340,h:245,alt:"A vertical charged sheet with plus signs. A string of length 1 m is attached at the top of the sheet and holds a +10 microcoulomb bob at 45 degrees to the sheet, away from the sheet.",d:[["l",60,20,60,225,"B"],["t",46,44,"+","m"],["t",46,74,"+","m"],["t",46,104,"+","m"],["t",46,134,"+","m"],["t",46,164,"+","m"],["t",46,194,"+","m"],["t",46,224,"+","m"],["t",60,240,"+\u03C3","m"],["t",70,14,"A"],["l",60,40,159,139],["c",159,139,8,"F"],["t",174,143,"+10 \u03BCC"],["t",75,86,"45\xB0"],["t",122,80,"l = 1 m"]]},o:["0.885 nC/m\xB2","17.7 nC/m\xB2","885 nC/m\xB2","1.77 nC/m\xB2"],c:3,s:`The bob is in equilibrium under its weight mg (down), the string tension, and the electric force qE (horizontal, away from the sheet).
The string is at 45\xB0 to the vertical sheet, so tan45\xB0 = qE/mg, which gives qE = mg.
E = mg/q = (10\u207B\u2074 kg \xD7 10)/(10\xD710\u207B\u2076 C) = 100 N/C.
For a non-conducting sheet E = \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080), so \u03C3 = 2\u03B5\u2080E = 2 \xD7 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 100 = 1.77\xD710\u207B\u2079 C/m\xB2.
\u03C3 = 1.77 nC/m\xB2, option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Consider two infinitely large plane parallel conducting plates as shown in the figure. The plates are uniformly charged with a surface charge density +\u03C3 and \u22122\u03C3. The force experienced by a point charge +q placed at the mid point of the plates will be",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Two large parallel plates a distance d apart: the left plate carries +sigma, the right plate carries -2 sigma. A charge +q sits at the midpoint, d/2 from each plate.",d:[["l",90,20,90,140,"B"],["l",250,20,250,140,"B"],["t",90,158,"+\u03C3","m"],["t",250,158,"\u22122\u03C3","m"],["c",170,80,5,"F"],["t",170,68,"+q","ms"],["l",90,105,170,105,"b"],["t",130,100,"d/2","ms"],["l",170,105,250,105,"b"],["t",210,100,"d/2","ms"]]},o:["{\u03C3q;4\u03B5\u2080}","{\u03C3q;2\u03B5\u2080}","{3\u03C3q;4\u03B5\u2080}","{3\u03C3q;2\u03B5\u2080}"],c:3,s:`For large parallel conducting plates the field between them is E = (\u03C3\u2081 \u2212 \u03C3\u2082)/(2\u03B5\u2080), where \u03C3\u2081 and \u03C3\u2082 are the total charge per unit area on the two plates.
Here \u03C3\u2081 = +\u03C3 and \u03C3\u2082 = \u22122\u03C3, so E = (\u03C3 + 2\u03C3)/(2\u03B5\u2080) = 3\u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080).
The field is uniform between the plates and points from the +\u03C3 plate to the \u22122\u03C3 plate.
Force on +q: F = qE = 3\u03C3q/(2\u03B5\u2080), directed towards the \u22122\u03C3 plate. Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Consider a circular loop that is uniformly charged and has a radius a\u221A2. Find the position along the positive z-axis of the cartesian coordinate system where the electric field is maximum if the ring was assumed to be placed in xy plane at the origin.",o:["0","a","{a;\u221A2}","{a;2}"],c:1,s:`On the axis of a ring of radius R, E = kQz/(z\xB2 + R\xB2)^(3/2).
For the maximum, dE/dz = 0: (z\xB2 + R\xB2) \u2212 3z\xB2 = 0, so z = R/\u221A2.
Here R = a\u221A2, so z = a\u221A2/\u221A2 = a.
The field is maximum at z = a, option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Two infinite identical charged sheets and a charged spherical body of charge density '\u03C1' are arranged as shown in the figure. Then the correct relation between the electric fields at A, B, C and D points is",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"Two parallel positively charged sheets, each with plus sigma. A positively charged sphere of radius R with density rho lies between them, nearer the right sheet. Points C and D lie 2 cm outside the left and right sheets. Point B is between the left sheet and the sphere and point A is on the left surface of the sphere.",d:[["l",110,25,110,150,"B"],["l",230,25,230,150,"B"],["t",100,44,"+","m"],["t",240,44,"+","m"],["t",100,64,"+","m"],["t",240,64,"+","m"],["t",100,84,"+","m"],["t",240,84,"+","m"],["t",100,104,"+","m"],["t",240,104,"+","m"],["t",100,124,"+","m"],["t",240,124,"+","m"],["t",100,144,"+","m"],["t",240,144,"+","m"],["t",110,16,"+\u03C3","m"],["t",230,16,"+\u03C3","m"],["c",185,88,30],["t",185,92,"+\u03C1","m"],["t",198,76,"R","s"],["c",155,88,3,"F"],["t",155,80,"A","ms"],["c",132,88,3,"F"],["t",132,80,"B","ms"],["c",70,88,3,"F"],["t",70,80,"C","ms"],["c",270,88,3,"F"],["t",270,80,"D","ms"],["l",70,128,110,128,"b"],["t",90,160,"2 cm","ms"],["l",230,128,270,128,"b"],["t",250,160,"2 cm","ms"]]},o:["E\u20D7_A = E\u20D7_B ; E\u20D7_C = E\u20D7_D","|E\u20D7_A| = |E\u20D7_B| ; E\u20D7_C > E\u20D7_D","E\u20D7_A > E\u20D7_B ; E\u20D7_C = E\u20D7_D","E\u20D7_C \u2260 E\u20D7_D ; E\u20D7_A > E\u20D7_B"],c:3,s:`Each sheet gives a field \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080) pointing away from it.
Between the two sheets (A and B) the sheet fields are equal and opposite and cancel. Only the sphere acts there. Its field falls with distance, and A is closer to the sphere than B, so E_A > E_B.
Outside the sheets (C and D) the two sheet fields add to the same value \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080 on both sides.
The sphere is not centred between the sheets: it lies nearer D, so its field is larger at D than at C. Hence E_C \u2260 E_D.
The correct relation is E_C \u2260 E_D and E_A > E_B, option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:`Two small spherical balls of mass 10 g each with charges \u22122 \u03BCC and 2 \u03BCC are attached to two ends of very light rigid rod of length 20 cm. The arrangement is now placed near an infinite non-conducting charge sheet with uniform charge density of 100 \u03BCC/m\xB2 such that length of rod makes an angle of 30\xB0 with electric field generated by charge sheet. Net torque acting on the rod is
(Take \u03B5\u2080 : 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2/N m\xB2)`,o:["112 N m","11.2 N m","1.12 N m","2.24 N m"],c:2,s:`Field of a non-conducting sheet: E = \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080) = 100\xD710\u207B\u2076/(2 \xD7 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2) = 5.65\xD710\u2076 N/C (uniform).
Dipole moment p = q \xD7 length = 2\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 0.2 = 4\xD710\u207B\u2077 C m.
Torque \u03C4 = pE sin\u03B8 = 4\xD710\u207B\u2077 \xD7 5.65\xD710\u2076 \xD7 sin30\xB0 = 1.13 N m.
This is \u2248 1.12 N m, option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"A metallic ring is uniformly charged as shown in the figure. AC and BD are two mutually perpendicular diameters. Electric field due to arc AB at 'O' is 'E' in magnitude. What would be the magnitude of electric field at 'O' due to arc ABC?",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"A uniformly charged ring with centre O. A is at the top, B at the right, C at the bottom and D at the left; AC and BD are perpendicular diameters and the arc AB is marked in bold.",d:[["c",170,88,60],["l",170,28,170,148],["l",110,88,230,88],["p","M170 28 A60 60 0 0 1 230 88","B"],["t",170,20,"A","ms"],["t",170,166,"C","ms"],["t",238,92,"B"],["t",102,92,"D","e"],["t",176,102,"O","s"]]},o:["2E","\u221A2 E","Zero","E/2"],c:1,s:`The ring is divided by the diameters into four equal quarter arcs.
Arc ABC is two quarter arcs: AB and BC. Each gives a field of magnitude E at O.
The field of a symmetric arc points along its bisector. The bisectors of AB and BC are 90\xB0 apart, so the two fields are perpendicular.
Resultant = \u221A(E\xB2 + E\xB2) = \u221A2 E, option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:`Electric charge is transferred to an irregular metallic disk as shown in the figure. If \u03C3\u2081, \u03C3\u2082, \u03C3\u2083 and \u03C3\u2084 are charge densities at given points then, choose the correct answer from the options given below:
A. \u03C3\u2081 > \u03C3\u2083 ; \u03C3\u2082 = \u03C3\u2084
B. \u03C3\u2081 > \u03C3\u2082 ; \u03C3\u2083 > \u03C3\u2084
C. \u03C3\u2081 > \u03C3\u2083 > \u03C3\u2082 = \u03C3\u2084
D. \u03C3\u2081 < \u03C3\u2083 < \u03C3\u2082 = \u03C3\u2084
E. \u03C3\u2081 = \u03C3\u2083 = \u03C3\u2082 = \u03C3\u2084`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"An irregular metallic disk shaped like a lemon with a sharp tip at the right (point 1), a rounded end at the left (point 3) and flatter top and bottom (points 2 and 4).",d:[["p","M50 100 C50 60 120 50 170 60 L300 100 L170 140 C110 150 50 140 50 100 Z","f"],["c",300,100,3,"F"],["t",308,104,"1"],["c",170,60,3,"F"],["t",170,50,"2","ms"],["c",50,100,3,"F"],["t",40,104,"3","e"],["c",170,140,3,"F"],["t",170,158,"4","ms"]]},o:["B and C Only","D and E Only","A, B and C Only","A and D Only"],c:2,s:`On a charged conductor the surface charge density is larger where the surface is more sharply curved (smaller radius of curvature).
Point 1 is on the sharpest tip, so \u03C3\u2081 is the largest. Point 3 is on a less sharply curved end, so \u03C3\u2083 is smaller than \u03C3\u2081 but larger than \u03C3\u2082 and \u03C3\u2084.
Points 2 and 4 are on equally flat parts, so \u03C3\u2082 = \u03C3\u2084 and these are the smallest.
So \u03C3\u2081 > \u03C3\u2083 > \u03C3\u2082 = \u03C3\u2084. This makes A, B and C true and D, E false.
Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:`An infinitely long wire has uniform linear charge density \u03BB = 2 nC/m. The net flux through a Gaussian cube of side \u221A3 cm, if the wire passes through any two corners of the cube that are maximally displaced from each other, would be x N m\xB2 C\u207B\xB9, where x is:
[Neglect any edge effects and use {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = 9\xD710\u2079 SI units]`,o:["2.16\u03C0","6.48\u03C0","1.44\u03C0","0.72\u03C0"],c:0,s:`Two corners that are farthest apart are the ends of a body diagonal, so the wire inside the cube has length \u221A3 \xD7 side = \u221A3 \xD7 \u221A3 cm = 3 cm = 0.03 m.
Enclosed charge q = \u03BB \xD7 0.03 = 2\xD710\u207B\u2079 \xD7 0.03 = 6\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB9 C.
Flux = q/\u03B5\u2080 = q \xD7 4\u03C0 \xD7 9\xD710\u2079 = 6\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB9 \xD7 36\u03C0\xD710\u2079 = 2.16\u03C0 N m\xB2/C.
Option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:`An electron is made to enter symmetrically between two parallel and equally but oppositely charged metal plates, each of 10 cm length. The electron emerges out of the electric field region with a horizontal component of velocity 10\u2076 m/s. If the magnitude of the electric field between the plates is 9.1 V/cm, then the vertical component of velocity of electron is
(mass of electron = 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg and charge of electron = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)`,o:["16 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s","0","16 \xD7 10\u2074 m/s","1 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s"],c:0,s:`The horizontal speed stays 10\u2076 m/s, so the time in the plates is t = 0.10 m / 10\u2076 m/s = 10\u207B\u2077 s.
E = 9.1 V/cm = 910 V/m, so the vertical acceleration is a = eE/m = (1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 910)/(9.1\xD710\u207B\xB3\xB9) = 1.6\xD710\xB9\u2074 m/s\xB2.
Vertical velocity v = at = 1.6\xD710\xB9\u2074 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 1.6\xD710\u2077 m/s = 16 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s.
Option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A line charge of length {a;2} is kept at the center of an edge BC of a cube ABCDEFGH having edge length 'a' as shown in the figure. If the density of line charge is \u03BB C per unit length, then the total electric flux through all the faces of the cube will be ______. (Take, \u03B5\u2080 as the free space permittivity)",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A cube of edge a. The middle half of the bottom front edge BC is drawn in bold: this is the line charge of length a/2.",d:[["r",60,70,100,100],["r",110,30,100,100],["l",60,70,110,30],["l",160,70,210,30],["l",60,170,110,130],["l",160,170,210,130],["l",85,170,135,170,"B"],["t",52,184,"B","e"],["t",168,184,"C"],["t",110,194,"a/2","ms"],["t",54,124,"a","e"]]},o:["{\u03BBa;16\u03B5\u2080}","{\u03BBa;2\u03B5\u2080}","{\u03BBa;4\u03B5\u2080}","{\u03BBa;8\u03B5\u2080}"],c:3,s:`Charge on the line: Q = \u03BB \xD7 a/2.
The line lies along an edge, and four identical cubes meet at an edge. By symmetry the flux of this charge is shared equally among the four cubes.
Flux through this cube = (1/4) \xD7 Q/\u03B5\u2080 = (1/4)(\u03BBa/2)/\u03B5\u2080 = \u03BBa/(8\u03B5\u2080).
Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"The electric flux is \u03C6 = \u03B1\u03C3 + \u03B2\u03BB, where \u03BB and \u03C3 are linear and surface charge density, respectively. ({\u03B1;\u03B2}) represents",o:["charge","displacement","area","electric field"],c:1,s:`Flux has the dimension of (charge)/\u03B5\u2080. Both terms \u03B1\u03C3 and \u03B2\u03BB must have this dimension.
\u03C3 = charge/area, so \u03B1 must have the dimension (area)/\u03B5\u2080.
\u03BB = charge/length, so \u03B2 must have the dimension (length)/\u03B5\u2080.
\u03B1/\u03B2 = area/length = length, which is a displacement.
Option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"A point particle of charge Q is located at P along the axis of an electric dipole 1 at a distance r as shown in the figure. The point P is also on the equatorial plane of a second electric dipole 2 at a distance r. The dipoles are made of opposite charge q separated by a distance 2a. For the charge particle at P not to experience any net force, which of the following correctly describes the situation?",fig:{w:340,h:245,alt:"Point P at the top left. Dipole 1 lies to the right of P on the same horizontal line, its centre at distance r from P, with +q on the left and -q on the right, separated by 2a. Dipole 2 lies directly below P at distance r with +q on the left and -q on the right, separated by 2a.",d:[["c",80,60,4,"F"],["t",80,48,"P","ms"],["c",200,60,5,"F"],["c",280,60,5,"F"],["t",200,50,"+q","ms"],["t",280,50,"\u2212q","ms"],["t",240,30,"Dipole 1","ms"],["l",240,40,240,100,"d"],["l",84,92,236,92,"b"],["t",160,87,"r","ms"],["l",200,65,200,115,"d"],["l",280,65,280,115,"d"],["l",200,110,280,110,"b"],["t",240,128,"2a","ms"],["l",80,66,80,186,"b"],["t",88,130,"r","s"],["c",40,190,5,"F"],["c",120,190,5,"F"],["t",40,180,"+q","ms"],["t",120,180,"\u2212q","ms"],["t",80,166,"Dipole 2","ms"],["l",40,195,40,218,"d"],["l",120,195,120,218,"d"],["l",40,213,120,213,"b"],["t",80,234,"2a","ms"]]},o:["a/r ~ 3","a/r ~ 20","a/r ~ 0.5","a/r ~ 10"],c:0,s:`Let x = a/r. Charges \xB1q at \xB1a from the centre of each dipole.
Dipole 1 (P on its axis, distance r from the centre): E\u2081 = kq[1/(r\u2212a)\xB2 \u2212 1/(r+a)\xB2] = 4kqar/(r\xB2\u2212a\xB2)\xB2.
Dipole 2 (P on its equatorial plane): E\u2082 = 2kqa/(r\xB2+a\xB2)^(3/2), pointing opposite to E\u2081 for the orientations shown.
No net force on Q needs E\u2081 = E\u2082, which gives 2(1+x\xB2)^(3/2) = (1\u2212x\xB2)\xB2.
Try x = 3: left side = 2 \xD7 10^(3/2) = 63.2 and right side = 64, nearly equal. The values 20, 10 and 0.5 do not satisfy it.
So a/r \u2248 3, option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A particle of mass 'm' and charge 'q' is fastened to one end 'A' of a massless string having equilibrium length l, whose other end is fixed at point 'O'. The whole system is placed on a frictionless horizontal plane and is initially at rest. If uniform electric field is switched on along the direction as shown in the figure, then the speed of the particle when it crosses the x-axis is",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Top view: point O at the origin, a string of length l from O to the particle A at 60 degrees above the positive x-axis, and a uniform electric field E pointing along the positive x-axis.",d:[["l",80,170,310,170,"a"],["t",314,174,"x","s"],["l",80,170,80,15,"a"],["t",86,20,"y","s"],["l",80,170,140,66],["c",140,66,5,"F"],["t",148,62,"A"],["t",66,186,"O"],["t",118,114,"l"],["p","M110 170 A30 30 0 0 0 95 144"],["t",114,164,"60\xB0","s"],["l",190,150,250,150,"a"],["t",172,154,"E"]]},o:["\u221A{2qEl;m}","\u221A{qEl;m}","\u221A{qEl;4m}","\u221A{qEl;2m}"],c:1,s:`The force qE acts along +x. The string stays taut, so the particle moves on a circle of radius l about O. The tension does no work, and the plane is horizontal so gravity does no work.
At the start A is at 60\xB0 above the x-axis, so its x-coordinate is l cos60\xB0 = l/2. On the x-axis its x-coordinate is l.
Work done by the field = qE \xD7 (l \u2212 l/2) = qEl/2.
\xBDmv\xB2 = qEl/2, so v = \u221A(qEl/m).
Option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"An electric dipole of mass m, charge q, and length l is placed in a uniform electric field E\u20D7 = E\u2080\xEE. When the dipole is rotated slightly from its equilibrium position and released, the time period of its oscillations will be",o:["{1;2\u03C0}\u221A{2ml;qE\u2080}","2\u03C0\u221A{ml;qE\u2080}","2\u03C0\u221A{ml;2qE\u2080}","{1;2\u03C0}\u221A{ml;2qE\u2080}"],c:2,s:`For a small angle \u03B8 from equilibrium, the restoring torque is \u03C4 = pE\u2080 sin\u03B8 \u2248 (ql)E\u2080\u03B8.
Take mass m at each end, at distance l/2 from the centre: I = 2 \xD7 m(l/2)\xB2 = ml\xB2/2.
I\u03B8'' = \u2212qlE\u2080\u03B8 gives \u03C9\xB2 = qlE\u2080/I = 2qE\u2080/(ml).
T = 2\u03C0/\u03C9 = 2\u03C0\u221A(ml/(2qE\u2080)).
Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:`A point charge causes an electric flux of \u22122 \xD7 10\u2074 N m\xB2 C\u207B\xB9 to pass through a spherical Gaussian surface of 8.0 cm radius, centred on the charge. The value of the point charge is
(Given \u03B5\u2080 = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2N\u207B\xB9m\u207B\xB2)`,o:["17.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 C","\u221215.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 C","\u221217.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 C","15.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 C"],c:2,s:`Gauss's law: flux = q/\u03B5\u2080, so q = \u03B5\u2080 \xD7 flux.
q = 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 (\u22122\xD710\u2074) = \u22121.77\xD710\u207B\u2077 C = \u221217.7\xD710\u207B\u2078 C.
The radius of the sphere does not matter.
Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2025",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:`Two co-axial conducting cylinders of same length l with radii \u221A2 R and 2R are kept. The charge on the inner cylinder is Q and the outer cylinder is grounded. The annular region between the cylinders is filled with a material of dielectric constant k = 5. Consider an imaginary plane of the same length l at a distance R from the common axis of the cylinders. This plane is parallel to the axis of the cylinders. The cross-sectional view of this arrangement is shown in the figure.
Ignoring edge effects, the flux of the electric field through the plane is (\u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of free space)`,fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Cross-section of two co-axial cylinders with radii root 2 R and 2 R, the space between them filled with dielectric k = 5. A vertical dashed line, the imaginary plane, lies at distance R from the axis.",d:[["c",120,105,80],["c",120,105,57],["l",160,15,160,195,"d"],["t",166,24,"plane","s"],["l",120,105,160,105,"a"],["t",140,100,"R","ms"],["c",120,105,2,"F"],["l",120,105,80,65],["t",76,62,"\u221A2 R","es"],["l",120,105,64,162],["t",60,174,"2R","es"],["t",120,40,"k = 5","ms"],["t",92,92,"Q","ms"],["t",10,208,"outer cylinder earthed","s"]]},o:["{Q;30\u03B5\u2080}","{Q;15\u03B5\u2080}","{Q;60\u03B5\u2080}","{Q;120\u03B5\u2080}"],c:2,s:`In the dielectric (\u221A2R < r < 2R) the field is radial: E = Q/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080 k l r). Inside the inner cylinder and outside the earthed outer one the field is zero.
On the plane (distance R from the axis) take y along the plane from the foot of the perpendicular. Points with \u221A2R < r < 2R are those with R < |y| < \u221A3R, since r\xB2 = R\xB2 + y\xB2.
The component of E normal to the plane is E cos\u03C6 = E \xD7 R/r.
Flux = 2 \u222B [Q/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080 k l r)](R/r) l dy from y = R to \u221A3R = (Q/(\u03C0\u03B5\u2080k)) \u222B R dy/(R\xB2+y\xB2) = (Q/(\u03C0\u03B5\u2080k)) [tan\u207B\xB9(y/R)] from 1 to \u221A3.
= (Q/(\u03C0\u03B5\u2080k))(\u03C0/3 \u2212 \u03C0/4) = Q/(12\u03B5\u2080k). With k = 5 the flux is Q/(60\u03B5\u2080).
Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"An infinitely long positively charged straight thread has a linear charge density \u03BB C m\u207B\xB9. An electron revolves along a circular path having axis along the length of the wire. The graph that correctly represents the variation of the kinetic energy of electron as a function of radius of circular path from the wire is",fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"Four small graphs of kinetic energy KE against radius r. (a) a straight line rising from the origin; (b) a horizontal line; (c) a curve falling steeply then flattening; (d) a curve rising slowly then steeply.",d:[["l",25,85,140,85,"a"],["l",25,85,25,20,"a"],["t",30,29,"KE","s"],["t",143,89,"r","s"],["l",25,85,125,30],["t",82,103,"(a)","m"],["l",190,85,305,85,"a"],["l",190,85,190,20,"a"],["t",195,29,"KE","s"],["t",308,89,"r","s"],["l",190,45,295,45],["t",247,103,"(b)","m"],["l",25,195,140,195,"a"],["l",25,195,25,130,"a"],["t",30,139,"KE","s"],["t",143,199,"r","s"],["p","M33 135 Q43 183 130 187"],["t",82,213,"(c)","m"],["l",190,195,305,195,"a"],["l",190,195,190,130,"a"],["t",195,139,"KE","s"],["t",308,199,"r","s"],["p","M190 195 Q275 187 290 135"],["t",247,213,"(d)","m"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:1,s:`The field of the thread at distance r is E = \u03BB/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r), and it attracts the electron.
This force supplies the centripetal force: e\u03BB/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r) = mv\xB2/r.
So mv\xB2 = e\u03BB/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) and KE = \xBDmv\xB2 = e\u03BB/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080).
This does not depend on r, so KE against r is a horizontal straight line.
Graph (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A charge q is placed at the centre of one of the surface of a cube. The flux linked with the cube is",o:["{q;8\u03B5\u2080}","{q;4\u03B5\u2080}","{q;2\u03B5\u2080}","zero"],c:2,s:`Put a second identical cube against the first so that q is now at the centre of the box formed by the two cubes.
The total flux through the closed surface of the two-cube box is q/\u03B5\u2080. The face where q sits has no flux (the field lines lie along it), and by symmetry each cube gets half.
Flux through the cube = q/(2\u03B5\u2080).
Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"Five charges +q, +5q, \u22122q, +3q and \u22124q are situated as shown in the figure. The electric flux due to this configuration through the surface S is",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"A closed surface S enclosing the charges +5q, +q and -2q. The charge -4q is outside on the left and +3q is outside on the right.",d:[["p","M75 85 A95 55 0 1 1 265 85 A95 55 0 1 1 75 85 Z"],["c",130,65,3,"F"],["t",120,58,"+5q","ms"],["c",200,65,3,"F"],["t",205,58,"+q","s"],["c",190,110,3,"F"],["t",205,114,"\u22122q","s"],["t",150,105,"S"],["c",35,85,3,"F"],["t",35,74,"\u22124q","ms"],["c",305,85,3,"F"],["t",305,74,"+3q","ms"]]},o:["{3q;\u03B5\u2080}","{q;\u03B5\u2080}","{5q;\u03B5\u2080}","{4q;\u03B5\u2080}"],c:3,s:`By Gauss's law the flux through S depends only on the charge enclosed by S.
Inside S: +5q, +q and \u22122q, so the enclosed charge is 5q + q \u2212 2q = 4q.
The charges +3q and \u22124q lie outside S and give no net flux.
Flux = 4q/\u03B5\u2080. Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"C\u2081 and C\u2082 are two hollow concentric cubes enclosing charges 2Q and 3Q respectively as shown in the figure. The ratio of electric flux passing through C\u2081 and C\u2082 is",fig:{w:340,h:230,alt:"Two concentric cubes. The inner cube C1 contains the charge 2Q. The charge 3Q sits in the space between the inner cube and the outer cube C2.",d:[["r",40,60,140,140],["r",75,25,140,140],["l",40,60,75,25],["l",180,60,215,25],["l",40,200,75,165],["l",180,200,215,165],["r",95,115,50,50],["r",109,101,50,50],["l",95,115,109,101],["l",145,115,159,101],["l",95,165,109,151],["l",145,165,159,151],["t",120,144,"2Q","ms"],["c",62,150,3,"F"],["t",68,154,"3Q"],["t",100,178,"C\u2081","s"],["t",190,196,"C\u2082","s"]]},o:["3 : 2","5 : 2","2 : 5","2 : 3"],c:2,s:`The flux through a closed surface is (charge enclosed)/\u03B5\u2080.
C\u2081, the inner cube, encloses only 2Q: flux = 2Q/\u03B5\u2080.
C\u2082, the outer cube, encloses 2Q + 3Q = 5Q: flux = 5Q/\u03B5\u2080.
Ratio = 2Q : 5Q = 2 : 5. Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"Two charges of 5Q and \u22122Q are situated at the points (3a, 0) and (\u22125a, 0) respectively. The electric flux through a sphere of radius '4a' having center at origin is",o:["{2Q;\u03B5\u2080}","{7Q;\u03B5\u2080}","{5Q;\u03B5\u2080}","{3Q;\u03B5\u2080}"],c:2,s:`The charge 5Q is at distance 3a from the origin. This is less than 4a, so it is inside the sphere.
The charge \u22122Q is at distance 5a from the origin. This is more than 4a, so it is outside.
Flux = (enclosed charge)/\u03B5\u2080 = 5Q/\u03B5\u2080.
Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"An electric field is given by (6\xEE + 5\u0135 + 3k\u0302) N/C. The electric flux through a surface of area 30\xEE m\xB2 lying in YZ-plane (in SI unit) is",o:["150","60","180","90"],c:2,s:`The area vector of a surface in the YZ-plane is along x, here A = 30\xEE m\xB2.
Flux = E \xB7 A = (6\xEE + 5\u0135 + 3k\u0302) \xB7 (30\xEE) = 6 \xD7 30 = 180 N m\xB2/C.
Only the x-component of the field passes through the surface. Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A particle of charge \u2212q and mass m moves in a circle of radius r around an infinitely long line charge of linear density +\u03BB. Then time period will be given as (Consider k as Coulomb's constant)",o:["T = 2\u03C0r\u221A{m;2k\u03BBq}","T = {1;2\u03C0}\u221A{2k\u03BBq;m}","T\xB2 = {4\u03C0\xB2m;2k\u03BBq}r\xB3","T = {1;2\u03C0r}\u221A{m;2k\u03BBq}"],c:0,s:`Field of a line charge at distance r: E = 2k\u03BB/r.
This attractive force provides the centripetal force: q \xD7 2k\u03BB/r = mv\xB2/r, so v = \u221A(2k\u03BBq/m).
T = 2\u03C0r/v = 2\u03C0r\u221A(m/(2k\u03BBq)).
Option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Two charges q and 3q are separated by a distance 'r' in air. At a distance x from charge q, the resultant electric field is zero. The value of x is",o:["{(1+\u221A3);r}","r(1+\u221A3)","{r;3(1+\u221A3)}","{r;(1+\u221A3)}"],c:3,s:`Take the two charges as like charges; then the field is zero at a point between them, at x from q and (r \u2212 x) from 3q.
kq/x\xB2 = k(3q)/(r \u2212 x)\xB2, so (r \u2212 x)/x = \u221A3.
r = x(1 + \u221A3), so x = r/(1 + \u221A3).
Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"A dipole comprises of two charged particles of identical magnitude q and opposite in nature. The mass 'm' of the positive charged particle is half of the mass of the negative charged particle. The two charges are separated by a distance 'l'. If the dipole is placed in a uniform electric field E\u20D7 in such a way that dipole axis makes a very small angle with the electric field E\u20D7. The angular frequency of the oscillations of the dipole when released is given by",o:["\u221A{8qE;3ml}","\u221A{8qE;ml}","\u221A{4qE;3ml}","\u221A{4qE;ml}"],c:2,s:`The positive particle has mass m and the negative one 2m, separated by l.
The centre of mass is 2l/3 from the positive particle and l/3 from the negative one.
Moment of inertia about it: I = m(2l/3)\xB2 + 2m(l/3)\xB2 = 2ml\xB2/3.
For a small angle \u03B8 the torque is qEl\u03B8, so \u03C9\xB2 = qEl/I = 3qE/(2ml).
Of the four given options, (c) \u221A(4qE/(3ml)) is the closest, and it is the option marked in the book.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"Graphical variation of electric field due to a uniformly charged insulating solid sphere of radius R, with distance r from the centre O is represented by",fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"Four graphs of electric field E against distance r with the sphere radius R marked. (a) E rises as a curve to R then stays constant; (b) E rises as a curve to R then falls linearly; (c) E is constant up to R then falls as a curve; (d) E rises linearly to R then falls as a curve.",d:[["l",25,80,140,80,"a"],["l",25,80,25,15,"a"],["t",30,24,"E","s"],["t",143,84,"r","s"],["l",80,30,80,80,"d"],["t",80,91,"R","ms"],["p","M25 80 Q55 30 80 30"],["l",80,30,135,30],["t",82,106,"(a)","m"],["l",190,80,305,80,"a"],["l",190,80,190,15,"a"],["t",195,24,"E","s"],["t",308,84,"r","s"],["l",245,30,245,80,"d"],["t",245,91,"R","ms"],["p","M190 80 Q220 30 245 30"],["l",245,30,300,55],["t",247,106,"(b)","m"],["l",25,190,140,190,"a"],["l",25,190,25,125,"a"],["t",30,134,"E","s"],["t",143,194,"r","s"],["l",80,140,80,190,"d"],["t",80,201,"R","ms"],["l",25,140,80,140],["p","M80 140 Q90 176 135 182"],["t",82,216,"(c)","m"],["l",190,190,305,190,"a"],["l",190,190,190,125,"a"],["t",195,134,"E","s"],["t",308,194,"r","s"],["l",245,140,245,190,"d"],["t",245,201,"R","ms"],["l",190,190,245,140],["p","M245 140 Q255 176 300 182"],["t",247,216,"(d)","m"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:3,s:`Inside a uniformly charged solid sphere E = \u03C1r/(3\u03B5\u2080), which rises linearly from zero at the centre.
At the surface E has its maximum value \u03C1R/(3\u03B5\u2080).
Outside the sphere E = kQ/r\xB2, which falls as 1/r\xB2 and equals the same value at r = R.
So the graph is a straight line rising up to R, then a falling 1/r\xB2 curve.
Graph (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"Two charges each magnitude 0.01 C and separated by a distance of 0.4 mm constitute an electric dipole. If the dipole is placed in a uniform electric field 'E\u20D7' of 10 dyne/C magnitude, such that the dipole makes an angle of 30\xB0 with E\u20D7, the magnitude of torque acting on the dipole is",o:["1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 Nm","1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 Nm","4.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 Nm","2.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 Nm"],c:3,s:`Dipole moment p = q \xD7 d = 0.01 \xD7 0.4\xD710\u207B\xB3 = 4\xD710\u207B\u2076 C m.
E = 10 dyne/C = 10 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N/C = 10\u207B\u2074 N/C.
Torque \u03C4 = pE sin\u03B8 = 4\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 sin30\xB0 = 2\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 N m.
Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"The electric field due to a short electric dipole at a large distance (r) from center of dipole on the equatorial plane varies with distance as",o:["{1;r\xB2}","{1;r}","{1;r\xB3}","r"],c:2,s:`At a large distance r on the equatorial plane of a short dipole, E = kp/r\xB3.
So E is proportional to 1/r\xB3. (On the axis it is 2kp/r\xB3, also proportional to 1/r\xB3.)
Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"Let \u03C3 be the uniform surface charge density of two infinite thin plane sheets shown in the figure (n\u0302 is the unit vector normal to the sheets, pointing from region I towards region III). Then the electric fields E_I, E_II and E_III in three different regions are",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"Two parallel sheets, each with surface charge density plus sigma, a distance d apart. Region I is to the left of both, region II is between them and region III is to the right. The unit vector n points to the right.",d:[["l",120,25,120,120,"B"],["l",220,25,220,120,"B"],["t",120,18,"+\u03C3","ms"],["t",220,18,"+\u03C3","ms"],["t",60,75,"I","m"],["t",170,62,"II","m"],["t",280,75,"III","m"],["l",120,100,220,100,"b"],["t",170,96,"d","ms"],["l",20,138,70,138,"a"],["t",76,142,"n\u0302"]]},o:["E\u20D7_I = 0, E\u20D7_II = {\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}n\u0302, E\u20D7_III = 0","E\u20D7_I = {2\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}n\u0302, E\u20D7_II = 0, E\u20D7_III = {2\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}n\u0302","E\u20D7_I = \u2212{\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}n\u0302, E\u20D7_II = 0, E\u20D7_III = {\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}n\u0302","E\u20D7_I = {\u03C3;2\u03B5\u2080}n\u0302, E\u20D7_II = 0, E\u20D7_III = {\u03C3;2\u03B5\u2080}n\u0302"],c:2,s:`Each positive sheet gives a field \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080) pointing away from itself.
Region I (left of both): both fields point to the left, so E = \u2212\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080 n\u0302.
Region II (between): the two fields are equal and opposite, so E = 0.
Region III (right of both): both fields point to the right, so E = +\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080 n\u0302.
Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"A point charge of 10 \u03BCC is placed at the origin. At what location on the X-axis should a point charge of 40 \u03BCC be placed so that the net electric field is zero at x = 2 cm on the X-axis?",o:["x = \u22124 cm","x = 4 cm","x = 8 cm","x = 6 cm"],c:3,s:`At x = 2 cm the 10 \u03BCC charge gives a field along +x proportional to 10/2\xB2 = 2.5 (units of k, with \u03BCC and cm).
The 40 \u03BCC charge must give an equal field along \u2212x, so it must lie on the +x side of the point, at distance d from it: 40/d\xB2 = 2.5.
d\xB2 = 16, so d = 4 cm.
Its position is x = 2 + 4 = 6 cm. Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"In a cuboid of dimension 2L \xD7 2L \xD7 L, a charge q is placed at the center of the surface 'S' having area of 4L\xB2. The flux through the opposite surface to 'S' is given by",o:["{q;12\u03B5\u2080}","{q;2\u03B5\u2080}","{q;3\u03B5\u2080}","{q;6\u03B5\u2080}"],c:3,s:`The opposite face is a 2L \xD7 2L square at distance L from the charge, which sits at the centre of face S.
Consider a cube of side 2L with q at its centre: each face is also a 2L \xD7 2L square at distance L from q. So the flux through our opposite face equals the flux through one face of that cube.
By symmetry each of the 6 faces of the cube gets 1/6 of q/\u03B5\u2080.
Flux = q/(6\u03B5\u2080). Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Electric field in a certain region is given by E\u20D7 = ({A;x\xB2}\xEE + {B;y\xB3}\u0135). The SI unit of A and B are",o:["Nm\xB2C\u207B\xB9 ; Nm\xB3C\u207B\xB9","Nm\xB3C ; Nm\xB2C","Nm\xB2C ; Nm\xB3C","Nm\xB3C\u207B\xB9 ; Nm\xB2C\u207B\xB9"],c:0,s:`E is in N/C, so each term A/x\xB2 and B/y\xB3 must be in N/C.
A = E \xD7 x\xB2 has unit (N/C) \xD7 m\xB2 = N m\xB2 C\u207B\xB9.
B = E \xD7 y\xB3 has unit (N/C) \xD7 m\xB3 = N m\xB3 C\u207B\xB9.
Option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"As shown in the figure, a point charge Q is placed at the centre of conducting spherical shell of inner radius a and outer radius b. The electric field due to charge Q in three different regions I, II and III is given by (I : r < a, II : a < r < b, III : r > b)",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"A point charge Q at the centre of a conducting spherical shell with inner radius a and outer radius b. Region I is inside the cavity, region II is within the shell material and region III is outside.",d:[["c",150,95,40],["c",150,95,75],["c",150,95,4,"F"],["t",142,92,"Q","e"],["l",150,95,178,123,"a"],["t",176,136,"a","s"],["l",150,95,203,42,"a"],["t",206,40,"b","s"],["t",166,80,"I","s"],["t",150,42,"II","ms"],["t",250,98,"III","m"]]},o:["E_I = 0, E_II = 0, E_III = 0","E_I \u2260 0, E_II = 0, E_III = 0","E_I = 0, E_II = 0, E_III \u2260 0","E_I \u2260 0, E_II = 0, E_III \u2260 0"],c:3,s:`Q at the centre induces \u2212Q on the inner surface (r = a) and +Q on the outer surface (r = b).
Region I (r < a): only Q is enclosed, so E = kQ/r\xB2 \u2260 0.
Region II (inside the conducting material): E = 0.
Region III (r > b): the enclosed charge is Q (\u2212Q + Q + Q), so E = kQ/r\xB2 \u2260 0.
So E_I \u2260 0, E_II = 0, E_III \u2260 0. Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A uniform electric field E = (8m/e) V/m is created between two parallel plates of length 1 m as shown in the figure (where m = mass of electron and e = charge of electron). An electron enters the field symmetrically between the plates with a speed of 2 m/s. The angle of the deviation (\u03B8) of the path of the electron as it comes out of the field will be ______.",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Two parallel plates of length 1 m, the upper positive and the lower negative. The electric field E points down. An electron enters at the middle with speed 2 m/s, bends upward towards the positive plate and leaves at an angle theta to its original direction.",d:[["l",60,50,280,50,"B"],["l",60,120,280,120,"B"],["t",70,44,"+","m"],["t",95,44,"+","m"],["t",120,44,"+","m"],["t",145,44,"+","m"],["t",170,44,"+","m"],["t",195,44,"+","m"],["t",220,44,"+","m"],["t",245,44,"+","m"],["t",270,44,"+","m"],["t",70,138,"\u2212","m"],["t",95,138,"\u2212","m"],["t",120,138,"\u2212","m"],["t",145,138,"\u2212","m"],["t",170,138,"\u2212","m"],["t",195,138,"\u2212","m"],["t",220,138,"\u2212","m"],["t",245,138,"\u2212","m"],["t",270,138,"\u2212","m"],["l",60,28,280,28,"b"],["t",170,22,"1 m","ms"],["l",10,85,58,85,"a"],["t",10,76,"2 m/s","s"],["p","M60 85 Q190 85 280 58"],["p","M280 58 L322 46","d"],["p","M280 58 L330 58","d"],["t",306,72,"\u03B8"],["l",100,92,100,114,"a"],["t",106,108,"E"]]},o:["tan\u207B\xB9(4)","tan\u207B\xB9(2)","tan\u207B\xB9({1;3})","tan\u207B\xB9(3)"],c:1,s:`Acceleration of the electron: a = eE/m = e \xD7 (8m/e)/m = 8 m/s\xB2.
Time between the plates: t = length/speed = 1/2 = 0.5 s (horizontal speed stays 2 m/s).
Vertical speed on leaving: v_y = at = 8 \xD7 0.5 = 4 m/s.
tan\u03B8 = v_y/v_x = 4/2 = 2, so \u03B8 = tan\u207B\xB9(2).
Option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:`A spherically symmetric charge distribution is considered with charge density varying as
\u03C1(r) = \u03C1\u2080({3;4} \u2212 {r;R}) for r \u2264 R, and zero for r > R,
where r (r < R) is the distance from the centre O (as shown in the figure). The electric field at point P will be`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A sphere of radius R with centre O. The point P lies inside the sphere at distance r from O.",d:[["c",150,100,75],["c",150,100,3,"F"],["t",140,114,"O","s"],["l",150,100,205,54],["c",205,54,4,"F"],["t",212,52,"P"],["t",168,66,"r","s"],["l",150,100,97,153,"d"],["t",100,140,"R","es"]]},o:["{\u03C1\u2080r;4\u03B5\u2080}({3;4} \u2212 {r;R})","{\u03C1\u2080r;3\u03B5\u2080}({3;4} \u2212 {r;R})","{\u03C1\u2080r;4\u03B5\u2080}(1 \u2212 {r;R})","{\u03C1\u2080r;5\u03B5\u2080}(1 \u2212 {r;R})"],c:2,s:`P is inside the sphere, so use a Gaussian sphere of radius r.
Charge inside = \u222B 4\u03C0r'\xB2 \u03C1\u2080(3/4 \u2212 r'/R) dr' from 0 to r = 4\u03C0\u03C1\u2080[r\xB3/4 \u2212 r\u2074/(4R)] = \u03C0\u03C1\u2080r\xB3(1 \u2212 r/R).
Gauss: E \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2 = Q_in/\u03B5\u2080, so E = \u03C1\u2080 r (1 \u2212 r/R)/(4\u03B5\u2080).
Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A vertical electric field of magnitude 4.9 \xD7 10\u2075 N/C just prevents a water droplet of a mass 0.1 g from falling. The value of charge on the droplet will be",o:["1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 C","2.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 C","3.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 C","0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 C"],c:1,s:`The droplet is at rest, so the electric force balances its weight: qE = mg.
q = mg/E = (0.1\xD710\u207B\xB3 kg \xD7 9.8 m/s\xB2)/(4.9\xD710\u2075 N/C) = 2.0\xD710\u207B\u2079 C.
Option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"A long cylindrical volume contains a uniformly distributed charge of density \u03C1. The radius of cylindrical volume is R. A charge particle (q) revolves around the cylinder in a circular path. The kinetic energy of the particle is",o:["{\u03C1qR\xB2;4\u03B5\u2080}","{\u03C1qR\xB2;2\u03B5\u2080}","{q\u03C1;4\u03B5\u2080R\xB2}","{4\u03B5\u2080R\xB2;q\u03C1}"],c:0,s:`Outside the cylinder (distance r > R), Gauss's law gives E \xD7 2\u03C0rl = \u03C1\u03C0R\xB2l/\u03B5\u2080, so E = \u03C1R\xB2/(2\u03B5\u2080r).
The electric force supplies the centripetal force: q\u03C1R\xB2/(2\u03B5\u2080r) = mv\xB2/r.
So mv\xB2 = q\u03C1R\xB2/(2\u03B5\u2080) and KE = \xBDmv\xB2 = \u03C1qR\xB2/(4\u03B5\u2080), independent of r.
Option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"In the figure, a very large plane sheet of positive charge is shown. P\u2081 and P\u2082 are two points at distance l and 2l from the charge distribution. If \u03C3 is the surface charge density, then the magnitude of electric fields E\u2081 and E\u2082 at P\u2081 and P\u2082 respectively are",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"A large vertical sheet of positive charge. Point P1 is at distance l to its right and point P2 at distance 2l; the fields E1 and E2 point away from the sheet.",d:[["l",60,15,60,155,"B"],["t",48,34,"+","m"],["t",48,59,"+","m"],["t",48,84,"+","m"],["t",48,109,"+","m"],["t",48,134,"+","m"],["c",160,85,4,"F"],["c",260,85,4,"F"],["t",160,100,"P\u2081","ms"],["t",260,100,"P\u2082","ms"],["l",166,85,205,85,"a"],["t",186,78,"E\u2081","ms"],["l",266,85,305,85,"a"],["t",286,78,"E\u2082","ms"],["l",60,122,160,122,"b"],["t",110,117,"l","ms"],["l",60,148,260,148,"b"],["t",160,143,"2l","ms"]]},o:["E\u2081 = {\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}, E\u2082 = {\u03C3;2\u03B5\u2080}","E\u2081 = {2\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}, E\u2082 = {\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}","E\u2081 = E\u2082 = {\u03C3;2\u03B5\u2080}","E\u2081 = E\u2082 = {\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}"],c:2,s:`A very large uniformly charged plane sheet gives a uniform field E = \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080) on each side.
The field does not depend on the distance from the sheet.
So E\u2081 = E\u2082 = \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080). Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"If a charge q is placed at the centre of a closed hemispherical non-conducting surface, the total flux passing through the flat surface would be",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"A closed hemispherical surface standing on its flat circular base, with the charge q at the centre of the base.",d:[["p","M60 140 A90 90 0 0 1 240 140"],["p","M60 140 A90 22 0 0 0 240 140"],["p","M60 140 A90 22 0 0 1 240 140","d"],["c",150,138,4,"F"],["t",158,132,"q"]]},o:["{q;\u03B5\u2080}","{q;2\u03B5\u2080}","{q;4\u03B5\u2080}","{q;2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}"],c:1,s:`The hemisphere together with its flat base forms a closed surface enclosing q, so the total flux is q/\u03B5\u2080.
Treat q as lying at the centre, just inside the base: the flat base then takes up half of all directions (solid angle 2\u03C0) and the curved surface the other half.
By symmetry the two parts get equal flux, so the flux through the flat surface is q/(2\u03B5\u2080). Option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"The three charges {q;2}, q and {q;2} are placed at the corners A, B and C of a square of side 'a' as shown in the figure. The magnitude of electric field (E) at the corner D of the square is",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"A square ABCD of side a. A (top left) holds charge q/2, B (bottom left) holds q, C (bottom right) holds q/2, and D is the empty top right corner.",d:[["r",110,30,110,110],["c",110,30,4,"F"],["c",110,140,4,"F"],["c",220,140,4,"F"],["c",220,30,4],["t",102,26,"A  q/2","e"],["t",102,156,"B  q","e"],["t",228,156,"C  q/2"],["t",228,26,"D"]]},o:["{q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2}({1;\u221A2}+{1;2})","{q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2}(1+{1;\u221A2})","{q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2}(1\u2212{1;\u221A2})","{q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2}({1;\u221A2}\u2212{1;2})"],c:0,s:`Let k = 1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080). Field at D (top right) from each charge:
From A (q/2, distance a): kq/(2a\xB2), pointing along +x (away from A).
From C (q/2, distance a): kq/(2a\xB2), pointing along +y.
From B (q, distance a\u221A2): kq/(2a\xB2) along the diagonal, so each of its x and y components is kq/(2a\xB2) \xD7 1/\u221A2.
Each total component = (kq/2a\xB2)(1 + 1/\u221A2), so E = \u221A2 \xD7 (kq/2a\xB2)(1 + 1/\u221A2) = (kq/a\xB2)(1/\u221A2 + 1/2).
Option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Two point charges A and B of magnitude +8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C and \u22128 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C respectively are placed at a distance d apart. The electric field at the middle point O between the charges is 6.4 \xD7 10\u2074 N C\u207B\xB9. The distance 'd' between the point charges A and B is",o:["2.0 m","3.0 m","1.0 m","4.0 m"],c:1,s:`At the midpoint both fields point the same way (away from +8 \u03BCC, towards \u22128 \u03BCC), so they add.
Each charge is at distance d/2: E = 2 \xD7 kq/(d/2)\xB2 = 8kq/d\xB2.
d\xB2 = 8kq/E = 8 \xD7 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 8\xD710\u207B\u2076 / (6.4\xD710\u2074) = 9.
d = 3.0 m. Option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A positive charge particle of 100 mg is thrown in opposite direction to a uniform electric field of strength 1 \xD7 10\u2075 N C\u207B\xB9. If the charge on the particle is 40 \u03BCC and the initial velocity is 200 m s\u207B\xB9, how much distance it will travel before coming to rest momentarily?",o:["1 m","5 m","10 m","0.5 m"],c:3,s:`Force on the charge F = qE = 40\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 1\xD710\u2075 = 4 N, along E, so it opposes the motion.
Deceleration a = F/m = 4/(100\xD710\u207B\u2076 kg) = 4\xD710\u2074 m/s\xB2.
Distance = v\xB2/(2a) = 200\xB2/(2 \xD7 4\xD710\u2074) = 4\xD710\u2074/(8\xD710\u2074) = 0.5 m.
Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Force between point charges",q:"Two points charges Q each are placed at a distance d apart. A third point charge q is placed at a distance x from mid-point on the perpendicular bisector. The value of x at which charge q will experience the maximum coulomb's force is",o:["x = d","x = {d;2}","x = {d;\u221A2}","x = {d;2\u221A2}"],c:3,s:`Each Q pulls or pushes q with force kQq/(x\xB2+d\xB2/4). The components along the line joining the two Q's cancel; the components along the bisector add.
F = 2kQq\xB7x/(x\xB2+d\xB2/4)^(3/2).
For maximum F, dF/dx = 0, which gives (x\xB2+d\xB2/4) \u2212 3x\xB2 = 0, so 2x\xB2 = d\xB2/4.
x\xB2 = d\xB2/8, so x = d/(2\u221A2). Answer (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A cube is placed inside an electric field, E\u20D7 = 150y\xB2\u0135. The side of the cube is 0.5 m and is placed in the field as shown in the given figure. The charge inside the cube is",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"A cube of side 0.5 m resting with its lower face on the xz plane at the origin, with x to the right, y up and z toward the viewer",d:[["l",30,170,310,170,"da"],["l",30,170,30,20,"da"],["l",30,170,6,194,"da"],["r",130,90,80,80],["r",154,70,80,80],["l",130,90,154,70],["l",210,90,234,70],["l",130,170,154,150],["l",210,170,234,150],["t",300,160,"x","s"],["t",38,26,"y","s"],["t",2,190,"z","s"]]},o:["8.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 C","3.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 C","3.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C","8.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C"],c:0,s:`E has only a y-component, so only the two faces perpendicular to y carry flux. The cube sits with one face on y = 0 and the opposite face at y = 0.5 m.
At y = 0, E = 0, so that face gives no flux. At y = 0.5 m, E = 150 \xD7 0.25 = 37.5 N/C and the face area is 0.5 \xD7 0.5 = 0.25 m\xB2.
Net outward flux \u03C6 = 37.5 \xD7 0.25 = 9.375 N m\xB2/C.
Gauss's law: q = \u03B5\u2080\u03C6 = 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 9.375 \u2248 8.3\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB9 C. Answer (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"A solid metal sphere of radius R having charge q is enclosed inside the concentric spherical shell of inner radius a and outer radius b (R < a < b). The approximate variation of electric field E\u20D7 as a function of distance r from centre O is given by",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"Four graphs of electric field E against distance r with marks at R, a and b; (a) zero until R then decays all the way, (b) zero until b then decays, (c) decays from R to a, zero between a and b, then decays again beyond b, (d) rises up to R then decays, zero between a and b, then decays beyond b",d:[["l",26,94,159,94,"a"],["l",26,94,26,10,"a"],["t",6,16,"(a)","s"],["t",159,107,"r","es"],["t",30,12,"E","s"],["l",56,91,56,97],["l",91,91,91,97],["l",126,91,126,97],["t",56,107,"R","ms"],["t",91,107,"a","ms"],["t",126,107,"b","ms"],["l",56,94,56,30],["p","M56,30 C64,60 96,86 152,91"],["l",196,94,329,94,"a"],["l",196,94,196,10,"a"],["t",176,16,"(b)","s"],["t",329,107,"r","es"],["t",200,12,"E","s"],["l",226,91,226,97],["l",261,91,261,97],["l",296,91,296,97],["t",226,107,"R","ms"],["t",261,107,"a","ms"],["t",296,107,"b","ms"],["l",296,94,296,30],["p","M296,30 C302,60 321,86 322,91"],["l",26,214,159,214,"a"],["l",26,214,26,130,"a"],["t",6,136,"(c)","s"],["t",159,227,"r","es"],["t",30,132,"E","s"],["l",56,211,56,217],["l",91,211,91,217],["l",126,211,126,217],["t",56,227,"R","ms"],["t",91,227,"a","ms"],["t",126,227,"b","ms"],["l",56,214,56,150],["p","M56,150 C62,175 81,212 91,214"],["l",126,214,126,164],["p","M126,164 C132,186 151,206 152,211"],["l",196,214,329,214,"a"],["l",196,214,196,130,"a"],["t",176,136,"(d)","s"],["t",329,227,"r","es"],["t",200,132,"E","s"],["l",226,211,226,217],["l",261,211,261,217],["l",296,211,296,217],["t",226,227,"R","ms"],["t",261,227,"a","ms"],["t",296,227,"b","ms"],["p","M196,214 C206,211 214,170 226,156"],["l",226,214,226,156],["p","M226,156 C232,178 251,212 261,214"],["l",296,214,296,164],["p","M296,164 C302,186 321,206 322,211"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:2,s:`Inside the metal sphere (r < R) the field is zero.
For R < r < a the field is that of charge q: E = q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2), falling as 1/r\xB2.
Inside the metal of the shell (a < r < b) the field is zero.
For r > b the field is again q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2), starting again from a finite value at r = b.
This is graph (c): zero, a drop-off from R to a, zero from a to b, then a fresh drop-off beyond b. Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"A uniformly charged disc of radius R having surface charge density \u03C3 is placed in the xy plane with its center at the origin. Find the electric field intensity along the z-axis at a distance Z from origin.",o:["E = {2\u03B5\u2080;\u03C3}({1;\u221A(Z\xB2+R\xB2)} + Z)","E = {\u03C3;2\u03B5\u2080}(1 + {Z;\u221A(Z\xB2+R\xB2)})","E = {\u03C3;2\u03B5\u2080}(1 \u2212 {Z;\u221A(Z\xB2+R\xB2)})","E = {\u03C3;2\u03B5\u2080}({1;Z\xB2+R\xB2} + {1;Z\xB2})"],c:2,s:`Take a thin ring of radius x and width dx on the disc: dq = \u03C3\xB72\u03C0x dx.
Its field on the axis is dE = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)\xB7dq\xB7Z/(Z\xB2+x\xB2)^(3/2).
Integrate x from 0 to R: E = (\u03C3Z/2\u03B5\u2080)\xB7[1/Z \u2212 1/\u221A(Z\xB2+R\xB2)].
So E = (\u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080)(1 \u2212 Z/\u221A(Z\xB2+R\xB2)). For R \u2192 \u221E this gives \u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080, as it should. Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"In Millikan's oil drop experiment, what is viscous force acting on an uncharged drop of radius 2.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m and density 1.2 \xD7 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3? Take viscosity of liquid = 1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N s m\u207B\xB2. (Neglect buoyancy due to air).",o:["5.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 N","3.9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 N","1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 N","3.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 N"],c:1,s:`The drop falls at constant (terminal) speed, so the viscous force equals its weight (buoyancy neglected).
Mass m = (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3\u03C1 = (4/3) \xD7 3.14 \xD7 (2\xD710\u207B\u2075)\xB3 \xD7 1.2\xD710\xB3 \u2248 4.0\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB9 kg.
F = mg = 4.0\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB9 \xD7 9.8 \u2248 3.9\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 N. Answer (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Figure shows a rod AB, which is bent in a 120\xB0 circular arc of radius R. A charge (\u2212Q) is uniformly distributed over rod AB. What is the electric field E\u20D7 at the centre of curvature O?",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"Circular arc AB of radius R centred at O, subtending 120 degrees, symmetric about the x-axis, with OA and OB each at 60 degrees to the axis",d:[["l",60,236,60,6,"a"],["l",60,120,310,120,"a"],["p","M115.0,24.7 A110,110 0 0 1 115.0,215.3","B"],["l",60,120,115,24.7,"d"],["l",60,120,115,215.3,"d"],["t",48,124,"O","es"],["t",119,22.7,"A"],["t",119,227.3,"B"],["t",92,106,"60\xB0","s"],["t",92,142,"60\xB0","s"],["t",300,114,"x","es"],["t",66,14,"y","s"],["t",88,170,"R","s"],["t",190,124,"\u2212Q on arc","s"]]},o:["{3\u221A3Q;8\u03C0\xB2\u03B5\u2080R\xB2}(\xEE)","{3\u221A3Q;8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2}(\xEE)","\u2212{3\u221A3Q;8\u03C0\xB2\u03B5\u2080R\xB2}(\xEE)","{3\u221A3Q;16\u03C0\xB2\u03B5\u2080R\xB2}(\xEE)"],c:0,s:`The arc subtends 120\xB0 at O, so its half-angle is 60\xB0. Charge per unit length \u03BB = Q/(R\xB72\u03C0/3) = 3Q/(2\u03C0R).
For a symmetric arc the field at the centre is E = (2k\u03BB/R)\xB7sin60\xB0 along the axis of symmetry (the sideways parts cancel).
E = 2 \xD7 (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) \xD7 3Q/(2\u03C0R) \xD7 (\u221A3/2) \xD7 1/R = 3\u221A3Q/(8\u03C0\xB2\u03B5\u2080R\xB2).
The charge is negative, so the field points towards the arc, i.e. along +x, which is \xEE. Answer (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Choose the incorrect statement.
(A) The electric lines of force entering into a Gaussian surface provide negative flux.
(B) A charge 'q' is placed at the centre of a cube. The flux through all the faces will be the same.
(C) In a uniform electric field net flux through a closed Gaussian surface containing no net charge, is zero.
(D) When electric field is parallel to a Gaussian surface, it provides a finite non-zero flux.
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["(B) and (D) only","(C) and (D) only","(A) and (C) only","(D) only"],c:3,s:`(A) is true: flux = E\xB7A is negative where lines enter the surface.
(B) is true: by symmetry every face of the cube gets the same share, q/6\u03B5\u2080.
(C) is true: as many lines leave as enter, so the net flux is zero when no charge is enclosed.
(D) is false: if E is parallel to the surface, the angle between E and the area vector is 90\xB0, so E\xB7A = 0.
Only (D) is incorrect. Answer (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 22 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"An electric dipole is placed on x-axis in proximity to a line charge of linear charge density 3.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C/m. Line charge is placed on z-axis and positive and negative charge of dipole is at a distance of 10 mm and 12 mm from the origin respectively. If total force of 4 N is exerted on the dipole, find out the amount of positive or negative charge of the dipole.",o:["4.44 \u03BCC","8.8 \u03BCC","0.485 mC","815.1 nC"],c:0,s:`Field of a line charge: E = 2k\u03BB/r = \u03BB/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r).
Both charges lie on the same side of the line. The +q at 10 mm is pushed outwards and the \u2212q at 12 mm is pulled inwards, so the forces oppose each other along the x-axis.
F = q\xB72k\u03BB(1/0.010 \u2212 1/0.012) = q \xD7 2 \xD7 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 3\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 (100 \u2212 83.3) = q \xD7 9\xD710\u2075 N/C.
q = 4/(9\xD710\u2075) = 4.44\xD710\u207B\u2076 C = 4.44 \u03BCC. Answer (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"Two ideal electric dipoles A and B, having their dipole moment p\u2081 and p\u2082 respectively are placed on a plane with their centres O as shown in the figure. At point C on the axis of dipole A, the resultant electric field is making an angle of 37\xB0 with the axis. The ratio of the dipole moment of A and B, {p\u2081;p\u2082} is (Take sin37\xB0 = {3;5})",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Dipole A along the horizontal axis through O with point C on that axis far to the right, and dipole B vertical through O with its negative charge on top and positive charge below",d:[["l",40,100,300,100],["l",150,45,150,155],["c",110,100,3,"F"],["c",190,100,3,"F"],["c",150,60,3,"F"],["c",150,140,3,"F"],["t",104,92,"A","es"],["t",100,115,"\u2212","es"],["t",196,115,"+","s"],["t",158,64,"\u2212  B","s"],["t",158,148,"+","s"],["t",150,116,"O","ms"],["t",295,92,"C","es"]]},o:["{4;3}","{3;2}","{2;3}","{3;8}"],c:2,s:`Point C is on the axis of A and on the equatorial line of B.
Field of A at C (axial): E\u2081 = 2kp\u2081/r\xB3, along the axis.
Field of B at C (equatorial): E\u2082 = kp\u2082/r\xB3, perpendicular to the axis.
The resultant makes 37\xB0 with the axis, so tan37\xB0 = E\u2082/E\u2081 = 3/4.
kp\u2082/(2kp\u2081) = 3/4, so p\u2082/p\u2081 = 3/2 and p\u2081/p\u2082 = 2/3. Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"What will be the magnitude of electric field at point O as shown in figure? Each side of the figure is l and perpendicular to each other.",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Charges at the eight points around O of a square grid of side l: A minus q, B plus q, C plus 2q, D plus q, G plus 2q, H minus q, F plus q, E plus 2q",d:[["l",115,55,115,110],["l",115,110,170,110],["l",170,110,170,55],["l",170,55,225,55],["l",170,110,225,110],["l",225,110,225,165],["l",170,110,170,165],["l",170,165,115,165],["c",115,55,3,"F"],["c",115,110,3,"F"],["c",170,55,3,"F"],["c",225,55,3,"F"],["c",225,110,3,"F"],["c",225,165,3,"F"],["c",170,165,3,"F"],["c",115,165,3,"F"],["c",170,110,2.5],["t",115,45,"A (\u2212q)","ms"],["t",108,114,"B (+q)","es"],["t",170,45,"C (+2q)","ms"],["t",225,45,"D (+q)","ms"],["t",233,114,"G (+2q)","s"],["t",225,184,"H (\u2212q)","ms"],["t",170,184,"F (+q)","ms"],["t",115,184,"E (+2q)","ms"],["t",176,104,"O","s"],["t",108,86,"l","es"],["t",142,126,"l","ms"],["t",177,86,"l","s"],["t",197,126,"l","ms"],["t",177,146,"l","s"]]},o:["{q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080\xB72l\xB2}","{1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}\xB7{q;2l\xB2}(2\u221A2 \u2212 1)","{1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}\xB7{q;l\xB2}","{1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}\xB7{2q;2l\xB2}(\u221A2)"],c:1,s:`Put O at the origin. The charges are: A(\u2212l, l) = \u2212q, B(\u2212l, 0) = +q, C(0, l) = +2q, D(l, l) = +q, G(l, 0) = +2q, H(l, \u2212l) = \u2212q, F(0, \u2212l) = +q, E(\u2212l, \u2212l) = +2q.
A and H are both \u2212q at opposite corners: their fields at O cancel.
B and G: net kq/l\xB2 towards \u2212x. C and F: net kq/l\xB2 towards \u2212y.
D and E lie on one diagonal: net kq/(2l\xB2) along the direction (+1, +1)/\u221A2, because E (2q) wins over D (q).
Ex = Ey = \u2212kq/l\xB2 + kq/(2\u221A2 l\xB2). Magnitude = \u221A2\xB7(kq/l\xB2)(1 \u2212 1/(2\u221A2)) = (kq/2l\xB2)(2\u221A2 \u2212 1).
E = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(q/2l\xB2)(2\u221A2 \u2212 1). Answer (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Find out the surface charge density at the intersection of point x = 3 m plane and x-axis, in the region of uniform line charge of 8 nC/m lying along the z-axis in free space.",o:["47.88 C m\u207B\xB2","0.424 nC m\u207B\xB2","0.07 nC m\u207B\xB2","4.0 nC m\u207B\xB2"],c:1,s:`The line charge along the z-axis gives a radial field E = \u03BB/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r) = 2k\u03BB/r.
At r = 3 m: E = 2 \xD7 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 8\xD710\u207B\u2079/3 = 48 N/C.
The surface charge density (flux density) there is \u03C3 = \u03B5\u2080E = 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 48 = 4.24\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 C/m\xB2 = 0.424 nC/m\xB2. Answer (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"An oil drop of radius 2 mm with a density 3 g cm\u207B\xB3 is held stationary under a constant electric field 3.55 \xD7 10\u2075 V m\u207B\xB9 in the Millikan's oil drop experiment. What is the number of excess electrons that the oil drop will possess? Consider g = 9.81 m/s\xB2",o:["17.3 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070","1.73 \xD7 10\xB9\xB2","1.73 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070","48.8 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9"],c:2,s:`The drop is stationary, so qE = mg, with q = ne.
m = (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3\u03C1 = (4/3)\u03C0(2\xD710\u207B\xB3)\xB3 \xD7 3000 \u2248 1.005\xD710\u207B\u2074 kg.
mg = 1.005\xD710\u207B\u2074 \xD7 9.81 \u2248 9.86\xD710\u207B\u2074 N.
q = mg/E = 9.86\xD710\u207B\u2074/(3.55\xD710\u2075) \u2248 2.78\xD710\u207B\u2079 C.
n = q/e = 2.78\xD710\u207B\u2079/(1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079) \u2248 1.73\xD710\xB9\u2070. Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"A cube of side 'a' has point charges +Q located at each of its vertices except at the origin where the charge is \u2212Q. The electric field at the centre of cube is",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"A cube of side a with charge plus Q at seven vertices and minus Q at the vertex at the origin, with x up, z to the right and y toward the viewer",d:[["l",150,150,240,150],["l",150,150,105,190],["l",150,150,150,60],["l",240,150,195,190],["l",240,150,240,60],["l",105,190,195,190],["l",105,190,105,100],["l",195,190,195,100],["l",150,60,240,60],["l",150,60,105,100],["l",240,60,195,100],["l",105,100,195,100],["l",150,150,150,20,"a"],["l",150,150,300,150,"a"],["l",150,150,66,226,"a"],["t",156,18,"x","s"],["t",302,154,"z","s"],["t",60,226,"y","es"],["c",150,150,3,"F"],["c",240,150,3,"F"],["c",105,190,3,"F"],["c",195,190,3,"F"],["c",150,60,3,"F"],["c",240,60,3,"F"],["c",105,100,3,"F"],["c",195,100,3,"F"],["t",158,166,"\u2212Q","s"],["t",156,56,"+Q","s"],["t",246,146,"+Q","s"],["t",246,56,"+Q","s"],["t",100,204,"+Q","es"],["t",98,96,"+Q","es"],["t",200,204,"+Q","s"],["t",200,96,"+Q","s"]]},o:["{\u22122Q;3\u221A3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2}(x\u0302 + y\u0302 + z\u0302)","{2Q;3\u221A3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2}(x\u0302 + y\u0302 + z\u0302)","{Q;3\u221A3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2}(x\u0302 + y\u0302 + z\u0302)","{\u2212Q;3\u221A3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2}(x\u0302 + y\u0302 + z\u0302)"],c:0,s:`If all eight vertices carried +Q, the field at the centre would be zero by symmetry.
The actual cube = (all +Q) + (a charge \u22122Q placed at the origin vertex). So the field is that of \u22122Q alone.
Distance from a vertex to the centre: r\xB2 = 3a\xB2/4. Magnitude = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(2Q)/(3a\xB2/4) = 2Q/(3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2).
The field of a negative charge points towards it, i.e. along \u2212(x\u0302y\u0302z\u0302)/\u221A3 (the origin is at the corner, axes along the edges).
E = \u22122Q/(3\u221A3 \u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2)(x\u0302 + y\u0302 + z\u0302). Answer (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A charge 'q' is placed at one corner of a cube as shown in figure. The flux of electrostatic field E\u20D7 through the shaded area is",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"A cube with charge q at the corner at the origin; the shaded area is a triangle on the top face together with a triangle on the right face",d:[["l",150,150,150,60],["l",150,150,240,150],["l",150,150,105,190],["l",150,60,240,60],["l",150,60,105,100],["l",240,150,240,60],["l",240,150,195,190],["l",240,60,195,100],["l",105,190,105,100],["l",105,190,195,190],["l",105,100,195,100],["l",195,190,195,100],["p","M150,60 L240,60 L195,100 Z","f"],["p","M240,60 L195,100 L195,190 Z","f"],["l",150,150,150,20,"a"],["l",150,150,310,150,"a"],["l",150,150,66,226,"a"],["t",156,18,"Z","s"],["t",312,154,"Y","s"],["t",60,226,"X","es"],["c",150,150,3.5,"F"],["t",156,168,"q","s"]]},o:["{q;8\u03B5\u2080}","{q;48\u03B5\u2080}","{q;4\u03B5\u2080}","{q;24\u03B5\u2080}"],c:3,s:`A charge at a corner is shared by 8 identical cubes, so the flux through this cube is q/(8\u03B5\u2080).
The three faces that meet at q have zero flux (the field lies in their planes). The other three faces share q/8\u03B5\u2080 equally by symmetry: q/(24\u03B5\u2080) each.
The shaded area is half of the top face plus half of the right face (each half carries half the flux by symmetry).
Flux = \xBD(q/24\u03B5\u2080) + \xBD(q/24\u03B5\u2080) = q/(24\u03B5\u2080). Answer (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Find the electric field at point P (as shown in figure) on the perpendicular bisector of a uniformly charged thin wire of length L carrying a charge Q. The distance of the point P from the centre of the rod is a = {\u221A3;2}L.",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A vertical uniformly charged rod of length L with centre O and a point P at distance a to the right on the perpendicular bisector",d:[["r",60,20,10,160,"f"],["l",36,20,36,180,"b"],["t",28,104,"L","es"],["c",65,100,2.5,"F"],["t",72,114,"O","s"],["l",65,100,280,100,"d"],["c",280,100,3,"F"],["t",284,94,"P","s"],["l",90,70,270,70,"b"],["t",180,64,"a","ms"],["t",65,196,"Q","ms"]]},o:["{Q;3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080L\xB2}","{\u221A3Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080L\xB2}","{Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080L\xB2}","{Q;2\u221A3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080L\xB2}"],c:3,s:`For a finite rod, the field at distance a on the perpendicular bisector is E = kQ/(a\u221A(a\xB2+L\xB2/4)).
Here a = (\u221A3/2)L, so a\xB2 + L\xB2/4 = 3L\xB2/4 + L\xB2/4 = L\xB2, and the square root is L.
E = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)\xB7Q/((\u221A3/2)L\xB7L) = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)\xB72Q/(\u221A3L\xB2).
E = Q/(2\u221A3 \u03C0\u03B5\u2080L\xB2). Answer (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A charged particle (mass m and charge q) moves along X-axis with velocity v\u2080. When it passes through the origin it enters a region having uniform electric field E\u20D7 = \u2212E\u0135 which extends upto x = d. Equation of path of electron in the region x > d is",o:["y = {qEd;mv\u2080\xB2}(x \u2212 d)","y = {qEd;mv\u2080\xB2}({d;2} \u2212 x)","y = {qEd;mv\u2080\xB2}x","y = {qEd\xB2;mv\u2080\xB2}x"],c:1,s:`In 0 < x < d the force is qE along \u2212y and the speed along x stays v\u2080, so the time taken is t = d/v\u2080.
At x = d: y\u2081 = \u2212\xBD(qE/m)(d/v\u2080)\xB2 = \u2212qEd\xB2/(2mv\u2080\xB2) and v_y = \u2212qEd/(mv\u2080).
Beyond x = d there is no field, so the path is a straight line with slope v_y/v\u2080 = \u2212qEd/(mv\u2080\xB2).
y = y\u2081 + slope\xB7(x \u2212 d) = \u2212qEd\xB2/(2mv\u2080\xB2) \u2212 (qEd/mv\u2080\xB2)(x \u2212 d) = (qEd/mv\u2080\xB2)(d/2 \u2212 x). Answer (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"Two charged thin infinite plane sheets of uniform surface charge density \u03C3\u208A and \u03C3\u208B, where |\u03C3\u208A| > |\u03C3\u208B|, intersect at right angle. Which of the following best represents the electric field lines for this system",fig:{w:340,h:244,alt:"Four field line patterns drawn around two crossing sheets: (a) concentric ellipses, (b) tall nested diamonds, (c) concentric circles, (d) wide nested diamonds",d:[["l",10,62,158,62],["l",84,6,84,118],["t",158,58,"\u03C3\u208A","es"],["t",88,12,"\u03C3\u208B","s"],["t",4,14,"(a)","s"],["p","M64,62 A20,13 0 1 1 104,62 A20,13 0 1 1 64,62 Z",""],["p","M46,62 A38,25 0 1 1 122,62 A38,25 0 1 1 46,62 Z",""],["p","M28,62 A56,38 0 1 1 140,62 A56,38 0 1 1 28,62 Z",""],["l",86,49,82,49,"a"],["l",86,37,82,37,"a"],["l",86,24,82,24,"a"],["l",180,62,328,62],["l",254,6,254,118],["t",328,58,"\u03C3\u208A","es"],["t",258,12,"\u03C3\u208B","s"],["t",174,14,"(b)","s"],["l",268,62,254,36,"a"],["l",240,62,254,36,"a"],["l",240,62,254,88,"a"],["l",268,62,254,88,"a"],["l",280,62,254,20,"a"],["l",228,62,254,20,"a"],["l",228,62,254,104,"a"],["l",280,62,254,104,"a"],["l",292,62,254,6,"a"],["l",216,62,254,6,"a"],["l",216,62,254,118,"a"],["l",292,62,254,118,"a"],["l",10,184,158,184],["l",84,128,84,240],["t",158,180,"\u03C3\u208A","es"],["t",88,134,"\u03C3\u208B","s"],["t",4,136,"(c)","s"],["c",84,184,16],["l",86,168,82,168,"a"],["c",84,184,32],["l",86,152,82,152,"a"],["c",84,184,48],["l",86,136,82,136,"a"],["l",180,184,328,184],["l",254,128,254,240],["t",328,180,"\u03C3\u208A","es"],["t",258,134,"\u03C3\u208B","s"],["t",174,136,"(d)","s"],["l",254,170,280,184,"a"],["l",254,170,228,184,"a"],["l",254,198,228,184,"a"],["l",254,198,280,184,"a"],["l",254,158,300,184,"a"],["l",254,158,208,184,"a"],["l",254,210,208,184,"a"],["l",254,210,300,184,"a"],["l",254,146,320,184,"a"],["l",254,146,188,184,"a"],["l",254,222,188,184,"a"],["l",254,222,320,184,"a"]]},o:["Pattern (a)","Pattern (b)","Pattern (c)","Pattern (d)"],c:1,s:`An infinite sheet gives a uniform field \u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080 on each side, normal to the sheet: away from the positive sheet and towards the negative one.
So in each quadrant the resultant field has a fixed size and direction: the field lines are straight and parallel there, not circles or ellipses. This rules out (a) and (c).
The \u03C3\u208A sheet gives the larger component, normal to itself, so every line makes more than 45\xB0 with the \u03C3\u208A sheet and the lines run steeply. The lines bend as they cross the \u03C3\u208B sheet, giving the tall nested-diamond pattern of (b).
Pattern (d) has lines flatter than 45\xB0, which would need |\u03C3\u208B| > |\u03C3\u208A|. Answer (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A particle of charge q and mass m is subjected to an electric field E = E\u2080(1 \u2212 ax\xB2) in the x-direction, where a and E\u2080 are constants. Initially the particle was at rest at x = 0. Other than the initial position the kinetic energy of the particle becomes zero when the distance of the particle from the origin is",o:["\u221A{2;a}","\u221A{1;a}","a","\u221A{3;a}"],c:3,s:`Work done by the field from 0 to x: W = q\u222BE dx = qE\u2080(x \u2212 ax\xB3/3).
The particle starts from rest, so its kinetic energy equals W.
KE = 0 again when x \u2212 ax\xB3/3 = 0. With x \u2260 0 this gives ax\xB2/3 = 1.
x\xB2 = 3/a, so x = \u221A(3/a). Answer (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Charges Q\u2081 and Q\u2082 are at points A and B of a right angle triangle OAB (see figure). The resultant electric field at point O is perpendicular to the hypotenuse, then Q\u2081/Q\u2082 is proportional to",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Right angled triangle OAB with the right angle at O, A directly above O at distance x1 carrying Q1 and B to the right of O at distance x2 carrying Q2",d:[["l",50,170,50,30],["l",50,170,270,170],["l",50,30,270,170],["t",52,22,"A  Q\u2081","s"],["t",40,104,"x\u2081","es"],["t",160,186,"x\u2082","ms"],["t",276,176,"B  Q\u2082","s"],["t",38,174,"O","es"]]},o:["{x\u2081\xB3;x\u2082\xB3}","{x\u2082;x\u2081}","{x\u2081;x\u2082}","{x\u2082\xB2;x\u2081\xB2}"],c:2,s:`Put O at the origin with A on the y-axis (OA = x\u2081) and B on the x-axis (OB = x\u2082).
Fields at O: E\u2081 = kQ\u2081/x\u2081\xB2 along the y-axis and E\u2082 = kQ\u2082/x\u2082\xB2 along the x-axis. The resultant makes angle \u03B1 with the x-axis, tan\u03B1 = E\u2081/E\u2082 = Q\u2081x\u2082\xB2/(Q\u2082x\u2081\xB2).
The hypotenuse AB has direction (x\u2082, \u2212x\u2081), so a line perpendicular to it has direction (x\u2081, x\u2082): tan\u03B1 = x\u2082/x\u2081.
Equate: Q\u2081x\u2082\xB2/(Q\u2082x\u2081\xB2) = x\u2082/x\u2081, so Q\u2081/Q\u2082 = x\u2081/x\u2082. Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"Consider the force F on a charge 'q' due to a uniformly charged spherical shell of radius R carrying charge Q distributed uniformly over it. Which one of the following statements is true for F, if 'q' is placed at distance r from the centre of the shell?",o:["F = {Qq;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2} for r < R","{qQ;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2} > F > 0 for r < R","F = {Qq;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2} for r > R","F = {Qq;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2} for all r"],c:2,s:`By Gauss's law, outside a uniformly charged shell the field is the same as if all the charge Q were at the centre: E = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2) for r > R.
So F = qE = Qq/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2) for r > R, which is statement (c).
Inside the shell (r < R) the field is zero, so F = 0 there; statements (a), (b) and (d) all fail for r < R. Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Two infinite planes each with uniform surface charge density +\u03C3 are kept in such a way that the angle between them is 30\xB0. The electric field in the region shown between them is given by",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Two planes seen edge on meeting at 30 degrees at a vertex on the right: one horizontal, one rising to the upper left, both carrying plus sigma, with x and y axes at the right",d:[["l",30,150,200,150],["l",200,150,53,65],["t",30,168,"+\u03C3","s"],["t",34,62,"+\u03C3","s"],["t",150,143,"30\xB0","s"],["p","M170,150 A30,30 0 0 0 174,135"],["l",265,165,265,40,"a"],["l",265,165,335,165,"a"],["t",270,48,"y","s"],["t",332,158,"x","es"]]},o:["{\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}[(1 + {\u221A3;2})y\u0302 + {x\u0302;2}]","{\u03C3;2\u03B5\u2080}[(1 + \u221A3)y\u0302 + {x\u0302;2}]","{\u03C3;2\u03B5\u2080}[(1 + \u221A3)y\u0302 \u2212 {x\u0302;2}]","{\u03C3;2\u03B5\u2080}[(1 \u2212 {\u221A3;2})y\u0302 \u2212 {x\u0302;2}]"],c:3,s:`Each plane gives a field \u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080, normal to the plane and pointing away from it (\u03C3 is positive).
Horizontal plane: in the wedge (above it) the field is (\u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080)y\u0302.
Inclined plane (30\xB0 to the horizontal, rising to the left): the wedge lies below it, and the unit normal pointing away from the plane there is (\u2212sin30\xB0, \u2212cos30\xB0) = (\u2212\xBD, \u2212\u221A3/2). Field = (\u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080)(\u2212x\u0302/2 \u2212 (\u221A3/2)y\u0302).
Add: E = (\u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080)[(1 \u2212 \u221A3/2)y\u0302 \u2212 x\u0302/2]. Answer (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Three charged particles A, B and C with charges \u22124q, 2q and \u22122q are present on the circumference of a circle of radius d. The charged particles A, C and centre O of the circle formed an equilateral triangle as shown in figure. Electric field at O along x-direction is",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"A circle of radius d centred at O with A minus 4q at 30 degrees above the x-axis, C minus 2q at 30 degrees below it and B plus 2q at 150 degrees",d:[["c",150,120,95],["l",20,120,330,120,"a"],["l",150,228,150,10,"a"],["l",150,120,232.3,72.5],["l",150,120,232.3,167.5],["l",150,120,67.7,72.5],["c",232.3,72.5,3,"F"],["c",232.3,167.5,3,"F"],["c",67.7,72.5,3,"F"],["t",238.3,68.5,"A (\u22124q)","s"],["t",238.3,179.5,"C (\u22122q)","s"],["t",63.7,66.5,"B (2q)","es"],["t",176,114,"30\xB0","s"],["t",176,136,"30\xB0","s"],["t",110,112,"150\xB0","s"],["t",326,114,"x","es"],["t",156,16,"y","s"],["t",144,134,"O","es"]]},o:["{\u221A3q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB2}","{3\u221A3q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB2}","{\u221A3q;\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB2}","{2\u221A3q;\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB2}"],c:2,s:`Use units of kq/d\xB2. Fields point towards a negative charge and away from a positive one.
A (\u22124q at 30\xB0 above +x): 4 units towards A: (4cos30\xB0, 4sin30\xB0).
C (\u22122q at 30\xB0 below +x): 2 units towards C: (2cos30\xB0, \u22122sin30\xB0).
B (+2q at 150\xB0): 2 units away from B: (2cos30\xB0, \u22122sin30\xB0).
x-components: (4 + 2 + 2)(\u221A3/2) = 4\u221A3. y-components: 2 \u2212 1 \u2212 1 = 0.
E = 4\u221A3 \xB7 q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB2) = \u221A3 q/(\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB2) along +x. Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A particle of mass m and charge q is released from rest in a uniform electric field. If there is no other force on the particle, the dependence of its speed v on the distance x travelled by it is correctly given by (graphs are schematic and not drawn to scale)",fig:{w:340,h:236,alt:"Four graphs of speed v against distance x: (a) a straight line through the origin, (b) a curve bending upward, (c) a curve rising steeply then flattening, (d) an S shaped curve",d:[["l",26,94,159,94,"a"],["l",26,94,26,10,"a"],["t",6,16,"(a)","s"],["t",159,107,"x","es"],["t",30,12,"v","s"],["l",26,94,131,26],["l",196,94,329,94,"a"],["l",196,94,196,10,"a"],["t",176,16,"(b)","s"],["t",329,107,"x","es"],["t",200,12,"v","s"],["p","M196,94 Q266,88 301,26"],["l",26,212,159,212,"a"],["l",26,212,26,128,"a"],["t",6,134,"(c)","s"],["t",159,225,"x","es"],["t",30,130,"v","s"],["p","M26,212 C34,167 71,147 131,142"],["l",196,212,329,212,"a"],["l",196,212,196,128,"a"],["t",176,134,"(d)","s"],["t",329,225,"x","es"],["t",200,130,"v","s"],["p","M196,212 C251,210 241,142 301,142"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:2,s:`The force qE is constant, so by the work-energy theorem qEx = \xBDmv\xB2.
Hence v = \u221A(2qEx/m), i.e. v \u221D \u221Ax.
The graph of v against x starts at the origin, rises steeply and then flattens (a parabola lying on its side). This is graph (c). Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"An electric dipole of moment p\u20D7 = (\u2212\xEE \u2212 3\u0135 + 2k\u0302) \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2079 C m is at the origin (0, 0, 0). The electric field due to this dipole at r\u20D7 = +\xEE + 3\u0135 + 5k\u0302 (note that r\u20D7\xB7p\u20D7 = 0) is parallel to",o:["(+\xEE \u2212 3\u0135 \u2212 2k\u0302)","(\u2212\xEE \u2212 3\u0135 + 2k\u0302)","(+\xEE + 3\u0135 \u2212 2k\u0302)","(\u2212\xEE + 3\u0135 \u2212 2k\u0302)"],c:2,s:`r\xB7p = (1)(\u22121) + (3)(\u22123) + (5)(2) = \u22121 \u2212 9 + 10 = 0, so the point lies on the equatorial plane of the dipole.
On the equatorial plane the field is E = \u2212kp/r\xB3, i.e. antiparallel to p.
So E is parallel to \u2212p, that is (\xEE + 3\u0135 \u2212 2k\u0302). Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"Consider a sphere of radius R which carries a uniform charge density \u03C1. If a sphere of radius {R;2} is carved out of it, as shown, the ratio {|E_A|;|E_B|} of magnitude of electric field E_A and E_B, respectively, at points A and B due to the remaining portion is",fig:{w:340,h:250,alt:"A sphere of radius R with centre A and a spherical cavity of radius R/2 whose centre is R/2 above A; point B is on the surface directly below A",d:[["c",170,120,100],["c",170,70,50,"d"],["l",170,10,170,244,"d"],["c",170,70,2.5,"F"],["c",170,120,2.5,"F"],["c",170,220,2.5,"F"],["l",170,70,122,70,"a"],["t",146,62,"R/2","ms"],["l",170,120,80,152,"da"],["t",112,150,"R","s"],["t",176,134,"A","s"],["t",176,238,"B","s"]]},o:["{21;34}","{18;54}","{17;54}","{18;34}"],c:3,s:`A is the centre of the big sphere; the cavity centre is R/2 above A. B is on the surface, R below A. Use superposition: remaining = full sphere (+\u03C1) + cavity sphere (\u2212\u03C1).
At A: the full sphere gives 0. A is on the surface of the cavity sphere (radius R/2), where a ball of density \u03C1 gives \u03C1(R/2)/(3\u03B5\u2080) = \u03C1R/(6\u03B5\u2080). So E_A = \u03C1R/(6\u03B5\u2080).
At B: the full sphere gives \u03C1R/(3\u03B5\u2080). The cavity charge \u03C1(4/3)\u03C0(R/2)\xB3 = \u03C0\u03C1R\xB3/6, at distance 3R/2, gives (\u03C0\u03C1R\xB3/6)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080\xB79R\xB2/4) = \u03C1R/(54\u03B5\u2080) in the opposite sense.
E_B = \u03C1R/(3\u03B5\u2080) \u2212 \u03C1R/(54\u03B5\u2080) = 17\u03C1R/(54\u03B5\u2080).
E_A/E_B = (1/6)/(17/54) = 9/17 = 18/34. Answer (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"The bob of a simple pendulum has mass 2 g and a charge of 5.0 \u03BCC. It is at rest in a uniform horizontal electric field of intensity 2000 V m\u207B\xB9. At equilibrium, the angle that the pendulum makes with the vertical is (take g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["tan\u207B\xB9(0.2)","tan\u207B\xB9(0.5)","tan\u207B\xB9(2.0)","tan\u207B\xB9(5.0)"],c:1,s:`In equilibrium the bob is acted on by weight mg (down), the electric force qE (horizontal) and the tension.
tan\u03B8 = qE/(mg) = (5.0\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 2000)/(2\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 10) = 10\u207B\xB2/(2\xD710\u207B\xB2) = 0.5.
\u03B8 = tan\u207B\xB9(0.5). Answer (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"An electric dipole is formed by two equal and opposite charges q with separation d. The charges have same mass m. It is kept in a uniform electric field E. If it is slightly rotated from its equilibrium orientation, then its angular frequency \u03C9 is",o:["\u221A{2qE;md}","\u221A{qE;2md}","2\u221A{qE;md}","\u221A{qE;md}"],c:0,s:`For a small angle \u03B8 the torque is \u03C4 = \u2212pE sin\u03B8 \u2248 \u2212(qd)E\u03B8.
Moment of inertia about the centre: I = 2 \xD7 m(d/2)\xB2 = md\xB2/2.
SHM: \u03C9\xB2 = qdE/I = qdE/(md\xB2/2) = 2qE/(md).
\u03C9 = \u221A(2qE/(md)). Answer (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"A positive point charge is released from rest at a distance r\u2080 from a positive line charge with uniform density. The speed (v) of the point charge, as a function of instantaneous distance r from line charge, is proportional to",o:["v \u221D ln(r/r\u2080)","v \u221D \u221A(ln(r/r\u2080))","v \u221D e^(+r/r\u2080)","v \u221D (r/r\u2080)"],c:1,s:`Field of a line charge: E = \u03BB/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r).
Work done by the field as the charge moves from r\u2080 to r: W = q\u222BE dr = (q\u03BB/2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) ln(r/r\u2080).
This equals the kinetic energy: \xBDmv\xB2 = (q\u03BB/2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) ln(r/r\u2080).
So v = \u221A[(q\u03BB/\u03C0\u03B5\u2080m) ln(r/r\u2080)], i.e. v \u221D \u221A(ln(r/r\u2080)). Answer (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"Four point charges \u2212q, +q, +q and \u2212q are placed on y-axis at y = \u22122d, y = \u2212d, y = +d and y = +2d, respectively. The magnitude of the electric field E at a point on the x-axis at x = D, with D \u226B d, will behave as",o:["E \u221D {1;D\xB3}","E \u221D {1;D}","E \u221D {1;D\u2074}","E \u221D {1;D\xB2}"],c:2,s:`Group the charges into two dipoles: (\u2212q at \u22122d, +q at \u2212d) has moment qd along +y; (+q at +d, \u2212q at +2d) has moment qd along \u2212y.
The point on the x-axis is (nearly) on the equatorial line of each, so each gives about kqd/D\xB3, in opposite directions: the 1/D\xB3 terms cancel.
Net charge and net dipole moment are both zero, so the leading term is the quadrupole term. The two dipoles are 3d apart, so what survives is smaller by a factor of about d/D.
E \u221D (1/D\xB3)(d/D) \u221D 1/D\u2074. Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A simple pendulum of length L is placed between the plates of a parallel plate capacitor having electric field E, as shown in figure. Its bob has mass m and charge q. The time period of the pendulum is given by",fig:{w:340,h:230,alt:"A simple pendulum of length L with a bob of mass m and charge q hanging between two vertical capacitor plates, positive on the left and negative on the right, with the field E pointing to the right",d:[["l",100,30,100,190,"B"],["l",240,30,240,190,"B"],["l",150,22,190,22,"B"],["l",170,22,170,150],["c",170,150,6,"F"],["t",180,154,"m, q","s"],["t",176,92,"L","s"],["l",110,208,230,208,"a"],["t",170,224,"E","ms"],["t",92,50,"+","es"],["t",92,90,"+","es"],["t",92,130,"+","es"],["t",92,170,"+","es"],["t",248,50,"\u2212","s"],["t",248,90,"\u2212","s"],["t",248,130,"\u2212","s"],["t",248,170,"\u2212","s"]]},o:["2\u03C0\u221A{L;g \u2212 qE/m}","2\u03C0\u221A{L;\u221A(g\xB2 + (qE/m)\xB2)}","2\u03C0\u221A{L;g + qE/m}","2\u03C0\u221A{L;\u221A(g\xB2 \u2212 q\xB2E\xB2/m\xB2)}"],c:1,s:`The bob feels gravity mg (down) and the electric force qE (horizontal). These are perpendicular, so the effective acceleration is g_eff = \u221A(g\xB2 + (qE/m)\xB2).
The pendulum swings about the new equilibrium direction as if gravity had this value.
T = 2\u03C0\u221A(L/g_eff) = 2\u03C0\u221A(L/\u221A(g\xB2 + (qE/m)\xB2)). Answer (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"Shown in the figure is a shell made of a conductor. It has inner radius a and outer radius b, and carries charge Q. At its centre is a dipole p\u20D7 as shown. In this case",fig:{w:340,h:230,alt:"A thick conducting spherical shell with inner radius a and outer radius b and a small dipole arrow p at its centre",d:[["c",170,115,100],["c",170,115,66],["l",170,128,170,98,"a"],["t",178,110,"p","s"],["l",170,115,236,115,"d"],["t",204,110,"a","ms"],["l",170,115,241,44,"d"],["t",214,70,"b","s"]]},o:["surface charge density on the inner surface of the shell is zero everywhere","surface charge density on the inner surface is uniform and equal to {Q/2;4\u03C0a\xB2}","electric field outside the shell is the same as that of a point charge at the centre of the shell","surface charge density on the outer surface depends on |p\u20D7|"],c:2,s:`The dipole is neutral, so the induced charge on the inner surface adds up to zero overall, but it is not zero everywhere: it is non-uniform (negative near the dipole's positive end and positive near its negative end). So (a) and (b) are false.
The shell carries Q and its inner surface holds net zero, so the outer surface holds Q. Inside the metal E = 0, so this charge spreads uniformly over the outer surface whatever p is; (d) is false.
Outside, the field is that of a uniformly charged sphere, the same as a point charge Q at the centre. Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:"Let a total charge 2Q be distributed in a sphere of radius R, with the charge density given by \u03C1(r) = kr, where r is the distance from the centre. Two charges A and B, of \u2212Q each, are placed on diametrically opposite points, at equal distance a, from the centre. If A and B do not experience any force, then",o:["a = 8^(\u22121/4) R","a = 2^(\u22121/4) R","a = {3R;2^(1/4)}","a = R/\u221A3"],c:0,s:`Total charge: 2Q = \u222B\u2080\u1D3F kr\xB74\u03C0r\xB2 dr = \u03C0kR\u2074, so k = 2Q/(\u03C0R\u2074).
Charge inside radius a: q_in = \u03C0ka\u2074 = 2Qa\u2074/R\u2074.
Force on A from the sphere (attractive, towards the centre): (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)\xB7Q\xB7q_in/a\xB2 = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)\xB72Q\xB2a\xB2/R\u2074.
Force on A from B (repulsive, distance 2a): (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)\xB7Q\xB2/(4a\xB2).
No net force: 2a\xB2/R\u2074 = 1/(4a\xB2), so a\u2074 = R\u2074/8 and a = 8^(\u22121/4) R. Answer (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"For a uniformly charged ring of radius R, the electric field on its axis has the largest magnitude at a distance h from its centre. Then value of h is",o:["{R;\u221A5}","{R;\u221A2}","R","R\u221A2"],c:1,s:`On the axis, E = kQh/(h\xB2+R\xB2)^(3/2).
For the maximum set dE/dh = 0: (h\xB2+R\xB2) \u2212 3h\xB2 = 0, so R\xB2 = 2h\xB2.
h = R/\u221A2. Answer (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Two point charges q\u2081(\u221A10 \u03BCC) and q\u2082(\u221225 \u03BCC) are placed on the x-axis at x = 1 m and x = 4 m respectively. The electric field (in V/m) at a point y = 3 m on y-axis is [Take {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = 9\xD710\u2079 N m\xB2 C\u207B\xB2]",o:["(63\xEE \u2212 27\u0135) \xD7 10\xB2","(81\xEE \u2212 81\u0135) \xD7 10\xB2","(\u221263\xEE + 27\u0135) \xD7 10\xB2","(\u221281\xEE + 81\u0135) \xD7 10\xB2"],c:0,s:`Take P = (0, 3). Distances: to q\u2081 at (1, 0), r\u2081 = \u221A10; to q\u2082 at (4, 0), r\u2082 = 5.
E\u2081 = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 \u221A10\xD710\u207B\u2076/10 = 900\u221A10 V/m, away from q\u2081 along (\u22121, 3)/\u221A10: E\u2081 = \u2212900\xEE + 2700\u0135.
E\u2082 = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 25\xD710\u207B\u2076/25 = 9000 V/m, towards q\u2082 (it is negative) along (4, \u22123)/5: E\u2082 = 7200\xEE \u2212 5400\u0135.
Sum = 6300\xEE \u2212 2700\u0135 = (63\xEE \u2212 27\u0135) \xD7 10\xB2. Answer (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"Charges \u2212q and +q located at A and B, respectively, constitute an electric dipole. Distance AB = 2a, O is the mid point of the dipole and OP is perpendicular to AB. A charge Q is placed at P where OP = y and y \u226B 2a. The charge Q experiences an electrostatic force F. If Q is now moved along the equatorial line to P\u2032 such that OP\u2032 = {y;3}, force on Q will be close to ({y;3} \u226B 2a)",o:["27F","3F","{F;3}","9F"],c:0,s:`P and P\u2032 both lie on the equatorial line, far from the dipole (y \u226B 2a).
There the dipole field is E = kp/y\xB3, so the force F = QE \u221D 1/y\xB3.
When the distance becomes y/3: F\u2032 = F \xD7 (y/(y/3))\xB3 = 27F. Answer (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"Determine the electric dipole moment of the system of three charges, placed on the vertices of an equilateral triangle, as shown in the figure.",fig:{w:340,h:230,alt:"Equilateral triangle of side l with plus q at the origin on the left, plus q on the x-axis at the right and minus 2q at the top vertex",d:[["l",50,205,50,12,"a"],["l",50,205,320,205,"a"],["l",50,205,240,205,"B"],["l",50,205,145,40],["l",145,40,240,205],["t",36,222,"+q","es"],["t",240,222,"+q","ms"],["t",152,36,"\u22122q","s"],["t",86,116,"l","es"],["t",204,116,"l","s"],["t",145,220,"l","ms"],["t",318,198,"x","es"],["t",56,16,"y","s"]]},o:["2ql\u0135","(ql){\xEE+\u0135;\u221A2}","\u221A3ql{\u0135\u2212\xEE;\u221A2}","\u2212\u221A3ql\u0135"],c:3,s:`The dipole moment is p = \u03A3 q\u1D62r\u1D62. Put +q at the origin, +q at (l, 0) and \u22122q at (l/2, \u221A3 l/2).
p = q(0) + q(l\xEE) \u2212 2q[(l/2)\xEE + (\u221A3/2)l\u0135]
= ql\xEE \u2212 ql\xEE \u2212 \u221A3 ql\u0135 = \u2212\u221A3 ql\u0135.
(The net charge is zero, so p does not depend on the choice of origin.) Answer (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A charge Q is placed at a distance {a;2} above the centre of the square surface of edge a as shown in the figure. The electric flux through the square surface is",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"A square surface of edge a drawn in perspective with a point charge Q a distance a/2 directly above its centre",d:[["p","M60,165 L230,165 L290,115 L120,115 Z","f"],["c",175,140,2,"F"],["c",175,45,3,"F"],["l",175,45,175,138,"d"],["t",183,98,"a/2","s"],["t",183,40,"Q","s"],["l",60,185,230,185,"b"],["t",145,200,"a","ms"]]},o:["{Q;3\u03B5\u2080}","{Q;6\u03B5\u2080}","{Q;\u03B5\u2080}","{Q;2\u03B5\u2080}"],c:1,s:`Imagine a cube of edge a with Q at its centre. Since Q is a/2 above the centre of the square, the square is exactly one face of this cube.
Total flux from Q through the cube = Q/\u03B5\u2080, shared equally by the 6 faces.
Flux through one face = Q/(6\u03B5\u2080). Answer (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:"A solid ball of radius R has a charge density \u03C1 given by \u03C1 = \u03C1\u2080(1 \u2212 {r;R}) for 0 \u2264 r \u2264 R. The electric field outside the ball is",o:["{\u03C1\u2080R\xB3;12\u03B5\u2080r\xB2}","{4\u03C1\u2080R\xB3;3\u03B5\u2080r\xB2}","{3\u03C1\u2080R\xB3;4\u03B5\u2080r\xB2}","{\u03C1\u2080R\xB3;\u03B5\u2080r\xB2}"],c:0,s:`Total charge: Q = \u222B\u2080\u1D3F \u03C1\u2080(1 \u2212 r/R)\xB74\u03C0r\xB2 dr = 4\u03C0\u03C1\u2080(R\xB3/3 \u2212 R\xB3/4) = \u03C0\u03C1\u2080R\xB3/3.
Outside the ball, Gauss's law gives E = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2) = (\u03C0\u03C1\u2080R\xB3/3)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2).
E = \u03C1\u2080R\xB3/(12\u03B5\u2080r\xB2). Answer (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A body of mass M and charge q is connected to a spring of spring constant k. It is oscillating along x-direction about its equilibrium position, taken to be at x = 0, with an amplitude A. An electric field E is applied along the x-direction. Which of the following statements is correct?",o:["The total energy of the system is {1;2}M\u03C9\xB2A\xB2 \u2212 {1;2}{q\xB2E\xB2;k}.","The new equilibrium position is at a distance {2qE;k} from x = 0.","The new equilibrium position is at a distance {qE;2k} from x = 0.","The total energy of the system is {1;2}M\u03C9\xB2A\xB2 + {1;2}{q\xB2E\xB2;k}."],c:3,s:`With the field, the net force is \u2212kx + qE = \u2212k(x \u2212 qE/k), so the new equilibrium position is x\u2080 = qE/k. This rules out (b) and (c).
The body now oscillates about x\u2080. Besides the oscillation energy \xBDM\u03C9\xB2A\xB2 (with \u03C9\xB2 = k/M), the system holds the spring energy of the shifted equilibrium, \xBDkx\u2080\xB2 = \xBDq\xB2E\xB2/k.
Total energy = \xBDM\u03C9\xB2A\xB2 + \xBDq\xB2E\xB2/k, which is statement (d), the official answer.`},{y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"An electric dipole has a fixed dipole moment p\u20D7, which makes angle \u03B8 with respect to x-axis. When subjected to an electric field E\u20D7\u2081 = E\xEE, it experiences a torque T\u20D7\u2081 = \u03C4k\u0302. When subjected to another electric field E\u20D7\u2082 = \u221A3E\u2081\u0135 it experiences a torque T\u20D7\u2082 = \u2212T\u20D7\u2081. The angle \u03B8 is",o:["30\xB0","45\xB0","60\xB0","90\xB0"],c:2,s:`Write p = p(cos\u03B8 \xEE + sin\u03B8 \u0135). The torque is T = p \xD7 E.
Field 1 (E\xEE): T\u2081 = \u2212pE sin\u03B8 k\u0302.
Field 2 (\u221A3E\u0135): T\u2082 = p cos\u03B8 \xD7 \u221A3E k\u0302 = \u221A3 pE cos\u03B8 k\u0302.
Given T\u2082 = \u2212T\u2081: \u221A3 pE cos\u03B8 = pE sin\u03B8, so tan\u03B8 = \u221A3.
\u03B8 = 60\xB0. Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:`Four closed surfaces and corresponding charge distributions are shown below.
Let the respective electric fluxes through the surfaces be \u03C6\u2081, \u03C6\u2082, \u03C6\u2083 and \u03C6\u2084. Then`,fig:{w:340,h:270,alt:"Four closed surfaces S1 to S4: S1 encloses 2q; S2 encloses q, minus q, q and q; S3 encloses q and q with 5q outside; S4 encloses 8q, minus 2q and minus 4q with 3q outside",d:[["p","M20,30 Q40,16 62,22 L125,18 Q136,48 128,75 L40,86 Q30,62 20,52 Z"],["c",85,48,2,"F"],["t",90,52,"2q","s"],["t",75,104,"S\u2081","ms"],["p","M185,28 Q200,8 225,14 Q245,24 262,10 Q282,20 292,40 Q300,70 280,86 L205,90 Q182,70 185,28 Z"],["c",205,28,2,"F"],["t",209,32,"q","s"],["c",262,32,2,"F"],["t",266,36,"\u2212q","s"],["c",205,68,2,"F"],["t",209,72,"q","s"],["c",272,68,2,"F"],["t",276,72,"q","s"],["t",240,106,"S\u2082","ms"],["p","M15,160 Q30,148 50,152 L60,158 Q70,172 88,160 L130,156 Q150,160 148,190 L145,216 L22,218 Q12,190 15,160 Z"],["c",28,198,2,"F"],["t",32,202,"q","s"],["c",128,198,2,"F"],["t",132,202,"q","s"],["c",80,146,2,"F"],["t",84,142,"5q","s"],["t",80,236,"S\u2083","ms"],["p","M212,132 Q222,122 250,125 L288,128 Q300,158 304,186 Q308,214 284,238 Q250,250 226,244 Q206,222 214,196 Q220,166 212,132 Z"],["c",234,146,2,"F"],["t",238,150,"8q","s"],["c",258,188,2,"F"],["t",262,192,"\u22122q","s"],["c",236,226,2,"F"],["t",240,230,"\u22124q","s"],["c",318,160,2,"F"],["t",322,164,"3q","s"],["t",252,262,"S\u2084","ms"]]},o:["\u03C6\u2081 = \u03C6\u2082 = \u03C6\u2083 = \u03C6\u2084","\u03C6\u2081 > \u03C6\u2083; \u03C6\u2082 < \u03C6\u2084","\u03C6\u2081 > \u03C6\u2082 > \u03C6\u2083 > \u03C6\u2084","\u03C6\u2081 < \u03C6\u2082 = \u03C6\u2083 > \u03C6\u2084"],c:0,s:`Flux through a closed surface = (charge enclosed)/\u03B5\u2080. Charges outside a surface do not change its net flux.
S\u2081 encloses 2q. S\u2082 encloses q \u2212 q + q + q = 2q. S\u2083 encloses q + q = 2q (the 5q lies outside the notch). S\u2084 encloses 8q \u2212 2q \u2212 4q = 2q (the 3q lies outside).
All four enclose 2q, so \u03C6\u2081 = \u03C6\u2082 = \u03C6\u2083 = \u03C6\u2084 = 2q/\u03B5\u2080. Answer (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2016",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:"The region between two concentric spheres of radii 'a' and 'b', respectively (see figure), has volume charge density \u03C1 = {A;r}, where A is a constant and r is the distance from the centre. At the centre of the spheres is a point charge Q. The value of A such that the electric field in the region between the spheres will be constant, is",fig:{w:340,h:230,alt:"Two concentric spheres of radii a and b with a point charge Q at the centre",d:[["c",170,115,100],["c",170,115,50],["c",170,115,2.5,"F"],["t",160,128,"Q","es"],["l",170,115,220,115],["t",196,110,"a","ms"],["l",170,115,241,186],["t",214,160,"b","s"]]},o:["{Q;2\u03C0a\xB2}","{Q;2\u03C0(b\xB2\u2212a\xB2)}","{2Q;\u03C0(a\xB2\u2212b\xB2)}","{2Q;\u03C0a\xB2}"],c:0,s:`At radius r (a < r < b) the enclosed charge is Q + \u222B\u2090\u02B3 (A/r\u2032)\xB74\u03C0r\u2032\xB2 dr\u2032 = Q + 2\u03C0A(r\xB2 \u2212 a\xB2).
E = [Q + 2\u03C0A(r\xB2 \u2212 a\xB2)]/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2) = (Q \u2212 2\u03C0Aa\xB2)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2) + A/(2\u03B5\u2080).
For E to be independent of r, the 1/r\xB2 term must vanish: Q = 2\u03C0Aa\xB2.
A = Q/(2\u03C0a\xB2). Answer (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2015",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A long cylindrical shell carries positive surface charge \u03C3 in the upper half and negative surface charge \u2212\u03C3 in the lower half. The electric field lines around the cylinder will look like figure given in (Figures are schematic and not drawn to scale)",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"Four sketches of field lines around a cylinder with plus charge on the top half and minus charge on the bottom half: (a) nearly straight lines passing by, (b) radial lines from top and bottom plus side loops, (c) closed loops from top to bottom on both sides, (d) radial lines out of the whole cylinder",d:[["c",87,58,20],["l",67,58,107,58],["t",87,52,"+ + +","ms"],["t",87,73,"\u2212 \u2212 \u2212","ms"],["t",4,14,"(a)","s"],["l",8,14,162,14,"a"],["l",8,26,162,26,"a"],["l",8,90,162,90,"a"],["l",8,102,162,102,"a"],["l",87,36,87,24,"a"],["l",87,92,87,80,"a"],["c",257,58,20],["l",237,58,277,58],["t",257,52,"+ + +","ms"],["t",257,73,"\u2212 \u2212 \u2212","ms"],["t",174,14,"(b)","s"],["l",276.1,47,293.4,37,"a"],["l",293.4,79,276.1,69,"a"],["l",268,38.9,278,21.6,"a"],["l",278,94.4,268,77.1,"a"],["l",257,36,257,16,"a"],["l",257,100,257,80,"a"],["l",246,38.9,236,21.6,"a"],["l",236,94.4,246,77.1,"a"],["l",237.9,47,220.6,37,"a"],["l",220.6,79,237.9,69,"a"],["p","M277,52 C317,28 317,88 277,64","a"],["p","M237,52 C197,28 197,88 237,64","a"],["c",87,178,20],["l",67,178,107,178],["t",87,172,"+ + +","ms"],["t",87,193,"\u2212 \u2212 \u2212","ms"],["t",4,134,"(c)","s"],["p","M91,158 C150,125 150,231 91,198","a"],["p","M83,158 C23,125 23,231 83,198","a"],["p","M91,158 C180,100 180,256 91,198","a"],["p","M83,158 C-6,100 -6,256 83,198","a"],["c",257,178,20],["l",237,178,277,178],["t",257,172,"+ + +","ms"],["t",257,193,"\u2212 \u2212 \u2212","ms"],["t",174,134,"(d)","s"],["l",279,178,301,178,"a"],["l",272.6,162.4,288.1,146.9,"a"],["l",257,156,257,134,"a"],["l",241.4,162.4,225.9,146.9,"a"],["l",235,178,213,178,"a"],["l",241.4,193.6,225.9,209.1,"a"],["l",257,200,257,222,"a"],["l",272.6,193.6,288.1,209.1,"a"]]},o:["Figure (a)","Figure (b)","Figure (c)","Figure (d)"],c:2,s:`Field lines start on positive charge and end on negative charge. Here the + half is on top and the \u2212 half on the bottom, so the lines leave the upper half, curve round both sides and return to the lower half, like the lines of a dipole.
The net charge is zero, so no lines go off to infinity: this rules out (b) and (d), where lines spread out radially. (a) shows nearly straight lines running past the cylinder, which does not match a charge-free source.
Figure (c) shows closed loops from + to \u2212 on both sides. Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"A thin disc of radius b = 2a has a concentric hole of radius a in it (see figure). It carries uniform surface charge \u03C3 on it. If the electric field on its axis at height h (h << a) from its centre is given as Ch then value of C is",fig:{w:340,h:120,alt:"A flat disc with a concentric circular hole, with a dotted axis through its centre; the hole has radius a and the disc radius b",d:[["p","M40,64 A130,40 0 1 1 300,64 A130,40 0 1 1 40,64 Z",""],["p","M105,64 A65,20 0 1 1 235,64 A65,20 0 1 1 105,64 Z",""],["l",170,8,170,118,"d"],["l",170,64,235,64],["t",203,58,"a","ms"],["l",170,64,40,64],["t",100,58,"b","ms"],["t",176,16,"h","s"]]},o:["{\u03C3;a\u03B5\u2080}","{\u03C3;2a\u03B5\u2080}","{\u03C3;4a\u03B5\u2080}","{\u03C3;8a\u03B5\u2080}"],c:2,s:`The axial field of a full disc of radius R is (\u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080)[1 \u2212 h/\u221A(h\xB2+R\xB2)].
The holed disc = disc of radius b minus disc of radius a: E = (\u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080)[h/\u221A(h\xB2+a\xB2) \u2212 h/\u221A(h\xB2+b\xB2)].
For h << a: \u221A(h\xB2+a\xB2) \u2248 a and \u221A(h\xB2+b\xB2) \u2248 b = 2a, so E \u2248 (\u03C3h/2\u03B5\u2080)(1/a \u2212 1/2a) = \u03C3h/(4a\u03B5\u2080).
So C = \u03C3/(4a\u03B5\u2080). Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"A wire of length L(= 20 cm), is bent into a semi-circular arc. If the two equal halves of the arc, were each to be uniformly charged with charges \xB1Q, [|Q| = 10\xB3\u03B5\u2080 coulomb where \u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity (in SI units) of free space] the net electric field at the centre O of the semi-circular arc would be",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"A semicircular arc above the x-axis with centre O; the left quarter carries plus Q and the right quarter minus Q",d:[["p","M70,130 A100,100 0 0 1 270,130","B"],["l",40,130,310,130,"a"],["l",170,130,170,10,"da"],["t",176,148,"O","s"],["t",108,54,"+Q","ms"],["t",236,54,"\u2212Q","ms"],["t",306,124,"x","es"],["t",176,16,"y","s"]]},o:["(50\xD710\xB3 N/C)\u0135","(25\xD710\xB3 N/C)\xEE","(25\xD710\xB3 N/C)\u0135","(50\xD710\xB3 N/C)\xEE"],c:1,s:`The arc length is \u03C0R = 0.20 m, so R = 0.2/\u03C0 m. Each half (a quarter circle) has charge Q, so \u03BB = Q/(\u03C0R/2) = 2Q/(\u03C0R).
From the figure the left half is +Q and the right half is \u2212Q. The field of each quarter at O has magnitude \u221A2 k\u03BB/R, pointing right-down for the + quarter and right-up for the \u2212 quarter. The y-components cancel and the x-components add.
E = 2k\u03BB/R = 2 \xD7 (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) \xD7 2Q/(\u03C0R\xB2) = Q/(\u03C0\xB2\u03B5\u2080R\xB2).
With R\xB2 = 0.04/\u03C0\xB2: E = Q/(0.04 \u03B5\u2080) = 25 Q/\u03B5\u2080 = 25\xD710\xB3 N/C along +x. Answer (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"The magnitude of the average electric field normally present in the atmosphere just above the surface of the Earth is about 150 N/C, directed inward towards the center of Earth. This gives the total net surface charge carried by the Earth to be [Given \u03B5\u2080 = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2/N-m\xB2, R_E = 6.37 \xD7 10\u2076 m]",o:["+670 kC","\u2212670 kC","\u2212680 kC","+680 kC"],c:2,s:`Just outside a charged sphere, E = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2) in magnitude.
Q = 4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2E = 4\u03C0 \xD7 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 (6.37\xD710\u2076)\xB2 \xD7 150 \u2248 6.8\xD710\u2075 C (about 680 kC).
The field points inward (towards the Earth), so the charge is negative.
Q \u2248 \u2212680 kC. Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"Charges Q, 2Q and 4Q are uniformly distributed in three dielectric solid spheres 1, 2 and 3 of radii R/2, R and 2R respectively, as shown in figure. If magnitudes of the electric fields at point P at a distance R from the centre of spheres 1, 2 and 3 are E\u2081, E\u2082 and E\u2083 respectively, then",fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"Three solid spheres of radii R/2, R and 2R carrying Q, 2Q and 4Q, each with a point P at distance R from its centre: outside sphere 1, on the surface of sphere 2, inside sphere 3",d:[["c",50,120,15],["c",140,120,30],["c",255,120,60],["l",50,120,50,92,"a"],["l",140,120,140,92,"a"],["l",255,120,255,92,"a"],["t",50,84,"P","ms"],["t",140,84,"P","ms"],["t",255,84,"P","ms"],["t",56,108,"R","s"],["t",146,108,"R","s"],["t",261,108,"R","s"],["t",50,196,"Sphere 1","ms"],["t",50,210,"Q, R/2","ms"],["t",140,196,"Sphere 2","ms"],["t",140,210,"2Q, R","ms"],["t",255,196,"Sphere 3","ms"],["t",255,210,"4Q, 2R","ms"]]},o:["E\u2081 > E\u2082 > E\u2083","E\u2083 > E\u2081 > E\u2082","E\u2082 > E\u2081 > E\u2083","E\u2083 > E\u2082 > E\u2081"],c:2,s:`Sphere 1: P (distance R) is outside the ball of radius R/2, so E\u2081 = kQ/R\xB2.
Sphere 2: P is on the surface (radius R), so E\u2082 = k(2Q)/R\xB2 = 2kQ/R\xB2.
Sphere 3: P is inside (R < 2R), so E\u2083 = k(4Q)R/(2R)\xB3 = kQ/(2R\xB2).
Hence E\u2082 > E\u2081 > E\u2083. Answer (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"The surface charge density of a thin charged disc of radius R is \u03C3. The value of the electric field at the centre of the disc is {\u03C3;2\u03B5\u2080}. With respect to the field at the centre, the electric field along the axis at a distance R from the centre of the disc",o:["reduces by 70.7%","reduces by 29.3%","reduces by 9.7%","reduces by 14.6%"],c:0,s:`The axial field of a disc is E = (\u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080)[1 \u2212 z/\u221A(z\xB2+R\xB2)].
At z = R: E = (\u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080)(1 \u2212 1/\u221A2) = 0.293 \xD7 (\u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080).
So the field is 29.3% of the central value, i.e. it reduces by 100 \u2212 29.3 = 70.7%. Answer (a).`},{y:"AIEEE 2012 \xB7 Online",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"In a uniformly charged sphere of total charge Q and radius R, the electric field E is plotted as a function of distance from the centre. The graph which would correspond to the above will be",fig:{w:340,h:236,alt:"Four graphs of E against r: (a) decaying from a large value, (b) straight rise to R then smooth decay, (c) straight rise to R then a drop to zero, (d) a smooth bell shaped curve",d:[["l",26,94,159,94,"a"],["l",26,94,26,10,"a"],["t",6,16,"(a)","s"],["t",159,107,"r","es"],["t",30,12,"E","s"],["p","M38,14 C42,44 66,86 136,91"],["l",196,94,329,94,"a"],["l",196,94,196,10,"a"],["t",176,16,"(b)","s"],["t",329,107,"r","es"],["t",200,12,"E","s"],["l",196,94,236,39],["p","M236,39 C244,59 271,82 306,88"],["l",236,91,236,97],["t",236,107,"R","ms"],["l",26,212,159,212,"a"],["l",26,212,26,128,"a"],["t",6,134,"(c)","s"],["t",159,225,"r","es"],["t",30,130,"E","s"],["l",26,212,66,157],["l",66,157,66,212],["t",66,225,"R","ms"],["l",196,212,329,212,"a"],["l",196,212,196,128,"a"],["t",176,134,"(d)","s"],["t",329,225,"r","es"],["t",200,130,"E","s"],["p","M196,212 C221,137 251,137 306,212"],["t",251,225,"R","ms"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:1,s:`Inside (r < R): E = Qr/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB3), which rises linearly from zero at the centre.
At r = R: E = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2), the maximum value, with no sudden jump.
Outside (r > R): E = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2), falling as 1/r\xB2.
So E rises linearly up to R and then decays smoothly: graph (b). Answer (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2011",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"Consider an electric field E\u20D7 = E\u2080x\u0302, where E\u2080 is a constant. The flux through the shaded area (as shown in the figure) due to this field is",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"A tilted rectangular shaded area with corners at (0,0,0), (0,a,0), (a,a,a) and (a,0,a) in x, y, z axes",d:[["l",150,150,240,150],["l",240,150,195,100],["l",195,100,105,100],["l",105,100,150,150],["p","M150,150 L240,150 L195,100 L105,100 Z","f"],["l",150,150,150,20,"a"],["l",150,150,300,150,"a"],["l",150,150,66,226,"a"],["t",156,18,"z","s"],["t",302,154,"y","s"],["t",60,226,"x","es"],["t",156,170,"(0,0,0)","s"],["t",240,170,"(0,a,0)","ms"],["t",105,92,"(a,0,a)","ms"],["t",195,92,"(a,a,a)","ms"]]},o:["2E\u2080a\xB2","\u221A2E\u2080a\xB2","E\u2080a\xB2","{E\u2080a\xB2;\u221A2}"],c:2,s:`The shaded region has vertices (0,0,0), (0,a,0), (a,a,a) and (a,0,a): a rectangle.
Its sides are a (along y) and a\u221A2 (along (a,0,a)), so its area is \u221A2 a\xB2.
A normal to it is n = (x\u0302 \u2212 z\u0302)/\u221A2 (perpendicular to both y\u0302 and x\u0302 + z\u0302).
Flux = E\xB7n\xB7A = E\u2080(1/\u221A2)(\u221A2 a\xB2) = E\u2080a\xB2. Answer (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2011",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A wooden block performs SHM on a frictionless surface with frequency, \u03BD\u2080. The block carries a charge +Q on its surface. If now a uniform electric field E\u20D7 is switched on as shown, then the SHM of the block will be",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"A charged block on a frictionless surface attached by a spring to a wall on the right, with an electric field E pointing to the right",d:[["l",20,120,320,120],["r",40,80,50,40,"f"],["t",65,104,"+Q","ms"],["p","M90,100 L110,100 L118,84 L130,116 L142,84 L154,116 L166,84 L178,116 L186,100 L270,100"],["l",270,70,270,120,"B"],["l",60,40,130,40,"a"],["t",150,44,"E","s"]]},o:["of the same frequency and with shifted mean position.","of the same frequency and with the same mean position.","of changed frequency and with shifted mean position.","of changed frequency and with the same mean position."],c:0,s:`Without the field the block oscillates about the natural length of the spring with \u03C9\u2080 = \u221A(k/m).
The field adds a constant extra force QE to the right. The net force becomes \u2212kx + QE = \u2212k(x \u2212 QE/k).
So the motion is still SHM with the same k and m, hence the same frequency, but about a new mean position shifted by QE/k. Answer (a).`},{y:"AIEEE 2010",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:"Let there be a spherically symmetric charge distribution with charge density varying as \u03C1(r) = \u03C1\u2080({5;4} \u2212 {r;R}) upto r = R, and \u03C1(r) = 0 for r > R, where r is the distance from the origin. The electric field at a distance r (r < R) from the origin is given by",o:["{\u03C1\u2080r;3\u03B5\u2080}({5;4} \u2212 {r;R})","{4\u03C0\u03C1\u2080r;3\u03B5\u2080}({5;3} \u2212 {r;R})","{\u03C1\u2080r;4\u03B5\u2080}({5;3} \u2212 {r;R})","{4\u03C1\u2080r;3\u03B5\u2080}({5;4} \u2212 {r;R})"],c:2,s:`By Gauss's law, E\xB74\u03C0r\xB2 = q_in/\u03B5\u2080.
q_in = \u222B\u2080\u02B3 \u03C1\u2080(5/4 \u2212 r\u2032/R)\xB74\u03C0r\u2032\xB2 dr\u2032 = 4\u03C0\u03C1\u2080(5r\xB3/12 \u2212 r\u2074/(4R)).
E = q_in/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2) = (\u03C1\u2080/\u03B5\u2080)(5r/12 \u2212 r\xB2/(4R)) = (\u03C1\u2080r/4\u03B5\u2080)(5/3 \u2212 r/R). Answer (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2010",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"A thin semi-circular ring of radius r has a positive charge q distributed uniformly over it. The net field E\u20D7 at the centre O is",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"A thin semicircular ring above the x-axis with centre O, with unit vectors i to the right and j upward",d:[["p","M70,130 A100,100 0 0 1 270,130","B"],["l",30,130,320,130,"a"],["l",170,130,170,10,"a"],["t",176,148,"O","s"],["t",316,124,"\xEE","es"],["t",176,16,"\u0135","s"]]},o:["{q;2\u03C0\xB2\u03B5\u2080r\xB2}\u0135","{q;4\u03C0\xB2\u03B5\u2080r\xB2}\u0135","\u2212{q;4\u03C0\xB2\u03B5\u2080r\xB2}\u0135","\u2212{q;2\u03C0\xB2\u03B5\u2080r\xB2}\u0135"],c:3,s:`Charge per unit length \u03BB = q/(\u03C0r). The field of each element at O points away from it; the x-components cancel by symmetry.
The y-component of each element is \u2212dE sin\u03B8, so E = \u222B\u2080^\u03C0 (k\u03BB r d\u03B8/r\xB2) sin\u03B8 = (k\u03BB/r)\xB72 = 2k\u03BB/r = 2kq/(\u03C0r\xB2).
E = 2q/(4\u03C0\xB2\u03B5\u2080r\xB2) = q/(2\u03C0\xB2\u03B5\u2080r\xB2).
The charge lies in the upper half, so the field at O points down: \u2212\u0135. Answer (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"A tiny spherical oil drop carrying a net charge q is balanced in still air with a vertical uniform electric field of strength {81\u03C0;7}\xD710\u2075 V m\u207B\xB9. When the field is switched off, the drop is observed to fall with terminal velocity 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m s\u207B\xB9. Given g = 9.8 m s\u207B\xB2, viscosity of the air = 1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N s m\u207B\xB2 and the density of oil = 900 kg m\u207B\xB3, the magnitude of q is",o:["1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C","3.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C","4.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C","8.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C"],c:3,s:`With the field off, the drop falls at terminal speed: weight = viscous force (air buoyancy neglected): (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3\u03C1g = 6\u03C0\u03B7rv.
r\xB2 = 9\u03B7v/(2\u03C1g) = 9 \xD7 1.8\xD710\u207B\u2075 \xD7 2\xD710\u207B\xB3/(2 \xD7 900 \xD7 9.8) = 1.84\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB9 m\xB2, so r \u2248 4.29\xD710\u207B\u2076 m.
mg = (4/3)\u03C0(4.29\xD710\u207B\u2076)\xB3 \xD7 900 \xD7 9.8 \u2248 2.9\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 N.
With the field on, qE = mg, so q = 2.9\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2/[(81\u03C0/7)\xD710\u2075] = 2.9\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2/(3.64\xD710\u2076) \u2248 8.0\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 C. Answer (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"A uniformly charged thin spherical shell of radius R carries uniform surface charge density of \u03C3 per unit area. It is made of two hemispherical shells, held together by pressing them with force F (see figure). F is proportional to",fig:{w:220,h:90,alt:"A sphere split along a vertical dashed line into two hemispheres, with equal forces F pressing the two halves together from left and right",d:[["c",110,45,35],["l",110,8,110,82,"d"],["l",12,45,73,45,"a"],["l",208,45,147,45,"a"],["t",12,36,"F"],["t",208,36,"F","e"]]},o:["{1;\u03B5\u2080}\u03C3\xB2R\xB2","{1;\u03B5\u2080}\u03C3\xB2R","{1;\u03B5\u2080}{\u03C3\xB2;R}","{1;\u03B5\u2080}{\u03C3\xB2;R\xB2}"],c:0,s:`Take one hemisphere. The outward electric pressure on a charged surface is \u03C3\xB2/(2\u03B5\u2080).
The net force on the hemisphere = pressure \xD7 projected area \u03C0R\xB2.
F = {\u03C3\xB2/2\u03B5\u2080} \xD7 \u03C0R\xB2 = \u03C0\u03C3\xB2R\xB2/(2\u03B5\u2080).
So F is proportional to \u03C3\xB2R\xB2/\u03B5\u2080. Answer: option (a).`},{y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:"Let \u03C1(r) = {Q;\u03C0R\u2074} r be the charge density distribution for a solid sphere of radius R and total charge Q. For a point 'p' inside the sphere at distance r\u2081 from the centre of the sphere, the magnitude of electric field is",o:["0","{Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\u2081\xB2}","{Qr\u2081\xB2;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\u2074}","{Qr\u2081\xB2;3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\u2074}"],c:2,s:`Charge inside radius r\u2081: q = \u222B\u03C1\xB74\u03C0r\xB2dr = (4Q/R\u2074)\u222Br\xB3dr (0 to r\u2081) = Qr\u2081\u2074/R\u2074.
Gauss's law: E \xD7 4\u03C0r\u2081\xB2 = q/\u03B5\u2080.
E = Qr\u2081\u2074/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\u2074r\u2081\xB2) = Qr\u2081\xB2/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\u2074).
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2009",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A disk of radius a/4 having a uniformly distributed charge 6 C is placed in the xy-plane with its centre at (\u2212a/2, 0, 0). A rod of length a carrying a uniformly distributed charge 8 C is placed on the x-axis from x = a/4 to x = 5a/4. Two point charges \u22127 C and 3 C are placed at (a/4, \u2212a/4, 0) and (\u22123a/4, 3a/4, 0), respectively. Consider a cubical surface formed by six surfaces x = \xB1 a/2, y = \xB1 a/2, z = \xB1 a/2. The electric flux through this cubical surface is",o:["{\u22122 C;\u03B5\u2080}","{2 C;\u03B5\u2080}","{10 C;\u03B5\u2080}","{12 C;\u03B5\u2080}"],c:0,s:`Flux = (charge inside the cube)/\u03B5\u2080.
Disk: its centre lies on the face x = \u2212a/2, so half of it (3 C) is inside.
Rod: only x = a/4 to a/2 is inside, which is 1/4 of its length, so 2 C.
Point charge \u22127 C at (a/4, \u2212a/4, 0) is inside. The 3 C charge at x = \u22123a/4 is outside.
Total inside = 3 + 2 \u2212 7 = \u22122 C, so flux = \u22122 C/\u03B5\u2080. Answer: option (a).`},{y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"A thin spherical shell of radius R has charge Q spread uniformly over its surface. Which of the following graphs most closely represents the electric field E(r) produced by the shell in the range 0 \u2264 r < \u221E, where r is the distance from the centre of the shell?",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"Four graphs of E against r. (a) rises smoothly to a peak at R then falls; (b) zero up to R, then jumps to a peak and falls; (c) constant up to R then falls; (d) rises linearly to a peak at R then falls",d:[["t",10,18,"(a)","s"],["l",35,98,35,22,"a"],["l",35,98,160,98,"a"],["t",40,24,"E(r)","s"],["t",162,102,"r","s"],["t",95,112,"R","ms"],["t",31,110,"O","es"],["p","M35 98 Q70 94 95 38"],["p","M95 38 Q107 86 155 92"],["t",180,18,"(b)","s"],["l",205,98,205,22,"a"],["l",205,98,330,98,"a"],["t",210,24,"E(r)","s"],["t",332,102,"r","s"],["t",265,112,"R","ms"],["t",201,110,"O","es"],["l",265,98,265,38],["p","M265 38 Q277 86 325 92"],["t",10,136,"(c)","s"],["l",35,216,35,140,"a"],["l",35,216,160,216,"a"],["t",40,142,"E(r)","s"],["t",162,220,"r","s"],["t",95,230,"R","ms"],["t",31,228,"O","es"],["p","M35 160 H95"],["p","M95 160 Q109 204 155 210"],["t",180,136,"(d)","s"],["l",205,216,205,140,"a"],["l",205,216,330,216,"a"],["t",210,142,"E(r)","s"],["t",332,220,"r","s"],["t",265,230,"R","ms"],["t",201,228,"O","es"],["l",205,216,265,156],["p","M265 156 Q277 204 325 210"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:1,s:`Inside a thin shell no charge is enclosed, so E = 0 for r < R.
Just outside, E = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2), which is not zero, so E jumps at r = R.
For r > R, E falls as 1/r\xB2.
Only graph (b) is zero up to R, then jumps and decays as 1/r\xB2. Answer: option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"Consider a neutral conducting sphere. A positive point charge is placed outside the sphere. The net charge on the sphere is then",o:["negative and distributed uniformly over the surface of the sphere","negative and appears only at the point on the sphere closest to the point charge","negative and distributed non-uniformly over the entire surface of the sphere","zero."],c:3,s:`The sphere was neutral and no charge is added to it.
The outside charge only causes induction: negative charge gathers on the near side and an equal positive charge on the far side.
The total charge of the sphere stays zero. Answer: option (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"A spherical portion has been removed from a solid sphere having a charge distributed uniformly in its volume as shown in the figure. The electric field inside the emptied space is",fig:{w:200,h:180,alt:"A solid sphere with a small spherical cavity cut out near its lower right edge",d:[["c",100,90,70],["c",128,118,18]]},o:["zero everywhere","non-zero and uniform","non-uniform","zero at its center."],c:1,s:`Treat the body as a full sphere of density +\u03C1 plus a small sphere of density \u2212\u03C1 filling the cavity.
Inside a uniform sphere E = \u03C1r\u20D7/(3\u03B5\u2080), measured from its own centre.
In the cavity: E = \u03C1r\u20D7\u2081/(3\u03B5\u2080) \u2212 \u03C1r\u20D7\u2082/(3\u03B5\u2080) = \u03C1(r\u20D7\u2081 \u2212 r\u20D7\u2082)/(3\u03B5\u2080) = \u03C1d\u20D7/(3\u03B5\u2080), where d\u20D7 joins the two centres.
This is the same at every point, so the field is non-zero and uniform. Answer: option (b).`},{y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"An electric dipole is placed at an angle of 30\xB0 to a non-uniform electric field. The dipole will experience",o:["a torque only","a translational force only in the direction of the field","a translational force only in a direction normal to the direction of the field","a torque as well as a translational force."],c:3,s:`At 30\xB0 to the field, the torque p \xD7 E is not zero.
In a non-uniform field the forces on +q and \u2212q have different magnitudes, so the net force is not zero.
So the dipole feels both a torque and a translational force. Answer: option (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Two point charges +8q and \u22122q are located at x = 0 and x = L respectively. The location of a point on the x axis at which the net electric field due to these two point charges is zero is",o:["8L","4L","2L","{L;4}"],c:2,s:`The field can be zero only outside the pair, on the side of the smaller charge (\u22122q), so x > L.
Equal magnitudes: 8q/x\xB2 = 2q/(x \u2212 L)\xB2.
So 4(x \u2212 L)\xB2 = x\xB2, which gives 2(x \u2212 L) = x, so x = 2L.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"A charged ball B hangs from a silk thread S, which makes an angle \u03B8 with a large charged conducting sheet P, as shown in the figure. The surface charge density \u03C3 of the sheet is proportional to",fig:{w:240,h:175,alt:"A vertical charged sheet P with plus signs; a thread S from the sheet carries a ball B and makes angle theta with the sheet",d:[["l",38,8,38,150,"B"],["t",30,20,"+","ms"],["t",30,36,"+","ms"],["t",30,52,"+","ms"],["t",30,68,"+","ms"],["t",30,84,"+","ms"],["t",30,100,"+","ms"],["t",30,116,"+","ms"],["t",30,132,"+","ms"],["t",30,148,"+","ms"],["l",38,30,38,138,"d"],["l",38,30,84.5,129.7],["c",84.5,129.7,6,"F"],["t",50,64,"\u03B8"],["t",68,86,"S"],["t",94.5,141.7,"B"],["t",12,84,"P"],["t",38,166,"sheet","ms"]]},o:["sin\u03B8","tan\u03B8","cos\u03B8","cot\u03B8"],c:1,s:`Forces on the ball: weight mg (down), tension T (along the thread), electric force qE (horizontal).
Near a large conducting sheet E = \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080.
T cos\u03B8 = mg and T sin\u03B8 = q\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080.
Dividing: tan\u03B8 = q\u03C3/(\u03B5\u2080mg), so \u03C3 \u221D tan\u03B8. Answer: option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Three infinitely long charge sheets are placed as shown in figure. The electric field at point P is",fig:{w:300,h:175,alt:"Three horizontal sheets seen edge on: sigma at z=3a, minus 2 sigma at z=a, minus sigma at z=-a; point P lies between z=a and z=3a",d:[["l",100,160,100,8,"da"],["t",106,12,"Z"],["l",20,30,290,30],["l",20,80,290,80],["l",20,105,292,105,"da"],["l",20,130,290,130],["t",24,24,"\u03C3"],["t",24,74,"\u22122\u03C3"],["t",24,124,"\u2212\u03C3"],["t",286,24,"Z = 3a","es"],["t",286,74,"Z = a","es"],["t",286,150,"Z = \u2212a","es"],["t",290,100,"X","e"],["c",135,55,2.5,"F"],["t",142,59,"P"]]},o:["{2\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080} k\u0302","{4\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080} k\u0302","\u2212{2\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080} k\u0302","\u2212{4\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080} k\u0302"],c:2,s:`Each sheet gives a field \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080) (for charge \u03C3) pointing away from a positive sheet and towards a negative one.
P lies between z = a and z = 3a. The \u03C3 sheet above pushes P down: \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080) along \u2212k\u0302.
The \u22122\u03C3 sheet below attracts P downward: \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080 along \u2212k\u0302.
The \u2212\u03C3 sheet at z = \u2212a attracts P downward: \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080) along \u2212k\u0302.
Total = \u2212(1/2 + 1 + 1/2)\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080 k\u0302 = \u22122\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080 k\u0302. Answer: option (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"A charged oil drop is suspended in a uniform field of 3 \xD7 10\u2074 V/m so that it neither falls nor rises. The charge on the drop will be (take the mass of the charge = 9.9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075 kg and g = 10 m/s\xB2)",o:["1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 C","3.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 C","3.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 C","4.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 C"],c:2,s:`The drop is in equilibrium, so qE = mg.
q = mg/E = (9.9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075 \xD7 10)/(3 \xD7 10\u2074) = 3.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 C.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Six charges of equal magnitude, 3 positive and 3 negative are to be placed on PQRSTU corners of a regular hexagon, such that field at the centre is double that of what it would have been if only one +ve charge is placed at R.",fig:{w:200,h:170,alt:"A regular hexagon with corners P, Q, R, S, T, U going clockwise from the upper left, and its centre O",d:[["p","M69.0 31.3 L131.0 31.3 L162.0 85.0 L131.0 138.7 L69.0 138.7 L38.0 85.0 Z"],["c",100,85,2,"F"],["t",105,99,"O","s"],["t",61,27.3,"P","e"],["t",139,27.3,"Q"],["t",170,89,"R"],["t",139,150.7,"S"],["t",61,150.7,"T","e"],["t",30,89,"U","e"]]},o:["+, +, +, \u2212, \u2212, \u2212","\u2212, +, +, \u2212, \u2212, \u2212","\u2212, +, +, \u2212, +, \u2212","+, \u2212, +, \u2212, +, \u2212"],c:2,s:`Let E be the field of one charge at the centre. Opposite corners are P-S, Q-T, R-U.
Two opposite corners with the same sign cancel. Two opposite corners with opposite signs add to 2E, pointing from the + corner towards the \u2212 corner.
A single + at R gives E pointing from R towards U, so we need 2E along R to U.
Option (c): P(\u2212) S(\u2212) cancel, Q(+) T(+) cancel, R(+) U(\u2212) give 2E along R to U. This is correct.
The other options fail: (a) gives 4E, (b) gives 2\u221A3 E, (d) gives zero. Answer: option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"A Gaussian surface in the figure is shown by dotted line. The electric field on the surface will be",fig:{w:240,h:170,alt:"A dotted closed Gaussian surface enclosing charges q1 and minus q1, with charge q2 outside it",d:[["c",100,95,65,"d"],["c",125,80,2.5,"F"],["t",131,78,"q\u2081"],["c",80,120,2.5,"F"],["t",50,134,"\u2212q\u2081"],["c",185,32,2.5,"F"],["t",192,36,"q\u2082"]]},o:["due to q\u2081 and q\u2082 only","due to q\u2082 only","zero","due to all."],c:3,s:`Gauss's law says only that the total flux depends on the enclosed charge (q\u2081 and \u2212q\u2081 here).
The electric field at a point is produced by all charges, inside and outside, including q\u2082.
So the field on the surface is due to all charges. Answer: option (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"If the electric flux entering and leaving an enclosed surface respectively is \u03C6\u2081 and \u03C6\u2082, the electric charge inside the surface will be",o:["(\u03C6\u2082 \u2212 \u03C6\u2081)\u03B5\u2080","{\u03C6\u2081 + \u03C6\u2082;\u03B5\u2080}","{\u03C6\u2082 \u2212 \u03C6\u2081;\u03B5\u2080}","(\u03C6\u2081 + \u03C6\u2082)\u03B5\u2080"],c:0,s:`Net outward flux = \u03C6\u2082 \u2212 \u03C6\u2081 (leaving minus entering).
Gauss's law: net flux = q/\u03B5\u2080.
So q = (\u03C6\u2082 \u2212 \u03C6\u2081)\u03B5\u2080. Answer: option (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"A metallic shell has a point charge q kept inside its cavity. Which one of the following diagrams correctly represents the electric lines of force?",fig:{w:340,h:250,alt:"Four diagrams of a thick metal shell with charge q inside the cavity. (a) lines of force run straight through the metal; (b) lines go straight to the inner wall and radial lines leave the outer surface; (c) lines curve to meet the inner wall at right angles and radial lines leave the outer surface; (d) like (c) but one line passes straight through the metal",d:[["t",13,25,"(a)","s"],["c",95,65,46],["c",95,65,33],["c",87,70,2.5,"F"],["t",91,84,"q","s"],["l",87,70,153.9,31,"a"],["l",87,70,61,6.1,"a"],["l",87,70,39.3,104,"a"],["l",87,70,129,123.9,"a"],["t",173,25,"(b)","s"],["c",255,65,46],["c",255,65,33],["c",247,70,2.5,"F"],["t",251,84,"q","s"],["l",247,70,283.6,48.5,"a"],["l",294.8,42,312.2,32,"a"],["l",247,70,238.5,36.4,"a"],["l",232,25.2,222,7.8,"a"],["l",247,70,228,83.9,"a"],["l",217.3,91.4,200.9,102.9,"a"],["l",247,70,271.5,93.6,"a"],["l",278,104.8,288,122.2,"a"],["t",13,145,"(c)","s"],["c",95,185,46],["c",95,185,33],["c",87,190,2.5,"F"],["t",91,204,"q","s"],["p","M87 190 Q111.5 175.5 123.6 168.5","a"],["l",134.8,162,152.2,152,"a"],["p","M87 190 Q85.5 168.5 78.5 156.4","a"],["l",72,145.2,62,127.8,"a"],["p","M87 190 Q79.4 195.9 68.0 203.9","a"],["l",57.3,211.4,40.9,222.9,"a"],["p","M87 190 Q104.5 201.5 111.5 213.6","a"],["l",118,224.8,128,242.2,"a"],["t",173,145,"(d)","s"],["c",255,185,46],["c",255,185,33],["c",247,190,2.5,"F"],["t",251,204,"q","s"],["p","M247 190 Q271.5 175.5 283.6 168.5","a"],["l",294.8,162,312.2,152,"a"],["p","M247 190 Q245.5 168.5 238.5 156.4","a"],["l",232,145.2,222,127.8,"a"],["p","M247 190 Q239.4 195.9 228.0 203.9","a"],["l",217.3,211.4,200.9,222.9,"a"],["l",247,190,289,243.9,"a"]]},o:["Diagram (a)","Diagram (b)","Diagram (c)","Diagram (d)"],c:2,s:`Inside the metal E = 0, so no line of force can pass through the shell wall.
Lines from q end on the induced \u2212q on the inner surface and meet the metal surface at right angles.
The induced +q on the outer surface starts new lines that go radially outward.
Diagram (c) shows this. Answer: option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"Three positive charges of equal value q are placed at the vertices of an equilateral triangle. The resulting lines of force should be sketched as in",fig:{w:340,h:250,alt:"Four sketches for three equal positive charges at the corners of an equilateral triangle. (a) curved lines joining the charges; (b) lines leaving each charge and also running to the centre; (c) lines spreading outward from each charge with none through the centre; (d) closed circles around each charge",d:[["t",13,25,"(a)","s"],["p","M95 35 L69 80 L121 80 Z"],["p","M95 35 Q60.0 35.5 69 80"],["p","M69 80 Q95.0 102.0 121 80"],["p","M121 80 Q130.0 35.5 95 35"],["c",95,35,3.5,"F"],["c",69,80,3.5,"F"],["c",121,80,3.5,"F"],["t",173,25,"(b)","s"],["l",255,35,255,65,"a"],["l",255,35,269.9,13.7,"a"],["l",255,35,255,9,"a"],["l",255,35,240.1,13.7,"a"],["l",229,80,255,65,"a"],["l",229,80,203.1,77.7,"a"],["l",229,80,206.5,93,"a"],["l",229,80,218,103.6,"a"],["l",281,80,255,65,"a"],["l",281,80,292,103.6,"a"],["l",281,80,303.5,93,"a"],["l",281,80,306.9,77.7,"a"],["c",255,35,3.5,"F"],["c",229,80,3.5,"F"],["c",281,80,3.5,"F"],["t",13,145,"(c)","s"],["l",95,155,116.4,137,"a"],["l",95,155,106.8,129.6,"a"],["l",95,155,95,127,"a"],["l",95,155,83.2,129.6,"a"],["l",95,155,73.6,137,"a"],["l",69,200,42.7,190.4,"a"],["l",69,200,41.1,202.4,"a"],["l",69,200,44.8,214,"a"],["l",69,200,52.9,222.9,"a"],["l",69,200,64.1,227.6,"a"],["l",121,200,125.9,227.6,"a"],["l",121,200,137.1,222.9,"a"],["l",121,200,145.2,214,"a"],["l",121,200,148.9,202.4,"a"],["l",121,200,147.3,190.4,"a"],["c",95,155,3.5,"F"],["c",69,200,3.5,"F"],["c",121,200,3.5,"F"],["t",173,145,"(d)","s"],["c",255,155,8],["c",255,155,14],["c",255,155,20],["c",229,200,8],["c",229,200,14],["c",229,200,20],["c",281,200,8],["c",281,200,14],["c",281,200,20],["c",255,155,2.5,"F"],["c",229,200,2.5,"F"],["c",281,200,2.5,"F"]]},o:["Diagram (a)","Diagram (b)","Diagram (c)","Diagram (d)"],c:2,s:`All three charges are positive, so lines start on the charges and go away; none join two like charges (so (a) is wrong).
By symmetry the field at the centre of the triangle is zero, so no line passes through the centre (so (b) is wrong).
Closed circles around the charges are not lines of force (so (d) is wrong).
The lines spread outward from each charge and bend away from each other, as in diagram (c). Answer: option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"An electron of mass m\u2091, initially at rest, moves through a certain distance in a uniform electric field in time t\u2081. A proton of mass m\u209A, also initially at rest, takes time t\u2082 to move through an equal distance in this uniform electric field. Neglecting the effect of gravity, the ratio of t\u2082/t\u2081 is nearly equal to",o:["1","(m\u209A/m\u2091)^(1/2)","(m\u2091/m\u209A)^(1/2)","1836"],c:1,s:`The force on each is eE, so a = eE/m.
d = \xBDat\xB2 gives t = \u221A(2d/a) = \u221A(2dm/eE), so t \u221D \u221Am.
t\u2082/t\u2081 = \u221A(m\u209A/m\u2091) (\u2248 43). Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:`A small electric dipole p\u20D7\u2080, having a moment of inertia I about its center, is kept at a distance r from the center of a spherical shell of radius R. The surface charge density \u03C3 is uniformly distributed on the spherical shell. The dipole is initially oriented at a small angle \u03B8 as shown in the figure. While staying at a distance r, the dipole is free to rotate about its center. If released from rest, then which of the following statement(s) is(are) correct?
[\u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of free space.]`,fig:{w:220,h:200,alt:"A small dipole p0 tilted by a small angle theta from the radial direction, at distance r above the centre O of a spherical shell of radius R",d:[["c",110,145,45],["c",110,145,2,"F"],["t",116,160,"O","s"],["l",110,145,110,12,"d"],["l",30,145,30,50,"b"],["t",24,100,"r","e"],["l",103,64,116,36,"a"],["t",121,44,"p\u2080"],["t",96,34,"\u03B8","e"],["l",110,145,144,167],["t",134,150,"R"]]},o:["The dipole will undergo small oscillations at any finite value of r.","The dipole will undergo small oscillations at any finite value of r > R.","The dipole will undergo small oscillations with an angular frequency of \u221A{2\u03C3p\u2080;\u03B5\u2080I} at r = 2R.","The dipole will undergo small oscillations with an angular frequency of \u221A{\u03C3p\u2080;100\u03B5\u2080I} at r = 10R."],c:[1,3],s:`Outside the shell (r > R) the field is radial: E = \u03C3R\xB2/(\u03B5\u2080r\xB2). Inside, E = 0.
For r > R a small tilt \u03B8 from the field gives a restoring torque \u2212p\u2080E\u03B8, so \u03C9 = \u221A(p\u2080E/I): small oscillations. For r < R there is no field, so no oscillation, and (a) is false; (b) is true.
At r = 2R: E = \u03C3/(4\u03B5\u2080), \u03C9 = \u221A(\u03C3p\u2080/(4\u03B5\u2080I)), so (c) is false.
At r = 10R: E = \u03C3/(100\u03B5\u2080), \u03C9 = \u221A(\u03C3p\u2080/(100\u03B5\u2080I)), so (d) is true.
Answer: (b) and (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A charged shell of radius R carries a total charge Q. Given \u03C6 as the flux of electric field through a closed cylindrical surface of height h, radius r and with its center same as that of the shell. Here, center of cylinder is a point on the axis of the cylinder which is equidistant from its top and bottom surfaces. Which of the following option(s) is/are correct? [\u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of free space]",o:["If h > 2R and r = {4R;5} then \u03C6 = {Q;5\u03B5\u2080}","If h > 2R and r = {3R;5} then \u03C6 = {Q;5\u03B5\u2080}","If h < {8R;5} and r = {3R;5} then \u03C6 = 0","If h > 2R and r > R then \u03C6 = {Q;\u03B5\u2080}"],c:[1,2,3],s:`Flux = (charge of the shell inside the cylinder)/\u03B5\u2080.
For h > 2R the cylinder covers the shell's full height. The shell part inside is two polar caps with sin\u03B8 < r/R. Each cap has area 2\u03C0R\xB2(1 \u2212 cos\u03B8).
(a) r = 4R/5: cos\u03B8 = 3/5, caps hold 2\xD7(1/5) = 2/5 of Q, so \u03C6 = 2Q/(5\u03B5\u2080). False.
(b) r = 3R/5: cos\u03B8 = 4/5, caps hold 2\xD7(1/10) = 1/5 of Q, so \u03C6 = Q/(5\u03B5\u2080). True.
(c) h < 8R/5 and r = 3R/5: the cylinder lies entirely inside the shell (corner distance < R), so \u03C6 = 0. True.
(d) h > 2R and r > R: the cylinder encloses the whole shell, \u03C6 = Q/\u03B5\u2080. True.
Answer: (b), (c), (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"An electric dipole with dipole moment {p\u2080;\u221A2}(\xEE + \u0135) is held fixed at the origin O in the presence of a uniform electric field of magnitude E\u2080. If the potential is constant on a circle of radius R centered at the origin as shown in figure, then the correct statement(s) is/are (\u03B5\u2080 is permittivity of free space. R \u226B dipole size)",fig:{w:240,h:220,alt:"A circle of radius R centred on the origin with a dipole at the origin pointing along the 45 degree line; point A on the circle along the dipole direction and point B at 135 degrees",d:[["c",120,110,80],["l",28,110,228,110,"a"],["l",120,205,120,12,"a"],["t",224,104,"x","e"],["t",126,18,"y"],["l",63.4,53.4,176.6,166.6,"d"],["l",120,110,142,88,"aB"],["c",176.6,53.4,3,"F"],["c",63.4,53.4,3,"F"],["t",182.6,49.4,"A"],["t",57.4,49.4,"B","e"],["t",114,124,"O","e"],["t",150,140,"R"]]},o:["The magnitude of total electric field on any two points of the circle will be same.","Total electric field at point A is E\u20D7(A) = \u221A2 E\u2080(\xEE + \u0135)","R = ({p\u2080;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080E\u2080})^(1/3)","Total electric field at point B is E\u20D7(B) = 0"],c:[2,3],s:`On the circle, dipole potential = p\u2080cos\u03B1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2) (\u03B1 from the dipole axis). The uniform field adds \u2212E\u2080R cos(\u03B1\u2032).
For the total to be constant, E\u20D7\u2080 must point along the dipole and p\u2080/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2) = E\u2080R, so R\xB3 = p\u2080/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080E\u2080). (c) is true.
At B (perpendicular to the dipole): dipole field = p\u2080/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB3) = E\u2080 opposite to p, uniform field E\u2080 along p, so net zero. (d) is true.
At A (on the axis): dipole gives 2E\u2080 and uniform field E\u2080 in the same direction, so 3E\u2080 along (\xEE + \u0135)/\u221A2, not \u221A2E\u2080(\xEE + \u0135) (magnitude 2E\u2080). (b) is false.
On the circle the field is radial with magnitude 3E\u2080|cos\u03B1|, not constant. (a) is false.
Answer: (c), (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"An infinitely long thin non-conducting wire is parallel to the z-axis and carries a uniform line charge density \u03BB. It pierces a thin non-conducting spherical shell of radius R in such a way that the arc PQ subtends an angle 120\xB0 at the centre O of the spherical shell, as shown in the figure. The permittivity of free space is \u03B5\u2080. Which of the following statements is (are) true?",fig:{w:260,h:220,alt:"A spherical shell with centre O and a vertical dashed line charge lambda passing through it to the left of the centre, cutting the shell at P and Q so that angle POQ is 120 degrees; the z axis passes through O",d:[["c",150,110,80],["l",110,6,110,214,"d"],["l",150,214,150,6,"da"],["t",154,14,"z"],["t",116,14,"\u03BB"],["l",150,110,110,40.7],["l",150,110,110,179.3],["c",150,110,2,"F"],["t",156,124,"O","s"],["c",110,40.7,3,"F"],["c",110,179.3,3,"F"],["t",100,36.7,"P","e"],["t",100,191.3,"Q","e"],["t",124,114,"120\xB0","s"],["t",126,78,"R","s"]]},o:["The electric flux through the shell is {\u221A3R\u03BB;\u03B5\u2080}","The z-component of the electric field is zero at all the points on the surface of the shell","The electric flux through the shell is {\u221A2R\u03BB;\u03B5\u2080}","The electric field is normal to the surface of the shell at all points"],c:[0,1],s:`Arc PQ = 120\xB0, so the wire is at distance R cos60\xB0 = R/2 from O.
Length of wire inside the shell = 2R sin60\xB0 = \u221A3R, so enclosed charge = \u221A3R\u03BB.
Flux = \u221A3R\u03BB/\u03B5\u2080: (a) true, (c) false.
The field of an infinite line is perpendicular to the wire, so it has no z-component: (b) true.
It is along the radial line from the wire, which is not along the sphere's normal everywhere: (d) false.
Answer: (a), (b).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"Consider a uniform spherical charge distribution of radius R\u2081 centred at the origin O. In this distribution, a spherical cavity of radius R\u2082, centred at P with distance OP = a = R\u2081 \u2212 R\u2082 (see figure) is made. If the electric field inside the cavity at position r\u20D7 is E\u20D7(r\u20D7), then the correct statement(s) is(are)",fig:{w:260,h:220,alt:"A large uniformly charged sphere of radius R1 with centre O and a small spherical cavity of radius R2 centred at P, with OP = a",d:[["c",130,120,95],["c",130,120,2,"F"],["t",134,136,"O","s"],["l",130,120,225,120],["t",180,114,"R\u2081"],["c",78,68,22],["c",78,68,2,"F"],["t",84,82,"P","s"],["l",78,68,78,46],["t",82,58,"R\u2082","s"],["l",130,120,78,68,"d"],["t",100,104,"a","s"]]},o:["E\u20D7 is uniform, its magnitude is independent of R\u2082 but its direction depends on r\u20D7.","E\u20D7 is uniform, its magnitude depends on R\u2082 and its direction depends on r\u20D7.","E\u20D7 is uniform, its magnitude is independent of a but its direction depends on r\u20D7.","E\u20D7 is uniform and both its magnitude and direction depend on a\u20D7."],c:3,s:`Fill the cavity with +\u03C1 and \u2212\u03C1. Inside the cavity E\u20D7 = \u03C1r\u20D7\u2081/(3\u03B5\u2080) \u2212 \u03C1r\u20D7\u2082/(3\u03B5\u2080) = \u03C1a\u20D7/(3\u03B5\u2080), where a\u20D7 = OP.
This is the same at all points of the cavity (uniform), so the direction does not depend on the position r\u20D7.
Its magnitude \u03C1a/(3\u03B5\u2080) does not depend on R\u2082, but depends on a, and its direction is along a\u20D7.
So only (d) is correct. Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"The figures below depict two situations in which two infinitely long static line charges of constant positive line charge density \u03BB are kept parallel to each other. In their resulting electric field, point charges q and \u2212q are kept in equilibrium between them. The point charges are confined to move in the x direction only. If they are given a small displacement about their equilibrium positions, then the correct statement(s) is(are)",fig:{w:340,h:130,alt:"Two situations, each with two parallel line charges lambda: on the left a charge +q midway between them, on the right a charge minus q midway between them; the x axis runs horizontally",d:[["l",30,16,30,116],["l",130,16,130,116],["t",30,12,"\u03BB","ms"],["t",130,12,"\u03BB","ms"],["l",12,70,152,70,"da"],["t",156,74,"x"],["c",80,70,3,"F"],["t",80,90,"+q","ms"],["l",200,16,200,116],["l",300,16,300,116],["t",200,12,"\u03BB","ms"],["t",300,12,"\u03BB","ms"],["l",182,70,322,70,"da"],["t",326,74,"x"],["c",250,70,3,"F"],["t",250,90,"\u2212q","ms"]]},o:["Both charges execute simple harmonic motion.","Both charges will continue moving in the direction of their displacement.","Charge +q executes simple harmonic motion while charge \u2212q continues moving in the direction of its displacement.","Charge \u2212q executes simple harmonic motion while charge +q continues moving in the direction of its displacement."],c:2,s:`Midway between two equal positive lines the field is zero (equilibrium).
+q displaced towards one line: the nearer line repels it more, so the net force points back to the middle and is proportional to the displacement. This is SHM.
\u2212q displaced towards one line: the nearer line attracts it more, so the force is along the displacement and it keeps moving away.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Let E\u2081(r), E\u2082(r) and E\u2083(r) be the respective electric fields at a distance r from a point charge Q, an infinitely long wire with constant linear charge density \u03BB, and an infinite plane with uniform surface charge density \u03C3. If E\u2081(r\u2080) = E\u2082(r\u2080) = E\u2083(r\u2080) at a given distance r\u2080, then",o:["Q = 4\u03C3\u03C0r\u2080\xB2","r\u2080 = {\u03BB;2\u03C0\u03C3}","E\u2081(r\u2080/2) = 2E\u2082(r\u2080/2)","E\u2082(r\u2080/2) = 4E\u2083(r\u2080/2)"],c:2,s:`E\u2081 = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2), E\u2082 = \u03BB/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r), E\u2083 = \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080) (constant).
E\u2081(r\u2080) = E\u2083: Q = 2\u03C0\u03C3r\u2080\xB2, so (a) is false. E\u2082(r\u2080) = E\u2083: r\u2080 = \u03BB/(\u03C0\u03C3), so (b) is false.
Let the common value be E. At r\u2080/2: E\u2081 = 4E, E\u2082 = 2E, E\u2083 = E.
So E\u2081 = 2E\u2082 (c is true), and E\u2082 = 2E\u2083 not 4E\u2083 (d is false). Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Two non-conducting solid spheres of radii R and 2R, having uniform volume charge densities \u03C1\u2081 and \u03C1\u2082 respectively, touch each other. The net electric field at a distance 2R from the centre of the smaller sphere, along the line joining the centres of the spheres, is zero. The ratio {\u03C1\u2081;\u03C1\u2082} can be",o:["\u22124","\u2212{32;25}","{32;25}","4"],c:[1,3],s:`Centres are 3R apart. The point is 2R from the small centre, either between the spheres or on the far side of the small sphere.
Case 1, between: distance from big centre is R (inside it). Small sphere: \u03C1\u2081R/(12\u03B5\u2080) one way; big sphere: \u03C1\u2082R/(3\u03B5\u2080) the other way. Zero gives \u03C1\u2081/12 = \u03C1\u2082/3, so \u03C1\u2081/\u03C1\u2082 = 4.
Case 2, far side: distance from big centre is 5R (outside it). Small: \u03C1\u2081R/(12\u03B5\u2080); big: charge (32/3)\u03C0R\xB3\u03C1\u2082 gives 8\u03C1\u2082R/(75\u03B5\u2080), both in the same direction. Zero gives \u03C1\u2081/12 = \u22128\u03C1\u2082/75, so \u03C1\u2081/\u03C1\u2082 = \u221232/25.
Answer: (b) and (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A cubical region of side a has its centre at the origin. It encloses three fixed point charges, \u2212q at (0, \u2212{a;4}, 0), +3q at (0, 0, 0) and \u2212q at (0, +{a;4}, 0). Choose the correct option(s).",o:["The net electric flux crossing the plane x = +{a;2} is equal to the net electric flux crossing the plane x = \u2212{a;2}.","The net electric flux crossing the plane y = +{a;2} is more than the net electric flux crossing the plane y = \u2212{a;2}.","The net electric flux crossing the entire region is {q;\u03B5\u2080}.","The net electric flux crossing the plane z = +{a;2} is equal to the net electric flux crossing the plane x = +{a;2}."],c:[0,2,3],s:`The charges lie on the y-axis, symmetric about the origin.
(a) Symmetric about the plane x = 0, so the two x-faces get equal flux. True.
(b) Symmetric about y = 0 too (\u2212q at \xB1a/4), so the y-faces get equal flux. False.
(c) Total charge = \u2212q + 3q \u2212 q = q, so total flux = q/\u03B5\u2080. True.
(d) The set-up is the same for x and z (charges on the y-axis), so the faces z = +a/2 and x = +a/2 get equal flux. True.
Answer: (a), (c), (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A few electric field lines for a system of two charges Q\u2081 and Q\u2082 fixed at two different points on the x-axis are shown in the figure. These lines suggest that",fig:{w:300,h:160,alt:"Field lines leave Q1 on the left; many curve around and run to Q2 on the right, which has fewer lines",d:[["c",70,80,4,"F"],["c",235,80,4,"F"],["t",64,100,"Q\u2081","e"],["t",241,100,"Q\u2082"],["l",70,80,235,80,"a"],["p","M70 80 C100 36 205 36 235 80","a"],["p","M70 80 C90 10 215 10 235 80","a"],["p","M70 80 C100 124 205 124 235 80","a"],["p","M70 80 C90 150 215 150 235 80","a"],["l",70,80,28,56,"a"],["l",70,80,22,80,"a"],["l",70,80,28,104,"a"],["l",70,80,40,34,"a"],["l",70,80,40,126,"a"],["l",290,40,238,76,"a"],["l",290,120,238,84,"a"]]},o:["|Q\u2081| > |Q\u2082|","|Q\u2081| < |Q\u2082|","at a finite distance to the left of Q\u2081 the electric field is zero","at a finite distance to the right of Q\u2082 the electric field is zero"],c:[0,3],s:`Lines run from Q\u2081 to Q\u2082, so the charges are opposite. More lines leave Q\u2081 than end on Q\u2082, so |Q\u2081| > |Q\u2082|: (a) true.
For unlike charges the zero-field point lies outside the pair, on the side of the smaller charge. That is to the right of Q\u2082: (d) true, (c) false.
Answer: (a), (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A positively charged thin metal ring of radius R is fixed in the xy plane with its centre at the origin O. A negatively charged particle P is released from rest at the point (0, 0, z\u2080) where z\u2080 > 0. Then the motion of P is",o:["periodic, for all values of z\u2080 satisfying 0 < z\u2080 < \u221E","simple harmonic, for all values of z\u2080 satisfying 0 < z\u2080 \u2264 R","approximately simple harmonic, provided z\u2080 \u226A R","such that P crosses O and continues to move along the negative z axis towards z = \u2212\u221E"],c:[0,2],s:`On the axis the ring's field is E = kQz/(R\xB2 + z\xB2)^(3/2), so the force on the negative particle always points towards O.
The particle passes through O, is pulled back on the other side, and oscillates between +z\u2080 and \u2212z\u2080. So the motion is periodic: (a) true, (d) false.
For z\u2080 \u226A R: F \u2248 \u2212(kQq/R\xB3)z, which is proportional to \u2212z, so it is approximately SHM: (c) true.
For larger z\u2080 the force is not linear in z, so (b) is false.
Answer: (a), (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"A non-conducting solid sphere of radius R is uniformly charged. The magnitude of the electric field due to the sphere at a distance r from its centre",o:["increases as r increases, for r < R","decreases as r increases, for 0 < r < \u221E","decreases as r increases, for R < r < \u221E","is discontinuous at r = R."],c:[0,2],s:`Inside (r < R): E = \u03C1r/(3\u03B5\u2080), which increases with r. (a) true, (b) false.
Outside (r > R): E = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2), which decreases with r. (c) true.
At r = R both formulas give Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2), so E is continuous. (d) false.
Answer: (a), (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"A metallic solid sphere is placed in a uniform electric field. The lines of force follow the path(s) shown in figure as",fig:{w:280,h:170,alt:"A metal sphere in a uniform field with four lines of force labelled 1 to 4 from the left; lines 1, 2 and 3 pass through the sphere, line 4 bends to meet the surface and does not enter it",d:[["c",140,82,52],["l",12,40,262,40,"a"],["p","M12 68 H100 Q140 96 180 68 H262","a"],["l",12,96,262,96,"a"],["p","M12 124 H62 Q92 124 106 114","a"],["p","M174 114 Q188 124 218 124 H262","a"],["t",2,44,"1"],["t",2,72,"2"],["t",2,100,"3"],["t",2,128,"4"],["t",268,44,"1"],["t",268,72,"2"],["t",268,100,"3"],["t",268,128,"4"]]},o:["1","2","3","4."],c:3,s:`Inside a metal E = 0, so lines cannot go through the sphere.
Lines must end on (or start from) the surface at right angles to it.
Only path 4 meets the surface perpendicularly and does not enter the sphere. Paths 1, 2 and 3 go through the conductor.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"The electric field in a region is given by E\u20D7 = (2\xEE + 3\u0135 + 6k\u0302) \xD7 10\xB3 N/C. The flux of the field through a rectangular surface parallel to x-z plane is 6.0 N m\xB2 C\u207B\xB9. The area of the surface is ______ cm\xB2.",n:20,s:`A surface parallel to the x-z plane has its normal along \u0135, so only the y-component of E gives flux.
Flux = E_y \xD7 A, so A = 6.0/E_y.
E_y = 3 \xD7 10\xB3 N/C, so A = 6.0/(3 \xD7 10\xB3) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2 = 20 cm\xB2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A square loop of sides a = 1 m is held normally in front of a point charge q = 1 C. The flux of the electric field through the shaded region is {5;p} \xD7 {1;\u03B5\u2080} N m\xB2/C, where the value of p is ______.",fig:{w:300,h:205,alt:"A square of side a divided into four equal quarters. The two lower quarters are shaded, and so is the triangle in the upper left quarter bounded by the left edge, the horizontal middle line and the diagonal to the centre. The point charge q is on the normal through the centre, a/2 in front",d:[["p","M60 100 H200 V170 H60 Z","f"],["p","M60 30 V100 H130 Z","f"],["r",60,30,140,140],["l",130,30,130,170],["l",60,100,200,100],["l",60,30,130,100,"d"],["c",130,100,3,"F"],["t",138,94,"q (a/2 in front)","s"],["t",130,24,"a","ms"],["l",44,30,44,100,"b"],["t",40,68,"a/2","es"]]},n:48,s:`The charge is on the normal through the loop's centre, a/2 away, so the loop is one face of a cube with q at its centre.
Flux through the whole cube = q/\u03B5\u2080, so through one face = q/(6\u03B5\u2080).
The shaded part is the lower half (1/2 of the face) plus a triangle that is half of the upper-left quarter (1/8). By symmetry about the diagonal the triangle gets half the quarter's flux. Shaded fraction = 5/8.
Flux = (5/8) \xD7 1/(6\u03B5\u2080) = (5/48)(1/\u03B5\u2080).
So p = 48.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"An infinite plane sheet of charge having uniform surface charge density + \u03C3\u209B C/m\xB2 is placed on x-y plane. Another infinitely long line charge having uniform linear charge density + \u03BB\u2091 C/m is placed at z = 4 m plane and parallel to y-axis. If the magnitude values |\u03C3\u209B| = 2 |\u03BB\u2091| then at point (0, 0, 2), the ratio of magnitudes of electric field values due to sheet charge to that of line charge is \u03C0\u221An : 1. The value of n is ______.",n:16,s:`Sheet: E\u2081 = \u03C3\u209B/(2\u03B5\u2080).
The point (0, 0, 2) is 4 \u2212 2 = 2 m from the line, so E\u2082 = \u03BB\u2091/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080 \xD7 2) = \u03BB\u2091/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080).
E\u2081/E\u2082 = [\u03C3\u209B/(2\u03B5\u2080)] \xD7 [4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080/\u03BB\u2091] = 2\u03C0\u03C3\u209B/\u03BB\u2091 = 2\u03C0 \xD7 2 = 4\u03C0.
\u03C0\u221An = 4\u03C0 gives \u221An = 4, so n = 16.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"The electric field at point p due to an electric dipole is E. The electric field at point R on equatorial line will be {E;x}. The value of x is ______.",fig:{w:300,h:240,alt:"A dipole with minus q and plus q on a horizontal line, centre O, point P on that line at distance r from O, point Q at distance r above O and point R at distance 2r above O on the perpendicular bisector",d:[["l",40,190,270,190],["c",100,190,4,"F"],["c",180,190,4,"F"],["c",140,190,2,"F"],["c",215,190,3,"F"],["t",100,208,"\u2212q","ms"],["t",180,208,"+q","ms"],["t",140,208,"O","ms"],["t",222,184,"P"],["l",140,190,140,40,"d"],["c",140,120,3,"F"],["c",140,50,3,"F"],["t",148,124,"Q"],["t",148,54,"R"],["l",120,190,120,50,"b"],["t",114,124,"2r","e"],["l",160,190,160,120,"b"],["t",166,160,"r"],["l",140,226,215,226,"b"],["t",178,238,"r","ms"]]},n:16,s:`P is on the axis at distance r from the centre: E = 2kp/r\xB3.
R is on the equatorial line at distance 2r: E_R = kp/(2r)\xB3 = kp/(8r\xB3).
E_R/E = (kp/8r\xB3)/(2kp/r\xB3) = 1/16.
So E_R = E/16 and x = 16.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Three infinitely long charged thin sheets are placed as shown in figure. The magnitude of electric field at the point P is {x\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}. The value of x is ______. (all quantities are measured in SI units).",fig:{w:320,h:160,alt:"Three parallel sheets seen edge on at x = -a (minus sigma), x = a (minus 2 sigma) and x = 3a (sigma); origin O between the first two and point P between x = a and x = 3a",d:[["l",60,22,60,132],["l",140,22,140,132],["l",220,22,220,132],["t",60,16,"\u2212\u03C3","ms"],["t",140,16,"\u22122\u03C3","ms"],["t",220,16,"\u03C3","ms"],["l",20,77,300,77,"da"],["t",298,70,"X","e"],["c",100,77,2.5,"F"],["t",100,92,"O","ms"],["c",180,77,2.5,"F"],["t",180,68,"P","ms"],["t",60,150,"x = \u2212a","ms"],["t",140,150,"x = a","ms"],["t",220,150,"x = 3a","ms"]]},n:2,s:`Each sheet gives a field of magnitude \u03C3_sheet/(2\u03B5\u2080). P is between x = a and x = 3a.
Sheet \u2212\u03C3 (x = \u2212a) attracts P to the left: \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080).
Sheet \u22122\u03C3 (x = a) attracts P to the left: 2\u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080) = \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080.
Sheet +\u03C3 (x = 3a) repels P to the left: \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080).
All point the same way: total = (1/2 + 1 + 1/2)\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080 = 2\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080. So x = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"An electric field, E\u20D7 = {2\xEE + 6\u0135 + 8k\u0302;\u221A6} passes through the surface of 4 m\xB2 area having unit vector, n\u0302 = ({2\xEE + \u0135 + k\u0302;\u221A6}). The electric flux for that surface is ______ V m.",n:12,s:`Flux = E\u20D7 \xB7 n\u0302 A.
E\u20D7 \xB7 n\u0302 = (2\xD72 + 6\xD71 + 8\xD71)/6 = 18/6 = 3 V/m.
Flux = 3 \xD7 4 = 12 V m.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"If the net electric field at point P along Y axis is zero, then the ratio of |q\u2082/q\u2083| is {8;5\u221Ax}, where x = ______.",fig:{w:240,h:195,alt:"A triangle with point P at the top, charge plus q2 at the lower left and minus q3 at the lower right; the perpendicular from P is 4 cm and meets the base 2 cm from q2 and 3 cm from q3",d:[["l",60,150,110,50,"d"],["l",185,150,110,50,"d"],["l",110,50,110,150],["p","M110 142 H118 V150"],["l",60,150,185,150],["c",60,150,3,"F"],["c",185,150,3,"F"],["t",110,42,"P","ms"],["t",116,104,"4 cm","s"],["t",60,168,"+q\u2082","ms"],["t",185,168,"\u2212q\u2083","ms"],["t",85,184,"2 cm","ms"],["t",148,184,"3 cm","ms"]]},n:5,s:`From the figure P is 4 cm above the base. Distance to q\u2082 = \u221A(2\xB2 + 4\xB2) = \u221A20 cm; distance to q\u2083 = \u221A(3\xB2 + 4\xB2) = 5 cm.
The vertical components of the two fields must cancel (q\u2082 is positive, so its field has an upward part; q\u2083 is negative, so its field has a downward part).
k|q\u2082|/20 \xD7 4/\u221A20 = k|q\u2083|/25 \xD7 4/5.
|q\u2082|/|q\u2083| = (20\u221A20)/(125) = 8/(5\u221A5).
So x = 5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"An electric field E\u20D7 = (2x\xEE) N C\u207B\xB9 exists in space. A cube of side 2 m is placed in the space as per figure given below. The electric flux through the cube is ______ N m\xB2/C.",fig:{w:300,h:175,alt:"Axes with origin O; a cube of side 2 m whose left face is 2 m from the origin along x, drawn in perspective",d:[["l",40,135,292,135,"a"],["l",40,135,40,8,"a"],["t",286,128,"X","e"],["t",46,14,"Y"],["t",34,148,"O","e"],["p","M110 135 H180 V65 H110 Z"],["p","M130 117 H200 V47 H130 Z"],["l",110,65,130,47],["l",180,65,200,47],["l",180,135,200,117],["l",110,135,130,117,"d"],["l",40,22,110,22,"b"],["t",75,16,"2 m","ms"],["l",110,158,180,158,"b"],["t",145,171,"2 m","ms"]]},n:16,s:`E has only an x-component, so only the two faces perpendicular to x give flux. Each has area 4 m\xB2.
From the figure the cube spans x = 2 m to x = 4 m.
Flux out of the face at x = 4: (2\xD74)\xD74 = 32. Flux into the face at x = 2: (2\xD72)\xD74 = 16.
Net flux = 32 \u2212 16 = 16 N m\xB2/C (Gauss: \u2207\xB7E = 2, times volume 8 = 16).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Suppose a uniformly charged wall provides a uniform electric field of 2 \xD7 10\u2074 N/C normally. A charged particle of mass 2 g being suspended through a silk thread of length 20 cm and remain stayed at a distance of 10 cm from the wall. Then the charge on the particle will be {1;\u221Ax} \u03BCC, where x = ______. [use g = 10 m/s\xB2]",n:3,s:`sin\u03B8 = 10/20 = 1/2, so \u03B8 = 30\xB0.
Equilibrium: T cos\u03B8 = mg, T sin\u03B8 = qE, so qE = mg tan\u03B8.
q = mg tan30\xB0/E = (2\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.577)/(2\xD710\u2074) = 5.77\xD710\u207B\u2077 C = 0.577 \u03BCC = 1/\u221A3 \u03BCC.
So x = 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"Two charges of \u22124 \u03BCC and +4 \u03BCC are placed at the points A(1, 0, 4) m and B(2, \u22121, 5) m located in an electric field E\u20D7 = 0.20 \xEE V/cm. The magnitude of the torque acting on the dipole is 8\u221A\u03B1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N m, where \u03B1 = ______.",n:2,s:`Dipole moment p\u20D7 = q \xD7 (vector from \u2212q to +q) = 4\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 (B \u2212 A) = 4\xD710\u207B\u2076 (\xEE \u2212 \u0135 + k\u0302) C m.
E\u20D7 = 0.20 V/cm = 20 V/m along \xEE.
\u03C4\u20D7 = p\u20D7 \xD7 E\u20D7 = 4\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 20 \xD7 (\xEE \u2212 \u0135 + k\u0302) \xD7 \xEE = 8\xD710\u207B\u2075 (\u0135 + k\u0302).
|\u03C4| = 8\xD710\u207B\u2075 \xD7 \u221A2 N m = 8\u221A2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N m.
So \u03B1 = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"An electron is moving under the influence of the electric field of a uniformly charged infinite plane sheet S having surface charge density +\u03C3. The electron at t = 0 is at a distance of 1 m from S and has a speed of 1 m/s. The maximum value of \u03C3 if the electrons strikes S at t = 1 s is \u03B1[{m\u03B5\u2080;e}] C/m\xB2, the value of \u03B1 is ______.",n:8,s:`The sheet attracts the electron with constant acceleration a = eE/m = e\u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080m).
To reach S at t = 1 s, the largest a occurs when the electron starts moving away from S (moving towards S would need a = 0).
Taking distance from S: 0 = 1 + (1)(1) \u2212 \xBDa(1)\xB2, so a = 4 m/s\xB2.
e\u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080m) = 4 gives \u03C3 = 8 m\u03B5\u2080/e.
So \u03B1 = 8.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A charge is kept at the central point P of a cylindrical region. The two edges subtend a half-angle \u03B8 at P, as shown in the figure. When \u03B8 = 30\xB0, then the electric flux through the curved surface of the cylinder is \u03C6. If \u03B8 = 60\xB0, then the electric flux through the curved surface becomes \u03C6/\u221An, where the value of n is ______.",fig:{w:220,h:235,alt:"A cylinder with a charge at its central point P; lines from P to the edges of the two end faces make half-angle theta with the axis",d:[["p","M60 35 A50 10 0 0 1 160 35 A50 10 0 0 1 60 35"],["p","M60 195 A50 10 0 0 0 160 195"],["l",60,35,60,195],["l",160,35,160,195],["l",110,18,110,215,"d"],["c",110,115,2.5,"F"],["t",100,119,"P","e"],["l",110,115,160,35,"d"],["l",110,115,60,35,"d"],["l",110,115,160,195,"d"],["l",110,115,60,195,"d"],["t",114,84,"\u03B8","s"],["t",114,152,"\u03B8","s"]]},n:3,s:`Flux through one flat end face = (q/\u03B5\u2080) \xD7 (1 \u2212 cos\u03B8)/2 (the face is a spherical cap of half-angle \u03B8 as seen from q).
Curved surface = q/\u03B5\u2080 \u2212 2 \xD7 (q/\u03B5\u2080)(1 \u2212 cos\u03B8)/2 = (q/\u03B5\u2080) cos\u03B8.
\u03B8 = 30\xB0: \u03C6 = (\u221A3/2) q/\u03B5\u2080. \u03B8 = 60\xB0: \u03C6\u2032 = (1/2) q/\u03B5\u2080.
\u03C6/\u03C6\u2032 = \u221A3, so \u03C6\u2032 = \u03C6/\u221A3 and n = 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"An electron revolves around an infinite cylindrical wire having uniform linear charge density 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 C m\u207B\xB9 in circular path under the influence of attractive electrostatic field. The velocity of electron with which it is revolving is ______ \xD7 10\u2076 m s\u207B\xB9. (Given mass of electron = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg)",n:8,s:`Field of the wire: E = \u03BB/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r) = 2k\u03BB/r.
The attraction gives the centripetal force: eE = mv\xB2/r, so v\xB2 = 2ke\u03BB/m (independent of r).
v\xB2 = 2 \xD7 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 2\xD710\u207B\u2078/(9\xD710\u207B\xB3\xB9) = 6.4\xD710\xB9\xB3.
v = 8 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"A thin infinite sheet charge and an infinite line charge of respective charge densities +\u03C3 and +\u03BB are placed parallel at 5 m distance from each other. Points 'P' and 'Q' are at {3;\u03C0} m and {4;\u03C0} m perpendicular distances from line charge towards sheet charge, respectively. E(P) and E(Q) are the magnitudes of resultant electric field intensities at point 'P' and 'Q', respectively. If {E(P);E(Q)} = {4;a} for 2|\u03C3| = |\u03BB|, then the value of a is ______.",n:6,s:`Between the two, the line's field points towards the sheet and the sheet's field points towards the line, so they subtract.
Sheet: \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080) = \u03BB/(4\u03B5\u2080) everywhere.
Line at P: \u03BB/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080 \xD7 3/\u03C0) = \u03BB/(6\u03B5\u2080). Line at Q: \u03BB/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080 \xD7 4/\u03C0) = \u03BB/(8\u03B5\u2080).
E(P) = \u03BB/4 \u2212 \u03BB/6 = \u03BB/12 (in units of 1/\u03B5\u2080). E(Q) = \u03BB/4 \u2212 \u03BB/8 = \u03BB/8.
E(P)/E(Q) = 8/12 = 2/3 = 4/a, so a = 6.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A stream of positively charged particles having {q;m} = 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 C kg\u207B\xB9 and velocity v\u20D7\u2080 = 3 \xD7 10\u2077 \xEE m s\u207B\xB9 is deflected by an electric field 1.8 \u0135 kV m\u207B\xB9. The electric field exists in a region of 10 cm along x direction. Due to the electric field, the deflection of the charge particles in the y direction is ______ mm.",n:2,s:`Time in the field: t = 0.10/(3\xD710\u2077) = 3.33\xD710\u207B\u2079 s.
Acceleration: a = (q/m)E = 2\xD710\xB9\xB9 \xD7 1800 = 3.6\xD710\xB9\u2074 m/s\xB2.
y = \xBDat\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 3.6\xD710\xB9\u2074 \xD7 (3.33\xD710\u207B\u2079)\xB2 = 2\xD710\u207B\xB3 m.
Deflection = 2 mm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A uniform electric field of 10 N/C is created between two parallel charged plates. An electron enters the field symmetrically between the plates with a kinetic energy 0.5 eV. The length of each plate is 10 cm. The angle (\u03B8) of deviation of the path of electron as it comes out of the field is ______ (in degree).",n:45,s:`Along the plates the speed stays v\u2093; across them a = eE/m for time t = L/v\u2093.
tan\u03B8 = v_y/v\u2093 = (eEL/m v\u2093)/v\u2093 = eEL/(mv\u2093\xB2) = eEL/(2 KE).
eEL = 10 \xD7 0.1 = 1 eV, and 2 KE = 1 eV.
tan\u03B8 = 1, so \u03B8 = 45\xB0.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A cuboid lies in a region with electric field E\u20D7 = (2x\xB2\xEE \u2212 4y\u0135 + 6k\u0302) N/C. One corner of the cuboid is at the origin and its edges lie along the x, y and z axes. The magnitude of charge within the cuboid is n\u03B5\u2080 C. The value of n is ______. (if dimension of cuboid is 1 \xD7 2 \xD7 3 m\xB3).",n:12,s:`Flux through faces perpendicular to x (area 2\xD73 = 6): at x = 1, E_x = 2 gives +12; at x = 0, E_x = 0 gives 0.
Faces perpendicular to y (area 1\xD73 = 3): at y = 2, E_y = \u22128 gives \u221224; at y = 0, E_y = 0 gives 0.
Faces perpendicular to z (area 1\xD72 = 2): E_z = 6 on both, so +12 and \u221212 cancel.
Net flux = 12 \u2212 24 = \u221212 = q/\u03B5\u2080, so |q| = 12\u03B5\u2080 and n = 12.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"Expression for an electric field is given by E\u20D7 = 4000x\xB2 \xEE {V;m}. The electric flux through the cube of side 20 cm when placed in electric field (cube with one corner at the origin and edges along the axes, so x runs from 0 to 20 cm) is ______ V cm.",n:640,s:`E is along x, so only the faces perpendicular to x give flux. Each has area (0.2)\xB2 = 0.04 m\xB2.
At x = 0, E = 0. At x = 0.2 m, E = 4000 \xD7 0.04 = 160 V/m.
Flux = 160 \xD7 0.04 = 6.4 V m = 640 V cm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"The volume charge density of a sphere of radius 6 m is 2 \u03BCC cm\u207B\xB3. The number of lines of force per unit surface area coming out from the surface of the sphere is ______ \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 N C\u207B\xB9. [Given : Permittivity of vacuum \u03B5\u2080 = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2 N\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB2]",n:45,s:`Lines per unit area = E at the surface = \u03C1R/(3\u03B5\u2080).
\u03C1 = 2 \u03BCC/cm\xB3 = 2\xD710\u207B\u2076/10\u207B\u2076 C/m\xB3 = 2 C/m\xB3.
E = (2 \xD7 6)/(3 \xD7 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2) = 4.52\xD710\xB9\xB9 = 45.2 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 N/C.
So the value is about 45.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"A long cylindrical volume contains a uniformly distributed charge of density \u03C1 C m\u207B\xB3. The electric field inside the cylindrical volume at a distance x = {2\u03B5\u2080;\u03C1} m from its axis is ______ V m\u207B\xB9.",n:1,s:`Gauss's law with a coaxial cylinder of radius x and length L: E \xD7 2\u03C0xL = \u03C1\u03C0x\xB2L/\u03B5\u2080.
So E = \u03C1x/(2\u03B5\u2080).
Put x = 2\u03B5\u2080/\u03C1: E = (\u03C1/2\u03B5\u2080)(2\u03B5\u2080/\u03C1) = 1 V/m.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor with width 4 cm, length 8 cm and separation between the plates of 4 mm is connected to a battery of 20 V. A dielectric slab of dielectric constant 5 having length 1 cm, width 4 cm and thickness 4 mm is inserted between the plates of parallel plate capacitor. The electrostatic energy of this system will be ______ \u03B5\u2080 J. (Where \u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of free space)",n:240,s:`The battery keeps V = 20 V. The slab fills the gap, so the capacitor is two capacitors in parallel.
With slab: area 1\xD74 = 4 cm\xB2 = 4\xD710\u207B\u2074 m\xB2, C\u2081 = 5\u03B5\u2080(4\xD710\u207B\u2074)/d. Without slab: area 7\xD74 = 28\xD710\u207B\u2074 m\xB2, C\u2082 = \u03B5\u2080(28\xD710\u207B\u2074)/d.
d = 4\xD710\u207B\xB3 m: C = \u03B5\u2080(20 + 28)\xD710\u207B\u2074/(4\xD710\u207B\xB3) = 1.2\u03B5\u2080.
U = \xBDCV\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 1.2\u03B5\u2080 \xD7 400 = 240\u03B5\u2080 J.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A charge q is surrounded by a closed surface consisting of an inverted cone of height h and base radius R, and a hemisphere of radius R as shown in the figure. The electric flux through the conical surface is {nq;6\u03B5\u2080} (in SI units). The value of n is ______.",fig:{w:240,h:225,alt:"A hemisphere of radius R sitting on the circular base of an inverted cone of height h with apex O; the charge q is at the centre of the base",d:[["p","M40 80 A80 80 0 0 1 200 80"],["l",40,80,200,80,"d"],["l",40,80,120,200],["l",200,80,120,200],["c",120,80,3,"F"],["t",126,74,"q"],["t",162,74,"R","ms"],["l",20,80,20,200,"b"],["t",14,144,"h","e"],["t",126,216,"O"]]},n:3,s:`The charge sits at the centre of the circular base, which is also the centre of the hemisphere.
Total flux through the closed surface = q/\u03B5\u2080.
By symmetry the hemisphere takes half of it: q/(2\u03B5\u2080).
The cone takes the rest: q/(2\u03B5\u2080) = 3q/(6\u03B5\u2080).
So n = 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A body having specific charge 8 \u03BCC g\u207B\xB9 is resting on a frictionless plane at a distance 10 cm from the wall (as shown in the figure). It starts moving towards the wall when a uniform electric field of 100 V m\u207B\xB9 is applied horizontally towards the wall. If the collision of the body with the wall is perfectly elastic, then the time period of the motion will be ______ s.",fig:{w:300,h:115,alt:"A body on a frictionless floor 10 cm from a brick wall, with an electric field of 100 V/m pointing toward the wall",d:[["l",10,70,290,70],["r",232,12,48,58,"f"],["r",50,48,34,22],["t",67,63,"Body","ms"],["l",96,38,200,38,"a"],["t",148,30,"100 V/m","ms"],["l",84,90,232,90,"b"],["t",158,106,"10 cm","ms"]]},n:1,s:`Specific charge = 8 \u03BCC/g = 8\xD710\u207B\xB3 C/kg.
a = (q/m)E = 8\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 100 = 0.8 m/s\xB2.
Time to reach the wall: 0.1 = \xBD(0.8)t\xB2, so t = 0.5 s.
After an elastic bounce it returns with the same speed, slows down at 0.8 m/s\xB2 and stops at the start after another 0.5 s.
Period = 0.5 + 0.5 = 1 s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 22 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:`The total charge enclosed in an incremental volume of 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 m\xB3 located at the origin is ______ nC, if electric flux density of its field is found as
D\u20D7 = e^(\u2212x) sin y \xEE \u2212 e^(\u2212x) cos y \u0135 + 2z k\u0302 C/m\xB2.`,n:4,s:`Gauss's law in differential form: charge density \u03C1 = divergence of D = \u2202Dx/\u2202x + \u2202Dy/\u2202y + \u2202Dz/\u2202z.
\u2202Dx/\u2202x = \u2212e^(\u2212x) sin y, \u2202Dy/\u2202y = +e^(\u2212x) sin y, \u2202Dz/\u2202z = 2.
The first two terms cancel, so \u03C1 = 2 C/m\xB3 (the same everywhere).
Charge = \u03C1 \xD7 volume = 2 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 C = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 C = 4 nC.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"The electric field in a region is given by E\u20D7 = {2;5}E\u2080 \xEE + {3;5}E\u2080 \u0135 with E\u2080 = 4.0 \xD7 10\xB3 N/C. The flux of this field through a rectangular surface area 0.4 m\xB2 parallel to the Y-Z plane is ______ N m\xB2 C\u207B\xB9.",n:640,s:`Flux = E \xB7 A. A surface parallel to the Y-Z plane has its normal along the x-axis, so only the x-component of the field passes through it.
Ex = (2/5)E\u2080 = 0.4 \xD7 4.0 \xD7 10\xB3 = 1600 N/C.
Flux = Ex \xD7 A = 1600 \xD7 0.4 = 640 N m\xB2 C\u207B\xB9.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A point charge of +12 \u03BCC is at a distance 6 cm vertically above the centre of a square of side 12 cm as shown in figure. The magnitude of the electric flux through the square will be ______ \xD7 10\xB3 N m\xB2/C.",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"A point charge +q hangs 6 cm above the centre of a horizontal square of side 12 cm",d:[["p","M80 140 L220 140 L260 105 L120 105 Z","f"],["c",170,40,4,"F"],["t",178,36,"+q"],["l",170,44,170,121,"da"],["t",176,86,"6 cm","s"],["c",170,122.5,2,"F"],["t",150,160,"12 cm","ms"],["t",252,130,"12 cm","s"]]},n:226,s:`Imagine the square as one face of a cube of side 12 cm. The charge is exactly 6 cm above the face centre, so it sits at the centre of the cube.
Total flux from the charge = q/\u03B5\u2080, shared equally by the 6 faces of the cube.
Flux through one face = q/(6\u03B5\u2080) = 12 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 / (6 \xD7 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2) \u2248 2.26 \xD7 10\u2075 N m\xB2/C.
That is 226 \xD7 10\xB3 N m\xB2/C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"The electric field in a region is given by E\u20D7 = ({3;5}E\u2080 \xEE + {4;5}E\u2080 \u0135) N/C. The ratio of flux of reported field through the rectangular surface of area 0.2 m\xB2 (parallel to y-z plane) to that of the surface of area 0.3 m\xB2 (parallel to x-z plane) is a : b, where a = ______. [Here \xEE, \u0135 and k\u0302 are unit vectors along x, y and z-axes respectively]",n:1,s:`A surface parallel to the y-z plane has its normal along x, so it only takes the x-component: flux\u2081 = (3/5)E\u2080 \xD7 0.2 = 0.12E\u2080.
A surface parallel to the x-z plane has its normal along y, so it only takes the y-component: flux\u2082 = (4/5)E\u2080 \xD7 0.3 = 0.24E\u2080.
Ratio = 0.12 : 0.24 = 1 : 2, so a : b = 1 : 2.
Hence a = 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"An electric field E\u20D7 = 4x \xEE \u2212 (y\xB2 + 1) \u0135 N/C passes through the box shown in figure. The flux of the electric field through surfaces ABCD and BCGF are marked as \u03C6I and \u03C6II respectively. The difference between (\u03C6I \u2212 \u03C6II) is (in N m\xB2/C) ______.",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"Box with corners A(0,0,2), B(3,0,2), C(3,2,2), D(0,2,2), E(0,0,0), F(3,0,0), G(3,2,0), H(0,2,0); top face ABCD and side face BCGF at x = 3",d:[["p","M140 40 L245 40 L201 68 L96 68 Z","f"],["p","M245 40 L201 68 L201 138 L245 110 Z","f"],["l",140,40,245,40,null],["l",245,40,201,68,null],["l",201,68,96,68,null],["l",96,68,140,40,null],["l",245,40,245,110,null],["l",201,68,201,138,null],["l",96,68,96,138,null],["l",245,110,201,138,null],["l",201,138,96,138,null],["l",140,110,245,110,"d"],["l",140,110,96,138,"d"],["l",140,40,140,110,"d"],["l",245,110,290,110,"a"],["t",294,114,"x"],["l",140,40,140,12,"a"],["t",146,14,"z"],["l",96,138,70,156,"a"],["t",58,164,"y"],["t",134,36,"A (0,0,2)","es"],["t",250,36,"B (3,0,2)","s"],["t",92,64,"D (0,2,2)","es"],["t",206,64,"C (3,2,2)","s"],["t",148,124,"E (0,0,0)","s"],["t",250,112,"F (3,0,0)","s"],["t",208,150,"G (3,2,0)","s"],["t",98,154,"H (0,2,0)","s"]]},n:48,s:`Flux through a face = E \xB7 A with the normal along the face's outward direction.
Face ABCD is the top face (z = 2), with normal along z. E has no z-component, so \u03C6I = 0.
Face BCGF is the face x = 3 (area 2 \xD7 2 = 4 m\xB2), normal along x. Ex = 4x = 12 N/C there, so \u03C6II = 12 \xD7 4 = 48 N m\xB2/C.
The difference between the two fluxes has magnitude |\u03C6I \u2212 \u03C6II| = 48 N m\xB2/C.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two large circular discs separated by a distance of 0.01 m are connected to a battery via a switch as shown in the figure. Charged oil drops of density 900 kg m\u207B\xB3 are released through a tiny hole at the center of the top disc. Once some oil drops achieve terminal velocity, the switch is closed to apply a voltage of 200 V across the discs. As a result, an oil drop of radius 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m stops moving vertically and floats between the discs. The number of electrons present in this oil drop is ______. (neglect the buoyancy force, take acceleration due to gravity = 10 m s\u207B\xB2 and charge on an electron e = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Two horizontal discs 0.01 m apart joined through a switch to a 200 V battery, with an oil drop floating between them",d:[["r",190,60,100,8,"f"],["r",190,120,100,8,"f"],["l",190,64,125,64],["l",100,64,60,64],["l",100,64,120,52],["t",92,46,"Switch","s"],["l",60,64,60,92],["l",48,92,72,92,"B"],["l",54,99,66,99,"B"],["l",60,99,60,124],["l",60,124,190,124],["t",78,100,"200 V","s"],["c",240,94,3,"F"],["t",248,92,"drop","s"],["l",304,68,304,120,"b"],["t",310,98,"0.01 m","s"]]},n:6,s:`The drop floats, so the upward electric force balances its weight: n e E = m g, with E = V/d.
Mass: m = \u03C1 \xD7 (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3 = 900 \xD7 4.19 \xD7 (8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077)\xB3 \u2248 1.93 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075 kg.
Field: E = 200/0.01 = 2 \xD7 10\u2074 V/m.
n = mg/(eE) = (1.93 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075 \xD7 10)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u2074) \u2248 6.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:"A circular disc of radius R carries surface charge density \u03C3(r) = \u03C3\u2080(1 \u2212 {r;R}), where \u03C3\u2080 is a constant and r is the distance from the center of the disc. Electric flux through a large spherical surface that encloses the charged disc completely is \u03C6\u2080. Electric flux through another spherical surface of radius {R;4} and concentric with the disc is \u03C6. Then the ratio {\u03C6\u2080;\u03C6} is ______.",n:6.4,s:`By Gauss's law, flux = (charge inside)/\u03B5\u2080. A ring of radius r and width dr holds charge \u03C3(r) \xB7 2\u03C0r dr.
Whole disc: Q = 2\u03C0\u03C3\u2080 \u222B\u2080\u1D3F (r \u2212 r\xB2/R) dr = 2\u03C0\u03C3\u2080(R\xB2/2 \u2212 R\xB2/3) = \u03C0\u03C3\u2080R\xB2/3.
Within radius R/4: q = 2\u03C0\u03C3\u2080(r\xB2/2 \u2212 r\xB3/3R) at r = R/4 = 2\u03C0\u03C3\u2080R\xB2(1/32 \u2212 1/192) = 5\u03C0\u03C3\u2080R\xB2/96.
\u03C6\u2080/\u03C6 = Q/q = (\u03C0/3)/(5\u03C0/96) = 96/15 = 6.4.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"One end of a spring of negligible unstretched length and spring constant k is fixed at the origin (0, 0). A point particle of mass m carrying a positive charge q is attached at its other end. The entire system is kept on a smooth horizontal surface. When a point dipole p\u20D7 pointing towards the charge q is fixed at the origin, the spring gets stretched to a length l and attains a new equilibrium position (see figure). If the point mass is now displaced slightly by \u0394l << l from its equilibrium position and released, it is found to oscillate at frequency {1;\u03B4}\u221A{k;m}. The value of \u03B4 is ______.",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"A dipole p at the origin pointing along +x, and a spring from the origin to a charge q at distance l on the x-axis",d:[["l",30,100,325,100,"a"],["l",30,100,30,20,"a"],["t",329,104,"x"],["t",36,22,"y"],["t",4,118,"(0, 0)","s"],["l",36,84,66,84,"a"],["t",50,76,"p","ms"],["p","M30 100 L50 100 L56 90 L66 110 L76 90 L86 110 L96 90 L106 110 L116 90 L126 110 L136 90 L146 110 L156 90 L166 110 L176 90 L186 110 L196 90 L206 110 L216 90 L226 110 L236 100 L260 100"],["c",262,100,5,"F"],["t",262,86,"q","ms"],["l",30,128,262,128,"b"],["t",146,124,"l","ms"]]},n:3.14,s:`On its axis the dipole's field is E = 2kp/x\xB3 (k = 1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) along p, so it pushes the charge q away from the origin: F = qp/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080x\xB3).
Equilibrium at x = l: k\xB7l = qp/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080l\xB3).
Net force F(x) = qp/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080x\xB3) \u2212 kx. Its slope at l is dF/dx = \u22123qp/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080l\u2074) \u2212 k = \u22123k \u2212 k = \u22124k (using qp/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080l\xB3) = kl).
So the effective spring constant is 4k, and f = (1/2\u03C0)\u221A(4k/m) = (1/\u03C0)\u221A(k/m).
Comparing with (1/\u03B4)\u221A(k/m) gives \u03B4 = \u03C0 \u2248 3.14.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"A particle of mass 10\u207B\xB3 kg and charge 1.0 C is initially at rest. At time t = 0, the particle comes under the influence of an electric field E\u20D7(t) = E\u2080 sin \u03C9t \xEE, where E\u2080 = 1.0 N C\u207B\xB9 and \u03C9 = 10\xB3 rad s\u207B\xB9. Consider the effect of only the electrical force on the particle. Then the maximum speed, in m s\u207B\xB9, attained by the particle at subsequent times is ______.",n:2,s:`Acceleration a = qE/m = (qE\u2080/m) sin \u03C9t = 10\xB3 sin(10\xB3t) m/s\xB2.
Integrate from rest: v = (qE\u2080/m\u03C9)(1 \u2212 cos \u03C9t) = (1 \xD7 1/(10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 10\xB3))(1 \u2212 cos \u03C9t) = 1 \u2212 cos \u03C9t.
The speed is largest when cos \u03C9t = \u22121, giving v = 1 \u2212 (\u22121) = 2 m/s.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"An infinitely long uniform line charge distribution of charge per unit length \u03BB lies parallel to the y-axis in the y-z plane at z = {\u221A3;2}a (see figure). If the magnitude of the flux of the electric field through the rectangular surface ABCD lying in the x-y plane with its centre at the origin is {\u03BBL;n\u03B5\u2080} (\u03B5\u2080 = permittivity of free space), then the value of n is ______.",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"Rectangle ABCD of sides L along y and a along x lying in the x-y plane centred at O, with a line charge parallel to y at height \u221A3a/2 above it",d:[["l",150,160,150,18,"a"],["t",156,20,"z"],["l",150,160,320,160,"a"],["t",324,164,"y"],["l",150,160,104,194,"a"],["t",92,202,"x"],["p","M118 140 L238 140 L182 180 L62 180 Z","f"],["t",54,188,"A","e"],["t",186,192,"B"],["t",242,138,"C"],["t",112,136,"D","e"],["t",156,176,"O","s"],["c",150,160,2,"F"],["l",118,129,238,129,"b"],["t",178,124,"L","ms"],["l",62,172,118,132,"b"],["t",84,150,"a","ms"],["l",30,70,310,70,"B"],["t",312,66,"\u03BB"],["l",270,160,270,70,"b"],["t",276,118,"\u221A3a/2","s"],["t",210,62,"line charge","ms"]]},n:6,s:`The rectangle is a flat surface. Look at it end-on along the y-direction: the line charge is a point at height h = (\u221A3/2)a above the centre, and the rectangle has half-width a/2.
The angle subtended at the line by each half: tan \u03B8 = (a/2)/(\u221A3a/2) = 1/\u221A3, so \u03B8 = 30\xB0. The whole width subtends 60\xB0.
Total flux from a length L of the line = \u03BBL/\u03B5\u2080 spread over all 360\xB0. The flux through the rectangle is the fraction 60/360 = 1/6 of it.
Flux = (1/6)(\u03BBL/\u03B5\u2080) = \u03BBL/(6\u03B5\u2080), so n = 6.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:`An infinitely long solid cylinder of radius R has a uniform volume charge density \u03C1. It has a spherical cavity of radius R/2 with its centre on the axis of the cylinder, as shown in the figure.
The magnitude of the electric field at the point P, which is at a distance 2R from the axis of the cylinder, is given by the expression {23\u03C1R;16k\u03B5\u2080}. The value of k is ______.`,fig:{w:340,h:185,alt:"Vertical cylinder of radius R with a spherical cavity of radius R/2 at its centre; point P lies at distance 2R from the axis along y",d:[["p","M110 40 Q170 12 230 40 Q170 68 110 40","d"],["p","M110 150 Q170 182 230 150"],["l",110,40,110,150,"d"],["l",230,40,230,150,"d"],["c",170,95,30],["l",170,95,170,8,"a"],["t",176,12,"z"],["l",170,95,318,95,"a"],["t",322,99,"y"],["l",170,95,128,132,"a"],["t",114,146,"x"],["l",170,95,149,74,"a"],["t",146,70,"R/2","e"],["l",170,70,230,70,"b"],["t",200,66,"R","ms"],["c",290,95,3,"F"],["t",292,86,"P"],["l",170,112,290,112,"b"],["t",230,126,"2R","ms"]]},n:6,s:`Use superposition: full cylinder (density +\u03C1) plus a sphere of density \u2212\u03C1 filling the cavity.
Cylinder, outside, at distance 2R from the axis: E\u2081 = \u03C1R\xB2/(2\u03B5\u2080 \xB7 2R) = \u03C1R/(4\u03B5\u2080) (by Gauss's law, charge per length = \u03C1\u03C0R\xB2).
Cavity sphere, charge \u2212\u03C1(4/3)\u03C0(R/2)\xB3 = \u2212\u03C1\u03C0R\xB3/6, at distance 2R from its centre: E\u2082 = (\u03C1\u03C0R\xB3/6)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080(2R)\xB2) = \u03C1R/(96\u03B5\u2080), pointing opposite to E\u2081.
E = \u03C1R/(4\u03B5\u2080) \u2212 \u03C1R/(96\u03B5\u2080) = (24 \u2212 1)\u03C1R/(96\u03B5\u2080) = 23\u03C1R/(96\u03B5\u2080).
Compare with 23\u03C1R/(16k\u03B5\u2080): 16k = 96, so k = 6.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2009",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:"A solid sphere of radius R has a charge Q distributed in its volume with a charge density \u03C1 = \u03BAr^a, where \u03BA and a are constants and r is the distance from its centre. If the electric field at r = R/2 is {1;8} times that at r = R, find the value of a.",n:2,s:`Charge inside radius r: q(r) = \u222B\u2080\u02B3 \u03BAr\u2032\u1D43 \xB7 4\u03C0r\u2032\xB2 dr\u2032 = 4\u03C0\u03BA r^(a+3)/(a+3).
Gauss's law: E(r) = q(r)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2) \u221D r^(a+3)/r\xB2 = r^(a+1).
So E(R/2)/E(R) = (1/2)^(a+1) = 1/8 = (1/2)\xB3.
Therefore a + 1 = 3, so a = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I
(A) Electric field inside (distance r > 0 from center) of a uniformly charged spherical shell with surface charge density \u03C3, and radius R
(B) Electric field at distance r > 0 from, a uniformly charged infinite plane sheet with surface charge density \u03C3.
(C) Electric field outside (distance r > 0 from center) of a uniformly charged spherical shell with surface charge density \u03C3, and radius R.
(D) Electric field between 2 oppositely charged infinite plane parallel sheets with uniform surface charge density \u03C3.
List-II
(I) \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080
(II) \u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080
(III) 0
(IV) {\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080r\xB2}
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)"],c:3,s:`(A) Inside a uniformly charged shell the field is zero: (III).
(B) An infinite plane sheet gives E = \u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080 at any distance: (II).
(C) Outside the shell all charge acts like a point charge at the centre: E = (\u03C3\xB74\u03C0R\xB2)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2) = \u03C3R\xB2/(\u03B5\u2080r\xB2), which falls as 1/r\xB2. Of the given entries this is (IV), the one with the 1/(\u03B5\u2080r\xB2) form.
(D) Between two oppositely charged parallel sheets the fields add: \u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080 + \u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080 = \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080: (I).
So A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2025",tp:"efl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`List-I shows four configurations, each consisting of a pair of ideal electric dipoles. Each dipole has a dipole moment of magnitude p, oriented as marked by arrows in the figures. In all the configurations the dipoles are fixed such that they are at a distance 2r apart along the x direction. The midpoint of the line joining the two dipoles is X. The possible resultant electric fields E\u20D7 at X are given in List-II. (In the figure x points to the right and y points up; \xEE and \u0135 are the unit vectors along x and y.)
Choose the option that describes the correct match between the entries in List-I to those in List-II.
List-I (P, Q, R, S): the four configurations in the figure
List-II
(1) E\u20D7 = 0
(2) E\u20D7 = \u2212{p;2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB3} \u0135
(3) E\u20D7 = \u2212{p;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB3}(\xEE \u2212 \u0135)
(4) E\u20D7 = {p;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB3}(2\xEE \u2212 \u0135)
(5) E\u20D7 = {p;\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB3} \xEE`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Four pairs of dipoles 2r apart along x with midpoint X: P both point up; Q left up and right down; R left up and right pointing +x; S both pointing +x",d:[["t",10,44,"(P)"],["l",40,40,140,40,"d"],["c",90,40,2,"F"],["t",90,18,"X","ms"],["l",40,70,140,70,"b"],["t",90,82,"2r","ms"],["l",40,54,40,26,"a"],["l",140,54,140,26,"a"],["t",180,44,"(Q)"],["l",210,40,310,40,"d"],["c",260,40,2,"F"],["t",260,18,"X","ms"],["l",210,70,310,70,"b"],["t",260,82,"2r","ms"],["l",210,54,210,26,"a"],["l",310,26,310,54,"a"],["t",10,134,"(R)"],["l",40,130,140,130,"d"],["c",90,130,2,"F"],["t",90,108,"X","ms"],["l",40,160,140,160,"b"],["t",90,172,"2r","ms"],["l",40,144,40,116,"a"],["l",126,130,154,130,"a"],["t",180,134,"(S)"],["l",210,130,310,130,"d"],["c",260,130,2,"F"],["t",260,108,"X","ms"],["l",210,160,310,160,"b"],["t",260,172,"2r","ms"],["l",196,130,224,130,"a"],["l",296,130,324,130,"a"]]},o:["P \u2192 3, Q \u2192 1, R \u2192 2, S \u2192 4","P \u2192 4, Q \u2192 5, R \u2192 3, S \u2192 1","P \u2192 2, Q \u2192 1, R \u2192 4, S \u2192 5","P \u2192 2, Q \u2192 1, R \u2192 3, S \u2192 5"],c:2,s:`Field of a dipole of moment p at distance r: on its axis E = 2p/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB3) along p; on its equatorial line E = p/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB3) opposite to p. Let k\u2080 = p/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB3).
P: X is on the equatorial line of both dipoles (both point up), so E = \u2212k\u2080\u0135 \u2212 k\u2080\u0135 = \u22122k\u2080\u0135 = \u2212p/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB3) \u0135: (2).
Q: the dipoles are opposite, so the two equatorial fields cancel: E = 0: (1).
R: left dipole (up) gives \u2212k\u2080\u0135; right dipole (along +x) has X on its axis, giving +2k\u2080\xEE. E = k\u2080(2\xEE \u2212 \u0135): (4).
S: both along +x, X on the axis of both: E = 2k\u2080\xEE + 2k\u2080\xEE = 4k\u2080\xEE = p/(\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB3) \xEE: (5).
So P\u21922, Q\u21921, R\u21924, S\u21925: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"efl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`The electric field E is measured at a point P(0, 0, d) generated due to various charge distributions and the dependence of E on d is found to be different for different charge distributions. List-I contains different relations between E and d. List-II describes different electric charge distributions, along with their locations. Match the functions in List-I with the related charge distributions in List-II.
List-I
P. E is independent of d
Q. E \u221D {1;d}
R. E \u221D {1;d\xB2}
S. E \u221D {1;d\xB3}
List-II
1. A point charge Q at the origin
2. A small dipole with point charges Q at (0, 0, l) and \u2212Q at (0, 0, \u2212l). Take 2l << d
3. An infinite line charge coincident with the x-axis, with uniform linear charge density \u03BB
4. Two infinite wires carrying uniform linear charge density parallel to the x-axis. The one along (y = 0, z = l) has a charge density +\u03BB and the one along (y = 0, z = \u2212l) has charge density \u2212\u03BB. Take 2l << d
5. Infinite plane charge coincident with the xy-plane with uniform surface charge density`,o:["P \u2192 5; Q \u2192 3, 4; R \u2192 1; S \u2192 2","P \u2192 5; Q \u2192 3; R \u2192 1, 4; S \u2192 2","P \u2192 5; Q \u2192 3; R \u2192 1, 2; S \u2192 4","P \u2192 4; Q \u2192 2, 3; R \u2192 1; S \u2192 5"],c:1,s:`P: an infinite plane gives a uniform field \u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080, independent of d: 5.
Q: an infinite line charge gives E = \u03BB/2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d \u221D 1/d: 3.
R: a point charge gives E \u221D 1/d\xB2: 1. Two long parallel wires with opposite charges (separation 2l << d) behave like a line dipole, whose field falls as 1/d\xB2: 4.
S: a small point dipole gives E \u221D 1/d\xB3: 2.
So P\u21925; Q\u21923; R\u21921, 4; S\u21922: option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:`The nuclear charge (Ze) is non-uniformly distributed within a nucleus of radius R. The charge density \u03C1(r) (charge per unit volume) is dependent only on the radial distance r from the centre of the nucleus as shown in figure (\u03C1 = d for r \u2264 a, then falling linearly to zero at r = R). The electric field is only along the radial direction.
The electric field at r = R is`,fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Graph of charge density \u03C1 against r: constant value d up to r = a, then falling linearly to zero at r = R",d:[["l",40,130,310,130,"a"],["l",40,130,40,12,"a"],["t",46,16,"\u03C1(r)","s"],["t",312,134,"r","s"],["l",40,45,130,45,"B"],["l",130,45,250,130,"B"],["l",130,45,130,130,"d"],["l",250,130,250,100,"d"],["t",34,49,"d","e"],["t",130,146,"a","ms"],["t",250,146,"R","ms"],["t",34,134,"0","es"]]},o:["independent of a","directly proportional to a","directly proportional to a\xB2","inversely proportional to a"],c:0,s:`Gauss's law: at r = R the closed surface encloses the whole nuclear charge Ze, whatever the shape of \u03C1(r) inside.
So E(R) = Ze/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2).
This contains no a, so the field at r = R is independent of a.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:`The nuclear charge (Ze) is non-uniformly distributed within a nucleus of radius R. The charge density \u03C1(r) (charge per unit volume) is dependent only on the radial distance r from the centre of the nucleus as shown in figure (\u03C1 = d for r \u2264 a, then falling linearly to zero at r = R). The electric field is only along the radial direction.
For a = 0, the value d (maximum value of \u03C1 as shown in the figure) is`,fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Graph of charge density \u03C1 against r: constant value d up to r = a, then falling linearly to zero at r = R",d:[["l",40,130,310,130,"a"],["l",40,130,40,12,"a"],["t",46,16,"\u03C1(r)","s"],["t",312,134,"r","s"],["l",40,45,130,45,"B"],["l",130,45,250,130,"B"],["l",130,45,130,130,"d"],["l",250,130,250,100,"d"],["t",34,49,"d","e"],["t",130,146,"a","ms"],["t",250,146,"R","ms"],["t",34,134,"0","es"]]},o:["{3Ze;4\u03C0R\xB3}","{3Ze;\u03C0R\xB3}","{4Ze;3\u03C0R\xB3}","{Ze;3\u03C0R\xB3}"],c:1,s:`For a = 0 the density falls linearly from d at the centre to zero at r = R: \u03C1(r) = d(1 \u2212 r/R).
Total charge: Ze = \u222B\u2080\u1D3F d(1 \u2212 r/R) 4\u03C0r\xB2 dr = 4\u03C0d(R\xB3/3 \u2212 R\xB3/4) = 4\u03C0d R\xB3/12 = \u03C0dR\xB3/3.
So d = 3Ze/(\u03C0R\xB3).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:`The nuclear charge (Ze) is non-uniformly distributed within a nucleus of radius R. The charge density \u03C1(r) (charge per unit volume) is dependent only on the radial distance r from the centre of the nucleus as shown in figure (\u03C1 = d for r \u2264 a, then falling linearly to zero at r = R). The electric field is only along the radial direction.
The electric field within the nucleus is generally observed to be linearly dependent on r. This implies`,fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Graph of charge density \u03C1 against r: constant value d up to r = a, then falling linearly to zero at r = R",d:[["l",40,130,310,130,"a"],["l",40,130,40,12,"a"],["t",46,16,"\u03C1(r)","s"],["t",312,134,"r","s"],["l",40,45,130,45,"B"],["l",130,45,250,130,"B"],["l",130,45,130,130,"d"],["l",250,130,250,100,"d"],["t",34,49,"d","e"],["t",130,146,"a","ms"],["t",250,146,"R","ms"],["t",34,134,"0","es"]]},o:["a = 0","a = {R;2}","a = R","a = {2R;3}"],c:2,s:`Inside the nucleus E(r) = q(r)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2).
For r \u2264 a the density is constant (= d), so q = (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3d and E = dr/(3\u03B5\u2080), which is linear in r.
Beyond r = a the density falls, so E is no longer proportional to r.
For E to be linear everywhere inside the nucleus, the constant-density region must reach the surface: a = R.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : Net dipole moment of a polar linear isotropic dielectric substance is not zero even in the absence of an external electric field.
Reason (R) : In absence of an external electric field, the different permanent dipoles of a polar dielectric substance are oriented in random directions.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],c:1,s:`In a polar dielectric each molecule has a permanent dipole moment.
Without an external field, thermal motion makes these dipoles point in random directions, so (R) is true.
Because of the random directions their vector sum is zero, so the net dipole moment of the substance is zero. Hence (A) is false.
Answer: (A) is not correct but (R) is correct.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecn",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : The outer body of an air craft is made of metal which protects persons sitting inside from lightning-strikes.
Reason (R) : The electric field inside the cavity enclosed by a conductor is zero.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)"],c:1,s:`The metal body of the aircraft is a closed conductor, so it works as an electrostatic shield.
The field inside the cavity enclosed by a conductor is zero (charge and fields stay on the outer surface), so (R) is true.
Because of this, the people inside feel no field when lightning strikes the body: (R) is exactly why (A) is true.
Both are correct and (R) explains (A).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A : If an electric dipole of dipole moment 30 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 C m is enclosed by a closed surface, the net flux coming out of the surface will be zero.
Reason R : Electric dipole consists of two equal and opposite charges.
In the light of above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A is false but R is true.","A is true but R is false.","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."],c:2,s:`A dipole is made of +q and \u2212q, so R is true.
The net charge inside the closed surface is q + (\u2212q) = 0.
By Gauss's law the net flux through the surface is (net charge)/\u03B5\u2080 = 0, so A is true. The value of the dipole moment does not matter.
A is true because the two charges are equal and opposite, so R explains A.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement-I : A point charge is brought in an electric field. At a point near to the charge, the field may increase if the charge is positive.
Statement-II : An electric dipole is placed in a non-uniform electric field. The net electric force on the dipole will not be zero.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both statement-I and statement-II are true.","Both statement-I and statement-II are false.","Statement-I is true but statement-II is false.","Statement-I is false but statement-II is true."],c:0,s:`Statement I: the resultant field is the vector sum of the original field and the field of the new charge. Near a positive charge, at points where the two fields point the same way, the total field is larger. So it may increase: true.
Statement II: in a non-uniform field the two charges of the dipole feel different fields, so the forces +qE\u2081 and \u2212qE\u2082 do not cancel. The net force is not zero: true.
Both statements are true.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I : An electric dipole is placed at the centre of a hollow sphere. The flux of electric field through the sphere is zero but the electric field is not zero anywhere in the sphere.
Statement II : If R is the radius of a solid metallic sphere and Q be the total charge on it. The electric field at any point on the spherical surface of radius r (< R) is zero but the electric flux passing through this closed spherical surface of radius r is not zero.
In the light of above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],c:1,s:`Statement I: the dipole has zero net charge, so the net flux through the sphere is zero. Its field is not zero at points around it (it only cancels in the net flux). True.
Statement II: inside a solid metallic sphere the field is zero everywhere. Flux = \u222EE\xB7dA = 0 for a surface of radius r < R (also no charge inside it). So the flux is zero, not non-zero. False.
Statement I is true but Statement II is false.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"efl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A thin fixed ring of radius 1 metre has a positive charge 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 coulomb uniformly distributed over it. A particle of mass 0.9 g and having a negative charge of 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 coulomb is placed on the axis at a distance of 1 cm from the centre of the ring. Show that the motion of the negatively charged particle is approximately simple harmonic. Calculate the time period of oscillations.",a:"0.628 s",s:`On the axis at distance x from the centre the ring's field is E = Qx/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080(R\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2)), directed away from the centre.
For x << R (x = 1 cm, R = 1 m): E \u2248 Qx/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB3).
The force on the negative charge \u2212q is towards the centre: F = \u2212qQx/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB3) = \u2212\u03BAx. A force proportional to displacement and opposite to it means (approximately) simple harmonic motion.
\u03BA = qQ/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB3) = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076/1 = 0.09 N/m.
T = 2\u03C0\u221A(m/\u03BA) = 2\u03C0\u221A(0.9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/0.09) = 2\u03C0 \xD7 0.1 = 0.628 s.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1979",tp:"efl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A charged particle is free to move in an electric field. Will it always move along an electric line of force?",o:["Yes","No"],c:1,s:`The force on the charge is along the tangent to the line of force at each point, so it starts moving along the line.
But the velocity does not follow the force instantly: the particle's inertia carries it along its present direction.
If the line of force is curved, the particle leaves the line (it needs a sideways force to turn, and the field gives only a force along the tangent).
It follows a line of force only when the line is straight (or the field is uniform) and it starts from rest. So the answer is No.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1979",tp:"efl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A pendulum bob of mass 80 mg and carrying a charge of 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 coulomb is at rest in a horizontal uniform electric field of 20,000 volt/metre. Find the tension in the thread of the pendulum and the angle it makes with the vertical. (Take g = 9.8 ms\u207B\xB2)",a:"T \u2248 8.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N (\u2248 9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N); angle with the vertical \u03B8 = tan\u207B\xB9(0.51) \u2248 27\xB0",s:`Three forces act on the bob: weight mg down, electric force qE horizontal, and tension T along the thread.
mg = 80 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 9.8 = 7.84 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N and qE = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u2074 = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N.
At rest, T balances the resultant: T = \u221A((mg)\xB2 + (qE)\xB2) = \u221A(61.5 + 16) \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \u2248 8.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N \u2248 9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N.
Angle with the vertical: tan \u03B8 = qE/mg = 4/7.84 \u2248 0.51, so \u03B8 \u2248 27\xB0.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"efl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A ring of radius R carries a uniformly distributed charge +Q. A point charge \u2212q is placed on the axis of the ring at a distance 2R from the centre of the ring and released from rest. The particle executes a simple harmonic motion along the axis of the ring.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`Simple harmonic motion needs a restoring force proportional to the displacement from the centre.
On the axis, F = qQx/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080(R\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2)) towards the centre. This is proportional to x only when x << R.
Here the particle starts at x = 2R, which is not small compared with R, so F is not proportional to x.
The particle does oscillate through the centre, but the motion is not simple harmonic. The statement is false.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"efl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"An electric line of force in the x-y plane is given by the equation x\xB2 + y\xB2 = 1. A particle with unit positive charge, initially at rest at the point x = 1, y = 0 in the x-y plane, will move along the circular line of force.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`A positive charge released from rest at (1, 0) feels a force along the tangent to the circle, so it starts moving along the tangent.
To keep moving round the circle it would need a force towards the centre, but the field is tangent to the circle everywhere and supplies none. Because of its inertia the particle leaves the circle and moves away along a (curved) path outside the line of force.
A charged particle follows a line of force only when the line is straight. The statement is false.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"ecn",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A small metal ball is suspended in a uniform electric field with the help of an insulated thread. If high energy X-ray beam falls on the ball, the ball will be deflected in the direction of the field.",o:["True","False"],c:0,s:`High-energy X-rays knock electrons out of the metal (photoelectric emission).
The ball loses electrons, so it becomes positively charged.
A positive charge feels a force along the electric field, so the ball is deflected in the direction of the field. The statement is true.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:"Two large plane parallel conducting plates are kept 10 cm apart as shown in figure. The potential difference between them is V. The potential difference between the points A and B (shown in the figure) is",fig:{w:340,h:185,alt:"Two parallel vertical plates 10 cm apart. Point A is 3 cm above C, B is 4 cm to the right of C, and AB is 5 cm",d:[["l",60,26,60,150,"B"],["l",280,26,280,150,"B"],["l",130,64,130,130,"d"],["l",130,130,218,130],["l",130,64,218,130,"d"],["l",130,118,142,118],["l",142,118,142,130],["t",134,60,"A"],["t",130,146,"C","ms"],["t",218,146,"B","ms"],["t",122,100,"3 cm","es"],["t",174,146,"4 cm","ms"],["t",186,92,"5 cm","s"],["l",60,166,280,166,"b"],["t",170,180,"10 cm","ms"]]},o:["{1;4}V","1 V","{2;5}V","{3;4}V"],c:2,s:`Between the plates the field is uniform and horizontal: E = V/d = V/(10 cm).
A and C lie on the same vertical line, so they are at the same potential (equipotential, perpendicular to the field).
So the potential difference between A and B equals that between C and B, which are 4 cm apart along the field.
\u0394V = E \xD7 4 cm = (V/10) \xD7 4 = 2V/5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"The electrostatic potential on the surface of uniformly charged spherical shell of radius R = 10 cm is 120 V. The potential at the centre of shell, at a distance r = 5 cm from centre, and at a distance r = 15 cm from the centre of the shell respectively, are",o:["120 V, 120 V, 80 V","40 V, 40 V, 80 V","0 V, 120 V, 40 V","0 V, 0 V, 80 V"],c:0,s:`Inside a uniformly charged shell the field is zero, so the potential is constant and equals the surface value: 120 V at the centre and at r = 5 cm.
Outside, V = kQ/r \u221D 1/r. On the surface (r = 10 cm) V = 120 V.
At r = 15 cm: V = 120 \xD7 (10/15) = 80 V.
Answer: 120 V, 120 V, 80 V.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"Two charges q\u2081 and q\u2082 are separated by a distance of 30 cm. A third charge q\u2083 initially at 'C' as shown in the figure, is moved along the circular path of radius 40 cm from C to D. If the difference in potential energy due to movement of q\u2083 from C to D is given by {q\u2083K;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}, the value of K is",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"q1 at A, q2 at B on a horizontal line with AB = 30 cm; q3 moves along a quarter circle of radius 40 cm centred at A from C (directly above A) to D (on the line AB, 40 cm from A)",d:[["l",60,160,60,20],["l",60,160,200,160],["p","M60 20 A140 140 0 0 1 200 160","d"],["c",60,160,3,"F"],["c",165,160,3,"F"],["c",60,20,4,"F"],["c",200,160,2,"F"],["t",54,150,"q\u2081","es"],["t",165,150,"q\u2082","ms"],["t",68,18,"q\u2083"],["t",52,24,"C","es"],["t",60,174,"A","ms"],["t",165,174,"B","ms"],["t",200,174,"D","ms"],["t",52,92,"40 cm","es"],["l",60,186,165,186,"b"],["t",112,200,"30 cm","ms"]]},o:["6q\u2081","6q\u2082","8q\u2081","8q\u2082"],c:3,s:`The circle is centred on q\u2081 (radius AC = AD = 40 cm), so q\u2081 is at the same distance 0.4 m from C and from D: its contribution to the potential energy does not change.
Only q\u2082 matters. Distance from q\u2082 to C: \u221A(0.3\xB2 + 0.4\xB2) = 0.5 m. Distance from q\u2082 to D: 0.4 \u2212 0.3 = 0.1 m.
\u0394U = U_D \u2212 U_C = (q\u2083q\u2082/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(1/0.1 \u2212 1/0.5) = (q\u2083q\u2082/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(10 \u2212 2) = q\u2083(8q\u2082)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080).
So K = 8q\u2082.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"A dipole with two electric charges of 2 \u03BCC magnitude each, with separation distance 0.5 \u03BCm, is placed between the plates of a capacitor such that its axis is parallel to an electric field established between the plates when a potential difference of 5 V is applied. Separation between the plates is 0.5 mm. If the dipole is rotated by 30\xB0 from the axis, it tends to realign in the direction due to torque. The value of torque is",o:["5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 Nm","2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 Nm","2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 Nm","5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 Nm"],c:3,s:`Field between the plates: E = V/d = 5/(0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 10\u2074 V/m.
Dipole moment: p = q \xD7 l = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C m.
Torque: \u03C4 = pE sin \u03B8 = 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 10\u2074 \xD7 sin 30\xB0 = 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 0.5 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 N m.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"For a short dipole placed at origin O, the dipole moment p\u20D7 is along x-axis, as shown in the figure. If the electric potential and electric field at A are V\u2080 and E\u2080, respectively, then the correct combination of the electric potential and electric field, respectively, at point B on the y-axis is given by",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Short dipole at the origin along +x; point A on the x-axis at distance r and point B on the y-axis at distance 2r",d:[["l",80,150,310,150,"a"],["l",80,150,80,14,"a"],["t",314,154,"x"],["t",86,16,"y"],["l",84,138,114,138,"a"],["t",99,130,"p","ms"],["t",68,166,"O","es"],["c",135,150,3,"F"],["t",135,166,"A","ms"],["c",80,40,3,"F"],["t",88,44,"B"],["l",80,160,135,160,"b"],["t",107,176,"r","ms"],["l",62,150,62,40,"b"],["t",56,98,"2r","es"]]},o:["zero and {E\u2080;16}","{V\u2080;2} and {E\u2080;16}","zero and {E\u2080;8}","V\u2080 and {E\u2080;4}"],c:0,s:`At A (on the axis, distance r): V\u2080 = kp/r\xB2 and E\u2080 = 2kp/r\xB3, where k = 1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080.
B is on the perpendicular bisector (equatorial line) of the dipole, at distance 2r. There the potential is zero.
The equatorial field is E = kp/(2r)\xB3 = kp/(8r\xB3).
Since kp/r\xB3 = E\u2080/2, E = (1/8)(E\u2080/2) = E\u2080/16.
So the potential is zero and the field is E\u2080/16.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"Two charges 7 \u03BCC and \u22124 \u03BCC are placed at (\u22127 cm, 0, 0) and (7 cm, 0, 0) respectively. Given, \u03B5\u2080 = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2 N\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB2, the electrostatic potential energy of the charge configuration is",o:["\u22121.2 J","\u22121.8 J","\u22121.5 J","\u22122.0 J"],c:1,s:`Distance between the charges: r = 7 + 7 = 14 cm = 0.14 m.
U = q\u2081q\u2082/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r) with 1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) \u2248 9 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\xB2/C\xB2.
U = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 (7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076)(\u22124 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076)/0.14 = \u22129 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 28 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2/0.14 = \u22120.252/0.14 = \u22121.8 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"Two point charges \u22124 \u03BCC and 4 \u03BCC, constituting an electric dipole, are placed at (\u22129, 0, 0) cm and (9, 0, 0) cm in a uniform electric field of strength 10\u2074 NC\u207B\xB9. The work done on the dipole in rotating it from the equilibrium through 180\xB0 is",o:["16.4 mJ","12.4 mJ","18.4 mJ","14.4 mJ"],c:3,s:`Dipole moment: p = q \xD7 (separation) = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 0.18 = 7.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 C m.
Potential energy U = \u2212pE cos \u03B8. At stable equilibrium (\u03B8 = 0) U = \u2212pE; after rotating by 180\xB0 U = +pE.
Work done = change in U = 2pE = 2 \xD7 7.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10\u2074 = 1.44 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 J = 14.4 mJ.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"In the first configuration (1) as shown in the figure, four identical charges (q\u2080) are kept at the corners A, B, C and D of square of side length 'a'. In the second configuration (2), the same charges are shifted to mid points G, E, H and F, of the square. If K = {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}, the difference between the potential energies of configuration (2) and (1) is given by:",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Two squares: in configuration 1 the four charges +q0 sit at the corners A, B, C, D; in configuration 2 they sit at the mid-points G, E, H, F of the sides",d:[["r",40,34,80,80],["r",210,34,80,80],["c",40,34,3.5,"F"],["t",35,28,"A +q\u2080","es"],["c",120,34,3.5,"F"],["t",125,28,"B +q\u2080","s"],["c",120,114,3.5,"F"],["t",125,128,"C +q\u2080","s"],["c",40,114,3.5,"F"],["t",35,128,"D +q\u2080","es"],["c",80,34,1.6],["c",120,74,1.6],["c",80,114,1.6],["c",40,74,1.6],["t",80,49,"G","ms"],["t",114,78,"E","es"],["t",80,108,"H","ms"],["t",46,78,"F","s"],["c",80,74,1.6,"F"],["t",84,70,"O","s"],["t",205,28,"A","es"],["t",295,28,"B","s"],["t",295,128,"C","s"],["t",205,128,"D","es"],["c",250,34,3.5,"F"],["t",250,26,"G +q\u2080","ms"],["c",290,74,3.5,"F"],["t",298,78,"E +q\u2080","s"],["c",250,114,3.5,"F"],["t",250,128,"H +q\u2080","ms"],["c",210,74,3.5,"F"],["t",202,78,"F +q\u2080","es"],["c",250,74,1.6,"F"],["t",254,70,"O","s"],["t",80,152,"Configuration (1)","ms"],["t",250,152,"Configuration (2)","ms"]]},o:["{Kq\u2080\xB2;a}(4\u221A2 \u2212 2)","{Kq\u2080\xB2;a}(3 \u2212 \u221A2)","{Kq\u2080\xB2;a}(3\u221A2 \u2212 2)","{Kq\u2080\xB2;a}(4 \u2212 2\u221A2)"],c:2,s:`Potential energy = sum of Kq\u2080\xB2/distance over all 6 pairs.
Configuration 1 (corners): 4 sides of length a and 2 diagonals of length \u221A2a: U\u2081 = (Kq\u2080\xB2/a)(4 + 2/\u221A2) = (Kq\u2080\xB2/a)(4 + \u221A2).
Configuration 2 (mid-points): 4 neighbouring pairs a/\u221A2 apart and 2 opposite pairs a apart: U\u2082 = (Kq\u2080\xB2/a)(4\u221A2 + 2).
U\u2082 \u2212 U\u2081 = (Kq\u2080\xB2/a)(4\u221A2 + 2 \u2212 4 \u2212 \u221A2) = (Kq\u2080\xB2/a)(3\u221A2 \u2212 2).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:"Three infinitely long wires with linear charge density \u03BB are placed along the x-axis, y-axis and z-axis respectively. Which of the following denotes an equipotential surface?",o:["(x\xB2 + y\xB2)(y\xB2 + z\xB2)(z\xB2 + x\xB2) = constant","xy + yz + zx = constant","(x + y)(y + z)(z + x) = constant","xyz = constant"],c:0,s:`Potential of a long line charge at perpendicular distance s: V = \u2212(\u03BB/2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) ln s + constant.
The distances of the point (x, y, z) from the x, y and z axes are \u221A(y\xB2 + z\xB2), \u221A(z\xB2 + x\xB2) and \u221A(x\xB2 + y\xB2).
Add the three potentials: V = \u2212(\u03BB/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) ln[(y\xB2 + z\xB2)(z\xB2 + x\xB2)(x\xB2 + y\xB2)] + constant.
V is constant when (x\xB2 + y\xB2)(y\xB2 + z\xB2)(z\xB2 + x\xB2) = constant.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"An electric dipole is placed at a distance of 2 cm from an infinite plane sheet having positive charge density \u03C3\u2080. Choose the correct option from the following.",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"Vertical positively charged sheet at the left; a dipole with -q nearer the sheet and +q farther, lying along the line perpendicular to the sheet, 2 cm away",d:[["l",60,20,60,130,"B"],["t",48,32,"+","ms"],["t",48,52,"+","ms"],["t",48,72,"+","ms"],["t",48,92,"+","ms"],["t",48,112,"+","ms"],["t",60,144,"\u03C3\u2080","ms"],["c",200,75,3,"F"],["c",270,75,3,"F"],["l",200,75,270,75,"d"],["t",200,64,"\u2212q","ms"],["t",270,64,"+q","ms"],["l",60,100,200,100,"b"],["t",130,114,"2 cm","ms"]]},o:["Potential energy and torque both are maximum.","Torque on dipole is zero and net force acts towards the sheet.","Potential energy of dipole is minimum and torque is zero.","Torque on dipole is zero and net force is directed away from the sheet."],c:2,s:`The sheet has positive charge, so its field is uniform and points away from the sheet (along +x).
The dipole moment points from \u2212q to +q, which is also away from the sheet: p is parallel to E.
Torque = p \xD7 E = 0, and U = \u2212pE cos 0 = \u2212pE is the minimum value (stable equilibrium).
The field is uniform, so the net force is zero. Answer: potential energy is minimum and torque is zero.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"An electric charge 10\u207B\u2076 \u03BCC is placed at origin (0, 0) m of X \u2013 Y co-ordinate system. Two points P and Q are situated at (\u221A3, \u221A3) m and (\u221A6, 0) m respectively. The potential difference between the points P and Q will be",o:["\u221A3 V","\u221A6 V","3 V","0 V"],c:3,s:`The potential of a point charge depends only on the distance from it: V = kq/r.
Distance of P: \u221A(3 + 3) = \u221A6 m. Distance of Q: \u221A6 m.
Both points are at the same distance, so V_P = V_Q = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2/\u221A6 \u2248 3.7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 V.
Potential difference = 0 V.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"The electrostatic potential due to an electric dipole at a distance 'r' varies as",o:["{1;r\xB3}","r","{1;r}","{1;r\xB2}"],c:3,s:`The two charges of a dipole have opposite signs, so their potentials nearly cancel at a large distance r.
V = kp cos \u03B8/r\xB2, where p is the dipole moment.
So V \u221D 1/r\xB2. (The field varies as 1/r\xB3.)`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"For a uniformly charged thin spherical shell, the electric potential (V) radially away from the centre (O) of shell can be graphically represented as",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Four V versus r graphs: (a) curve rising to a peak at r = R then falling linearly; (b) linear rise to R then falling hyperbola; (c) constant up to R then falling hyperbola; (d) constant up to R then falling straight line",d:[["l",25,85,160,85,"a"],["l",25,85,25,21,"a"],["t",21,25,"V","es"],["t",163,89,"r","s"],["t",95,97,"R","ms"],["l",95,85,95,29,"d"],["t",129,31,"(a)","ms"],["p","M25 79 Q75 77 95 30"],["l",95,30,150,81],["l",200,85,335,85,"a"],["l",200,85,200,21,"a"],["t",196,25,"V","es"],["t",338,89,"r","s"],["t",270,97,"R","ms"],["l",270,85,270,29,"d"],["t",304,31,"(b)","ms"],["l",200,85,270,30],["p","M270 30 Q285 65 330 75"],["l",25,190,160,190,"a"],["l",25,190,25,126,"a"],["t",21,130,"V","es"],["t",163,194,"r","s"],["t",95,202,"R","ms"],["l",95,190,95,134,"d"],["t",129,136,"(c)","ms"],["l",25,145,95,145],["p","M95 145 Q110 170 155 180"],["l",200,190,335,190,"a"],["l",200,190,200,126,"a"],["t",196,130,"V","es"],["t",338,194,"r","s"],["t",270,202,"R","ms"],["l",270,190,270,134,"d"],["t",304,136,"(d)","ms"],["l",200,145,270,145],["l",270,145,330,184]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:2,s:`Inside a uniformly charged shell the field is zero, so V is constant (equal to its surface value kQ/R) for r \u2264 R.
Outside, V = kQ/r: it falls as 1/r, a curve (hyperbola) that starts at kQ/R at r = R without a jump.
So the graph is flat up to r = R and then a falling 1/r curve: graph (c). (Graph (d) has a straight line, which is wrong.)`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"Electric potential at a point 'P' due to a point charge of 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 C is 50 V. The distance of 'P' from the point charge is (Assume {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\xB2 C\u207B\xB2)",o:["3 cm","0.9 cm","9 cm","90 cm"],c:3,s:`V = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) q/r, so r = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) q/V.
r = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079/50 = 45/50 = 0.9 m = 90 cm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"The electric potential at the centre of two concentric half rings of radii R\u2081 and R\u2082, having same linear charge density \u03BB is",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Two concentric half rings of radii R1 and R2, both carrying uniform positive charge density \u03BB, with the centre O at the middle of their straight edge",d:[["p","M60 150 A110 110 0 0 1 280 150"],["p","M100 150 A70 70 0 0 1 240 150"],["c",170,150,2,"F"],["t",176,166,"O","s"],["t",289.2,133,"+","ms"],["t",262.7,76.2,"+","ms"],["t",211.4,40.3,"+","ms"],["t",149,34.8,"+","ms"],["t",92.2,61.3,"+","ms"],["t",56.3,112.6,"+","ms"],["t",49.5,143.5,"+","ms"],["t",249.2,137.2,"+","ms"],["t",227.3,96.7,"+","ms"],["t",184.1,74.2,"+","ms"],["t",142.3,77.9,"+","ms"],["t",99.9,113.5,"+","ms"],["t",90.2,139.9,"+","ms"],["l",170,150,225,54.7],["l",170,150,205,89.4],["t",206,114,"R\u2081","s"],["t",222,78,"R\u2082","s"],["t",170,28,"+\u03BB","ms"],["t",170,64,"+\u03BB","ms"]]},o:["{2\u03BB;\u03B5\u2080}","{\u03BB;2\u03B5\u2080}","{\u03BB;4\u03B5\u2080}","{\u03BB;\u03B5\u2080}"],c:1,s:`Potential at O is the sum over all charge elements: V = \u03A3 k dq/r.
Every element of a half ring of radius R is at distance R from O, so V = k(total charge)/R.
For ring 1: charge \u03BB\u03C0R\u2081, so V\u2081 = k\u03BB\u03C0R\u2081/R\u2081 = k\u03BB\u03C0 = \u03BB/(4\u03B5\u2080). Likewise V\u2082 = \u03BB/(4\u03B5\u2080).
V = V\u2081 + V\u2082 = \u03BB/(4\u03B5\u2080) + \u03BB/(4\u03B5\u2080) = \u03BB/(2\u03B5\u2080).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"Which of the following correctly represents the variation of electric potential (V) of a charged spherical conductor of radius (R) with radial distance (r) from the center?",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Four V versus r graphs: (a) linear rise to R then falling curve; (b) single falling curve from r = 0; (c) zero up to R then a jump and falling curve; (d) constant up to R then falling curve",d:[["l",25,85,160,85,"a"],["l",25,85,25,21,"a"],["t",21,25,"V","es"],["t",163,89,"r","s"],["t",95,97,"R","ms"],["l",95,85,95,29,"d"],["t",129,31,"(a)","ms"],["l",25,85,95,30],["p","M95 30 Q110 65 155 75"],["l",200,85,335,85,"a"],["l",200,85,200,21,"a"],["t",196,25,"V","es"],["t",338,89,"r","s"],["t",270,97,"R","ms"],["l",270,85,270,29,"d"],["t",304,31,"(b)","ms"],["p","M204 27 Q225 71 330 79"],["l",25,190,160,190,"a"],["l",25,190,25,126,"a"],["t",21,130,"V","es"],["t",163,194,"r","s"],["t",95,202,"R","ms"],["l",95,190,95,134,"d"],["t",129,136,"(c)","ms"],["l",95,190,95,142],["p","M95 142 Q110 170 155 180"],["l",200,190,335,190,"a"],["l",200,190,200,126,"a"],["t",196,130,"V","es"],["t",338,194,"r","s"],["t",270,202,"R","ms"],["l",270,190,270,134,"d"],["t",304,136,"(d)","ms"],["l",200,145,270,145],["p","M270 145 Q285 170 330 180"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:3,s:`A charged conductor is an equipotential: the field inside is zero, so V is constant for r \u2264 R.
Outside, V = kQ/r falls smoothly as 1/r and meets the constant value kQ/R at r = R (potential is continuous).
So V is flat up to R and then decays like 1/r: graph (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"Considering a group of positive charges, which of the following statements is correct ?",o:["Net potential of the system cannot be zero at a point but net electric field can be zero at that point.","Net potential of the system at a point can be zero but net electric field can't be zero at that point.","Both the net potential and the net electric field cannot be zero at a point.","Both the net potential and the net field can be zero at a point."],c:0,s:`Potential is a scalar: every positive charge adds a positive term kq/r, so the sum can never be zero.
Electric field is a vector: fields of different charges can point in opposite directions and cancel (for example at the midpoint of two equal positive charges).
So the net potential cannot be zero, but the net field can be zero.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2023",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"An electric dipole is formed by two charges +q and \u2212q located in xy-plane at (0, 2) mm and (0, \u22122) mm, respectively, as shown in the figure. The electric potential at point P (100, 100) mm due to the dipole is V\u2080. The charges +q and \u2212q are then moved to the points (\u22121, 2) mm and (1, \u22122) mm, respectively. What is the value of electric potential at P due to the new dipole?",fig:{w:340,h:195,alt:"Original dipole with +q at (0, 2) mm and -q at (0, -2) mm; moved charges +q at (-1, 2) mm and -q at (1, -2) mm; point P far away at (100, 100) mm",d:[["l",110,120,300,120,"a"],["l",110,170,110,12,"a"],["t",304,124,"x"],["t",116,16,"y"],["l",110,120,270,30,"d"],["c",270,30,3,"F"],["t",264,24,"P (100, 100) mm","es"],["c",110,96,3,"F"],["c",110,144,3,"F"],["c",98,96,3],["c",122,144,3],["t",92,86,"+q (\u22121, 2) mm","es"],["t",116,90,"+q (0, 2) mm","s"],["t",104,160,"\u2212q (0, \u22122) mm","es"],["t",128,160,"\u2212q (1, \u22122) mm","s"],["t",170,188,"filled: original charges; open: moved charges","ms"]]},o:["V\u2080/4","V\u2080/2","V\u2080/\u221A2","3V\u2080/4"],c:1,s:`P is far from the charges (about 141 mm away, the charges are only a few mm apart), so use the dipole formula V = k p\u20D7\xB7r\u0302/r\xB2, with r\u0302 = (\xEE + \u0135)/\u221A2.
Original dipole: p = q(0, 4) mm (from \u2212q to +q), so p\xB7r\u0302 = 4q/\u221A2 (mm units) and V\u2080 = k(4q/\u221A2)/r\xB2.
New dipole: p\u2032 = q[(\u22121 \u2212 1), (2 \u2212 (\u22122))] mm = q(\u22122, 4) mm, so p\u2032\xB7r\u0302 = q(\u22122 + 4)/\u221A2 = 2q/\u221A2.
The distance is the same, so V = V\u2080 \xD7 (2/4) = V\u2080/2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecn",st:"Charge sharing and earthing",q:"Two uniformly charged spherical conductors A and B of radii 5 mm and 10 mm are separated by a distance of 2 cm. If the spheres are connected by a conducting wire, then in equilibrium condition, the ratio of the magnitudes of the electric fields at the surface of the sphere A and B will be",o:["1 : 2","2 : 1","1 : 1","1 : 4"],c:1,s:`Joined by a wire, the two spheres reach the same potential V.
For a sphere, V = kq/R and the surface field is E = kq/R\xB2 = V/R.
So E_A/E_B = (V/R_A)/(V/R_B) = R_B/R_A = 10/5 = 2.
The ratio is 2 : 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecn",st:"Charge sharing and earthing",q:"Sixty four conducting drops each of radius 0.02 m and each carrying a charge of 5 \u03BCC are combined to form a bigger drop. The ratio of surface density of bigger drop to the smaller drop will be",o:["1 : 4","4 : 1","1 : 8","8 : 1"],c:1,s:`Volume is conserved: 64 \xD7 (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3 = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3, so R = 4r = 0.08 m.
Charge adds up: Q = 64q.
Surface density \u03C3 = charge/(4\u03C0 \xD7 radius\xB2).
\u03C3_big/\u03C3_small = (64q/R\xB2)/(q/r\xB2) = 64 \xD7 (r/R)\xB2 = 64/16 = 4.
The ratio is 4 : 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:"If the electric potential at any point (x, y, z) m in space is given by V = 3x\xB2 volt. The electric field at the point (1, 0, 3) m will be",o:["3 V m\u207B\xB9, directed along positive x-axis","3 V m\u207B\xB9, directed along negative x-axis","6 V m\u207B\xB9, directed along positive x-axis","6 V m\u207B\xB9, directed along negative x-axis"],c:3,s:`E = \u2212\u2207V, so E\u2093 = \u2212\u2202V/\u2202x = \u22126x, and E_y = E_z = 0.
At x = 1: E\u2093 = \u22126 V/m.
The magnitude is 6 V m\u207B\xB9 and the minus sign means it points along the negative x-axis.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"The two thin coaxial rings, each of radius a and having charges +Q and \u2212Q respectively are separated by a distance of s. The potential difference between the centres of the two rings is",o:["{Q;2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}[{1;a} \u2212 {1;\u221A(s\xB2+a\xB2)}]","{Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}[{1;a} \u2212 {1;\u221A(s\xB2+a\xB2)}]","{Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}[{1;a} + {1;\u221A(s\xB2+a\xB2)}]","{Q;2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}[{1;a} + {1;\u221A(s\xB2+a\xB2)}]"],c:0,s:`Every point of a ring of radius a is at distance a from its own centre, and \u221A(s\xB2 + a\xB2) from the other ring's centre.
Centre of ring 1 (+Q): V\u2081 = kQ/a \u2212 kQ/\u221A(s\xB2+a\xB2).
Centre of ring 2 (\u2212Q): V\u2082 = \u2212kQ/a + kQ/\u221A(s\xB2+a\xB2) = \u2212V\u2081.
V\u2081 \u2212 V\u2082 = 2kQ[1/a \u2212 1/\u221A(s\xB2+a\xB2)] = (Q/2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)[1/a \u2212 1/\u221A(s\xB2+a\xB2)].`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"A charge Q is distributed over two concentric conducting thin spherical shells radii r and R (R > r). If the surface charge densities on the two shells are equal, the electric potential at the common centre is",o:["{1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}{(R+r);2(R\xB2+r\xB2)}Q","{1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}{(R+r);(R\xB2+r\xB2)}Q","{1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}{(2R+r);(R\xB2+r\xB2)}Q","{1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}{(R+2r);2(R\xB2+r\xB2)}Q"],c:1,s:`Let the common surface density be \u03C3. Charges: q_r = 4\u03C0r\xB2\u03C3 and q_R = 4\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3, so Q = 4\u03C0\u03C3(r\xB2 + R\xB2) and 4\u03C0\u03C3 = Q/(R\xB2 + r\xB2).
Potential at the centre = sum of the shell potentials (each shell's potential inside equals kq/radius):
V = k[4\u03C0r\xB2\u03C3/r + 4\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3/R] = k\xB74\u03C0\u03C3(r + R) = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) Q(R + r)/(R\xB2 + r\xB2).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecn",st:"Charge sharing and earthing",q:"Two isolated conducting spheres S\u2081 and S\u2082 of radius {2;3}R and {1;3}R have 12 \u03BCC and \u22123 \u03BCC charges, respectively and are at a large distance from each other. They are now connected by a conducting wire. A long time after this is done, the charges on S\u2081 and S\u2082 are respectively",o:["4.5 \u03BCC on both","4.5 \u03BCC and \u22124.5 \u03BCC","3 \u03BCC and 6 \u03BCC","6 \u03BCC and 3 \u03BCC"],c:3,s:`Total charge is conserved: 12 + (\u22123) = 9 \u03BCC.
After joining, both spheres have the same potential: kq\u2081/r\u2081 = kq\u2082/r\u2082, so q\u2081/q\u2082 = r\u2081/r\u2082 = (2R/3)/(R/3) = 2.
q\u2081 + q\u2082 = 9 \u03BCC with q\u2081 = 2q\u2082 gives q\u2082 = 3 \u03BCC and q\u2081 = 6 \u03BCC.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"Consider a metallic hollow spheres of radii R and 4R hold charges Q\u2081 and Q\u2082 respectively. Given that surface charge density of the concentric spheres are equal, the potential difference V(R) \u2212 V(4R) is",o:["{3Q\u2081;16\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}","{3Q\u2082;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}","{Q\u2082;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}","{3Q\u2081;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}"],c:0,s:`Equal surface densities: Q\u2081/(4\u03C0R\xB2) = Q\u2082/(4\u03C0(4R)\xB2), so Q\u2082 = 16Q\u2081.
V(R) = k(Q\u2081/R + Q\u2082/4R) and V(4R) = k(Q\u2081 + Q\u2082)/4R (outside the inner shell the field is that of Q\u2081 alone).
V(R) \u2212 V(4R) = kQ\u2081(1/R \u2212 1/4R) = 3kQ\u2081/(4R) = 3Q\u2081/(16\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"Hydrogen ion and singly ionized helium atom are accelerated, from rest, through the same potential difference. The ratio of final speeds of hydrogen and helium ions is close to",o:["1 : 2","10 : 7","2 : 1","5 : 7"],c:2,s:`A charge q accelerated through a potential difference V gains kinetic energy qV: \xBDmv\xB2 = qV.
So v = \u221A(2qV/m).
Both ions carry the same charge e, and a helium atom has about 4 times the mass of a hydrogen atom.
v(H)/v(He) = \u221A(m(He)/m(H)) = \u221A4 = 2.
The ratio is 2 : 1, option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"A two point charges 4q and \u2212q are fixed on the x-axis at x = \u2212{d;2} and x = {d;2}, respectively. If a third point charge 'q' is taken from the origin to x = d along the semicircle as shown in the figure, the energy of the charge will",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"Charge 4q at x = -d/2, the origin O, charge -q at x = d/2 on the x-axis, and a semicircle from the origin to x = d above the axis",d:[["l",20,110,320,110,"a"],["l",140,110,140,30,"d"],["c",80,110,4,"F"],["c",200,110,4,"F"],["l",260,106,260,114],["p","M140 110 A60 60 0 0 1 260 110","a"],["t",80,128,"4q","m"],["t",140,128,"O","m"],["t",200,128,"\u2212q","m"],["t",260,128,"x = d","m"],["t",318,104,"x","e"]]},o:["increase by {3q\xB2;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d}","decrease by {q\xB2;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d}","decrease by {4q\xB2;3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d}","increase by {2q\xB2;3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d}"],c:2,s:`The change in the energy of the charge q depends only on the end points: \u0394U = q(V_end \u2212 V_start).
At the origin: V\u2081 = k(4q/(d/2) \u2212 q/(d/2)) = 6kq/d.
At x = d: the distances are 3d/2 from 4q and d/2 from \u2212q, so V\u2082 = k(8q/(3d) \u2212 2q/d) = 2kq/(3d).
\u0394U = kq\xB2(2/3 \u2212 6)/d = \u221216kq\xB2/(3d) = \u22124q\xB2/(3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d), using k = 1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080).
The energy decreases by 4q\xB2/(3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d), option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"A solid sphere of radius R carries a charge Q + q distributed uniformly over its volume. A very small point like a piece of it of mass m gets detached from the bottom of the sphere and falls down vertically under gravity. This piece carries charge q. If it acquires a speed v when it has fallen through a vertical height y (see figure), then (assume the remaining portion to be spherical)",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"A charged sphere of radius R with charge Q; a small piece with charge q at its bottom falls a vertical distance y with speed v",d:[["c",150,70,60],["c",150,70,2,"F"],["l",150,70,192,112],["t",172,84,"R"],["t",132,66,"Q","e"],["l",100,130,200,130,"d"],["c",150,130,3,"F"],["t",158,126,"q"],["l",90,130,90,200,"a"],["l",80,200,200,200,"d"],["t",80,170,"y","e"],["l",150,138,150,196,"a"],["t",158,176,"v"]]},o:["v\xB2 = y[{qQ;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2ym} + g]","v\xB2 = y[{qQ;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R(R+y)m} + g]","v\xB2 = 2y[{qQR;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080(R+y)\xB3m} + g]","v\xB2 = 2y[{qQ;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R(R+y)m} + g]"],c:3,s:`The remaining sphere (charge Q) acts like a point charge Q at its centre. The piece starts at distance R and ends at R + y.
Energy conservation: \xBDmv\xB2 = mgy + kQq/R \u2212 kQq/(R+y).
The last two terms combine to kQq\xB7y/(R(R+y)).
v\xB2 = 2gy + 2qQy/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mR(R+y)).
So v\xB2 = 2y[qQ/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R(R+y)m) + g], option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:`Ten charges are placed on the circumference of a circle of radius R with constant angular separation between successive charges. Alternate charges 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 have charge (+q) each, while 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 have charge (\u2212q) each. The potential V and the electric field E at the centre of the circle are respectively
(Take V = 0 at infinity.)`,o:["V = {10q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}; E = 0","V = 0; E = {10q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2}","V = 0; E = 0","V = {10q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}; E = {10q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2}"],c:2,s:`Potential is a scalar. Five +q and five \u2212q charges are all at distance R, so V = 5kq/R \u2212 5kq/R = 0.
The five +q charges sit at the corners of a regular pentagon (every 72\xB0), so their fields at the centre cancel.
The five \u2212q charges also form a regular pentagon, so their fields cancel too.
Hence V = 0 and E = 0, option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"Two identical electric point dipoles have dipole moments p\u20D7\u2081 = p\xEE and p\u20D7\u2082 = \u2212p\xEE and are held on the x-axis at distance 'a' from each other. When released, they move along the x-axis with the direction of their dipole moments remaining unchanged. If the mass of each dipole is 'm', their speed when they are infinitely far apart is",o:["{p;a}\u221A{1;\u03C0\u03B5\u2080ma}","{p;a}\u221A{1;2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080ma}","{p;a}\u221A{2;\u03C0\u03B5\u2080ma}","{p;a}\u221A{3;2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080ma}"],c:1,s:`The field of p\u20D7\u2081 on its axis at distance a is E = 2kp/a\xB3 (along +x).
Interaction energy of p\u20D7\u2082 = \u2212p\xEE in it: U = \u2212p\u20D7\u2082\xB7E\u20D7 = +2kp\xB2/a\xB3 (positive, so they repel).
At infinity U = 0, so all of it becomes kinetic energy of the two dipoles: 2 \xD7 \xBDmv\xB2 = 2kp\xB2/a\xB3.
v\xB2 = 2kp\xB2/(ma\xB3) = p\xB2/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080ma\xB3), so v = (p/a)\u221A(1/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080ma)).
Option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"In finding the electric field using Gauss law the formula |E\u20D7| = {q(enc);\u03B5\u2080|A|} is applicable. In the formula \u03B5\u2080 is permittivity of free space, A is the area of Gaussian surface and q(enc) is charge enclosed by the Gaussian surface. This equation can be used in which of the following situation?",o:["For any choice of Gaussian surface.","Only when the Gaussian surface is an equipotential surface.","Only when the Gaussian surface is an equipotential surface and |E\u20D7| is constant on the surface.","Only when |E\u20D7| = constant on the surface."],c:2,s:`Gauss's law is \u222EE\u20D7\xB7dA\u20D7 = q(enc)/\u03B5\u2080. It reduces to E\xB7A = q(enc)/\u03B5\u2080 only if E is perpendicular to the surface everywhere and has the same magnitude everywhere on it.
An equipotential surface is everywhere perpendicular to the field, which gives the first condition.
The magnitude |E\u20D7| must also be constant on the surface for the second condition.
Both are needed, so the answer is (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"Consider two charged metallic spheres S\u2081 and S\u2082 of radii R\u2081 and R\u2082, respectively. The electric fields E\u2081 (on S\u2081) and E\u2082 (on S\u2082) on their surfaces are such that E\u2081/E\u2082 = R\u2081/R\u2082. Then the ratio V\u2081 (on S\u2081)/V\u2082 (on S\u2082) of the electrostatic potentials on each sphere is",o:["(R\u2081/R\u2082)\xB3","(R\u2082/R\u2081)","R\u2081/R\u2082","(R\u2081/R\u2082)\xB2"],c:3,s:`For a charged metallic sphere, E = kQ/R\xB2 on the surface and V = kQ/R.
So V = E\xB7R.
V\u2081/V\u2082 = (E\u2081R\u2081)/(E\u2082R\u2082) = (E\u2081/E\u2082)(R\u2081/R\u2082) = (R\u2081/R\u2082)(R\u2081/R\u2082) = (R\u2081/R\u2082)\xB2.
Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"A charge Q is distributed over three concentric spherical shells of radii a, b, c (a < b < c) such that their surface charge densities are equal to one another. The total potential at a point at distance r from their common centre, where r < a, would be",o:["{Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080(a+b+c)}","{Q;12\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}\xB7{ab+bc+ca;abc}","{Q(a+b+c);4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080(a\xB2+b\xB2+c\xB2)}","{Q(a\xB2+b\xB2+c\xB2);4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080(a\xB3+b\xB3+c\xB3)}"],c:2,s:`A point inside all the shells has potential V = k(Q\u2081/a + Q\u2082/b + Q\u2083/c), where each shell has charge 4\u03C0\u03C3(radius)\xB2.
So V = 4\u03C0k\u03C3(a + b + c) = \u03C3(a+b+c)/\u03B5\u2080.
Total charge Q = 4\u03C0\u03C3(a\xB2 + b\xB2 + c\xB2), so \u03C3 = Q/(4\u03C0(a\xB2+b\xB2+c\xB2)).
V = Q(a+b+c)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080(a\xB2+b\xB2+c\xB2)).
Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"A solid conducting sphere, having a charge Q, is surrounded by an uncharged conducting hollow spherical shell. Let the potential difference between the surface of the solid sphere and that of the outer surface of the hollow shell be V. If the shell is now given a charge of \u22124Q, the new potential difference between the same two surfaces is",o:["4 V","V","2 V","\u22122 V"],c:1,s:`Let the solid sphere have radius a, and the shell have inner radius b. The shell is a conductor, so its outer surface is at the same potential as its inner surface.
The field in the gap a < r < b depends only on the charge Q of the inner sphere: E = kQ/r\xB2.
So the potential difference is V = kQ(1/a \u2212 1/b), whatever charge the shell carries.
Charge on the shell shifts the potential of both surfaces by the same amount and does not change the difference.
The difference stays V, option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:"The electric field in a region is given by E\u20D7 = (Ax + B)\xEE, where E is in N C\u207B\xB9 and x is in meters. The values of constants are A = 20 SI unit and B = 10 SI unit. If the potential at x = 1 is V\u2081 and that at x = \u22125 is V\u2082, then V\u2081 \u2212 V\u2082 is",o:["\u2212520 V","\u221248 V","180 V","320 V"],c:2,s:`V\u2081 \u2212 V\u2082 = V(1) \u2212 V(\u22125) = \u2212\u222B E dx taken from x = \u22125 to x = 1.
= \u2212\u222B(20x + 10)dx = \u2212[10x\xB2 + 10x] from \u22125 to 1.
At x = 1: 10 + 10 = 20. At x = \u22125: 250 \u2212 50 = 200.
V\u2081 \u2212 V\u2082 = \u2212(20 \u2212 200) = 180 V, option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"A system of three charges are placed as shown in the figure. If D >> d, the potential energy of the system is best given by",fig:{w:340,h:125,alt:"Charges +q and -q a distance d apart on a line, with charge Q a distance D from the middle of the pair",d:[["l",20,70,320,70],["c",60,70,3,"F"],["c",150,70,3,"F"],["c",260,70,3,"F"],["t",60,60,"+q","m"],["t",150,60,"\u2212q","m"],["t",260,60,"Q","m"],["l",60,70,60,98,"d"],["l",150,70,150,98,"d"],["l",60,95,150,95,"b"],["t",105,114,"d","m"],["l",105,30,105,70,"d"],["l",260,30,260,70,"d"],["l",105,34,260,34,"b"],["t",182,26,"D","m"]]},o:["{1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}[\u2212{q\xB2;d} \u2212 {qQd;D\xB2}]","{1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}[+{q\xB2;d} + {qQd;D\xB2}]","{1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}[\u2212{q\xB2;d} \u2212 {qQd;2D\xB2}]","{1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}[\u2212{q\xB2;d} + {2qQd;D\xB2}]"],c:0,s:`Add the three pair energies. D is measured from the middle of the +q, \u2212q pair to Q.
Pair +q, \u2212q: \u2212kq\xB2/d.
Pairs with Q: kqQ/(D + d/2) \u2212 kqQ/(D \u2212 d/2) = \u2212kqQ\xB7d/(D\xB2 \u2212 d\xB2/4) \u2248 \u2212kqQd/D\xB2 for D >> d.
U \u2248 (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)[\u2212q\xB2/d \u2212 qQd/D\xB2], option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"A uniformly charged ring of radius 3a and total charge q is placed in xy-plane centred at origin. A point charge q is moving towards the ring along the z-axis and has speed v at z = 4a. The minimum value of v such that it crosses the origin is",o:["\u221A({2;m}\xB7{2;15}\xB7{q\xB2;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a})","\u221A({2;m}\xB7{1;5}\xB7{q\xB2;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a})","\u221A({2;m}\xB7{4;15}\xB7{q\xB2;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a})","\u221A({2;m}\xB7{1;15}\xB7{q\xB2;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a})"],c:0,s:`On the axis the potential of the ring is V = kq/\u221A(9a\xB2 + z\xB2).
At z = 4a: V = kq/(5a). At the centre (z = 0): V = kq/(3a).
The moving charge is repelled, and the potential is largest at the centre, so it only needs enough energy to just reach the centre.
\xBDmv\xB2 = q(V_centre \u2212 V_4a) = kq\xB2(1/(3a) \u2212 1/(5a)) = 2kq\xB2/(15a).
v = \u221A( (2/m)\xB7(2/15)\xB7q\xB2/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a) ), option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:`In free space, a particle A of charge 1 \u03BCC is held fixed at a point P. Another particle B of the same charge and mass 4 mg is kept at a distance of 1 mm from P. If B is released, then its velocity at a distance of 9 mm from P is
[Take {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = 9\xD710\u2079 N m\xB2 C\u207B\xB2]`,o:["3.0 \xD7 10\u2074 m/s","1.0 m/s","1.5 \xD7 10\xB2 m/s","2.0 \xD7 10\xB3 m/s"],c:3,s:`Energy conservation: \xBDmv\xB2 = kq\xB2(1/r\u2081 \u2212 1/r\u2082), with r\u2081 = 1 mm and r\u2082 = 9 mm.
kq\xB2 = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 (10\u207B\u2076)\xB2 = 9\xD710\u207B\xB3 J\xB7m, and 1/r\u2081 \u2212 1/r\u2082 = 1000 \u2212 111 = 889 m\u207B\xB9, so \xBDmv\xB2 = 8 J and v\xB2 = 16/m.
With m = 4 mg = 4\xD710\u207B\u2076 kg: v = \u221A(16/(4\xD710\u207B\u2076)) = 2\xD710\xB3 m/s.
Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"A point dipole p\u20D7 = p\u2080x\u0302 is kept at the origin. The potential and electric field due to this dipole on the y-axis at a distance d are, respectively (Take V = 0 at infinity)",o:["0, {\u2212p\u20D7;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB3}","{|p\u20D7|;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB2}, {p\u20D7;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB3}","0, {p\u20D7;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB3}","{|p\u20D7|;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB2}, {\u2212p\u20D7;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB3}"],c:0,s:`The y-axis is the perpendicular bisector of the dipole (it is along the x-axis). Every point on it is at the same distance from +q and \u2212q, so V = 0.
On the equatorial line the field is E\u20D7 = \u2212p\u20D7/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB3), directed opposite to p\u20D7.
So the answer is 0 and \u2212p\u20D7/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB3), option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"Four equal point charges Q each are placed in the xy plane at (0, 2), (4, 2), (4, \u22122) and (0, \u22122). The work required to put a fifth charge Q at the origin of the coordinate system will be",o:["{Q\xB2;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}(1 + {1;\u221A5})","{Q\xB2;2\u221A2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}","{Q\xB2;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}(1 + {1;\u221A3})","{Q\xB2;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}"],c:0,s:`Work needed = Q \xD7 (potential at the origin due to the four charges).
Distances from the origin: 2 m (to (0,2)), 2 m (to (0,\u22122)), \u221A20 = 2\u221A5 m (to (4,2)) and 2\u221A5 m (to (4,\u22122)).
V = kQ(1/2 + 1/2 + 1/(2\u221A5) + 1/(2\u221A5)) = kQ(1 + 1/\u221A5).
W = QV = (Q\xB2/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(1 + 1/\u221A5), option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"Three charges Q, +q and +q are placed at the vertices of a right-angle isosceles triangle as shown in the figure. The net electrostatic energy of the configuration is zero, if the value of Q is",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Right-angled isosceles triangle with charge Q at the top vertex above the left +q, and the other +q at the right end of the base",d:[["l",90,20,90,130],["l",90,130,200,130],["l",90,20,200,130],["c",90,20,3,"F"],["c",90,130,3,"F"],["c",200,130,3,"F"],["t",82,18,"Q","e"],["t",82,134,"+q","e"],["t",208,134,"+q"]]},o:["+q","\u22122q","{\u2212\u221A2q;\u221A2+1}","{\u2212q;1+\u221A2}"],c:2,s:`Let the equal sides be a, so the hypotenuse (Q to the far +q) is a\u221A2.
Total energy U = k[Qq/a + q\xB2/a + Qq/(a\u221A2)] = 0.
Qq(1 + 1/\u221A2) = \u2212q\xB2, so Q = \u2212q/(1 + 1/\u221A2) = \u2212\u221A2q/(\u221A2 + 1).
Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"The given graph shows variation (with distance r from centre) of",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"A circle of radius r0 with its centre marked, and a graph that is flat up to r0 then falls off with r",d:[["c",60,70,45],["c",60,70,2,"F"],["l",60,70,92,38],["t",82,68,"r\u2080","s"],["l",150,120,150,20],["l",150,120,320,120,"a"],["t",322,136,"r","e"],["l",150,40,210,40],["l",210,40,210,120,"d"],["p","M210 40 Q225 95 300 112"],["t",210,136,"r\u2080","ms"]]},o:["Electric field of a uniformly charged spherical shell","Electric field of a uniformly charged sphere","Potential of a uniformly charged spherical shell","Potential of a uniformly charged sphere"],c:2,s:`The graph is constant up to r\u2080 and then falls smoothly as 1/r.
For a uniformly charged shell, the potential is constant inside (= kQ/r\u2080) and equals kQ/r outside: this matches.
The field of a shell is zero inside and falls as 1/r\xB2 outside, so it does not match.
For a solid sphere, the field rises linearly inside and the potential keeps changing inside, so those do not match either.
The graph shows the potential of a uniformly charged spherical shell, option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"An electric field of 1000 V/m is applied to an electric dipole at angle of 45\xB0. The value of electric dipole moment is 10\u207B\xB2\u2079 C m. What is the potential energy of the electric dipole?",o:["\u221210 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2079 J","\u22127 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 J","\u221220 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 J","\u22129 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 J"],c:1,s:`Potential energy of a dipole in a field: U = \u2212p\u20D7\xB7E\u20D7 = \u2212pE cos\u03B8.
U = \u2212(10\u207B\xB2\u2079)(1000)(cos 45\xB0) = \u221210\u207B\xB2\u2076 \xD7 0.707.
U \u2248 \u22127.07 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 J \u2248 \u22127 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 J, option (b).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"A thin spherical insulating shell of radius R carries a uniformly distributed charge such that the potential at its surface is V\u2080. A hole with small area \u03B14\u03C0R\xB2 (\u03B1 << 1) is made on the shell without affecting the rest of the shell. Which one of the following statements is correct?",o:["The ratio of the potential at the center of the shell to that of the point at \xBDR from center towards the hole will be {1\u2212\u03B1;1\u22122\u03B1}","The potential at the centre of shell is reduced by 2\u03B1V\u2080","The magnitude of electric field at the center of the shell is reduced by {\u03B1V\u2080;2R}","The magnitude of electric field at a point, located on a line passing through the hole and shell's center, on a distance 2R from the center of the spherical shell will be reduced by {\u03B1V\u2080;2R}"],c:0,s:`Shell with hole = full shell + a small charge \u2212\u03B1Q at the hole. For the full shell, V\u2080 = kQ/R.
The removed charge \u03B1Q is at distance R from the centre: it lowers the potential there by k\u03B1Q/R = \u03B1V\u2080, so V(centre) = V\u2080(1 \u2212 \u03B1).
The point \xBDR from the centre towards the hole is \xBDR from the hole: reduction = k\u03B1Q/(R/2) = 2\u03B1V\u2080, so V = V\u2080(1 \u2212 2\u03B1).
Ratio = (1 \u2212 \u03B1)/(1 \u2212 2\u03B1), so (a) is correct.
(b) is wrong (reduction is \u03B1V\u2080). In (c) and (d) the field change is k\u03B1Q/R\xB2 = \u03B1V\u2080/R, not \u03B1V\u2080/(2R).`},{y:"JEE Main 2018",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"Three concentric metal shells A, B and C of respective radii, a, b and c (a < b < c) have surface charge densities +\u03C3, \u2212\u03C3 and +\u03C3 respectively. The potential of shell B is",o:["{\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}[{a\xB2\u2212b\xB2;a} + c]","{\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}[{a\xB2\u2212b\xB2;b} + c]","{\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}[{b\xB2\u2212c\xB2;b} + a]","{\u03C3;\u03B5\u2080}[{b\xB2\u2212c\xB2;c} + a]"],c:1,s:`Charges: Q(A) = 4\u03C0\u03C3a\xB2, Q(B) = \u22124\u03C0\u03C3b\xB2, Q(C) = 4\u03C0\u03C3c\xB2.
Potential on shell B = (potential of A and B at radius b) + (potential of C, which is constant inside C).
V(B) = k(Q(A) + Q(B))/b + kQ(C)/c = (\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080)[(a\xB2 \u2212 b\xB2)/b + c].
Option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:"There is a uniform electrostatic field in a region. The potential at various points on a small sphere centred at P, in the region, is found to vary between the limits 589.0 V to 589.8 V. What is the potential at a point on the sphere whose radius vector makes an angle of 60\xB0 with the direction of the field?",o:["589.2 V","589.6 V","589.5 V","589.4 V"],c:0,s:`In a uniform field, V changes by E\xB7\u0394x along the field. On the sphere, V = V(P) \u2212 Er cos\u03B8, where \u03B8 is the angle between the radius vector and E.
The maximum and minimum are at \u03B8 = 0\xB0 and 180\xB0, so V(P) = (589.0 + 589.8)/2 = 589.4 V and Er = 0.4 V.
At \u03B8 = 60\xB0: V = 589.4 \u2212 0.4 \xD7 cos 60\xB0 = 589.4 \u2212 0.2 = 589.2 V.
Option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:`The potential (in volts) of a charge distribution is given by
V(z) = 30 \u2212 5z\xB2 for |z| \u2264 1 m
V(z) = 35 \u2212 10|z| for |z| \u2265 1 m
V(z) does not depend on x and y. If this potential is generated by a constant charge per unit volume \u03C1\u2080 (in units of \u03B5\u2080) which is spread over a certain region, then choose the correct statement.`,o:["\u03C1\u2080 = 20 \u03B5\u2080 in the entire region","\u03C1\u2080 = 10 \u03B5\u2080 for |z| \u2264 1 m and \u03C1\u2080 = 0 elsewhere","\u03C1\u2080 = 20 \u03B5\u2080 for |z| \u2264 1 m and \u03C1\u2080 = 0 elsewhere","\u03C1\u2080 = 40 \u03B5\u2080 in the entire region"],c:1,s:`For a potential depending only on z, Poisson's equation is d\xB2V/dz\xB2 = \u2212\u03C1/\u03B5\u2080.
For |z| \u2264 1: V = 30 \u2212 5z\xB2, so d\xB2V/dz\xB2 = \u221210, giving \u03C1\u2080 = 10\u03B5\u2080.
For |z| \u2265 1: V is linear in |z|, so d\xB2V/dz\xB2 = 0 and \u03C1\u2080 = 0.
Option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:"Within a spherical charge distribution of charge density \u03C1(r), N equipotential surfaces of potential V\u2080, V\u2080 + \u0394V, V\u2080 + 2\u0394V, ..., V\u2080 + N\u0394V (\u0394V > 0), are drawn and have increasing radii r\u2080, r\u2081, r\u2082, ..., r(N), respectively. If the difference in the radii of the surfaces is constant for all values of V\u2080 and \u0394V then",o:["\u03C1(r) = constant","\u03C1(r) \u221D {1;r\xB2}","\u03C1(r) \u221D {1;r}","\u03C1(r) \u221D r"],c:2,s:`Equal potential steps \u0394V over equal radius steps \u0394r means E = \u0394V/\u0394r is the same everywhere: E is constant.
By Gauss's law, E = kQ(enc)/r\xB2 = constant, so Q(enc) \u221D r\xB2.
Then dQ/dr = 4\u03C0r\xB2\u03C1 \u221D 2r, which gives \u03C1 \u221D 1/r.
Option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:"An electric field E\u20D7 = (25\xEE + 30\u0135) N C\u207B\xB9 exists in a region of space. If the potential at the origin is taken to be zero then the potential at x = 2 m, y = 2 m is",o:["\u2212130 V","\u2212120 V","\u2212140 V","\u2212110 V"],c:3,s:`V = \u2212\u222BE\u20D7\xB7dr\u20D7 from the origin. For a uniform field, V = \u2212(E\u2093x + E_y y).
V = \u2212(25 \xD7 2 + 30 \xD7 2) = \u2212(50 + 60) = \u2212110 V.
The options print the unit as J, but potential is in volt. Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2014",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:"Assume that an electric field E\u20D7 = 30x\xB2\xEE exists in space. Then the potential difference V_A \u2212 V\u2080 where V\u2080 is the potential at the origin and V_A the potential at x = 2 m is",o:["80 V","120 V","\u2212120 V","\u221280 V"],c:3,s:`V_A \u2212 V\u2080 = \u2212\u222BE dx from x = 0 to x = 2.
= \u2212\u222B30x\xB2 dx = \u221210x\xB3 evaluated from 0 to 2.
= \u221210 \xD7 8 = \u221280 V (the options print J, but it is volt). Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"A charge Q is uniformly distributed over a long rod AB of length L as shown in the figure. The electric potential at the point O lying at a distance L from the end A is",fig:{w:340,h:120,alt:"Point O, then a gap of length L to end A of a charged rod AB of length L",d:[["l",30,50,310,50,"d"],["c",40,50,3,"F"],["t",40,38,"O","m"],["t",140,38,"A","m"],["t",240,38,"B","m"],["r",140,44,100,12,"f"],["l",40,50,40,90,"d"],["l",140,50,140,90,"d"],["l",240,50,240,90,"d"],["l",40,86,140,86,"b"],["t",90,104,"L","m"],["l",140,86,240,86,"b"],["t",190,104,"L","m"]]},o:["{Q ln2;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080L}","{Q ln2;8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080L}","{3Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080L}","{Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080L ln2}"],c:0,s:`Take a small piece dx of the rod at distance x from O, where L \u2264 x \u2264 2L. Its charge is dq = (Q/L)dx.
V = \u222B k dq/x = k(Q/L)\u222Bdx/x from L to 2L = k(Q/L) ln(2L/L).
V = Q ln2/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080L), option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"Two small equal point charges of magnitude q are suspended from a common point on the ceiling by insulating massless strings of equal lengths. They come to equilibrium with each string making angle \u03B8 from the vertical. If the mass of each charge is m, then the electrostatic potential at the centre of line joining them will be ({1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = k)",o:["2\u221A(k mg tan\u03B8)","\u221A(k mg tan\u03B8)","4\u221A({k mg;tan\u03B8})","4\u221A(k mg tan\u03B8)"],c:3,s:`Let the distance between the charges be r. For one charge in equilibrium: tan\u03B8 = F/(mg) = kq\xB2/(r\xB2mg).
So q\xB2 = mg r\xB2 tan\u03B8/k and q = r\u221A(mg tan\u03B8/k).
Potential at the midpoint (each charge is r/2 away): V = 2kq/(r/2) = 4kq/r.
V = 4k\u221A(mg tan\u03B8/k) = 4\u221A(k mg tan\u03B8). Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"Consider a finite, insulated, uncharged conductor placed near a finite positively charged conductor. The uncharged body must have a potential",o:["less than the charged conductor and more than at infinity.","more than the charged conductor and less than at infinity.","more than the charged conductor and more than at infinity.","less than the charged conductor and less than at infinity."],c:0,s:`Electric field lines start on the positively charged conductor and potential falls along a field line.
The lines reaching the uncharged conductor come from the charged one, and lines leaving it go on to infinity (or end on the negative induced charge).
So its potential is lower than that of the charged conductor, but higher than the potential at infinity (zero).
Option (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"Two large vertical and parallel metal plates having a separation of 1 cm are connected to a DC voltage source of potential difference X. A proton is released at rest midway between the two plates. It is found to move at 45\xB0 to the vertical just after release. Then X is nearly",o:["1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 V","1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 V","1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 V","1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 V"],c:2,s:`Gravity gives the proton a vertical acceleration g. The plates give a horizontal acceleration a = eE/m = eX/(md).
Motion at 45\xB0 to the vertical means the two accelerations are equal: eX/(md) = g.
X = mgd/e = (1.67\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2077)(9.8)(10\u207B\xB2)/(1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079) \u2248 1\xD710\u207B\u2079 V.
Option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"Consider a thin spherical shell of radius R with its centre at the origin, carrying uniform positive surface charge density. The variation of the magnitude of the electric field |E\u20D7(r)| and the electric potential V(r) with the distance r from the centre, is best represented by which graph?",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"Four small graphs (a) to (d), each with a solid curve for the field magnitude and a dashed curve for the potential against r, with R marked",d:[["t",10,28,"(a)"],["l",48,93,48,18,"a"],["l",48,93,165,93,"a"],["t",165,107,"r","es"],["t",96,107,"R","ms"],["t",52,22,"|E|, V","s"],["l",96,93,96,38],["p","M96 38 Q118 83 160 88"],["l",48,93,96,26,"d"],["p","M96 26 Q126 63 160 76","d"],["t",175,28,"(b)"],["l",213,93,213,18,"a"],["l",213,93,330,93,"a"],["t",330,107,"r","es"],["t",261,107,"R","ms"],["t",217,22,"|E|, V","s"],["l",213,53,261,53],["p","M261 53 Q286 86 325 90"],["l",213,26,261,26,"d"],["p","M261 26 Q291 63 325 76","d"],["t",10,140,"(c)"],["l",48,205,48,130,"a"],["l",48,205,165,205,"a"],["t",165,219,"r","es"],["t",96,219,"R","ms"],["t",52,134,"|E|, V","s"],["l",48,165,96,165],["p","M96 165 Q121 198 160 202"],["l",48,205,96,138,"d"],["p","M96 138 Q126 175 160 188","d"],["t",175,140,"(d)"],["l",213,205,213,130,"a"],["l",213,205,330,205,"a"],["t",330,219,"r","es"],["t",261,219,"R","ms"],["t",217,134,"|E|, V","s"],["l",261,205,261,150],["p","M261 150 Q283 195 325 200"],["l",213,138,261,138,"d"],["p","M261 138 Q291 175 325 188","d"],["t",170,232,"solid line: |E(r)|     dashed line: V(r)","ms"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:3,s:`For a uniformly charged thin shell: E = 0 inside (r < R), jumps to \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080 at r = R, then falls as 1/r\xB2 outside.
V is constant inside, equal to kQ/R, and falls as 1/r outside (V is continuous at R).
Graph (d) shows E zero inside with a jump at R, and V flat inside then decreasing.
In (a) and (c) V rises inside; in (b) and (c) E is non-zero inside. Option (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2011",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:"The electrostatic potential inside a charged spherical ball is given by \u03C6 = ar\xB2 + b where r is the distance from the centre; a, b are constants. Then the charge density inside the ball is",o:["\u221224\u03C0a\u03B5\u2080r","\u22126a\u03B5\u2080r","\u221224\u03C0a\u03B5\u2080","\u22126a\u03B5\u2080"],c:3,s:`The field is E = \u2212d\u03C6/dr = \u22122ar.
Gauss's law for a sphere of radius r with uniform density \u03C1: E\xB74\u03C0r\xB2 = (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3\u03C1/\u03B5\u2080, so \u03C1 = 3\u03B5\u2080E/r.
\u03C1 = 3\u03B5\u2080(\u22122ar)/r = \u22126a\u03B5\u2080.
Option (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"Two points P and Q are maintained at the potentials of 10 V and \u22124 V respectively. The work done in moving 100 electrons from P to Q is",o:["\u22129.60 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2077 J","9.60 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2077 J","\u22122.24 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2076 J","2.24 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2076 J"],c:3,s:`Work done in moving a charge q from P to Q: W = q(V_Q \u2212 V_P).
Here q = \u2212100e = \u22121.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2077 C, V_Q \u2212 V_P = \u22124 \u2212 10 = \u221214 V.
W = (\u22121.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2077)(\u221214) = +2.24\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2076 J.
Option (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"Consider a system of three charges {q;3}, {q;3} and \u2212{2q;3} placed at points A, B and C, respectively, as shown in the figure. Take O to be the centre of the circle of radius R and angle CAB = 60\xB0",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Circle of radius R with centre O; A and B at opposite ends of a diameter, C on the circle on the negative x side, with the x and y axes",d:[["c",170,110,75],["l",132.5,175,207.5,45,"d"],["l",95,110,207.5,45],["l",95,110,132.5,175],["l",170,110,290,110,"d"],["l",170,195,170,18,"d"],["c",95,110,3,"F"],["c",132.5,175,3,"F"],["c",207.5,45,3,"F"],["t",82,114,"C","e"],["t",122,192,"A","m"],["t",214,40,"B"],["t",176,102,"O"],["t",296,114,"x"],["t",176,18,"y"],["t",132,128,"60\xB0","s"]]},o:["the electric field at point O is {q;8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2} directed along the negative x-axis","the potential energy of the system is zero","the magnitude of the force between the charges at C and B is {q\xB2;54\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2}","the potential at point O is {q;12\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}"],c:2,s:`A and B are the ends of a diameter (the dotted line through O), so angle ACB = 90\xB0 and BC = AB sin 60\xB0 = \u221A3 R.
Force between C and B: k(q/3)(2q/3)/(3R\xB2) = 2kq\xB2/(27R\xB2) = q\xB2/(54\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2). So (c) is correct.
V at O = k(q/3 + q/3 \u2212 2q/3)/R = 0, so (d) is wrong. The potential energy is not zero.
Field at O: A and B cancel; C gives k(2q/3)/R\xB2 = q/(6\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2), not q/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\xB2), so (a) is wrong.`},{y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:`The potential at a point x (measured in \u03BCm) due to some charges situated on the x-axis is given by
V(x) = {20;x\xB2\u22124} volt
The electric field E at x = 4 \u03BCm is given by`,o:["(10/9) volt/\u03BCm and in the +ve x direction","(5/3) volt/\u03BCm and in the \u2212ve x direction","(5/3) volt/\u03BCm and in the +ve x direction","(10/9) volt/\u03BCm in the \u2212ve x direction"],c:0,s:`E = \u2212dV/dx = 20 \xD7 2x/(x\xB2 \u2212 4)\xB2 = 40x/(x\xB2 \u2212 4)\xB2.
At x = 4: E = 160/(16 \u2212 4)\xB2 = 160/144 = 10/9 volt/\u03BCm.
The sign is positive, so E points along +x.
Option (a). (The book prints 'x = 4 mm', but the units of V(x) are \u03BCm and the options in volt/\u03BCm need x = 4 \u03BCm.)`},{y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"Charges are placed on the vertices of a square as shown. Let E\u20D7 be the electric field and V the potential at the centre. If the charges on A and B are interchanged with those on D and C respectively, then",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Square ABCD with charge q at A and B on the top edge and charge -q at D and C on the bottom edge",d:[["r",110,30,120,120],["t",116,46,"A"],["t",224,46,"B","e"],["t",116,144,"D"],["t",224,144,"C","e"],["t",110,22,"q","m"],["t",230,22,"q","m"],["t",110,166,"\u2212q","m"],["t",230,166,"\u2212q","m"]]},o:["E\u20D7 changes, V remains unchanged","E\u20D7 remains unchanged, V changes","both E\u20D7 and V change","E\u20D7 and V remain unchanged"],c:0,s:`Initially A, B carry +q and D, C carry \u2212q. The centre is equidistant from all four, so V = 0.
The field at the centre points from the +q side towards the \u2212q side (from the top edge to the bottom edge).
After the interchange A, B carry \u2212q and D, C carry +q: V is still 0 (unchanged), but the field direction is reversed.
So E\u20D7 changes and V remains unchanged. Option (a).`},{y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"An electric charge 10\u207B\xB3 \u03BCC is placed at the origin (0, 0) of X\u2013Y co-ordinate system. Two points A and B are situated at (\u221A2, \u221A2) and (2, 0) respectively. The potential difference between the points A and B will be",o:["4.5 volt","9 volt","zero","2 volt"],c:2,s:`The potential of a point charge depends only on the distance from it.
Distance of A: \u221A(2 + 2) = 2. Distance of B: 2.
Both points are at the same distance, so V_A = V_B = kq/2.
The potential difference is zero. Option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"A long, hollow conducting cylinder is kept coaxially inside another long, hollow conducting cylinder of larger radius. Both the cylinders are initially electrically neutral.",o:["A potential difference appears between the two cylinders when a charge density is given to the inner cylinder.","A potential difference appears between the two cylinders when a charge density is given to the outer cylinder.","No potential difference appears between the two cylinders when a uniform line charge is kept along the axis of the cylinders.","No potential difference appears between the two cylinders when same charge density is given to both the cylinders."],c:0,s:`(a) Charge on the inner cylinder produces a field in the gap (induced opposite charge on the inner face of the outer one), so there is a potential difference. True.
(b) Charge on the outer cylinder only: the field inside a charged cylindrical shell is zero, so no potential difference. False.
(c) A line charge on the axis induces charge on the cylinders and a field appears in the gap, so a potential difference exists. False.
(d) The field in the gap is due to the inner charge only, so a potential difference exists. False.
Only (a) is correct.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"Positive and negative point charges of equal magnitude are kept at (0, 0, {a;2}) and (0, 0, \u2212{a;2}) respectively. The workdone by the electric field when another positive point charge is moved from (\u2212a, 0, 0) to (0, a, 0) is",o:["positive","negative","zero","depends on the path connecting the initial and final positions."],c:2,s:`The two charges form a dipole along the z-axis. The plane z = 0 is its perpendicular bisector and is an equipotential at V = 0.
Both (\u2212a, 0, 0) and (0, a, 0) lie in this plane, so the potential difference is zero.
Work W = q(V_i \u2212 V_f) = 0, whatever the path (the field is conservative).
Option (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:`Two insulating plates are both uniformly charged in such a way that the potential difference between them is V\u2082 \u2212 V\u2081 = 20 V. (i.e. plate 2 is at a higher potential). The plates are separated by d = 0.1 m and can be treated as infinitely large. An electron is released from rest on the inner surface of plate 1. What is its speed when it hits plate 2?
(e = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C, m\u2091 = 9.11 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg)`,fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"Two parallel plates, 1 on the left and 2 on the right, separated by 0.1 m, with X and Y axes drawn beside them",d:[["r",100,20,12,100],["r",200,20,12,100],["t",106,138,"1","m"],["t",206,138,"2","m"],["l",112,60,200,60,"b"],["t",156,52,"0.1 m","ms"],["l",260,120,260,30,"a"],["t",266,34,"Y"],["l",260,120,320,120,"a"],["t",318,112,"X","e"]]},o:["32 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 m/s","2.65 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s","7.02 \xD7 10\xB9\xB2 m/s","1.87 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s"],c:1,s:`The electron moves towards the plate at higher potential and gains kinetic energy equal to e \xD7 (V\u2082 \u2212 V\u2081).
\xBDmv\xB2 = 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 20 = 3.2\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2078 J.
v = \u221A(2 \xD7 3.2\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2078 / 9.11\xD710\u207B\xB3\xB9) = \u221A(7.03\xD710\xB9\xB2) \u2248 2.65\xD710\u2076 m/s.
Option (b).`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"Two thin wire rings each having a radius R are placed at a distance d apart with their axes coinciding. The charges on the two rings are +Q and \u2212Q. The potential difference between the centers of the two rings is",o:["zero","{Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}[{1;R} \u2212 {1;\u221A(R\xB2+d\xB2)}]","{QR;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB2}","{Q;2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}[{1;R} \u2212 {1;\u221A(R\xB2+d\xB2)}]"],c:3,s:`At the centre of the +Q ring: V\u2081 = kQ/R \u2212 kQ/\u221A(R\xB2 + d\xB2) (the \u2212Q ring is at distance \u221A(R\xB2 + d\xB2)).
By symmetry the potential at the centre of the \u2212Q ring is V\u2082 = \u2212V\u2081.
V\u2081 \u2212 V\u2082 = 2V\u2081 = 2kQ[1/R \u2212 1/\u221A(R\xB2 + d\xB2)] = (Q/2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)[1/R \u2212 1/\u221A(R\xB2 + d\xB2)].
Option (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"A charged particle q is shot towards another charged particle Q which is fixed, with a speed v. It approaches Q upto a closest distance r and then returns. If q were given a speed 2v, the closest distances of approach would be",o:["r","2r","r/2","r/4"],c:3,s:`At the closest distance all the kinetic energy has become electric potential energy: \xBDmv\xB2 = kQq/r.
With speed 2v: \xBDm(2v)\xB2 = 4 \xD7 \xBDmv\xB2 = kQq/r\u2032.
So kQq/r\u2032 = 4kQq/r, giving r\u2032 = r/4.
Option (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"A thin spherical conducting shell of radius R has a charge q. Another charge Q is placed at the centre of the shell. The electrostatic potential at a point P at a distance R/2 from the centre of the shell is",o:["{2Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}","{2Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R} \u2212 {2q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}","{2Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R} + {q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}","{(q+Q);4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}\xB7{2;R}"],c:2,s:`Charge Q at the centre induces \u2212Q on the inner surface of the shell and +Q on the outer surface, so the outer surface carries q + Q.
Potential at P (R/2 from the centre) = (Q at distance R/2) + (\u2212Q on the shell at R) + ((q + Q) on the shell at R).
V = kQ/(R/2) \u2212 kQ/R + k(q + Q)/R = 2kQ/R + kq/R.
V = 2Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R) + q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R), option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"Two equal point charges are fixed at x = \u2212a and x = +a on the axis. Another point charge Q is placed at the origin. The change in the electrical potential energy of Q, when it is displaced by a small distance x along the x-axis, is approximately proportional to",o:["x","x\xB2","x\xB3","1/x"],c:1,s:`Let the fixed charges be q. At position x the potential energy of Q is U = kqQ[1/(a \u2212 x) + 1/(a + x)] = 2kqQa/(a\xB2 \u2212 x\xB2).
For small x: U \u2248 (2kqQ/a)(1 + x\xB2/a\xB2).
The change in U from x = 0 is (2kqQ/a\xB3)x\xB2, which is proportional to x\xB2.
Option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:"A uniform electric field pointing in positive x-direction exists in a region. Let A be the origin, B be the point on the x-axis at x = +1 cm and C be the point on the y-axis at y = +1 cm. Then the potentials at the points A, B and C satisfy",o:["V_A < V_B","V_A > V_B","V_A < V_C","V_A > V_C"],c:1,s:`Potential falls in the direction of the electric field. B lies further along +x than A, so V_B < V_A, i.e. V_A > V_B.
C is displaced from A along y, perpendicular to the field, so no work is done and V_C = V_A.
So only V_A > V_B is true. Option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"Three charges Q, + q and + q are placed at the vertices of a right-angled isosceles triangle as shown. The net electrostatic energy of the configuration is zero if Q is equal to",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Right-angled isosceles triangle with charge Q at the top vertex above the left +q, and the other +q at the right end of the base of length a",d:[["l",90,20,90,120],["l",90,120,200,120],["l",90,20,200,120],["c",90,20,3,"F"],["c",90,120,3,"F"],["c",200,120,3,"F"],["t",82,18,"Q","e"],["t",82,124,"+q","e"],["t",208,124,"+q"],["l",90,142,200,142,"b"],["t",145,160,"a","m"]]},o:["{\u2212q;1+\u221A2}","{\u22122q;2+\u221A2}","\u22122q","+q"],c:1,s:`Equal sides a, hypotenuse a\u221A2 (Q to the far +q).
Total energy U = k[Qq/a + q\xB2/a + Qq/(a\u221A2)] = 0.
Q(1 + 1/\u221A2) = \u2212q, so Q = \u2212q/(1 + 1/\u221A2) = \u2212\u221A2q/(\u221A2 + 1) = \u22122q/(2 + \u221A2) after multiplying top and bottom by \u221A2.
Option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"A non-conducting ring of radius 0.5 m carries a total charge of 1.11 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 C distributed non-uniformly on its circumference, producing an electric field E\u20D7 everywhere in space. The value of the integral \u222B \u2212E\u20D7\xB7dl\u20D7 with the limits l = \u221E (lower) to l = 0 (upper), (l = 0 being centre of the ring) in volt is",o:["+2","\u22121","\u22122","zero."],c:0,s:`\u222B from \u221E to 0 of (\u2212E\u20D7\xB7dl\u20D7) = V(0) \u2212 V(\u221E) = V(centre), since V(\u221E) = 0.
Every bit of charge is at distance R from the centre, so V = kQ/R whatever the distribution.
V = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 1.11\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 / 0.5 \u2248 2 V.
Option (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1995",tp:"efl",st:"Field of point charges and distributions",q:"Two point charges +q and \u2212q are held fixed at (\u2212d, 0) and (d, 0) respectively of a x-y coordinate system. Then",o:["The electric field E at all points on x-axis has same direction.","Electric field at all points on y-axis is along x-axis.","Work has to be done in bringing a test charge from \u221E to the origin.","The dipole moment is 2qd along the x-axis."],c:1,s:`(a) Between the charges E points along +x, but beyond the \u2212q charge the nearer \u2212q dominates and E points along \u2212x. So the direction changes. False.
(b) On the y-axis the y-components of the two fields cancel, leaving a field along +x. True.
(c) The potential at the origin is zero, so no work is needed to bring a test charge from infinity. False.
(d) The dipole moment points from \u2212q to +q, i.e. along \u2212x, so it is not 'along the x-axis' as stated. False.
Option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"A hollow metal sphere of radius 5 cm is charged such that the potential on its surface is 10 volt. The potential at the centre of the sphere is",o:["zero","10 volt","same as at a point 5 cm away from the surface","same as at a point 25 cm away from the surface"],c:1,s:`Inside a hollow charged conductor the field is zero, so the potential is the same everywhere inside, equal to the surface potential: 10 V.
5 cm outside the surface (10 cm from centre) V = 10 \xD7 5/10 = 5 V; 25 cm outside (30 cm from centre) V = 10 \xD7 5/30 \u2248 1.7 V.
Only (b) is correct.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2025",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:`Six infinitely large and thin non-conducting sheets are fixed in configurations I and II. As shown in the figure, the sheets carry uniform surface charge densities which are indicated in terms of \u03C3\u2080. The separation between any two consecutive sheets is 1 \u03BCm. The various regions between the sheets are denoted as 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5. If \u03C3\u2080 = 9 \u03BCC/m\xB2, then which of the following statements is/are correct?
(Take permittivity of free space \u03B5\u2080 = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 F/m)`,fig:{w:340,h:185,alt:"Two sets of six parallel sheets with charge densities marked on top and regions 1 to 5 between them: configuration I alternates +sigma0 and -sigma0, configuration II has sigma0/2, -sigma0, +sigma0, -sigma0, +sigma0, -sigma0/2",d:[["l",20,50,20,150],["t",20,44,"+\u03C3\u2080","ms"],["l",46,50,46,150],["t",46,34,"\u2212\u03C3\u2080","ms"],["l",72,50,72,150],["t",72,44,"+\u03C3\u2080","ms"],["l",98,50,98,150],["t",98,34,"\u2212\u03C3\u2080","ms"],["l",124,50,124,150],["t",124,44,"+\u03C3\u2080","ms"],["l",150,50,150,150],["t",150,34,"\u2212\u03C3\u2080","ms"],["t",33,104,"1","ms"],["t",59,104,"2","ms"],["t",85,104,"3","ms"],["t",111,104,"4","ms"],["t",137,104,"5","ms"],["l",190,50,190,150],["t",190,44,"+\u03C3\u2080/2","ms"],["l",216,50,216,150],["t",216,34,"\u2212\u03C3\u2080","ms"],["l",242,50,242,150],["t",242,44,"+\u03C3\u2080","ms"],["l",268,50,268,150],["t",268,34,"\u2212\u03C3\u2080","ms"],["l",294,50,294,150],["t",294,44,"+\u03C3\u2080","ms"],["l",320,50,320,150],["t",320,34,"\u2212\u03C3\u2080/2","ms"],["t",203,104,"1","ms"],["t",229,104,"2","ms"],["t",255,104,"3","ms"],["t",281,104,"4","ms"],["t",307,104,"5","ms"],["t",85,172,"Configuration I","ms"],["t",255,172,"Configuration II","ms"]]},o:["In region 4 of the configuration I, the magnitude of the electric field is zero.","In region 3 of the configuration II, the magnitude of the electric field is {\u03C3\u2080;\u03B5\u2080}.","Potential difference between the first and the last sheets of the configuration I is 5 V.","Potential difference between the first and the last sheets of the configuration II is zero."],c:[0],s:`The total charge of each configuration is zero, so the field outside is zero and the field in a region equals (charge to its left)/\u03B5\u2080. Here \u03C3\u2080/\u03B5\u2080 = 10\u2076 V/m, i.e. 1 V across each 1 \u03BCm gap.
Configuration I: left charges are \u03C3\u2080, 0, \u03C3\u2080, 0, \u03C3\u2080 for regions 1 to 5. Region 4 has zero field, so (a) is true.
Total potential difference I = 3 \xD7 1 V = 3 V, not 5 V, so (c) is false.
Configuration II: left charges are \u03C3\u2080/2, \u2212\u03C3\u2080/2, \u03C3\u2080/2, \u2212\u03C3\u2080/2, \u03C3\u2080/2, so region 3 has field \u03C3\u2080/(2\u03B5\u2080), not \u03C3\u2080/\u03B5\u2080: (b) is false. The potential difference is 0.5 V, not zero: (d) is false.
Only (a) is correct.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2025",tp:"ecn",st:"Charge sharing and earthing",q:"A positive point charge of 10\u207B\u2078 C is kept at a distance of 20 cm from the center of a neutral conducting sphere of radius 10 cm. The sphere is then grounded and the charge on the sphere is measured. The grounding is then removed and subsequently the point charge is moved by a distance of 10 cm further away from the center of the sphere along the radial direction. Taking {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 Nm\xB2/C\xB2 (where \u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of free space), which of the following statements is/are correct?",o:["Before the grounding, the electrostatic potential of the sphere is 450 V.","Charge flowing from the sphere to the ground because of grounding is 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 C.","After the grounding is removed, the charge on the sphere is \u22125 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 C.","The final electrostatic potential of the sphere is 300 V."],c:[0,1,2],s:`(a) The neutral sphere has net induced charge zero, so its potential is that of the outside charge at the centre: V = kq/d = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078/0.2 = 450 V. True.
(b), (c) Grounded: V = 0, so kQ'/R + kq/d = 0, giving Q' = \u2212qR/d = \u221210\u207B\u2078 \xD7 0.1/0.2 = \u22125\xD710\u207B\u2079 C. The sphere ends with \u22125\xD710\u207B\u2079 C, which means +5\xD710\u207B\u2079 C flowed out to the ground. Both true.
(d) Charge moved to 30 cm: V = kq/0.3 + k(\u22125\xD710\u207B\u2079)/0.1 = 300 \u2212 450 = \u2212150 V, not 300 V. False.
Correct: (a), (b), (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:`Six charges are placed around a regular hexagon of side length a as shown in the figure. Five of them have charge q, and the remaining one has charge x. The perpendicular from each charge to the nearest hexagon side passes through the center O of the hexagon and is bisected by the side.
Which of the following statement(s) is (are) correct in SI units?`,fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"Regular hexagon with centre O and six charges placed outside, one beyond each side, five marked q and the bottom one marked x",d:[["p","M132 110 L151 77.1 L189 77.1 L208 110 L189 142.9 L151 142.9 Z"],["l",170,44,170,176,"d"],["c",170,44.2,3,"F"],["t",170,36,"q","m"],["c",227,77.1,3,"F"],["t",236,76,"q",""],["c",227,142.9,3,"F"],["t",236,148,"q",""],["c",170,175.8,3,"F"],["t",170,192,"x","m"],["c",113,142.9,3,"F"],["t",104,148,"q","e"],["c",113,77.1,3,"F"],["t",104,76,"q","e"],["t",176,106,"O"],["t",206,134,"a","s"],["t",178,64,"90\xB0","s"]]},o:["When x = q, the magnitude of the electric field at O is zero.","When x = \u2212q, the magnitude of the electric field at O is {q;6\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2}.","When x = 2q, the potential at O is {7q;4\u221A3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a}.","When x = \u22123q, the potential at O is \u2212{3q;4\u221A3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a}."],c:[0,1,2],s:`The perpendicular from a charge to the side is bisected by the side and passes through O, so each charge is at distance 2 \xD7 (apothem) = 2 \xD7 (\u221A3a/2) = \u221A3a from O.
(a) Six equal charges at the corners of a regular hexagon: the fields cancel at O. True.
(b) x = \u2212q equals six charges q (zero field) plus \u22122q at the x position: E = k\xB72q/(3a\xB2) = q/(6\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2). True.
(c) V = k(5q + 2q)/(\u221A3a) = 7q/(4\u221A3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a). True.
(d) V = k(5q \u2212 3q)/(\u221A3a) = 2q/(4\u221A3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a), which is positive, so the given value is wrong. False.
Correct: (a), (b), (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:`In the figure, the inner (shaded) region A represents a sphere of radius r_A = 1, within which the electrostatic charge density varies with the radial distance r from the center as \u03C1_A = kr, where k is positive.
In the spherical shell B of outer radius r_B, the electrostatic charge density varies as \u03C1_B = {2k;r}.
Assume that dimensions are taken care of. All physical quantities are in their SI units.
Which of the following statement(s) is(are) correct?`,fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Two concentric circles: the inner shaded sphere A of radius rA and the surrounding spherical shell B of outer radius rB",d:[["c",110,95,75],["c",110,95,50,"f"],["l",110,95,145,60],["t",136,70,"rA","s"],["l",110,95,163,148],["t",142,128,"rB","s"],["t",100,98,"A","m"],["t",46,98,"B","m"]]},o:["If r_B = \u221A{3;2}, then the electric field is zero everywhere outside B.","If r_B = 3/2, then the electric potential just outside B is {k;\u03B5\u2080}.","If r_B = 2, then the total charge of the configuration is 15\u03C0k.","If r_B = 5/2, then the magnitude of the electric field just outside B is {13\u03C0k;\u03B5\u2080}."],c:[1],s:`Charge in A: \u222B\u2080\xB9 kr\xB74\u03C0r\xB2 dr = \u03C0k.
Charge in B: \u222B from 1 to r_B of (2k/r)\xB74\u03C0r\xB2 dr = 4\u03C0k(r_B\xB2 \u2212 1).
Total Q = \u03C0k(4r_B\xB2 \u2212 3).
(a) r_B\xB2 = 3/2: Q = 3\u03C0k \u2260 0, so the field outside is not zero. False.
(b) r_B = 3/2: Q = 6\u03C0k, V = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r_B) = 6\u03C0k/(6\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) = k/\u03B5\u2080. True.
(c) r_B = 2: Q = 13\u03C0k, not 15\u03C0k. False.
(d) r_B = 5/2: Q = 22\u03C0k, E = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r_B\xB2) = 22k/(25\u03B5\u2080), not 13\u03C0k/\u03B5\u2080. False.
Only (b) is correct.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:`A disk of radius R with uniform positive charge density \u03C3 is placed on the xy plane with its center at the origin. The Coulomb potential along the z-axis is
V(z) = {\u03C3;2\u03B5\u2080}(\u221A(R\xB2+z\xB2) \u2212 z).
A particle of positive charge q is placed initially at rest at a point on the z axis with z = z\u2080 and z\u2080 > 0. In addition to the Coulomb force, the particle experiences a vertical force F\u20D7 = \u2212ck\u0302 with c > 0. Let \u03B2 = {2c\u03B5\u2080;q\u03C3}. Which of the following statement(s) is(are) correct?`,o:["For \u03B2 = {1;4} and z\u2080 = {25;7}R, the particle reaches the origin.","For \u03B2 = {1;4} and z\u2080 = {3;7}R, the particle reaches the origin.","For \u03B2 = {1;4} and z\u2080 = {R;\u221A3}, the particle returns back to z = z\u2080.","For \u03B2 > 1 and z\u2080 > 0, the particle always reaches the origin."],c:[0,2,3],s:`Potential energy: U(z) = qV + cz = (q\u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080)[\u221A(R\xB2+z\xB2) \u2212 (1 \u2212 \u03B2)z]. Let f(z) = \u221A(R\xB2+z\xB2) \u2212 (1 \u2212 \u03B2)z. The particle moves along z; force = \u2212dU/dz.
\u03B2 = 1/4: f\u2032 = z/\u221A(R\xB2+z\xB2) \u2212 3/4 is zero at z = 3R/\u221A7. f falls from f(0) = R to a minimum there, then rises, and f(z) = R again at z = 24R/7.
(a) z\u2080 = 25R/7 > 24R/7, so f(z\u2080) > f(0): the particle gains enough energy to reach the origin. True.
(b) z\u2080 = 3R/7: here f\u2032 < 0, so the force pushes the particle away from the origin. False.
(c) z\u2080 = R/\u221A3: f\u2032 < 0, the particle moves out, climbs the other side of the minimum and stops where f equals f(z\u2080), then returns. True.
(d) \u03B2 > 1: f\u2032 > 0 for all z, so the net force always points towards the origin. True.
Correct: (a), (c), (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:`Two electric dipoles, A, B with respective dipole moments d\u20D7_A = \u22124qa\xEE and d\u20D7_B = \u22122qa\xEE are placed on the x-axis with a separation R, as shown in the figure
The distance from A at which both of them produce the same potential is`,fig:{w:340,h:105,alt:"Points A and B on the x-axis separated by R, with the x-axis arrow to the right",d:[["l",20,70,310,70,"a"],["t",314,66,"X"],["c",70,70,3,"F"],["c",230,70,3,"F"],["t",70,90,"A","m"],["t",230,90,"B","m"],["l",70,35,70,70,"d"],["l",230,35,230,70,"d"],["l",70,38,230,38,"b"],["t",150,30,"R","m"]]},o:["{R;\u221A2\u22121}","{R;\u221A2+1}","{\u221A2R;\u221A2+1}","{\u221A2R;\u221A2\u22121}"],c:3,s:`On its axis a dipole has potential V = kp\xB7r\u0302/r\xB2. Both dipole moments point along \u2212x. Let the point be x from A.
Between A and B: V_A = \u22124kqa/x\xB2 is negative while V_B = +2kqa/(R \u2212 x)\xB2 is positive, so the potentials can never be equal there (only their magnitudes could be).
To the left of A: 4/x\xB2 = 2/(R + |x|)\xB2 has no solution because \u221A2(R + |x|) = |x| is impossible.
Beyond B both potentials are negative: 4/x\xB2 = 2/(x \u2212 R)\xB2, so x = \u221A2(x \u2212 R) and x = \u221A2R/(\u221A2 \u2212 1).
Option (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A point charge +Q is placed just outside an imaginary hemispherical surface of radius R as shown. Which of the following statements is/are correct?",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"A hemispherical dome of radius R standing on a flat dashed base, with a point charge +Q just above its top",d:[["p","M80 150 A90 90 0 0 1 260 150"],["l",80,150,260,150,"d"],["l",170,150,170,64,"a"],["t",178,110,"R"],["c",170,46,4,"F"],["t",180,44,"+Q"]]},o:["The circumference of the flat surface is an equipotential.","The component of the electric field normal to the flat surface is constant over the surface.","Total flux through the curved and the flat surfaces is Q/\u03B5\u2080.","The electric flux passing through the curved surface of the hemisphere is \u2212{Q;2\u03B5\u2080}(1 \u2212 {1;\u221A2})."],c:[0,3],s:`The charge is on the axis of the hemisphere, at distance R above the centre of the flat face (just outside the top of the dome).
(a) Every point of the rim of the flat face is at the same distance \u221A2 R from the charge, so the rim is an equipotential: true.
(b) The angle between the field and the normal changes from point to point on the flat face, so the normal component is not constant: false.
(c) The charge is outside the closed surface (curved + flat), so the total flux through it is zero, not Q/\u03B5\u2080: false.
(d) The flat face (radius R) is seen from the charge at a half-angle of 45\xB0, so the flux through it is Q(1 \u2212 cos45\xB0)/(2\u03B5\u2080) = Q/(2\u03B5\u2080)(1 \u2212 1/\u221A2), leaving the closed surface. The same amount enters through the curved surface, so its (outward) flux is \u2212Q/(2\u03B5\u2080)(1 \u2212 1/\u221A2): true.
Answer: (a) and (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2015",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"A uniformly charged solid sphere of radius R has potential V\u2080 (measured with respect to \u221E) on its surface. For this sphere the equipotential surfaces with potentials {3V\u2080;2}, {5V\u2080;4}, {3V\u2080;4} and {V\u2080;4} have radius R\u2081, R\u2082, R\u2083 and R\u2084 respectively. Then",o:["R\u2081 = 0 and R\u2082 < (R\u2084 \u2212 R\u2083)","2R < R\u2084","R\u2081 = 0 and R\u2082 > (R\u2084 \u2212 R\u2083)","R\u2081 \u2260 0 and (R\u2082 \u2212 R\u2081) > (R\u2084 \u2212 R\u2083)"],c:[0,1],s:`Inside the sphere V(r) = (V\u2080/2)(3 \u2212 r\xB2/R\xB2). Outside, V(r) = V\u2080R/r.
3V\u2080/2: 3 \u2212 r\xB2/R\xB2 = 3 gives r = 0, so R\u2081 = 0.
5V\u2080/4: 3 \u2212 r\xB2/R\xB2 = 5/2 gives R\u2082 = R/\u221A2 \u2248 0.71R.
3V\u2080/4 and V\u2080/4 are less than V\u2080, so these surfaces are outside: R\u2083 = 4R/3 and R\u2084 = 4R.
R\u2084 \u2212 R\u2083 = 8R/3 \u2248 2.67R, which is greater than R\u2082, so (a) is true and (c) is false. R\u2081 = 0 makes (d) false.
2R < 4R = R\u2084, so (b) is true. Answer: (a) and (b).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"Two non-conducting spheres of radii R\u2081 and R\u2082 and carrying uniform volume charge densities +\u03C1 and \u2212\u03C1, respectively, are placed such that they partially overlap, as shown in the figure. At all points in the overlapping region,",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Two partly overlapping circles, the larger carrying charge density rho with radius R1 and the smaller carrying minus rho with radius R2, with the overlap shaded",d:[["c",105,100,80],["c",205,100,60],["p","M169 52 A60 60 0 0 0 169 148 A80 80 0 0 0 169 52 Z","f"],["t",70,100,"\u03C1","m"],["t",235,70,"\u2212\u03C1","m"],["l",105,100,105,178,"a"],["t",112,150,"R\u2081"],["l",205,100,205,158,"a"],["t",212,140,"R\u2082"]]},o:["the electrostatic field is zero.","the electrostatic potential is constant.","the electrostatic field is constant in magnitude.","the electrostatic field has same direction."],c:[2,3],s:`Inside a uniformly charged sphere the field at position r\u20D7 from its centre is \u03C1r\u20D7/(3\u03B5\u2080).
At a point in the overlap, with r\u20D7\u2081 and r\u20D7\u2082 measured from the centres of the +\u03C1 and \u2212\u03C1 spheres: E\u20D7 = \u03C1r\u20D7\u2081/(3\u03B5\u2080) \u2212 \u03C1r\u20D7\u2082/(3\u03B5\u2080) = \u03C1(r\u20D7\u2081 \u2212 r\u20D7\u2082)/(3\u03B5\u2080).
r\u20D7\u2081 \u2212 r\u20D7\u2082 is the fixed vector from the centre of the +\u03C1 sphere to the centre of the \u2212\u03C1 sphere, the same for every point.
So the field is not zero, has the same magnitude and the same direction everywhere in the overlap: (c) and (d) are true, (a) is false.
A non-zero uniform field means the potential changes from point to point, so (b) is false. Answer: (c) and (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"Six point charges are kept at the vertices of a regular hexagon of side L and centre O, as shown in the figure. Given that K = {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080}{q;L\xB2}, which of the following statement(s) is (are) correct?",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"Regular hexagon ABCDEF of side L with charges A plus 2q, B plus q, C minus q, D minus 2q, E minus q, F plus q, centre O, line PR vertical and line ST horizontal through O",d:[["p","M140 68 L200 68 L230 120 L200 172 L140 172 L110 120 Z"],["l",170,120,140,68,"d"],["l",170,120,200,68,"d"],["l",170,120,230,120],["l",170,120,110,120],["l",170,120,200,172,"d"],["l",170,120,140,172,"d"],["l",170,68,170,172],["c",140,68,3,"F"],["c",200,68,3,"F"],["c",230,120,3,"F"],["c",200,172,3,"F"],["c",140,172,3,"F"],["c",110,120,3,"F"],["t",134,72,"F +q","e"],["t",206,72,"E \u2212q"],["t",238,124,"D \u22122q"],["t",206,188,"C \u2212q"],["t",134,188,"B +q","e"],["t",102,124,"A +2q","e"],["l",140,58,200,58,"b"],["t",170,50,"L","m"],["t",176,92,"P"],["t",176,160,"R"],["t",124,114,"S"],["t",208,114,"T"],["t",160,134,"O","e"]]},o:["The electric field at O is 6K along OD.","The potential at O is zero.","The potential at all points on the line PR is same.","The potential at all points on the line ST is same."],c:[0,1,2],s:`From the figure the charges are A +2q, B +q, C \u2212q, D \u22122q, E \u2212q, F +q, each at distance L from O.
Field at O: A and D together give 2K + 2K = 4K along OD. B and E give 2K along OE, and C and F give 2K along OC. OE and OC are 60\xB0 on either side of OD, so their parts perpendicular to OD cancel and along OD they add 2 \xD7 2K cos60\xB0 = 2K.
Total field = 4K + 2K = 6K along OD: (a) true.
Potential at O = (k/L)(2q + q \u2212 q \u2212 2q \u2212 q + q) = 0: (b) true.
Line PR is the perpendicular bisector of FE, BC and AD, and each pair has equal and opposite charges, so V = 0 at every point of PR: (c) true.
On ST (the line AD) the points near A are at higher potential than those near D, so V is not the same: (d) false.
Answer: (a), (b) and (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"ecn",st:"Charge sharing and earthing",q:"A spherical metal shell A of radius R_A and a solid metal sphere B of radius R_B (< R_A) are kept far apart and each is given charge +Q. Now they are connected by a thin metal wire. Then",o:["E_A^(inside) = 0","Q_A > Q_B","\u03C3_A/\u03C3_B = R_B/R_A","E_A^(on surface) < E_B^(on surface)"],c:[0,1,2,3],s:`The wire makes the potentials equal: kQ_A/R_A = kQ_B/R_B (k = 1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080), so Q_B = Q_A R_B/R_A.
Since R_B < R_A, Q_B < Q_A, so (b) is true. The field inside a charged conducting shell is zero, so (a) is true.
Surface charge density \u03C3 = Q/(4\u03C0R\xB2): \u03C3_A/\u03C3_B = (Q_A/Q_B)(R_B\xB2/R_A\xB2) = (R_A/R_B)(R_B\xB2/R_A\xB2) = R_B/R_A, so (c) is true.
The field at the surface of a conductor is \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080. Since \u03C3_A < \u03C3_B (as R_B/R_A < 1), E_A < E_B at the surfaces: (d) is true.
All four statements are correct.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"egs",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?",o:["If the electric field due to a point charge varies as r^(\u22122.5) instead of r\u207B\xB2, then the Gauss law will still be valid.","The Gauss law can be used to calculate the field distribution around an electric dipole.","If the electric field between two point charges is zero somewhere, then the sign of the two charges is the same.","The workdone by the external force in moving a unit positive charge from point A at potential V_A to point B at potential V_B is (V_B \u2212 V_A)."],c:[2,3],s:`(a) Gauss's law holds because the field of a point charge falls as 1/r\xB2. With r^(\u22122.5) the flux through a sphere would depend on its radius: false.
(b) Gauss's law is always true, but it helps to calculate the field only when the charge distribution has enough symmetry. A dipole has none: false.
(c) If the field between two point charges is zero somewhere, the two fields cancel there, which is possible only if the charges have the same sign: true.
(d) The external force just balances the electric force, so the work done by it equals the gain in potential energy: W = q(V_B \u2212 V_A) = V_B \u2212 V_A for a unit charge: true.
Answer: (c) and (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:`A spherical symmetric charge system is centred at origin. Given, electric potential
\u03C6 = {Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\u2080} (r \u2264 R\u2080),
\u03C6 = {Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r} (r > R\u2080).`,fig:{w:340,h:140,alt:"Graph of potential against r, constant up to R0 and then falling smoothly",d:[["l",50,110,320,110,"a"],["l",50,110,50,10,"a"],["t",58,16,"\u03C6"],["t",316,126,"r","e"],["l",50,35,150,35],["p","M150 35 Q175 80 300 103"],["l",150,35,150,110,"d"],["t",150,126,"R\u2080","m"]]},o:["within r = 2R\u2080 total enclosed net charge is Q","electric field is discontinued at r = R\u2080","charge is only present at r = R\u2080","electrostatic energy is zero for r < R\u2080."],c:[0,1,2,3],s:`Inside (r \u2264 R\u2080) the potential is constant, so E = \u2212d\u03C6/dr = 0 and no field energy is stored there: (d) is true. Gauss's law with E = 0 inside also means no charge lies inside r < R\u2080.
Outside, \u03C6 = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r) is the potential of a total charge Q, so the net charge enclosed within r = 2R\u2080 is Q: (a) is true.
Outside E = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2), so just outside R\u2080 it is Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\u2080\xB2), while just inside it is 0. The field jumps at r = R\u2080: (b) is true.
Since there is no charge inside R\u2080 and none outside R\u2080 (the outside field is exactly that of charge Q at the centre), the whole charge Q must be a surface charge at r = R\u2080, a thin shell: (c) is true.
All four statements are correct. (The book's answer key lists only (a), (b) and (d).)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"An ellipsoidal cavity is carved within a perfect conductor (figure). A positive charge q is placed at the centre of the cavity. The points A and B are on the cavity surface as shown in the figure. Then",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Ellipsoidal cavity with charge q at its centre, point A on the top wall close to the charge and point B at the right end of the cavity far from it",d:[["p","M50 100 A120 55 0 1 1 290 100 A120 55 0 1 1 50 100 Z"],["c",170,100,3,"F"],["t",176,116,"q"],["c",170,45,3,"F"],["t",170,36,"A","m"],["c",290,100,3,"F"],["t",296,96,"B"]]},o:["electric field near A in the cavity = electric field near B in the cavity","charge density at A = charge density at B","potential at A = potential at B","total electric flux through the surface of the cavity is q/\u03B5\u2080."],c:[2,3],s:`The wall of the cavity is the surface of a conductor, so it is an equipotential: V_A = V_B. Option (c) is true.
The field lines from q end on the induced negative charge. A is closer to q than B, so the field and the induced charge density are larger at A than at B: (a) and (b) are false.
By Gauss's law the flux through the cavity surface = enclosed charge/\u03B5\u2080 = q/\u03B5\u2080: (d) is true.
Answer: (c) and (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"A charge +q is fixed at each of the points x = x\u2080, x = 3x\u2080, x = 5x\u2080, ..., x = \u221E on the x-axis, and a charge \u2212q is fixed at each of the points x = 2x\u2080, x = 4x\u2080, x = 6x\u2080, ..., x = \u221E. Here x\u2080 is a positive constant. Take the potential at a point due to a charge Q at a distance r from it to be Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r). Then the potential at the origin due to the above system of charges is",o:["0","{q;8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080x\u2080 ln2}","\u221E","{q ln2;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080x\u2080}"],c:3,s:`Add the potentials of all the charges at the origin:
V = (q/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080x\u2080)[1 \u2212 1/2 + 1/3 \u2212 1/4 + ...].
The series 1 \u2212 1/2 + 1/3 \u2212 1/4 + ... equals ln 2.
So V = q ln2/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080x\u2080): option (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"Two identical thin rings, each of radius R metre, are coaxially placed a distance R metre apart. If Q\u2081 coulomb and Q\u2082 coulomb, are respectively the charges uniformly spread on the two rings, the workdone in moving a charge q from the centre of one ring to that of the other is",o:["zero","{q(Q\u2081 \u2212 Q\u2082)(\u221A2 \u2212 1);4\u221A2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}","{q\u221A2(Q\u2081 + Q\u2082);4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}","{q(Q\u2081 + Q\u2082)(\u221A2 + 1);4\u221A2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}"],c:1,s:`Every point of the other ring is at distance \u221A(R\xB2 + R\xB2) = \u221A2 R from the centre of a ring.
Potential at the centre of ring 1: V\u2081 = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)[Q\u2081/R + Q\u2082/(\u221A2R)]. At the centre of ring 2: V\u2082 = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)[Q\u2082/R + Q\u2081/(\u221A2R)].
Moving q from the centre of the ring carrying Q\u2082 to the centre of the ring carrying Q\u2081: W = q(V\u2081 \u2212 V\u2082).
V\u2081 \u2212 V\u2082 = (Q\u2081 \u2212 Q\u2082)(1 \u2212 1/\u221A2)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R) = (Q\u2081 \u2212 Q\u2082)(\u221A2 \u2212 1)/(4\u221A2 \u03C0\u03B5\u2080R).
So W = q(Q\u2081 \u2212 Q\u2082)(\u221A2 \u2212 1)/(4\u221A2 \u03C0\u03B5\u2080R): option (b). (Moving the other way only changes the sign.)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"ecn",st:"Induced charge and shielding",q:"A solid conducting sphere having a charge Q is surrounded by an uncharged concentric conducting hollow spherical shell. Let the potential difference between the surface of the solid sphere and that of the outer surface of the hollow shell be V. If the shell is now given a charge of \u22123Q, the new potential difference between the same two surfaces is",o:["V","2V","4V","\u22122V"],c:0,s:`The field in the gap between the sphere and the shell depends only on the charge Q of the inner sphere (the shell's inner surface carries \u2212Q).
So the potential difference between the sphere and the shell is Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(1/a \u2212 1/b) and does not depend on any extra charge given to the shell.
A conducting shell is at one potential throughout, so the inner and outer surface of the shell are at the same potential.
Giving the shell \u22123Q shifts the potentials of the shell and the sphere by the same amount, so the difference stays V: option (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1987",tp:"ecl",st:"Equilibrium and motion of charges",q:"A charge q is placed at the centre of the line joining two equal charges Q. The system of the three charges will be in equilibrium if q is equal to",o:["\u2212Q/2","\u2212Q/4","+Q/4","+Q/2"],c:1,s:`Let the distance between the two Q charges be d. The middle charge is in equilibrium by symmetry.
For one Q charge, the repulsion from the other Q must be balanced by the pull of q, so q must be negative.
kQ\xB2/d\xB2 = kQ|q|/(d/2)\xB2 = 4kQ|q|/d\xB2
So |q| = Q/4 and q = \u2212Q/4: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"An electric dipole of dipole moment 6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 Cm is placed in uniform electric field of magnitude 10\u2076 V/m. Initially, the dipole moment is parallel to electric field. The work that needs to be done on the dipole to make its dipole moment opposite to the field, will be ____ J.",n:12,s:`Potential energy of a dipole: U = \u2212pE cos\u03B8.
Parallel: U\u2081 = \u2212pE. Opposite: U\u2082 = +pE.
Work needed = U\u2082 \u2212 U\u2081 = 2pE.
W = 2 \xD7 6\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10\u2076 = 12 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"At the centre of a half ring of radius R = 10 cm and linear charge density 4 nC m\u207B\xB9, the potential is x \u03C0 V. The value of x is ____.",n:36,s:`Every element of the half ring is at the same distance R from the centre, so V = kQ/R.
The charge is Q = \u03BB \xD7 \u03C0R, so V = k\u03BB\u03C0R/R = k\u03BB\u03C0.
V = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 4\xD710\u207B\u2079 \xD7 \u03C0 = 36\u03C0 volt.
So x = 36.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:`The distance between charges +q and \u2212q is 2l and between +2q and \u22122q is 4l. The electrostatic potential at point P at a distance r from centre O (r \u226B l) is \u2212\u03B1[{ql;r\xB2}]\xD710\u2079 V where the value of \u03B1 is ____.
(Use {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = 9\xD710\u2079 Nm\xB2C\u207B\xB2)`,fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"Charges plus 2q, minus q, plus q and minus 2q on a line with centre O in the middle, and point P on a line making 60 degrees with the axis",d:[["l",20,110,320,110],["c",40,110,3,"F"],["c",100,110,3,"F"],["c",160,110,3,"F"],["c",220,110,3,"F"],["c",280,110,3,"F"],["t",40,128,"+2q","m"],["t",100,128,"\u2212q","m"],["t",160,128,"O","m"],["t",220,128,"+q","m"],["t",280,128,"\u22122q","m"],["l",160,110,225,30],["c",225,30,3,"F"],["t",233,28,"P"],["t",172,100,"60\xB0","s"]]},n:27,s:`The charges lie on a line with O at the centre: +2q at \u22122l, \u2212q at \u2212l, +q at +l, \u22122q at +2l.
Net charge = 2q \u2212 q + q \u2212 2q = 0, so far away only the dipole term is left.
Dipole moment (along x): p = \u03A3qx = (2q)(\u22122l) + (\u2212q)(\u2212l) + (q)(l) + (\u22122q)(2l) = \u22124ql + ql + ql \u2212 4ql = \u22126ql.
V = k p cos\u03B8/r\xB2 with \u03B8 = 60\xB0: V = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 (\u22126ql) \xD7 \xBD / r\xB2 = \u221227\xD710\u2079 (ql/r\xB2) V.
So \u03B1 = 27.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:`An infinitely long thin wire, having a uniform charge density per unit length of 5 nC/m, is passing through a spherical shell of radius 1 m, as shown in the figure. A 10 nC charge is distributed uniformly over the spherical shell. If the configuration of the charges remains static, the magnitude of the potential difference between points P and R, in Volt, is ____.
[Given: In SI units {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = 9\xD710\u2079, ln 2 = 0.7. Ignore the area pierced by the wire.]`,fig:{w:340,h:260,alt:"Spherical shell of radius 1 m with a long straight wire through its centre; point P is 0.5 m from the wire inside the shell and point R is 2 m from the wire outside",d:[["c",130,130,100],["l",130,130,130,12,"a"],["l",130,130,130,248,"a"],["t",136,22,"\u221E"],["t",136,246,"\u2212\u221E"],["l",130,130,330,130],["c",180,130,3,"F"],["t",180,122,"P","m"],["c",330,130,3,"F"],["t",330,122,"R","e"],["l",130,144,180,144,"b"],["t",155,158,"0.5 m","ms"],["l",130,178,330,178,"b"],["t",230,194,"2 m","ms"],["l",130,130,60,60,"a"],["t",80,100,"1 m","e"]]},n:171,s:`The potential difference is the sum of the wire part and the shell part. P is inside the shell, 0.5 m from the wire. R is outside, 2 m from the wire and from the centre.
Wire: V_P \u2212 V_R = (\u03BB/2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) ln(2/0.5) = 2k\u03BB ln4 = 2 \xD7 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 5\xD710\u207B\u2079 \xD7 2 \xD7 0.7 = 126 V.
Shell: inside, V = kQ/1 = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 10\xD710\u207B\u2079 = 90 V. At R, V = kQ/2 = 45 V. Difference = 45 V.
Both parts make P the higher potential, so they add: 126 + 45 = 171 V.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"efl",st:"Electric dipole",q:"An electric dipole of dipole moment 6.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C m placed in a uniform electric field of 1.5 \xD7 10\xB3 N C\u207B\xB9 in such a way that dipole moment is along electric field. The work done in rotating dipole by 180\xB0 in this field will be ____ m J.",n:18,s:`Turning the dipole from \u03B8 = 0\xB0 to \u03B8 = 180\xB0 changes its energy from \u2212pE to +pE.
Work = 2pE = 2 \xD7 6.0\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 1.5\xD710\xB3 = 1.8\xD710\u207B\xB2 J.
1.8\xD710\u207B\xB2 J = 18 mJ.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"Three concentric spherical metallic shells X, Y and Z of radius a, b and c respectively [a < b < c] have surface charge densities \u03C3, \u2212\u03C3 and \u03C3, respectively. If the shells X and Z are at same potential. If the radii of X and Y are 2 cm and 3 cm, respectively. The radius of shell Z is ____ cm.",n:5,s:`Charges: Q_X = 4\u03C0a\xB2\u03C3, Q_Y = \u22124\u03C0b\xB2\u03C3, Q_Z = 4\u03C0c\xB2\u03C3.
V_X = k(Q_X/a + Q_Y/b + Q_Z/c) = (\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080)(a \u2212 b + c).
V_Z = k(Q_X + Q_Y + Q_Z)/c = (\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080)(a\xB2 \u2212 b\xB2 + c\xB2)/c.
V_X = V_Z gives ac \u2212 bc + c\xB2 = a\xB2 \u2212 b\xB2 + c\xB2, so c(a \u2212 b) = (a \u2212 b)(a + b) and c = a + b.
c = 2 + 3 = 5 cm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"64 identical drops each charged upto potential of 10 mV combined to form a bigger drop. The potential of the bigger drop will be ____ mV.",n:160,s:`Charge is conserved: Q = 64q. Volume is conserved: R\xB3 = 64r\xB3, so R = 4r.
V = kQ/R = k \xD7 64q/(4r) = 16 \xD7 (kq/r).
Each small drop has kq/r = 10 mV, so V = 16 \xD7 10 = 160 mV.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Non-uniform charge distributions",q:`For a charged spherical ball, electrostatic potential inside the ball varies with r as V = 2ar\xB2 + b. Here, a and b are constant and r is the distance from the center. The volume charge density inside the ball is \u2212\u03BBa\u03B5. The value of \u03BB is ____.
(\u03B5 = permittivity of the medium)`,n:12,s:`Field: E = \u2212dV/dr = \u22124ar (along the radius).
E grows in proportion to r, so the density is uniform and E = \u03C1r/(3\u03B5) inside (Gauss's law).
\u03C1 = 3\u03B5E/r = 3\u03B5(\u22124a) = \u221212a\u03B5.
Comparing with \u03C1 = \u2212\u03BBa\u03B5 gives \u03BB = 12.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"27 identical drops are charged at 22 V each. They combine to form a bigger drop. The potential of the bigger drop will be ____ V.",n:198,s:`Charge: Q = 27q. Volume: R\xB3 = 27r\xB3, so R = 3r.
V = kQ/R = k \xD7 27q/(3r) = 9 \xD7 (kq/r).
V = 9 \xD7 22 = 198 V.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential of charges and distributions",q:"512 identical drops of mercury are charged to a potential of 2 V each. The drops are joined to form a single drop. The potential of this drop is ____ V.",n:128,s:`Charge: Q = 512q. Volume: R\xB3 = 512r\xB3, so R = 8r.
V = kQ/R = k \xD7 512q/(8r) = 64 \xD7 (kq/r).
V = 64 \xD7 2 = 128 V.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"In an electrical circuit, a battery is connected to pass 20 C of charge through it in a certain given time. The potential difference between two plates of the battery is maintained at 15 V. The work done by the battery is ____ J.",n:300,s:`Work done in moving a charge q through a potential difference V is W = qV.
W = 20 C \xD7 15 V = 300 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"27 similar drops of mercury are maintained at 10 V each. All these spherical drops combine into a single big drop. The potential energy of the bigger drops is ____ times that of a smaller drop.",n:243,s:`Small drop: charge q, radius r. Big drop: charge 27q, radius R = 3r.
Energy of a charged sphere: U = (1/2)QV = Q\xB2/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R).
U_big/U_small = (27q)\xB2/(3r) \xF7 q\xB2/r = 729/3 = 243.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:`Two point charges \u2212Q and +{Q;\u221A3} are placed in the xy-plane at the origin (0, 0) and a point (2, 0), respectively, as shown in the figure. This results in an equipotential circle of radius R and potential V = 0 in the xy-plane with its centre at (b, 0). All lengths are measured in metres.
The value of R is ____ metre.`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"x and y axes with charge minus Q at the origin, charge plus Q over root 3 at x = 2, and a dashed circle centred at x = 3 passing around the second charge",d:[["l",20,100,330,100,"a"],["l",40,190,40,10,"a"],["t",326,116,"x","e"],["t",48,14,"y"],["c",40,100,3,"F"],["t",48,92,"\u2212Q"],["c",140,100,3,"F"],["t",126,120,"+Q/\u221A3"],["c",190,100,86.6,"d"]]},n:1.73,s:`At a point on the V = 0 circle: k(\u2212Q/d\u2081 + (Q/\u221A3)/d\u2082) = 0, so d\u2081 = \u221A3 d\u2082 (d\u2081 from the origin, d\u2082 from (2, 0)).
x\xB2 + y\xB2 = 3[(x \u2212 2)\xB2 + y\xB2] \u2192 2x\xB2 + 2y\xB2 \u2212 12x + 12 = 0 \u2192 (x \u2212 3)\xB2 + y\xB2 = 3.
So the circle has centre (3, 0) and radius R = \u221A3 = 1.73 m.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"evp",st:"Field-potential relation and equipotentials",q:`Two point charges \u2212Q and +{Q;\u221A3} are placed in the xy-plane at the origin (0, 0) and a point (2, 0), respectively, as shown in the figure. This results in an equipotential circle of radius R and potential V = 0 in the xy-plane with its centre at (b, 0). All lengths are measured in metres.
The value of b is ____ metre.`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"x and y axes with charge minus Q at the origin, charge plus Q over root 3 at x = 2, and a dashed circle centred at x = 3 passing around the second charge",d:[["l",20,100,330,100,"a"],["l",40,190,40,10,"a"],["t",326,116,"x","e"],["t",48,14,"y"],["c",40,100,3,"F"],["t",48,92,"\u2212Q"],["c",140,100,3,"F"],["t",126,120,"+Q/\u221A3"],["c",190,100,86.6,"d"]]},n:3,s:`At a point on the V = 0 circle: k(\u2212Q/d\u2081 + (Q/\u221A3)/d\u2082) = 0, so d\u2081 = \u221A3 d\u2082 (d\u2081 from the origin, d\u2082 from (2, 0)).
x\xB2 + y\xB2 = 3[(x \u2212 2)\xB2 + y\xB2] \u2192 2x\xB2 + 2y\xB2 \u2212 12x + 12 = 0 \u2192 (x \u2212 3)\xB2 + y\xB2 = 3.
So the centre is at (3, 0): b = 3 m (and R = \u221A3 m).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"evp",st:"Potential energy and work",q:"A point charge q of mass m is suspended vertically by a string of length l. A point dipole of dipole moment p\u20D7 is now brought towards q from infinity so that the charge moves away. The final equilibrium position of the system including the direction of the dipole, the angles and distances is shown in the figure. If the workdone in bringing the dipole to this position is N \xD7 (mgh), where g is the acceleration due to gravity, then the value of N is ____. (Note that for three coplanar forces keeping a point mass in equilibrium, {F;sin\u03B8} is the same for all forces, where F is any one of the forces and \u03B8 is the angle between the other two forces.)",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Pendulum string of length l hanging from a top point to the dipole position, the string of charge q swung by angle alpha, the charge raised by h above the dipole level and the dipole pointing towards the charge",d:[["l",150,20,150,170,"d"],["l",150,20,265,116],["l",150,170,265,116,"d"],["l",150,170,300,170,"d"],["l",290,170,290,116,"b"],["t",296,148,"h"],["c",265,116,5,"F"],["t",274,108,"q"],["t",140,100,"l","e"],["t",216,56,"l"],["t",158,44,"\u03B1"],["l",150,170,190,151,"aB"],["t",142,186,"p\u20D7","e"],["t",172,190,"distance 2l sin(\u03B1/2)","s"]]},n:2,s:`Forces on q: weight mg (down), tension T (along the string) and the push F of the dipole (along the line joining them, away from the dipole).
The triangle (suspension point, dipole, q) is isosceles with sides l, l and top angle \u03B1. So the distance is r = 2l sin(\u03B1/2) and the line from the dipole to q makes angle \u03B1/2 with the horizontal.
The angle between mg and F is 90\xB0 + \u03B1/2, between T and F also 90\xB0 + \u03B1/2, and between mg and T it is 180\xB0 \u2212 \u03B1. So F/sin\u03B1 = mg/cos(\u03B1/2), giving F = 2mg sin(\u03B1/2).
On the axis of a dipole F = q \xD7 2kp/r\xB3, and the interaction energy is U = kpq/r\xB2 = Fr/2 = (2mg sin(\u03B1/2))(2l sin(\u03B1/2))/2 = 2mgl sin\xB2(\u03B1/2).
The charge rises by h = l(1 \u2212 cos\u03B1) = 2l sin\xB2(\u03B1/2), so U = mgh.
Work done = gain in gravitational energy + interaction energy = mgh + mgh = 2mgh. So N = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : Work done in moving a test charge between two points inside a uniformly charged spherical shell is zero, no matter which path is chosen.
Reason (R) : Electrostatic potential inside a uniformly charged spherical shell is constant and is same as that on the surface of the shell.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["A is false but R is true","A is true but R is false","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A"],c:2,s:`Inside a uniformly charged shell the field is zero, so the potential is constant and equal to the value on the surface: R is true.
Work between two points = q \xD7 (potential difference) = 0, whatever the path, because the potential is the same everywhere inside: A is true.
R is exactly the reason for A: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : Work done by electric field on moving a positive charge on an equipotential surface is always zero.
Reason (R) : Electric lines of force are always perpendicular to equipotential surfaces.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct.","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct."],c:0,s:`On an equipotential surface \u0394V = 0, so the work W = q\u0394V = 0: A is correct.
Field lines are perpendicular to equipotential surfaces (a field component along the surface would make V change along it): R is correct.
Because the force qE is perpendicular to any displacement along the surface, the work is zero. So R explains A: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecn",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements.
Statement I : Electric potential is constant within and at the surface of each conductor.
Statement II : Electric field just outside a charged conductor is perpendicular to the surface of the conductor at every point.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are correct.","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct."],c:0,s:`In equilibrium E = 0 inside a conductor, so no work is needed to move a charge within it: the potential is the same inside and on the surface. Statement I is correct.
If the field just outside had a component along the surface, the surface charges would move. So in equilibrium it is perpendicular to the surface at every point. Statement II is correct.
Both statements are correct: option (a).`},{y:"AIEEE 2012",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`This question has Statement 1 and Statement 2. Of the four choices given after the statements, choose the one that best describes the two statements.
An insulating solid sphere of radius R has a uniformly positive charge density \u03C1. As a result of this uniform charge distribution there is a finite value of electric potential at the centre of the sphere, at the surface of the sphere and also at a point outside the sphere. The electric potential at infinity is zero.
Statement 1 : When a charge q is taken from the centre to the surface of the sphere, its potential energy changes by {q\u03C1;3\u03B5\u2080}.
Statement 2 : The electric field at a distance r (r < R) from the centre of the sphere is {\u03C1r;3\u03B5\u2080}.`,o:["Statement 1 is true, Statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is false, Statement 2 is true.","Statement 1 is true, Statement 2 is the correct explanation of Statement 1.","Statement 1 is true, Statement 2 is true; Statement 2 is not the correct explanation of Statement 1."],c:1,s:`Inside a uniformly charged sphere E = \u03C1r/(3\u03B5\u2080) (Gauss's law), so Statement 2 is true.
Potential difference between the centre and the surface: V(0) \u2212 V(R) = \u222BE dr from 0 to R = \u03C1R\xB2/(6\u03B5\u2080).
So the change in potential energy is q\u03C1R\xB2/(6\u03B5\u2080), not q\u03C1/(3\u03B5\u2080) (which does not even have the dimension of energy). Statement 1 is false.
Statement 1 false, Statement 2 true: option (b).`},{y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Statement-1 : For a charged particle moving from point P to point Q, the net work done by an electrostatic field on the particle is independent of the path connecting point P to point Q.
Statement-2 : The net work done by a conservative force on an object moving along a closed loop is zero.
This question contains Statement-1 and Statement-2. Of the four choices given after the statements, choose the one that best describes the two statements.`,o:["Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is false","Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is true; Statement-2 is the correct explanation of Statement-1","Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is true; Statement-2 is not the correct explanation of Statement-1","Statement-1 is false, Statement-2 is true"],c:2,s:`Statement 1 is true: the electrostatic force is conservative, so the work from P to Q depends only on the end points.
Statement 2 is true: for any conservative force the work around a closed loop is zero.
Statement 2 is a general property of conservative forces. It does not say why the electrostatic force is conservative (that comes from Coulomb's law being a central force), so the official key does not treat it as the explanation of Statement 1.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"ecn",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Statement-1 : For practical purposes, the earth is used as a reference at zero potential in electrical circuits.
Statement-2 : The electrical potential of a sphere of radius R with charge Q uniformly distributed on the surface is given by {Q;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R}.`,o:["Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is false","Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is true; Statement-2 is the correct explanation of Statement-1","Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is true; Statement-2 is not the correct explanation of Statement-1","Statement-1 is false, Statement-2 is true"],c:1,s:`Statement 2 is true: it is the potential of a charged conducting sphere.
For the earth R is very large (about 6400 km), so even a large charge added to or removed from it changes V = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R) by a negligible amount. Its potential stays practically constant, so it can be taken as the zero reference. Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 explains it.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A conducting liquid bubble of radius a and thickness t (t \u226A a) is charged to potential V. If the bubble collapses to a droplet, find the potential on the droplet.",a:"V (a/3t)^(1/3)",s:`Charge on the bubble: Q = 4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080aV. Charge is conserved when it collapses.
Volume of liquid = 4\u03C0a\xB2t. Droplet of radius r: (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3 = 4\u03C0a\xB2t, so r = (3a\xB2t)^(1/3).
Potential of the droplet: V\u2032 = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r) = aV/r = aV/(3a\xB2t)^(1/3).
V\u2032 = V (a/3t)^(1/3).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Two uniformly charged plane sheets S\u2081 and S\u2082 having charge densities \u03C3\u2081 and \u03C3\u2082 (\u03C3\u2081 > \u03C3\u2082) are placed at a distance d parallel to each other. A charge q\u2080 is moved along a line of length a (a < d) at an angle 45\xB0 with the normal to S\u2081. Calculate the workdone by the electric field.",a:"q\u2080(\u03C3\u2081 \u2212 \u03C3\u2082)a/(2\u221A2 \u03B5\u2080)",s:`Each large sheet gives a uniform field \u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080). Between the sheets the two fields partly cancel: E = (\u03C3\u2081 \u2212 \u03C3\u2082)/(2\u03B5\u2080), along the normal.
Only the component of the displacement along the normal counts: a cos45\xB0 = a/\u221A2.
W = q\u2080 E a cos45\xB0 = q\u2080 (\u03C3\u2081 \u2212 \u03C3\u2082)/(2\u03B5\u2080) \xD7 a/\u221A2.
W = q\u2080(\u03C3\u2081 \u2212 \u03C3\u2082)a/(2\u221A2 \u03B5\u2080).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"efl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A charge +Q is fixed at the origin of the co-ordinate system while a small electric dipole of dipole moment p\u20D7 pointing away from the charge along the x-axis is set free from a point far away from the origin.
(a) Calculate the kinetic energy of the dipole when it reaches a point (d, 0).
(b) Calculate the force on the charge +Q at this moment.`,a:"(a) Qp/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB2)   (b) (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(2pQ/d\xB3) \xEE",s:`At (d, 0) the field of +Q is E = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB2) along +x, the same direction as p.
(a) Energy of the dipole there U = \u2212pE = \u2212Qp/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB2), and U = 0 far away. Energy conservation: KE = 0 \u2212 U = Qp/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB2).
(b) The force on the dipole is F = \u2212dU/dd = \u22122Qp/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB3), i.e. it is attracted towards Q (the nearer end of the dipole is negative).
By Newton's third law the force on +Q is 2pQ/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d\xB3) along +x (towards the dipole).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Charges +q and \u2212q are located at the corners of a cube of side a as shown in the figure. Find the work done to separate the charges to infinity.",fig:{w:260,h:230,alt:"Cube with alternating charges plus q and minus q at its eight corners",d:[["r",50,110,100,100],["l",90,70,190,70],["l",190,70,190,170],["l",50,110,90,70],["l",150,110,190,70],["l",150,210,190,170],["l",90,170,50,210,"d"],["l",90,170,190,170,"d"],["l",90,170,90,70,"d"],["c",50,110,2.5,"F"],["c",150,110,2.5,"F"],["c",50,210,2.5,"F"],["c",150,210,2.5,"F"],["c",90,70,2.5,"F"],["c",190,70,2.5,"F"],["c",90,170,2.5,"F"],["c",190,170,2.5,"F"],["t",44,108,"+q","e"],["t",156,124,"\u2212q"],["t",44,222,"\u2212q","e"],["t",154,224,"+q"],["t",84,62,"\u2212q","e"],["t",196,66,"+q"],["t",96,186,"+q"],["t",196,184,"\u2212q"]]},a:"5.824 (q\xB2/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a)",s:`Adjacent corners (opposite signs) and all other pairs: the 28 pairs are 12 edges (distance a, unlike charges), 12 face diagonals (a\u221A2, like charges) and 4 body diagonals (a\u221A3, unlike charges).
Potential energy U = (kq\xB2/a)[\u221212 + 12/\u221A2 \u2212 4/\u221A3] = (kq\xB2/a)(\u221212 + 8.485 \u2212 2.309) = \u22125.824 kq\xB2/a.
To separate all the charges to infinity the work needed is W = \u2212U = 5.824 q\xB2/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Four points charge + 8 \u03BCC, \u2212 1 \u03BCC, \u2212 1 \u03BCC, and + 8 \u03BCC are fixed at the points \u2212\u221A{27;2} m, \u2212\u221A{3;2} m, +\u221A{3;2} m and +\u221A{27;2} m respectively on the y-axis. A particle of mass 6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 kg and charge + 0.1 \u03BCC moves along the x-direction. Its speed at x = + \u221E is v\u2080. Find the least value of v\u2080 for which the particle is at the origin. Find also the kinetic energy of the particle at the origin. Assume that space is gravity free.
Given {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 Nm\xB2/C\xB2.`,a:"v\u2080 = 3 m/s; kinetic energy at the origin \u2248 2.5\xD710\u207B\u2074 J (about 3\xD710\u207B\u2074 J)",s:`At distance x on the x-axis: V(x) = 10\u207B\u2076k[16/\u221A(x\xB2 + 13.5) \u2212 2/\u221A(x\xB2 + 1.5)].
The particle is repelled, so the least v\u2080 is set by the highest point of V on the x-axis. dV/dx = 0 gives 4(x\xB2 + 1.5) = x\xB2 + 13.5, so x\xB2 = 2.5.
There V_max = 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 (16/4 \u2212 2/2) = 2.7\xD710\u2074 V. Energy needed: qV_max = 0.1\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 2.7\xD710\u2074 = 2.7\xD710\u207B\xB3 J = \xBDmv\u2080\xB2, so v\u2080\xB2 = 2 \xD7 2.7\xD710\u207B\xB3/(6\xD710\u207B\u2074) = 9 and v\u2080 = 3 m/s.
At the origin V(0) = 9\xD710\xB3 \xD7 (16/\u221A13.5 \u2212 2/\u221A1.5) = 2.45\xD710\u2074 V, so qV(0) = 2.45\xD710\u207B\xB3 J.
KE at origin = 2.7\xD710\u207B\xB3 \u2212 2.45\xD710\u207B\xB3 \u2248 2.5\xD710\u207B\u2074 J (about 3\xD710\u207B\u2074 J).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A non-conducting disc of radius a and uniform positive surface charge density \u03C3 is placed on the ground, with its axis vertical. A particle of mass m and positive charge q is dropped, along the axis of the disc, from a height H with zero initial velocity. The particle has q/m = {4\u03B5\u2080g;\u03C3}.
(a) Find the value of H if the particle just reaches the disc.
(b) Sketch the potential energy of the particle as a function of its height and find its equilibrium position.`,fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"Graph of potential energy against height: starts at 2mga, dips to a minimum of root 3 mga at height a over root 3, then rises steadily",d:[["l",50,220,320,220,"a"],["l",50,220,50,16,"a"],["t",58,18,"U"],["t",316,236,"z","e"],["p","M50 140 L55 143.6 L62.5 147.5 L78.9 150.7 L100 146.9 L125 135.8 L150 121.1 L200 87 L250 50.2 L275 31"],["l",78.9,150.7,78.9,220,"d"],["t",79,234,"a/\u221A3","ms"],["l",50,150.7,78.9,150.7,"d"],["t",46,168,"\u221A3 mga","e"],["t",46,138,"2mga","e"]]},a:"(a) H = 4a/3   (b) equilibrium (stable) at height a/\u221A3, where the potential energy is \u221A3 mga",s:`Potential on the axis at height z: V(z) = (\u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080)(\u221A(a\xB2 + z\xB2) \u2212 z). Note q\u03C3/(2\u03B5\u2080) = 2mg.
(a) Energy conservation from H to the disc (speed zero at both): mgH = q[V(0) \u2212 V(H)] = 2mg[a \u2212 \u221A(a\xB2 + H\xB2) + H]. This gives 2\u221A(a\xB2 + H\xB2) = 2a + H, so 3H\xB2 = 4aH and H = 4a/3.
(b) Total potential energy U(z) = mgz + qV = mg[2\u221A(a\xB2 + z\xB2) \u2212 z]. U(0) = 2mga, and for large z it grows like mgz.
dU/dz = 0: 2z/\u221A(a\xB2 + z\xB2) = 1, so z = a/\u221A3.
There U = \u221A3 mga, the minimum of the curve: a stable equilibrium at height a/\u221A3.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"ecn",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A conducting sphere S\u2081 of radius r is attached to an insulating handle. Another conducting sphere S\u2082 of radius R is mounted on an insulating stand. S\u2082 is initially uncharged. S\u2081 is given a charge Q, brought into contact with S\u2082, and removed. S\u2081 is recharged such that the charge on it is again Q, and it is again brought into contact with S\u2082 and removed. This procedure is repeated n times.
(a) Find the electrostatic energy of S\u2082 after n such contacts.
(b) What is the limiting value of this energy as n \u2192 \u221E?`,a:"(a) U = (Q\xB2R/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2))[1 \u2212 (R/(R+r))\u207F]\xB2   (b) Q\xB2R/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2)",s:`On contact the two spheres reach the same potential, so the total charge divides in proportion to the radii: S\u2082 gets the fraction R/(R + r).
Let q_n be the charge on S\u2082 after n contacts: q_n = (R/(R + r))(q_(n\u22121) + Q), with q\u2080 = 0. Summing the geometric series gives q_n = (QR/r)[1 \u2212 (R/(R + r))\u207F].
(a) Energy of S\u2082: U = q_n\xB2/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R) = (Q\xB2R/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2))[1 \u2212 (R/(R + r))\u207F]\xB2.
(b) As n \u2192 \u221E the bracket tends to 1: U \u2192 Q\xB2R/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2). (Then S\u2082 is at the potential Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r) of the charged S\u2081 and no more charge flows.)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1993",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A circular ring of radius R with uniform positive charge density \u03BB per unit length is located in the y-z plane with its centre at the origin O. A particle of mass m and positive charge q is projected from the point P(R\u221A3, 0, 0) on the positive x-axis directly towards O, with an initial speed v. Find the smallest (non-zero) value of the speed v such that the particle does not return to P.",a:"\u221A(q\u03BB/(2\u03B5\u2080m))",s:`Potential on the axis of a ring of charge 2\u03C0R\u03BB: V(x) = \u03BBR/(2\u03B5\u2080\u221A(R\xB2 + x\xB2)).
At P (x = \u221A3R): V_P = \u03BBR/(2\u03B5\u2080 \xD7 2R) = \u03BB/(4\u03B5\u2080). At O: V_O = \u03BB/(2\u03B5\u2080), the highest value on the axis.
The positive particle is repelled, so it must climb to O. If it just reaches O it is pushed on beyond it and does not come back.
\xBDmv\xB2 = q(V_O \u2212 V_P) = q\u03BB/(4\u03B5\u2080), so v = \u221A(q\u03BB/(2\u03B5\u2080m)).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`(a) A charge of Q coulomb is uniformly distributed over a spherical volume of radius R metre. Obtain an expression for the energy of the system.
(b) What will be the corresponding expression for the energy needed to completely disassemble the planet earth against the gravitational pull amongst its constituent particles? Assume the earth to be a sphere of uniform mass density. Calculate this energy, given the product of the mass and the radius of the earth to be 2.5 \xD7 10\xB3\xB9 kg/m.
(c) If the same charge Q coulomb as in part (a) above is given to a spherical conductor of the same radius R, what would be the energy of the system?`,a:"(a) 3Q\xB2/(20\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R)   (b) 3GM\xB2/(5R) = (3/5)gMR \u2248 1.5\xD710\xB3\xB2 J   (c) Q\xB2/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R)",s:`(a) Build the sphere layer by layer. Charge inside radius r is Qr\xB3/R\xB3; the layer dr carries 3Qr\xB2dr/R\xB3. dU = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(Qr\xB3/R\xB3)(3Qr\xB2dr/R\xB3)/r = 3Q\xB2r\u2074dr/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\u2076). Integrating from 0 to R: U = 3Q\xB2/(20\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R).
(b) Replace Q\xB2/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) by GM\xB2 (attraction instead of repulsion): the energy needed is 3GM\xB2/(5R). Using GM = gR\xB2: 3GM\xB2/(5R) = (3/5)gMR = 0.6 \xD7 9.8 \xD7 2.5\xD710\xB3\xB9 \u2248 1.5\xD710\xB3\xB2 J.
(c) In a conductor all the charge sits on the surface: U = Q\xB2/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1991",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Two fixed charges \u22122Q and Q are located at the points with coordinates (\u22123a, 0) and (+3a, 0) respectively in the x-y plane.
(a) Show that all points in the x - y plane, where the electric potential due to the two charges is zero, lie on a circle. Find its radius and the location of its centre.
(b) Give the expression V(x) at a general point on the x-axis and sketch the function V(x) on the whole x-axis.
(c) If a particle of charge +q starts from rest at the centre of the circle, show by a short quantitative argument that the particle will eventually cross the circle. Find its speed when it does so.`,fig:{w:340,h:220,alt:"Sketch of V against x: negative and falling to minus infinity at x = -3a, rising through zero at a to plus infinity at 3a, then falling through zero at 9a and tending to zero from below",d:[["l",10,110,330,110,"a"],["t",326,126,"x","e"],["l",130,215,130,5,"a"],["t",136,14,"V"],["l",70,10,70,215,"d"],["l",190,10,190,215,"d"],["p","M12 114 Q55 125 68 212"],["p","M72 212 C75 150 110 118 130 114 C150 108 185 70 188 8"],["p","M192 8 C200 80 250 106 310 110 L335 113"],["t",70,128,"\u22123a","ms"],["t",150,128,"a","ms"],["t",190,128,"3a","ms"],["t",310,128,"9a","ms"]]},a:"(a) circle of radius 4a, centre (5a, 0)   (b) V(x) = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)[\u22122Q/|x+3a| + Q/|x\u22123a|]   (c) the particle crosses the circle at (9a, 0) with speed \u221A(qQ/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080ma))",s:`(a) V = 0 where \u22122Q/d\u2081 + Q/d\u2082 = 0, i.e. d\u2081 = 2d\u2082 (d\u2081 from (\u22123a, 0), d\u2082 from (3a, 0)). Then (x + 3a)\xB2 + y\xB2 = 4[(x \u2212 3a)\xB2 + y\xB2], which simplifies to x\xB2 + y\xB2 \u2212 10ax + 9a\xB2 = 0, i.e. (x \u2212 5a)\xB2 + y\xB2 = (4a)\xB2. A circle of radius 4a with centre (5a, 0).
(b) V(x) = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)[\u22122Q/|x + 3a| + Q/|x \u2212 3a|]. V \u2192 \u2212\u221E at x = \u22123a, V \u2192 +\u221E at x = 3a, V = 0 at x = a and x = 9a, and V \u2192 0 (from below) as x \u2192 \xB1\u221E.
(c) At the centre (5a, 0): V = (Q/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(\u22122/8a + 1/2a) = Q/(16\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a) > 0. The field there is Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)(1/4a\xB2 \u2212 2/64a\xB2) = 7Q/(128\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a\xB2) along +x, so the charge moves along the x-axis. For 3a < x < 9a, V(x) = Q(9a \u2212 x)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080(x\xB2 \u2212 9a\xB2)) is positive and decreasing (dV/dx < 0), reaching 0 at x = 9a, which is on the circle. So the charge keeps speeding up and crosses the circle at (9a, 0).
Energy: \xBDmv\xB2 = q[V(5a) \u2212 0] = qQ/(16\u03C0\u03B5\u2080a), so v = \u221A(qQ/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080ma)), where m is the mass of the particle.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1990",tp:"ecn",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Three concentric spherical metallic shells A, B and C of radii a, b and c (a < b < c) have surface charge densities \u03C3, \u2212\u03C3 and \u03C3 respectively.
(i) Find the potential of the three shells A, B and C.
(ii) If the shells A and C are at the same potential, obtain the relation between the radii a, b and c.`,a:"(i) V_A = \u03C3(a \u2212 b + c)/\u03B5\u2080, V_B = \u03C3[(a\xB2 \u2212 b\xB2)/b + c]/\u03B5\u2080, V_C = \u03C3(a\xB2 \u2212 b\xB2 + c\xB2)/(\u03B5\u2080c)   (ii) c = a + b",s:`Charges: Q_A = 4\u03C0a\xB2\u03C3, Q_B = \u22124\u03C0b\xB2\u03C3, Q_C = 4\u03C0c\xB2\u03C3 (k = 1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080).
V_C = k(Q_A + Q_B + Q_C)/c = \u03C3(a\xB2 \u2212 b\xB2 + c\xB2)/(\u03B5\u2080c).
V_B = k(Q_A + Q_B)/b + kQ_C/c = \u03C3(a\xB2 \u2212 b\xB2)/(\u03B5\u2080b) + \u03C3c/\u03B5\u2080.
V_A = kQ_A/a + kQ_B/b + kQ_C/c = (\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080)(a \u2212 b + c).
(ii) V_A = V_C: a \u2212 b + c = (a\xB2 \u2212 b\xB2 + c\xB2)/c, so c(a \u2212 b) = a\xB2 \u2212 b\xB2 = (a \u2212 b)(a + b). Hence c = a + b.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1987",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Three point charges q, 2q and 8q are to be placed on a 9 cm long straight line. Find the positions where the charges should be placed such that the potential energy of this system is minimum. In this situation, what is the electric field at the position of the charge q due to the other two charges?",a:"2q and 8q at the ends, q between them 3 cm from 2q (6 cm from 8q); the electric field at q is zero",s:`All charges are positive, so the energy is lowest when they are as far apart as possible: 2q and 8q at the two ends, 9 cm apart. Compare which charge goes in the middle: with q in the middle the minimum energy is 3.78 kq\xB2/cm, with 2q in the middle 4.15, with 8q in the middle 5.41 (same units). So q goes in the middle.
Let q be at distance x from 2q: U = kq\xB2[2/x + 8/(9 \u2212 x) + 16/9] (x in cm).
dU/dx = 0: 2/x\xB2 = 8/(9 \u2212 x)\xB2, so 9 \u2212 x = 2x and x = 3 cm.
So q is 3 cm from 2q and 6 cm from 8q.
Field at q: due to 2q it is k\xB72q/3\xB2 and due to 8q it is k\xB78q/6\xB2 = k\xB72q/9 (per cm\xB2), in opposite directions. The net field is zero.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1985",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Two fixed, equal, positive charges, each of magnitude 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 C are located at points A and B separated by a distance of 6 m. An equal and opposite charge moves towards them along the line COD, the perpendicular bisector of the line AB. The moving charge, when it reaches the point C at a distance of 4 m from O, has a kinetic energy of 4 joule. Calculate the distance of the farthest point D which the negative charge will reach before returning towards C.",fig:{w:340,h:220,alt:"Charges plus q at A (top) and B (bottom), 6 m apart, with the moving negative charge on the perpendicular bisector line D O C, C being 4 m from O",d:[["l",170,30,170,190,"d"],["l",40,110,310,110],["c",170,30,4,"F"],["c",170,190,4,"F"],["t",178,34,"A  +q"],["t",178,196,"B  +q"],["t",160,104,"O","e"],["c",290,110,4,"F"],["t",290,98,"C  \u2212q","m"],["t",50,98,"D","m"],["l",210,30,210,190,"b"],["t",216,76,"6 m"],["l",170,128,290,128,"b"],["t",230,146,"4 m","m"]]},a:"8.48 m",s:`Let q = 5\xD710\u207B\u2075 C. A and B are 3 m from O on either side. The moving charge \u2212q at distance y from O has U = \u22122kq\xB2/\u221A(9 + y\xB2).
2kq\xB2 = 2 \xD7 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 (5\xD710\u207B\u2075)\xB2 = 45 J\xB7m.
At C (y = 4): \u221A25 = 5, so U_C = \u221245/5 = \u22129 J. Total energy = 4 \u2212 9 = \u22125 J.
At the farthest point D the KE is zero: \u221245/\u221A(9 + y\xB2) = \u22125, so \u221A(9 + y\xB2) = 9 and y\xB2 = 72.
OD = \u221A72 = 8.48 m.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1981",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A charge Q is distributed over two concentric hollow spheres of radii r and R (> r) such that the surface charge densities are equal. Find the potential at the common centre.",a:"V = Q(r + R)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080(r\xB2 + R\xB2))",s:`Equal surface density \u03C3: q\u2081 = 4\u03C0r\xB2\u03C3 on the inner sphere and q\u2082 = 4\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3 on the outer sphere.
Total Q = 4\u03C0\u03C3(r\xB2 + R\xB2), so \u03C3 = Q/(4\u03C0(r\xB2 + R\xB2)).
Potential at the centre: V = k(q\u2081/r + q\u2082/R) = (\u03C3/\u03B5\u2080)(r + R).
V = Q(r + R)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080(r\xB2 + R\xB2)).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1981",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"The workdone in carrying a point charge from one point to another in an electrostatic field depends on the path along which the point charge is carried.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`The electrostatic force is conservative.
The work done in carrying a charge from A to B equals q(V_B \u2212 V_A) (by the external agent), which depends only on the end points.
So it does not depend on the path: the statement is false.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"The electric potential V at any point x, y, z (all in metre) in space is given by V = 4x\xB2 volt. The electric field at the point (1 m, 0, 2 m) is ____ V/m.",n:-8,s:`E = \u2212\u2207V, so E_x = \u2212\u2202V/\u2202x = \u22128x, and E_y = E_z = 0.
At x = 1 m: E_x = \u22128 V/m.
The field is 8 V/m in the negative x-direction.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A point charge q moves from point P to point S along the path PQRS (figure) in a uniform electric field E\u20D7 pointing parallel to the positive direction of the X-axis. The coordinates of the points P, Q, R and S are (a, b, 0), (2a, 0, 0), (a, \u2212b, 0) and (0, 0, 0) respectively. The workdone by the field in the above process is given by the expression ____.",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Path P to Q to R to S in a uniform field pointing along the positive x-axis, with S at the origin, P at (a, b), Q at (2a, 0) and R at (a, -b)",d:[["l",20,15,320,15,"a"],["l",20,70,320,70,"a"],["l",20,110,320,110,"a"],["l",20,165,320,165,"a"],["l",20,90,330,90,"a"],["l",60,175,60,5,"a"],["t",326,86,"X","e"],["t",66,12,"Y"],["l",140,50,220,90],["l",220,90,140,130],["l",140,130,60,90],["t",134,44,"P","e"],["t",226,86,"Q"],["t",134,146,"R","e"],["t",52,104,"S","e"],["t",300,60,"E\u20D7"]]},a:"\u2212qEa",s:`The field is uniform and the electrostatic force is conservative, so the work depends only on the end points P and S.
Displacement from P to S along the field direction: \u0394x = 0 \u2212 a = \u2212a.
W = qE \xD7 \u0394x = \u2212qEa.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"evp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Figure shows lines of constant potential in a region in which an electric field is present. The values of the potential are written in brackets. Of the points A, B and C, the magnitude of the electric field is greatest at the point ____.",fig:{w:280,h:170,alt:"Four curved equipotential lines labelled 50 V, 40 V, 30 V and 10 V, with points A, B and C; the lines are closest together near B",d:[["p","M50 20 Q130 80 50 140"],["p","M100 20 Q190 80 100 140"],["p","M150 20 Q250 80 150 140"],["p","M200 20 Q260 80 200 140"],["t",50,14,"50 V","ms"],["t",100,14,"40 V","ms"],["t",150,14,"30 V","ms"],["t",200,14,"10 V","ms"],["c",156,45,2.5,"F"],["t",162,43,"A"],["c",215,80,2.5,"F"],["t",221,78,"B"],["c",192,125,2.5,"F"],["t",198,123,"C"]]},o:["A","B","C"],c:1,s:`The field is E = \u2212dV/dr: its magnitude is the potential difference divided by the gap between neighbouring equipotential lines. So E is largest where the lines are closest together.
In the figure the lines are closest around B (between 30 V and 10 V), and further apart near A and C.
The field is greatest at B.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor is filled equally (half) with two dielectrics of dielectric constants \u03B5\u2081 and \u03B5\u2082, as shown in figures. The distance between the plates is d and area of each plate is A. If capacitance in first configuration and second configuration are C\u2081 and C\u2082 respectively, then {C\u2081;C\u2082} is",fig:{w:340,h:260,alt:"First configuration: capacitor with two dielectric layers eps2 above eps1 stacked between the plates. Second configuration: two dielectrics eps2 and eps1 side by side between the plates",d:[["t",20,16,"First configuration","s"],["l",170,22,170,44],["l",80,44,260,44,"B"],["r",80,44,180,30],["l",80,74,260,74,"d"],["t",170,64,"\u03B5\u2082","m"],["t",170,94,"\u03B5\u2081","m"],["l",80,104,260,104,"B"],["l",170,104,170,126],["t",20,146,"Second configuration","s"],["l",170,152,170,174],["l",80,174,260,174,"B"],["r",80,174,90,60],["r",170,174,90,60],["t",125,208,"\u03B5\u2082","m"],["t",215,208,"\u03B5\u2081","m"],["l",80,234,260,234,"B"],["l",170,234,170,254]]},o:["{\u03B5\u2081\u03B5\u2082\xB2;(\u03B5\u2081 + \u03B5\u2082)\xB2}","{\u03B5\u2081\u03B5\u2082;\u03B5\u2081 + \u03B5\u2082}","{\u03B5\u2080(\u03B5\u2081 + \u03B5\u2082);2}","{4\u03B5\u2081\u03B5\u2082;(\u03B5\u2081 + \u03B5\u2082)\xB2}"],c:3,s:`First configuration: two layers, each of thickness d/2 and area A, are in series.
C\u2081 = \u03B5\u2080A/[(d/2)/\u03B5\u2081 + (d/2)/\u03B5\u2082] = 2\u03B5\u2080A\u03B5\u2081\u03B5\u2082/[d(\u03B5\u2081 + \u03B5\u2082)].
Second configuration: two halves of area A/2 side by side are in parallel: C\u2082 = \u03B5\u2080(A/2)\u03B5\u2081/d + \u03B5\u2080(A/2)\u03B5\u2082/d = \u03B5\u2080A(\u03B5\u2081 + \u03B5\u2082)/(2d).
C\u2081/C\u2082 = [2\u03B5\u2081\u03B5\u2082/(\u03B5\u2081 + \u03B5\u2082)] \xD7 [2/(\u03B5\u2081 + \u03B5\u2082)] = 4\u03B5\u2081\u03B5\u2082/(\u03B5\u2081 + \u03B5\u2082)\xB2: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Using a battery, a 100 pF capacitor is charged to 60 V and then the battery is removed. After that, a second uncharged capacitor is connected to the first capacitor in parallel. If the final voltage across the second capacitor is 20 V, its capacitance is (in pF)",o:["100","600","200","400"],c:2,s:`With the battery removed, the charge is fixed: Q = CV = 100 pF \xD7 60 V = 6000 pC.
In parallel the capacitors share this charge at a common voltage of 20 V: (100 + C\u2082) \xD7 20 = 6000.
100 + C\u2082 = 300, so C\u2082 = 200 pF: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Three parallel plate capacitors C\u2081, C\u2082 and C\u2083 each of capacitance 5 \u03BCF are connected as shown in figure. The effective capacitance between points A and B, when the space between the parallel plates of C\u2081 capacitor is filled with a dielectric medium having dielectric constant of 4, is",fig:{w:300,h:160,alt:"Circuit between A and B: C1 and C2 in series along the top wire, C3 on a lower wire in parallel with them",d:[["l",20,50,98,50],["l",98,38,98,62],["l",90,38,90,62],["l",98,50,170,50],["l",170,38,170,62],["l",178,38,178,62],["l",178,50,255,50],["l",45,50,45,120],["l",45,120,130,120],["l",130,108,130,132],["l",138,108,138,132],["l",138,120,255,120],["l",255,50,255,120],["c",45,50,2.5,"F"],["c",255,50,2.5,"F"],["t",14,54,"A","e"],["t",262,54,"B"],["t",94,30,"C\u2081","m"],["t",174,30,"C\u2082","m"],["t",134,102,"C\u2083","m"]]},o:["7.5 \u03BCF","30 \u03BCF","9 \u03BCF","22.5 \u03BCF"],c:2,s:`With the dielectric (K = 4), C\u2081 becomes 4 \xD7 5 = 20 \u03BCF.
C\u2081 and C\u2082 are in series: (20 \xD7 5)/(20 + 5) = 4 \u03BCF.
This series pair is in parallel with C\u2083: C_eff = 4 + 5 = 9 \u03BCF: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel-plate capacitor of capacitance 40 \xB5F is connected to a 100 V power supply. Now the intermediate space between the plates is filled with a dielectric material of dielectric constant K = 2. Due to the introduction of dielectric material, the extra charge and the change in the electrostatic energy in the capacitor, respectively, are",o:["4 mC and 0.2 J","2 mC and 0.2 J","8 mC and 2.0 J","2 mC and 0.4 J"],c:0,s:`The battery stays connected, so V = 100 V stays fixed.
Initial charge Q\u2081 = CV = 40 \xB5F \xD7 100 V = 4 mC.
With the dielectric, C' = KC = 80 \xB5F, so Q\u2082 = 80 \xB5F \xD7 100 V = 8 mC.
Extra charge = 8 \u2212 4 = 4 mC.
Change in energy = \xBD(C' \u2212 C)V\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 40\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10\u2074 = 0.2 J.
Answer: 4 mC and 0.2 J, option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Which one of the following is the correct dimensional formula for the capacitance in F? M, L, T and C stand for unit of mass, length, time and charge.",o:["[F] = [C\xB2M\u207B\xB9L\u207B\xB2T\xB2]","[F] = [CM\u207B\xB2L\u207B\xB2T\u207B\xB2]","[F] = [CM\u207B\xB9L\u207B\xB2T\xB2]","[F] = [C\xB2M\u207B\xB2L\xB2T\xB2]"],c:0,s:`Capacitance = charge/potential and potential = work/charge, so capacitance = (charge)\xB2/work.
Dimensions of work = M L\xB2 T\u207B\xB2.
[F] = C\xB2 / (M L\xB2 T\u207B\xB2) = [C\xB2 M\u207B\xB9 L\u207B\xB2 T\xB2].
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"Consider a parallel plate capacitor of area A (of each plate) and separation 'd' between the plates. If E is the electric field and \u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of free space between the plates, then potential energy stored in the capacitor is",o:["{1;2}\u03B5\u2080E\xB2Ad","\u03B5\u2080E\xB2Ad","{3;4}\u03B5\u2080E\xB2Ad","{1;4}\u03B5\u2080E\xB2Ad"],c:0,s:`The energy stored per unit volume in an electric field is \xBD\u03B5\u2080E\xB2.
The field fills the volume between the plates, which is A \xD7 d.
U = \xBD\u03B5\u2080E\xB2 \xD7 Ad.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:`A parallel plate capacitor was made with two rectangular plates, each with a length of l = 3 cm and breadth of b = 1 cm. The distance between the plates is 3 \xB5m. Out of the following, which are the ways to increase the capacitance by a factor of 10?
A. l = 30 cm, b = 1 cm, d = 1 \xB5m
B. l = 3 cm, b = 1 cm, d = 30 \xB5m
C. l = 6 cm, b = 5 cm, d = 3 \xB5m
D. l = 1 cm, b = 1 cm, d = 10 \xB5m
E. l = 5 cm, b = 2 cm, d = 1 \xB5m
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["C only","B and D only","A only","C and E only"],c:3,s:`C = \u03B5\u2080lb/d, so compare lb/d (in cm\xB2 per \xB5m).
Original: 3 \xD7 1 / 3 = 1.
A: 30 \xD7 1 / 1 = 30 (30 times, no).
B: 3 \xD7 1 / 30 = 0.1 (no).  D: 1 \xD7 1 / 10 = 0.1 (no).
C: 6 \xD7 5 / 3 = 10 (yes).  E: 5 \xD7 2 / 1 = 10 (yes).
Answer: C and E only, option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A parallel plate capacitor of capacitance 1 \xB5F is charged to a potential difference of 20 V. The distance between plates is 1 \xB5m. The energy density between plates of capacitor is",o:["2 \xD7 10\xB2 J/m\xB3","1.8 \xD7 10\xB3 J/m\xB3","2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J/m\xB3","1.8 \xD7 10\u2075 J/m\xB3"],c:1,s:`Electric field between the plates E = V/d = 20 / 10\u207B\u2076 = 2\xD710\u2077 V/m.
Energy density u = \xBD\u03B5\u2080E\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 (2\xD710\u2077)\xB2.
u = \xBD \xD7 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 4\xD710\xB9\u2074 \u2248 1.8\xD710\xB3 J/m\xB3.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A capacitor, C\u2081 = 6 \xB5F is charged to a potential difference of V\u2080 = 5 V using a 5 V battery. The battery is removed and another capacitor, C\u2082 = 12 \xB5F is inserted in place of the battery (C\u2081 and C\u2082 are joined through a switch S). When the switch 'S' is closed, the charge flows between the capacitors for some time until equilibrium condition is reached. What are the charges (q\u2081 and q\u2082) on the capacitors C\u2081 and C\u2082 when equilibrium condition is reached?",o:["q\u2081 = 20 \xB5C, q\u2082 = 10 \xB5C","q\u2081 = 15 \xB5C, q\u2082 = 30 \xB5C","q\u2081 = 10 \xB5C, q\u2082 = 20 \xB5C","q\u2081 = 30 \xB5C, q\u2082 = 15 \xB5C"],c:2,s:`Charge on C\u2081 at first: Q = C\u2081V\u2080 = 6 \xD7 5 = 30 \xB5C.
Once the switch closes, this total charge of 30 \xB5C is shared and both capacitors reach the same potential V.
V = 30 / (6 + 12) = 5/3 V.
q\u2081 = 6 \xD7 5/3 = 10 \xB5C and q\u2082 = 12 \xD7 5/3 = 20 \xB5C.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A capacitor has air as dielectric medium and two conducting plates of area 12 cm\xB2 and they are 0.6 cm apart. When a slab of dielectric having area 12 cm\xB2 and 0.6 cm thickness is inserted between the plates, one of the conducting plates has to be moved by 0.2 cm to keep the capacitance same as in previous case. The dielectric constant of the slab is (Given \u03B5\u2080 = 8.834 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 F/m)",o:["1.50","1","0.66","1.33"],c:0,s:`Original capacitance C = \u03B5\u2080A / 0.6 cm.
The slab fills the 0.6 cm gap and raises C, so to bring C back one plate is moved away by 0.2 cm. New gap = 0.8 cm.
C' = \u03B5\u2080A / [(0.8 \u2212 0.6) + 0.6/K] = \u03B5\u2080A / (0.2 + 0.6/K).
Set C' = C: 0.2 + 0.6/K = 0.6, so 0.6/K = 0.4.
K = 1.5. Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"A capacitor is made of a flat plate of area A and a second plate having a stair-like structure as shown in figure. If the area of each stair is {A;3} and the height of each stair is d, the capacitance of the arrangement is",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"A flat bottom plate of area A and a top plate in three steps, each of area A/3, with gaps d, 2d and 3d above the flat plate",d:[["l",40,140,300,140,"B"],["p","M40 120 H127 V100 H214 V80 H300","B"],["t",83,113,"A/3","ms"],["t",170,93,"A/3","ms"],["t",257,73,"A/3","ms"],["l",30,120,30,140,"b"],["t",24,134,"d","e"],["l",118,120,118,100,"b"],["t",112,114,"d","e"],["l",205,100,205,80,"b"],["t",199,94,"d","e"],["t",170,160,"A","m"]]},o:["{11\u03B5\u2080A;20d}","{18\u03B5\u2080A;11d}","{11\u03B5\u2080A;18d}","{13\u03B5\u2080A;17d}"],c:2,s:`The three stairs act as three capacitors in parallel, each of area A/3.
Their gaps from the flat plate are d, 2d and 3d.
C = \u03B5\u2080(A/3)(1/d + 1/2d + 1/3d) = (\u03B5\u2080A/3d)(6 + 3 + 2)/6.
C = (\u03B5\u2080A/3d)(11/6) = 11\u03B5\u2080A/18d.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"Two identical capacitors have same capacitance C. One of them is charged to the potential V and other to the potential 2V. The negative ends of both are connected together. When the positive ends are also joined together, the decrease in energy of the combined system is",o:["{1;2}CV\xB2","2CV\xB2","{3;4}CV\xB2","{1;4}CV\xB2"],c:3,s:`Charges: Q\u2081 = CV and Q\u2082 = 2CV. Joined like to like, so total charge = 3CV on total capacitance 2C.
Common potential V' = 3CV / 2C = 1.5V.
Initial energy = \xBDCV\xB2 + \xBDC(2V)\xB2 = 2.5CV\xB2.
Final energy = \xBD(2C)(1.5V)\xB2 = 2.25CV\xB2.
Decrease = 2.5CV\xB2 \u2212 2.25CV\xB2 = \xBCCV\xB2.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"The equivalent capacitance of the combination shown is",fig:{w:340,h:140,alt:"Two terminals; a capacitor C in the top branch and one in the bottom branch near the left, and two capacitors C in vertical branches that join the top and bottom wires on the right side",d:[["l",60,20,60,110],["l",60,20,96,20],["l",96,12,96,28],["l",104,12,104,28],["l",104,20,280,20],["l",60,110,96,110],["l",96,102,96,118],["l",104,102,104,118],["l",104,110,280,110],["l",280,20,280,110],["l",20,65,60,65],["c",20,65,3,"F"],["l",280,65,320,65],["c",320,65,3,"F"],["l",170,20,170,55],["l",162,55,178,55],["l",162,63,178,63],["l",170,63,170,110],["t",182,63,"C","s"],["l",230,20,230,55],["l",222,55,238,55],["l",222,63,238,63],["l",230,63,230,110],["t",242,63,"C","s"],["t",100,11,"C","ms"],["t",100,128,"C","ms"]]},o:["{C;2}","{5;3}C","2C","4C"],c:2,s:`Look at the two end terminals. The top C and the bottom C each connect the left rail to the right rail, so they are in parallel.
The two middle capacitors join the top wire to the bottom wire. Both of these wires are joined to the right rail, so both plates of each middle capacitor are at the same potential: they are shorted and carry no charge.
C_eq = C + C = 2C.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"The distance between two plates of a capacitor is d and its capacitance is C\u2081, when air is the medium between the plates. If a metal sheet of thickness {2d;3} and of the same area as plate is introduced between the plates, the capacitance of the capacitor becomes C\u2082. The ratio {C\u2082;C\u2081} is",o:["1 : 1","2 : 1","3 : 1","4 : 1"],c:2,s:`Originally C\u2081 = \u03B5\u2080A/d.
A metal sheet of thickness t reduces the effective gap to d \u2212 t.
New gap = d \u2212 2d/3 = d/3.
C\u2082 = \u03B5\u2080A/(d/3) = 3\u03B5\u2080A/d = 3C\u2081.
C\u2082 : C\u2081 = 3 : 1. Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A parallel plate capacitor of capacitance 2 F is charged to a potential V. The energy stored in the capacitor is E\u2081. The capacitor is now connected to another uncharged identical capacitor in parallel combination. The energy stored in the combination is E\u2082. The ratio E\u2082/E\u2081 is",o:["2 : 1","1 : 2","1 : 4","2 : 3"],c:1,s:`Initially Q = CV and E\u2081 = Q\xB2/2C.
After joining the identical uncharged capacitor in parallel, the charge Q is shared, total capacitance = 2C.
E\u2082 = Q\xB2/(2 \xD7 2C) = E\u2081/2.
E\u2082 : E\u2081 = 1 : 2. Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"egs",st:"Electric flux",q:"A capacitor of capacitance C is charged to a potential V. The flux of the electric field through a closed surface enclosing the positive plate of the capacitor is",o:["Zero","{CV;\u03B5\u2080}","{CV;2\u03B5\u2080}","{2CV;\u03B5\u2080}"],c:1,s:`By Gauss's law, the flux through a closed surface = (charge enclosed)/\u03B5\u2080.
The surface encloses only the positive plate, which carries Q = CV.
Flux = CV/\u03B5\u2080.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor has plate area 40 cm\xB2 and plates separation 2 mm. The space between the plates is filled with a dielectric medium of a thickness 1 mm and dielectric constant 5. The capacitance of the system is",o:["{10;3}\u03B5\u2080F","{3;10}\u03B5\u2080F","24\u03B5\u2080F","10\u03B5\u2080F"],c:0,s:`For a slab of thickness t in a gap d: C = \u03B5\u2080A / (d \u2212 t + t/K).
A = 40\xD710\u207B\u2074 m\xB2, d = 2\xD710\u207B\xB3 m, t = 10\u207B\xB3 m, K = 5.
Denominator = (2 \u2212 1 + 1/5)\xD710\u207B\xB3 = 1.2\xD710\u207B\xB3 m.
C = \u03B5\u2080 \xD7 40\xD710\u207B\u2074 / (1.2\xD710\u207B\xB3) = (10/3)\u03B5\u2080 F.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2023",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A container has a base of 50 cm \xD7 5 cm and height 50 cm. It has two parallel electrically conducting walls each of area 50 cm \xD7 50 cm (these are the large faces, 5 cm apart). The remaining walls of the container are thin and non-conducting. The container is being filled with a liquid of dielectric constant 3 at a uniform rate of 250 cm\xB3 s\u207B\xB9. What is the value of the capacitance of the container after 10 seconds? [Given: Permittivity of free space \u03B5\u2080 = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2 N\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB2, the effects of the non-conducting walls on the capacitance are negligible]",o:["27 pF","63 pF","81 pF","135 pF"],c:1,s:`Volume of liquid after 10 s = 250 \xD7 10 = 2500 cm\xB3.
Base area = 50 \xD7 5 = 250 cm\xB2, so the liquid height h = 2500/250 = 10 cm.
The two conducting walls form two capacitors side by side (parallel): liquid part of area 50 \xD7 10 = 500 cm\xB2 and air part of area 50 \xD7 40 = 2000 cm\xB2. Gap = 5 cm.
C = \u03B5\u2080(K \xD7 500 + 2000)\xD710\u207B\u2074 / 0.05 = 9\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 (3500\xD710\u207B\u2074)/0.05 = 9\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 7.
C = 63 pF. Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"A condenser of 2 \xB5F capacitance is charged steadily from 0 to 5 C. Which of the following graph represents correctly the variation of potential difference (V) across its plates with respect to the charge (Q) on the condenser?",fig:{w:340,h:250,alt:"Four small V versus Q graphs: (a) straight line to 2.5 at Q=5, (b) curve bending upward to 5 at Q=5, (c) straight line to 5 at Q=5, (d) curve bending over and flattening at 2.5",d:[["t",4,24,"(a)"],["l",30,100,130,100,"a"],["l",30,100,30,28,"a"],["t",34,23,"V (\xD710\u2076 V)","s"],["t",134,114,"Q(C)","s"],["t",30,112,"0","ms"],["t",115,112,"5","ms"],["t",26,74,"2.5","es"],["t",26,44,"5","es"],["l",115,97,115,103],["l",30,100,115,70,"B"],["l",30,70,115,70,"d"],["l",115,70,115,100,"d"],["t",164,24,"(b)"],["l",190,100,290,100,"a"],["l",190,100,190,28,"a"],["t",194,23,"V (\xD710\u2076 V)","s"],["t",294,114,"Q(C)","s"],["t",190,112,"0","ms"],["t",275,112,"5","ms"],["t",186,74,"2.5","es"],["t",186,44,"5","es"],["l",275,97,275,103],["p","M190 100 Q255 96 275 40","B"],["l",190,40,275,40,"d"],["l",275,40,275,100,"d"],["t",4,144,"(c)"],["l",30,220,130,220,"a"],["l",30,220,30,148,"a"],["t",34,143,"V (\xD710\u2076 V)","s"],["t",134,234,"Q(C)","s"],["t",30,232,"0","ms"],["t",115,232,"5","ms"],["t",26,194,"2.5","es"],["t",26,164,"5","es"],["l",115,217,115,223],["l",30,220,115,160,"B"],["l",30,160,115,160,"d"],["l",115,160,115,220,"d"],["t",164,144,"(d)"],["l",190,220,290,220,"a"],["l",190,220,190,148,"a"],["t",194,143,"V (\xD710\u2076 V)","s"],["t",294,234,"Q(C)","s"],["t",190,232,"0","ms"],["t",275,232,"5","ms"],["t",186,194,"2.5","es"],["t",186,164,"5","es"],["l",275,217,275,223],["p","M190 220 Q198 190 275 190","B"],["l",190,190,275,190,"d"],["l",275,190,275,220,"d"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:0,s:`For a capacitor V = Q/C, so V is directly proportional to Q: the graph should be a straight line through the origin.
At Q = 5 C, V = 5 / (2\xD710\u207B\u2076) = 2.5\xD710\u2076 V.
A straight line from the origin to 2.5 (\xD710\u2076 V) at Q = 5 C is the line drawn in graph (a).
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Capacitance of an isolated conducting sphere of radius R\u2081 becomes n times when it is enclosed by a concentric conducting sphere of radius R\u2082 connected to earth. The ratio of their radii ({R\u2082;R\u2081}) is",o:["{n;n\u22121}","{2n;2n+1}","{n+1;n}","{2n+1;n}"],c:0,s:`Isolated sphere: C = 4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\u2081.
With the earthed outer shell: C' = 4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R\u2081R\u2082/(R\u2082 \u2212 R\u2081).
C' = nC gives R\u2082/(R\u2082 \u2212 R\u2081) = n, so R\u2082 = nR\u2082 \u2212 nR\u2081.
R\u2082(n \u2212 1) = nR\u2081, so R\u2082/R\u2081 = n/(n \u2212 1).
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"The total charge on the system of capacitance C\u2081 = 1 \xB5F, C\u2082 = 2 \xB5F, C\u2083 = 4 \xB5F and C\u2084 = 3 \xB5F connected in parallel is (Assume a battery of 20 V is connected to the combination)",o:["200 \xB5C","200 C","10 \xB5C","10 C"],c:0,s:`In parallel the capacitances add: C = 1 + 2 + 4 + 3 = 10 \xB5F.
Total charge Q = CV = 10 \xB5F \xD7 20 V = 200 \xB5C.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A source of potential difference V is connected to the combination of two identical capacitors as shown in the figure. When key K is closed, the total energy stored across the combination is E\u2081. Now key K is opened and dielectric of dielectric constant 5 is introduced between the plates of the two capacitors. The total energy stored across the combination is now E\u2082. The ratio E\u2081/E\u2082 will be",fig:{w:340,h:165,alt:"A source V on the left, a key K in the top wire, and two identical capacitors C in parallel branches; the key sits between the middle branch and the right branch",d:[["l",60,30,170,30],["l",170,30,205,16],["c",170,30,2,"F"],["c",212,30,2],["l",212,30,260,30],["c",140,30,2,"F"],["l",140,30,140,72],["l",132,72,148,72],["l",132,80,148,80],["l",140,80,140,140],["l",260,30,260,72],["l",252,72,268,72],["l",252,80,268,80],["l",260,80,260,140],["l",60,140,260,140],["c",140,140,2,"F"],["l",60,30,60,80],["l",48,80,72,80],["l",54,88,66,88,"B"],["l",60,88,60,140],["t",44,88,"V","e"],["t",190,10,"K","m"],["t",152,80,"C"],["t",272,80,"C"]]},o:["{1;10}","{2;5}","{5;13}","{5;26}"],c:2,s:`K closed: both capacitors are across V, so E\u2081 = \xBD(2C)V\xB2 = CV\xB2.
K opened: the capacitor in the middle branch stays connected to the source (its V is fixed). The other one is cut off and keeps its charge CV.
Connected one with K = 5: C' = 5C at voltage V, energy = \xBD(5C)V\xB2 = 2.5CV\xB2.
Isolated one: charge CV fixed, C' = 5C, energy = (CV)\xB2/(2 \xD7 5C) = 0.1CV\xB2.
E\u2082 = 2.6CV\xB2 = 13CV\xB2/5, so E\u2081/E\u2082 = 5/13.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"Two capacitors, each having capacitance 40 \xB5F are connected in series. The space between one of the capacitors is filled with dielectric material of dielectric constant K such that the equivalent capacitance of the system became 24 \xB5F. The value of K will be",o:["1.5","2.5","1.2","3"],c:0,s:`The capacitor with the dielectric has capacitance 40K \xB5F.
Series: (40 \xD7 40K)/(40 + 40K) = 24.
1600K = 24(40 + 40K) = 960 + 960K.
640K = 960, so K = 1.5.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A slab of dielectric constant K has the same cross-sectional area as the plates of a parallel plate capacitor and thickness {3;4}d, where d is the separation of the plates. The capacitance of the capacitor when the slab is inserted between the plates will be (Given C\u2080 = capacitance of capacitor with air as medium between plates.)",o:["{4KC\u2080;3+K}","{3KC\u2080;3+K}","{3+K;4KC\u2080}","{K;4+K}"],c:0,s:`With a slab of thickness t: C = \u03B5\u2080A / (d \u2212 t + t/K).
t = 3d/4: denominator = d/4 + 3d/(4K) = d(K + 3)/(4K).
C = 4K\u03B5\u2080A / (d(K + 3)) = 4KC\u2080/(3 + K), since C\u2080 = \u03B5\u2080A/d.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Two identical thin metal plates have charge q\u2081 and q\u2082 respectively such that q\u2081 > q\u2082. The plates were brought close to each other to form a parallel plate capacitor of capacitance C. The potential difference between them is",o:["{q\u2081+q\u2082;C}","{q\u2081\u2212q\u2082;C}","{q\u2081\u2212q\u2082;2C}","{2(q\u2081\u2212q\u2082);C}"],c:2,s:`When the plates are brought close, the charges rearrange. Each outer face carries (q\u2081 + q\u2082)/2.
The inner faces then carry +(q\u2081 \u2212 q\u2082)/2 and \u2212(q\u2081 \u2212 q\u2082)/2.
So the charge on the capacitor is Q = (q\u2081 \u2212 q\u2082)/2.
V = Q/C = (q\u2081 \u2212 q\u2082)/2C.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:`A parallel plate capacitor is formed by two plates each of area 30\u03C0 cm\xB2, separated by 1 mm. A material of dielectric strength 3.6 \xD7 10\u2077 V m\u207B\xB9 is filled between the plates. If the maximum charge that can be stored on the capacitor without causing any dielectric breakdown is 7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C, the value of the dielectric constant of the material is
[Use 1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\xB2 C\u207B\xB2]`,o:["1.66","1.75","2.25","2.33"],c:3,s:`Field inside the dielectric E = \u03C3/(K\u03B5\u2080) = Q/(K\u03B5\u2080A). At breakdown E = 3.6\xD710\u2077 V/m, so K = Q/(\u03B5\u2080AE).
\u03B5\u2080 = 1/(4\u03C0 \xD7 9\xD710\u2079), A = 30\u03C0\xD710\u207B\u2074 m\xB2, so \u03B5\u2080A = 30\u03C0\xD710\u207B\u2074/(36\u03C0\xD710\u2079) = 8.33\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2074.
K = 7\xD710\u207B\u2076 / (8.33\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2074 \xD7 3.6\xD710\u2077) = 7\xD710\u207B\u2076 / (3\xD710\u207B\u2076) = 2.33.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"If the charge on a capacitor is increased by 2 C, the energy stored in it increases by 44%. The original charge on the capacitor is (in C)",o:["10","20","30","40"],c:0,s:`Energy U = Q\xB2/2C, so U is proportional to Q\xB2.
(Q + 2)\xB2/Q\xB2 = 1.44, so (Q + 2)/Q = 1.2.
Q + 2 = 1.2Q, so 0.2Q = 2 and Q = 10 C.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"Two metallic plates form a parallel plate capacitor. The distance between the plates is 'd'. A metal sheet of thickness {d;2} and of area equal to area of each plate is introduced between the plates. What will be the ratio of the new capacitance to the original capacitance of the capacitor?",o:["2 : 1","1 : 2","1 : 4","4 : 1"],c:0,s:`Original C = \u03B5\u2080A/d.
A metal sheet of thickness d/2 cuts the effective gap to d \u2212 d/2 = d/2.
New C = \u03B5\u2080A/(d/2) = 2\u03B5\u2080A/d.
Ratio new : original = 2 : 1.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"Two capacitors having capacitance C\u2081 and C\u2082 respectively are connected through a switch S. Initially, capacitor C\u2081 is charged to a potential difference V volt by a battery. The battery is then removed and the charged capacitor C\u2081 is now connected to uncharged capacitor C\u2082 by closing the switch S. The amount of charge on the capacitor C\u2082, after equilibrium, is",o:["{C\u2081C\u2082;C\u2081+C\u2082}V","{C\u2081+C\u2082;C\u2081C\u2082}V","(C\u2081 + C\u2082)V","(C\u2081 \u2212 C\u2082)V"],c:0,s:`Initial charge on C\u2081 is Q = C\u2081V.
After S is closed the charge is shared, and both capacitors reach the same potential V'.
V' = Q/(C\u2081 + C\u2082) = C\u2081V/(C\u2081 + C\u2082).
Charge on C\u2082 = C\u2082V' = C\u2081C\u2082V/(C\u2081 + C\u2082).
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Three capacitors of capacitance 10 \xB5F, 15 \xB5F and 20 \xB5F are connected in series across a 13 V battery. The charge on the capacitor of capacitance 15 \xB5F is",o:["60 \xB5C","130 \xB5C","260 \xB5C","585 \xB5C"],c:0,s:`In series, 1/C = 1/10 + 1/15 + 1/20 = (6 + 4 + 3)/60 = 13/60, so C = 60/13 \xB5F.
Charge from the battery Q = CV = (60/13) \xD7 13 = 60 \xB5C.
In series every capacitor carries the same charge, so the 15 \xB5F capacitor has 60 \xB5C.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor with plate area A and plate separation d = 2 m has a capacitance of 4 \xB5F. The new capacitance of the system if half of the space between them is filled with a dielectric material of dielectric constant K = 3 (as shown in figure) will be",fig:{w:340,h:185,alt:"Two vertical plates separated by d; a dielectric slab K = 3 of thickness d/2 touches the right plate and fills its full height",d:[["l",100,20,100,140,"B"],["l",240,20,240,140,"B"],["r",170,20,70,120,"f"],["l",170,20,170,140],["t",205,85,"K = 3","m"],["l",100,158,240,158,"b"],["t",170,176,"d","m"]]},o:["2 \xB5F","32 \xB5F","6 \xB5F","8 \xB5F"],c:2,s:`The slab fills half the gap (thickness d/2) over the full plate area, so the system is two capacitors in series.
Air part: gap d/2, so C\u2081 = 2 \xD7 4 = 8 \xB5F.
Slab part: gap d/2 with K = 3, so C\u2082 = 3 \xD7 8 = 24 \xB5F.
C = C\u2081C\u2082/(C\u2081 + C\u2082) = 8 \xD7 24 / 32 = 6 \xB5F.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A force of 10 N acts on a charged particle placed between two plates of a charged capacitor. If one plate of capacitor is removed, then the force acting on that particle will be",o:["5 N","10 N","20 N","Zero"],c:0,s:`Each plate is a large sheet that produces a field \u03C3/2\u03B5\u2080. Between the plates the two fields add to \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080.
If one plate is removed, only one sheet is left and the field becomes half.
Force = qE, so the force also becomes half: 10/2 = 5 N.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A parallel plate capacitor filled with a medium of dielectric constant 10, is connected across a battery and is charged. The dielectric slab is replaced by another slab of dielectric constant 15. Then the energy of capacitor will",o:["increase by 50%","decrease by 15%","increase by 25%","increase by 33%"],c:0,s:`The battery stays connected, so V is constant.
Energy U = \xBDCV\xB2 is proportional to C, and C is proportional to K.
U'/U = 15/10 = 1.5.
So the energy increases by 50%.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A capacitor is connected to a 20 V battery through a resistance of 10 \u03A9. It is found that the potential difference across the capacitor rises to 2 V in 1 \xB5s. The capacitance of the capacitor is ______ \xB5F. (Given ln(10/9) = 0.105)",o:["0.95","9.52","1.85","0.105"],c:0,s:`While charging, V_C = V\u2080(1 \u2212 e^(\u2212t/RC)).
2 = 20(1 \u2212 e^(\u2212t/RC)) gives e^(\u2212t/RC) = 0.9, so t/RC = ln(10/9) = 0.105.
RC = t/0.105 = 10\u207B\u2076/0.105 s, so C = 10\u207B\u2076 / (0.105 \xD7 10) F.
C = 0.952\xD710\u207B\u2076 F \u2248 0.95 \xB5F.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"The material filled between the plates of a parallel plate capacitor has resistivity 200 \u03A9 m. The value of capacitance of the capacitor is 2 pF. If a potential difference of 40 V is applied across the plates of the capacitor, then the value of leakage current flowing out of the capacitor is (Given the value of relative permittivity of material is 50)",o:["0.9 mA","9.0 mA","9.0 \xB5A","0.9 \xB5A"],c:0,s:`Resistance of the material between the plates R = \u03C1d/A.
Capacitance C = K\u03B5\u2080A/d, so d/A = K\u03B5\u2080/C.
R = \u03C1K\u03B5\u2080/C = 200 \xD7 50 \xD7 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 / (2\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2) \u2248 4.4\xD710\u2074 \u03A9.
Leakage current I = V/R = 40 / (4.4\xD710\u2074) \u2248 0.9\xD710\u207B\xB3 A = 0.9 mA.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel-plate capacitor with plate area A has separation d between the plates. Two dielectric slabs of dielectric constant K\u2081 and K\u2082 of same area A/2 and thickness d/2 are inserted in the space between the plates. The capacitance of the capacitor will be given by",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Two horizontal plates with +Q on top and -Q below, separated by d; on the right half of the gap two slabs K1 above K2 each of thickness d/2, the left half is air",d:[["l",40,30,300,30,"B"],["l",40,130,300,130,"B"],["r",170,30,110,50,"f"],["r",170,80,110,50,"f"],["t",225,60,"K\u2081","m"],["t",225,110,"K\u2082","m"],["t",105,84,"air","m"],["l",320,30,320,130,"b"],["t",326,84,"d"],["t",34,34,"+Q","e"],["t",34,134,"\u2212Q","e"]]},o:["{\u03B5\u2080A;d}({1;2} + {K\u2081K\u2082;K\u2081+K\u2082})","{\u03B5\u2080A;d}({1;2} + {2(K\u2081+K\u2082);K\u2081K\u2082})","{\u03B5\u2080A;d}({1;2} + {K\u2081+K\u2082;K\u2081K\u2082})","{\u03B5\u2080A;d}({1;2} + {K\u2081+K\u2082;2(K\u2081+K\u2082)})"],c:0,s:`The half of the plate area without slabs is an air capacitor: C_air = \u03B5\u2080(A/2)/d = \u03B5\u2080A/2d.
The other half has K\u2081 and K\u2082 stacked, each of thickness d/2 and area A/2, so they are in series. Each has C = K\u03B5\u2080(A/2)/(d/2) = K\u03B5\u2080A/d.
Series: (\u03B5\u2080A/d) \xD7 K\u2081K\u2082/(K\u2081 + K\u2082).
The two halves are in parallel: C = (\u03B5\u2080A/d)[1/2 + K\u2081K\u2082/(K\u2081 + K\u2082)].
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Three capacitors C\u2081 = 2 \xB5F, C\u2082 = 6 \xB5F and C\u2083 = 12 \xB5F are connected as shown in figure (C\u2082 and C\u2083 are in series in the upper branch between A and B, C\u2081 is in the lower branch, and the battery V is connected across A and B). Find the ratio of the charges on capacitors C\u2081, C\u2082 and C\u2083 respectively.",fig:{w:340,h:182,alt:"Between nodes A and B, an upper branch with C2 and C3 in series (point D between them) and a lower branch with C1; a battery V is connected across A and B through the outer loop",d:[["l",80,30,110,30],["l",110,22,110,38],["l",118,22,118,38],["l",118,30,150,30],["c",150,30,2,"F"],["l",150,30,182,30],["l",182,22,182,38],["l",190,22,190,38],["l",190,30,260,30],["l",80,30,80,110],["l",260,30,260,110],["c",80,70,2,"F"],["c",260,70,2,"F"],["l",80,110,166,110],["l",166,102,166,118],["l",174,102,174,118],["l",174,110,260,110],["l",80,70,30,70],["l",30,70,30,150],["l",30,150,140,150],["l",140,140,140,160],["l",148,145,148,155,"B"],["l",148,150,300,150],["l",300,150,300,70],["l",300,70,260,70],["t",114,20,"C\u2082","m"],["t",150,20,"D","m"],["t",186,20,"C\u2083","m"],["t",170,130,"C\u2081","m"],["t",72,66,"A","e"],["t",268,66,"B"],["t",144,174,"V","m"]]},o:["3 : 4 : 4","2 : 3 : 3","2 : 1 : 1","1 : 2 : 2"],c:3,s:`C\u2082 and C\u2083 are in series: C\u2082\u2083 = (6 \xD7 12)/(6 + 12) = 4 \xB5F.
This 4 \xB5F branch is in parallel with C\u2081 = 2 \xB5F, so both branches have the same voltage V_AB.
Charge on C\u2081 = 2V_AB. Charge on the 4 \xB5F branch = 4V_AB, and in series C\u2082 and C\u2083 each carry this same charge.
q\u2081 : q\u2082 : q\u2083 = 2 : 4 : 4 = 1 : 2 : 2.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:`A parallel plate capacitor with plate area 'A' and distance of separation 'd' is filled with a dielectric. What is the capacity of the capacitor when permittivity of the dielectric varies as \u03B5(x) = \u03B5\u2080 + kx, for (0 < x \u2264 {d;2})
\u03B5(x) = \u03B5\u2080 + k(d \u2212 x), for ({d;2} \u2264 x \u2264 d)`,o:["{kA;2}ln({2\u03B5\u2080;2\u03B5\u2080\u2212kd})","(\u03B5\u2080 + {kd;2})^(2/kA)","{kA;2ln({2\u03B5\u2080+kd;2\u03B5\u2080})}","0"],c:2,s:`Thin layers of thickness dx are in series, so 1/C = \u222B dx/(\u03B5(x)A).
The two halves of the gap are identical by symmetry: 1/C = (2/A)\u222B\u2080^(d/2) dx/(\u03B5\u2080 + kx).
= (2/kA) ln[(\u03B5\u2080 + kd/2)/\u03B5\u2080] = (2/kA) ln[(2\u03B5\u2080 + kd)/(2\u03B5\u2080)].
So C = kA / {2 ln[(2\u03B5\u2080 + kd)/(2\u03B5\u2080)]}.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"If q_f is the free charge on the capacitor plates and q_b is the bound charge on the dielectric slab of dielectric constant k placed between the capacitor plates, then bound charge q_b can be expressed as",o:["q_b = q_f(1 \u2212 {1;k})","q_b = q_f(1 + {1;k})","q_b = q_f(1 \u2212 {1;\u221Ak})","q_b = q_f(1 + {1;\u221Ak})"],c:0,s:`Free charge alone would give a field q_f/(\u03B5\u2080A) between the plates.
The bound charge on the slab faces opposes it, so the net field is (q_f \u2212 q_b)/(\u03B5\u2080A).
With a dielectric the field is reduced to E\u2080/k = q_f/(k\u03B5\u2080A).
So q_f \u2212 q_b = q_f/k, giving q_b = q_f(1 \u2212 1/k).
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"Two capacitors of capacities 2C and C are joined in parallel and charged up to potential V. The battery is removed and the capacitor of capacity C is filled completely with a medium of dielectric constant K. The potential difference across the capacitors will now be",o:["{3V;K+2}","{3V;K}","{V;K+2}","{V;K}"],c:0,s:`Total charge (battery removed, so it stays fixed): Q = (2C + C)V = 3CV.
After filling, the capacitances are 2C and KC, still in parallel: C_total = (2 + K)C.
New potential V' = Q/C_total = 3CV/((K + 2)C) = 3V/(K + 2).
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"In the reported figure, a capacitor is formed by placing a compound dielectric between the plates of parallel plate capacitor. The expression for the capacity of the said capacitor will be (Given, area of plate = A)",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Two vertical plates with three dielectric slabs side by side between them, constants K, 3K and 5K and thicknesses d, 2d and 3d, forming capacitors C1, C2, C3",d:[["l",60,26,60,116,"B"],["l",280,26,280,116,"B"],["r",60,26,36,90,"f"],["r",96,26,73,90,"f"],["r",169,26,111,90,"f"],["t",78,76,"K","m"],["t",132,76,"3K","m"],["t",224,76,"5K","m"],["t",78,20,"C\u2081","m"],["t",132,20,"C\u2082","m"],["t",224,20,"C\u2083","m"],["l",60,132,96,132,"b"],["t",78,148,"d","m"],["l",96,132,169,132,"b"],["t",132,148,"2d","m"],["l",169,132,280,132,"b"],["t",224,148,"3d","m"]]},o:["{9K\u03B5\u2080A;6d}","{15K\u03B5\u2080A;6d}","{25K\u03B5\u2080A;6d}","{15K\u03B5\u2080A;34d}"],c:3,s:`The three slabs have thicknesses d, 2d, 3d and constants K, 3K, 5K, each covering the full plate area A, so they are in series.
1/C = d/(K\u03B5\u2080A) + 2d/(3K\u03B5\u2080A) + 3d/(5K\u03B5\u2080A) = (d/K\u03B5\u2080A)(1 + 2/3 + 3/5).
1 + 2/3 + 3/5 = (15 + 10 + 9)/15 = 34/15.
C = 15K\u03B5\u2080A/(34d).
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A simple pendulum of mass m, length l and charge '+q' suspended in the electric field produced by two conducting parallel plates as shown. The value of deflection of pendulum in equilibrium position will be (C\u2081 and C\u2082 are the capacitances of the air gap and of the dielectric-filled part respectively.)",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"Two vertical plates at potentials +V2 (left) and -V1 (right) separated by d; a dielectric of constant K and thickness t touches the left plate; a charged pendulum of length l and mass m hangs in the air gap",d:[["l",60,20,60,170,"B"],["l",250,20,250,170,"B"],["r",60,20,70,150,"f"],["t",95,50,"Medium","ms"],["t",95,64,"(K)","ms"],["t",52,98,"+V\u2082","e"],["t",258,98,"\u2212V\u2081"],["l",160,20,220,20,"B"],["l",190,20,190,110],["c",190,110,6,"F"],["t",196,65,"l"],["t",200,128,"+q, m","s"],["t",215,155,"air"],["l",60,182,130,182,"b"],["t",95,178,"t","m"],["l",60,198,250,198,"b"],["t",155,194,"d","m"]]},o:["tan\u207B\xB9[{q;mg} \xD7 {C\u2082(V\u2081+V\u2082);(C\u2081+C\u2082)(d\u2212t)}]","tan\u207B\xB9[{q;mg} \xD7 {C\u2081(V\u2081+V\u2082);(C\u2081+C\u2082)(d\u2212t)}]","tan\u207B\xB9[{q;mg} \xD7 {C\u2082(V\u2082\u2212V\u2081);(C\u2081+C\u2082)(d\u2212t)}]","tan\u207B\xB9[{q;mg} \xD7 {C\u2081(V\u2082\u2212V\u2081);(C\u2081+C\u2082)(d\u2212t)}]"],c:0,s:`The plates are at +V\u2082 and \u2212V\u2081, so the potential difference is V\u2081 + V\u2082.
The air gap (C\u2081 = \u03B5\u2080A/(d\u2212t)) and the dielectric part (C\u2082 = K\u03B5\u2080A/t) are in series. In series the voltage divides inversely to C, so the air gap gets V_air = (V\u2081 + V\u2082)C\u2082/(C\u2081 + C\u2082).
Field in air E = V_air/(d \u2212 t).
The bob is in equilibrium under mg, qE and tension: tan\u03B8 = qE/mg.
\u03B8 = tan\u207B\xB9[(q/mg) \xD7 C\u2082(V\u2081 + V\u2082)/((C\u2081 + C\u2082)(d \u2212 t))].
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"For changing the capacitance of a given parallel plate capacitor, a dielectric material of dielectric constant K is used, which has the same area as the plates of the capacitor. The thickness of the dielectric slab is {3;4}d, where 'd' is the separation between the plates of parallel plate capacitor. The new capacitance (C') in terms of original capacitance (C\u2080) is given by the following relation.",o:["C' = {3+K;4K}C\u2080","C' = {4;3+K}C\u2080","C' = {4+K;3}C\u2080","C' = {4K;K+3}C\u2080"],c:3,s:`With a slab of thickness t: C' = \u03B5\u2080A / (d \u2212 t + t/K).
t = 3d/4: d \u2212 t + t/K = d/4 + 3d/(4K) = d(K + 3)/(4K).
C' = 4K\u03B5\u2080A/(d(K + 3)) = 4KC\u2080/(K + 3), since C\u2080 = \u03B5\u2080A/d.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Two equal capacitors are first connected in series and then in parallel. The ratio of the equivalent capacities in the two cases will be",o:["1 : 4","2 : 1","4 : 1","1 : 2"],c:0,s:`Let each capacitor be C.
Series: C_s = C/2. Parallel: C_p = 2C.
C_s : C_p = (C/2) : 2C = 1 : 4.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"efl",st:"Field lines and motion in a field",q:"An electron with kinetic energy K\u2081 enters between parallel plates of a capacitor at an angle '\u03B1' with the plates. It leaves the plates at angle '\u03B2' with kinetic energy K\u2082. Then the ratio of kinetic energies K\u2081 : K\u2082 will be",o:["{sin\xB2\u03B2;cos\xB2\u03B1}","{cos\u03B2;cos\u03B1}","{cos\u03B2;sin\u03B1}","{cos\xB2\u03B2;cos\xB2\u03B1}"],c:3,s:`The field between the plates is perpendicular to the plates, so there is no force along the plates.
The velocity component parallel to the plates stays constant: v\u2081cos\u03B1 = v\u2082cos\u03B2.
Kinetic energy is proportional to v\xB2, so K\u2081/K\u2082 = v\u2081\xB2/v\u2082\xB2 = cos\xB2\u03B2/cos\xB2\u03B1.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A 10 \xB5F capacitor is fully charged to a potential difference of 50 V. After removing the source voltage it is connected to an uncharged capacitor in parallel. Now the potential difference across them becomes 20 V. The capacitance of the second capacitor is",o:["15 \xB5F","20 \xB5F","30 \xB5F","10 \xB5F"],c:0,s:`Charge on the first capacitor: Q = 10 \xB5F \xD7 50 V = 500 \xB5C. This charge is conserved.
After joining in parallel: Q = (10 + C)V' with V' = 20 V.
500 = (10 + C) \xD7 20, so 10 + C = 25.
C = 15 \xB5F.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"In the circuit shown in the figure, the total charge is 750 \xB5C and the voltage across capacitor C\u2082 is 20 V. Then the charge on capacitor C\u2082 is",fig:{w:340,h:158,alt:"Battery V in a loop with C1 = 15 microfarad in series with a parallel pair: C2 in the upper branch and C3 = 8 microfarad in the lower branch",d:[["l",40,40,80,40],["l",80,32,80,48],["l",88,32,88,48],["l",88,40,130,40],["c",130,40,2,"F"],["l",130,40,175,40],["l",175,32,175,48],["l",183,32,183,48],["l",183,40,230,40],["c",230,40,2,"F"],["l",130,40,130,75],["l",130,75,175,75],["l",175,67,175,83],["l",183,67,183,83],["l",183,75,230,75],["l",230,75,230,40],["l",230,40,270,40],["l",40,40,40,120],["l",40,120,140,120],["l",140,110,140,130],["l",148,115,148,125,"B"],["l",148,120,270,120],["l",270,120,270,40],["t",84,28,"C\u2081 = 15 \xB5F","m"],["t",179,26,"C\u2082","m"],["t",179,98,"C\u2083 = 8 \xB5F","m"],["t",144,146,"V","m"]]},o:["450 \xB5C","590 \xB5C","160 \xB5C","650 \xB5C"],c:1,s:`C\u2081 is in series with the parallel pair C\u2082, C\u2083. The total charge 750 \xB5C flows through C\u2081 and then splits between C\u2082 and C\u2083.
C\u2082 and C\u2083 are in parallel, so both have 20 V across them.
Charge on C\u2083 = 8 \xB5F \xD7 20 V = 160 \xB5C.
Charge on C\u2082 = 750 \u2212 160 = 590 \xB5C.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A capacitor C is fully charged with voltage V\u2080. After disconnecting the voltage source, it is connected in parallel with another uncharged capacitor of capacitance {C;2}. The energy loss in the process after the charge is distributed between the two capacitors is",o:["{1;3}CV\u2080\xB2","{1;4}CV\u2080\xB2","{1;2}CV\u2080\xB2","{1;6}CV\u2080\xB2"],c:3,s:`Initial energy U\u2081 = \xBDCV\u2080\xB2. Charge Q = CV\u2080 is conserved.
After sharing, total capacitance = C + C/2 = 3C/2, so U\u2082 = Q\xB2/(2 \xD7 3C/2) = C\xB2V\u2080\xB2/(3C) = CV\u2080\xB2/3.
Loss = \xBDCV\u2080\xB2 \u2212 \u2153CV\u2080\xB2 = \u2159CV\u2080\xB2.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"Two capacitors of capacitances C and 2C are charged to potential differences V and 2V respectively. These are then connected in parallel in such a manner that the positive terminal of one is connected to the negative terminal of the other. The final energy of this configuration is",o:["{25;6}CV\xB2","{3;2}CV\xB2","zero","{9;2}CV\xB2"],c:1,s:`Charges: Q\u2081 = CV and Q\u2082 = 2C \xD7 2V = 4CV.
Joined positive to negative, the charges partly cancel: net charge = 4CV \u2212 CV = 3CV.
Total capacitance = 3C, so the common potential V' = 3CV/3C = V.
Final energy = \xBD(3C)V\xB2 = 3CV\xB2/2.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"In the circuit shown, charge on the 5 \xB5F capacitor is (both 6 V batteries have their positive terminals towards the outer ends of the circuit, and the lower middle point is at 0 V)",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Two outer capacitors 2 microfarad and 4 microfarad in the top wire meeting at a middle point, a 5 microfarad capacitor from that point down to a 0 volt node, and a 6 volt battery in the bottom wire on each side",d:[["l",40,30,90,30],["l",90,22,90,38],["l",98,22,98,38],["l",98,30,150,30],["c",150,30,2,"F"],["l",150,30,190,30],["l",190,22,190,38],["l",198,22,198,38],["l",198,30,270,30],["l",150,30,150,70],["l",142,70,158,70],["l",142,78,158,78],["l",150,78,150,140],["c",150,140,2,"F"],["l",40,30,40,140],["l",40,140,81,140],["l",81,130,81,150],["l",89,135,89,145,"B"],["l",89,140,150,140],["l",150,140,211,140],["l",211,135,211,145,"B"],["l",219,130,219,150],["l",219,140,270,140],["l",270,140,270,30],["t",94,20,"2 \xB5F","m"],["t",194,20,"4 \xB5F","m"],["t",162,78,"5 \xB5F"],["t",85,166,"6 V","m"],["t",215,166,"6 V","m"],["t",150,166,"0","m"]]},o:["18.00 \xB5C","10.90 \xB5C","16.36 \xB5C","5.45 \xB5C"],c:2,s:`Take the lower middle point as 0 V. Each battery makes the outer end of the circuit +6 V.
Let the potential of the top middle point M be V. The 2 \xB5F and 4 \xB5F capacitors are connected between the +6 V ends and M, and the 5 \xB5F between M and 0 V.
The plates joined to M carry no net charge: 2(6 \u2212 V) + 4(6 \u2212 V) = 5V.
36 \u2212 6V = 5V, so V = 36/11 = 3.27 V.
Charge on the 5 \xB5F = 5 \xD7 36/11 = 16.36 \xB5C.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor has plate of length 'l', width 'w' and separation of plates is 'd'. It is connected to a battery of emf V. A dielectric slab of the same thickness 'd' and of dielectric constant K = 4 is being inserted between the plates of the capacitor. At what length of the slab inside plates, will the energy stored in the capacitor be two times the initial energy stored?",o:["2l/3","l/3","l/4","l/2"],c:1,s:`Let the slab be inside by length x. The part with slab and the part without are in parallel.
C = \u03B5\u2080w(l \u2212 x)/d + K\u03B5\u2080wx/d = C\u2080[1 + (K \u2212 1)x/l] = C\u2080(1 + 3x/l).
The battery stays connected, so U = \xBDCV\xB2 is proportional to C.
For U = 2U\u2080: 1 + 3x/l = 2, so x = l/3.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor has plates of area A separated by distance 'd' between them. It is filled with a dielectric which has a dielectric constant that varies as k(x) = K(1 + \u03B1x) where 'x' is the distance measured from one of the plates. If (\u03B1d) << 1, the total capacitance of the system is best given by the expression",o:["{A\u03B5\u2080K;d}(1 + ({\u03B1d;2})\xB2)","{AK\u03B5\u2080;d}(1 + {\u03B1d;2})","{A\u03B5\u2080K;d}(1 + {\u03B1\xB2d\xB2;2})","{AK\u03B5\u2080;d}(1 + \u03B1d)"],c:1,s:`Thin layers of thickness dx are in series: 1/C = \u222B\u2080^d dx/(\u03B5\u2080K(1 + \u03B1x)A) = ln(1 + \u03B1d)/(\u03B5\u2080KA\u03B1).
For \u03B1d << 1: ln(1 + \u03B1d) \u2248 \u03B1d \u2212 (\u03B1d)\xB2/2.
1/C \u2248 (d/\u03B5\u2080KA)(1 \u2212 \u03B1d/2).
So C \u2248 (\u03B5\u2080KA/d)(1 \u2212 \u03B1d/2)\u207B\xB9 \u2248 (AK\u03B5\u2080/d)(1 + \u03B1d/2).
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Effective capacitance of parallel combination of two capacitors C\u2081 and C\u2082 is 10 \xB5F. When these capacitors are individually connected to a voltage source of 1 V, the energy stored in the capacitor C\u2082 is 4 times that of C\u2081. If these capacitors are connected in series, their effective capacitance will be",o:["1.6 \xB5F","8.4 \xB5F","3.2 \xB5F","4.2 \xB5F"],c:0,s:`Parallel: C\u2081 + C\u2082 = 10 \xB5F.
Energy at 1 V is \xBDC, so \xBDC\u2082 = 4 \xD7 \xBDC\u2081, giving C\u2082 = 4C\u2081.
5C\u2081 = 10, so C\u2081 = 2 \xB5F and C\u2082 = 8 \xB5F.
Series: C = (2 \xD7 8)/(2 + 8) = 1.6 \xB5F.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"A capacitor is made of two square plates each of side 'a' making a very small angle \u03B1 between them, as shown in figure. The capacitance will be close to",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Two square plates of side a: a horizontal lower plate at potential V2 and an upper plate at potential V1 tilted by a small angle alpha, with gap d at the left end",d:[["l",50,120,290,120,"B"],["l",50,95,290,25,"B"],["l",50,95,290,95,"d"],["t",130,91,"\u03B1"],["l",38,95,38,120,"b"],["t",32,112,"d","e"],["l",50,145,290,145,"b"],["t",170,162,"a","m"],["t",296,124,"V\u2082"],["t",296,28,"V\u2081"]]},o:["{\u03B5\u2080a\xB2;d}(1 \u2212 {\u03B1a;4d})","{\u03B5\u2080a\xB2;d}(1 + {\u03B1a;d})","{\u03B5\u2080a\xB2;d}(1 \u2212 {\u03B1a;2d})","{\u03B5\u2080a\xB2;d}(1 \u2212 {3\u03B1a;2d})"],c:2,s:`Cut the plate into thin strips of width dx at distance x from the narrow end (gap d). The gap there is d + \u03B1x.
Each strip has C = \u03B5\u2080a dx/(d + \u03B1x), and the strips are in parallel.
C = \u03B5\u2080a \u222B\u2080^a dx/(d + \u03B1x) = (\u03B5\u2080a/\u03B1) ln(1 + \u03B1a/d).
For small \u03B1: ln(1 + \u03B1a/d) \u2248 \u03B1a/d \u2212 (\u03B1a/d)\xB2/2, so C \u2248 (\u03B5\u2080a\xB2/d)(1 \u2212 \u03B1a/2d).
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"Voltage rating of a parallel plate capacitor is 500 V. Its dielectric can withstand a maximum electric field of 10\u2076 V m\u207B\xB9. The plate area is 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2. What is the dielectric constant if the capacitance is 15 pF? (given \u03B5\u2080 = 8.86 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2 N\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB2)",o:["3.8","8.5","6.2","4.5"],c:1,s:`The gap must be at least d = V/E = 500 / 10\u2076 = 5\xD710\u207B\u2074 m.
C = K\u03B5\u2080A/d, so K = Cd/(\u03B5\u2080A).
K = 15\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 5\xD710\u207B\u2074 / (8.86\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) = 7.5\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2075 / 8.86\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2076.
K \u2248 8.46 \u2248 8.5.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"A parallel plate capacitor has 1 \xB5F capacitance. One of its two plates is given +2 \xB5C charge and the other plate, +4 \xB5C charge. The potential difference developed across the capacitor is",o:["3 V","2 V","5 V","1 V"],c:3,s:`Only the difference of the plate charges sets up the field between the plates.
The inner faces carry \xB1(4 \u2212 2)/2 = \xB11 \xB5C, so the charge on the capacitor is Q = 1 \xB5C.
V = Q/C = 1 \xB5C / 1 \xB5F = 1 V.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A capacitor with capacitance 5 \u03BCF is charged to 5 \u03BCC. If the plates are pulled apart to reduce the capacitance to 2 \u03BCF, how much work is done?",o:["6.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J","3.75 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J","2.16 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J","2.55 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J"],c:1,s:`The capacitor is isolated, so its charge stays Q = 5 \u03BCC.
Initial energy U\u2081 = Q\xB2/2C\u2081 = (5\xD710\u207B\u2076)\xB2/(2\xD75\xD710\u207B\u2076) = 2.5\xD710\u207B\u2076 J.
Final energy U\u2082 = Q\xB2/2C\u2082 = (25\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2)/(4\xD710\u207B\u2076) = 6.25\xD710\u207B\u2076 J.
Work done = U\u2082 \u2212 U\u2081 = 3.75\xD710\u207B\u2076 J.
Answer: (b)`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"The parallel combination of two air filled parallel plate capacitors of capacitance C and nC is connected to a battery of voltage, V. When the capacitors are fully charged, the battery is removed and after that a dielectric material of dielectric constant K is placed between the two plates of the first capacitor. The new potential difference of the combined system is",o:["V","{(n+1)V;K+n}","{nV;K+n}","{V;K+n}"],c:1,s:`Total charge: Q = (C + nC)V = (n+1)CV. It stays the same after the battery is removed.
The dielectric makes the first capacitance KC, so the parallel combination is KC + nC = (K+n)C.
New potential difference V\u2032 = Q/C_new = (n+1)CV/[(K+n)C] = (n+1)V/(K+n).
Answer: (b)`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Figure shows charge (q) versus voltage (V) graph for series and parallel combination of two given capacitors. The capacitances are",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Charge q in microcoulomb against voltage V: line A rises to 500 at 10 V and line B rises to 80 at 10 V, both from the origin.",d:[["l",60,150,300,150,"a"],["l",60,150,60,12,"a"],["l",60,150,240,21],["l",60,150,272,126],["l",60,50,200,50,"d"],["l",200,150,200,50,"d"],["l",60,134,200,134,"d"],["l",200,146,200,154],["t",52,54,"500","es"],["t",52,138,"80","es"],["t",200,166,"10 V","ms"],["t",70,12,"q (\u03BCC)","s"],["t",304,154,"V (volt)","s"],["t",246,20,"A"],["t",276,126,"B"]]},o:["50 \u03BCF and 30 \u03BCF","20 \u03BCF and 30 \u03BCF","60 \u03BCF and 40 \u03BCF","40 \u03BCF and 10 \u03BCF"],c:3,s:`On a q\u2013V graph the slope is the capacitance. The parallel combination has the larger slope (line A), the series combination the smaller (line B).
Line A: 500 \u03BCC at 10 V, so C\u2081 + C\u2082 = 50 \u03BCF.
Line B: 80 \u03BCC at 10 V, so C\u2081C\u2082/(C\u2081+C\u2082) = 8 \u03BCF, giving C\u2081C\u2082 = 8 \xD7 50 = 400.
Two numbers with sum 50 and product 400 are 40 and 10.
Answer: (d) 40 \u03BCF and 10 \u03BCF`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:`Two identical parallel plate capacitors, of capacitance C each, have plates of area A, separated by a distance d. The space between the plates of the two capacitors, is filled with three dielectrics, of equal thickness and dielectric constants K\u2081, K\u2082 and K\u2083. The first capacitor is filled as shown in figure I, and the second one is filled as shown in figure II.
If these two modified capacitors are charged by the same potential V, the ratio of the energy stored in the two, would be (E\u2081 refers to capacitor (I) and E\u2082 to capacitor (II))`,fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Two capacitors between horizontal plates: figure I has three horizontal layers K1, K2, K3 stacked one above the other; figure II has three vertical columns K1, K2, K3 side by side.",d:[["l",24,30,146,30,"B"],["l",24,130,146,130,"B"],["l",184,30,306,30,"B"],["l",184,130,306,130,"B"],["r",30,30,110,33,"f"],["r",30,63,110,34,"f"],["r",30,97,110,33,"f"],["r",190,30,37,100,"f"],["r",227,30,36,100,"f"],["r",263,30,37,100,"f"],["t",85,51,"K\u2081","m"],["t",85,84,"K\u2082","m"],["t",85,118,"K\u2083","m"],["t",208,84,"K\u2081","m"],["t",245,84,"K\u2082","m"],["t",281,84,"K\u2083","m"],["t",85,154,"(I)","m"],["t",245,154,"(II)","m"]]},o:["E\u2081/E\u2082 = {(K\u2081+K\u2082+K\u2083)(K\u2082K\u2083+K\u2083K\u2081+K\u2081K\u2082);K\u2081K\u2082K\u2083}","E\u2081/E\u2082 = {(K\u2081+K\u2082+K\u2083)(K\u2082K\u2083+K\u2083K\u2081+K\u2081K\u2082);9K\u2081K\u2082K\u2083}","E\u2081/E\u2082 = {9K\u2081K\u2082K\u2083;(K\u2081+K\u2082+K\u2083)(K\u2082K\u2083+K\u2083K\u2081+K\u2081K\u2082)}","E\u2081/E\u2082 = {K\u2081K\u2082K\u2083;(K\u2081+K\u2082+K\u2083)(K\u2082K\u2083+K\u2083K\u2081+K\u2081K\u2082)}"],c:2,s:`Same V, so the energy ratio equals the capacitance ratio: E\u2081/E\u2082 = C_I/C_II.
(I): three layers of thickness d/3 in series. 1/C_I = (d/3\u03B5\u2080A)(1/K\u2081+1/K\u2082+1/K\u2083), so C_I = 3\u03B5\u2080A K\u2081K\u2082K\u2083/[d(K\u2081K\u2082+K\u2082K\u2083+K\u2083K\u2081)].
(II): three side-by-side parts of area A/3 in parallel: C_II = \u03B5\u2080A(K\u2081+K\u2082+K\u2083)/(3d).
Divide: E\u2081/E\u2082 = 9K\u2081K\u2082K\u2083 / [(K\u2081+K\u2082+K\u2083)(K\u2082K\u2083+K\u2083K\u2081+K\u2081K\u2082)].
Answer: (c)`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"In the given circuit, the charge on 4 \u03BCF capacitor will be",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Circuit: a 4 microfarad capacitor in series with a parallel pair of 1 and 5 microfarad, in parallel with a 3 microfarad capacitor, all across a 10 V battery.",d:[["l",40,36,40,145],["l",250,36,250,145],["l",40,36,72,36],["l",72,28,72,44],["l",78,28,78,44],["l",78,36,110,36],["l",110,18,110,54],["l",200,18,200,54],["l",110,18,152,18],["l",152,10,152,26],["l",158,10,158,26],["l",158,18,200,18],["l",110,54,152,54],["l",152,46,152,62],["l",158,46,158,62],["l",158,54,200,54],["l",200,36,250,36],["l",40,100,142,100],["l",142,92,142,108],["l",148,92,148,108],["l",148,100,250,100],["l",40,145,142,145],["l",142,135,142,155],["l",148,140,148,150],["l",148,145,250,145],["c",110,36,2.4,"F"],["c",200,36,2.4,"F"],["c",40,100,2.4,"F"],["c",250,100,2.4,"F"],["t",75,24,"4 \u03BCF","m"],["t",155,8,"1 \u03BCF","m"],["t",155,76,"5 \u03BCF","m"],["t",145,118,"3 \u03BCF","m"],["t",145,172,"10 V","m"]]},o:["9.6 \u03BCC","24 \u03BCC","5.4 \u03BCC","13.4 \u03BCC"],c:1,s:`The 1 \u03BCF and 5 \u03BCF are in parallel: 6 \u03BCF. This is in series with the 4 \u03BCF.
Series value = (4 \xD7 6)/(4 + 6) = 2.4 \u03BCF.
This branch and the 3 \u03BCF branch are both directly across the 10 V battery, so the 3 \u03BCF does not affect it.
Charge of the branch = 2.4 \u03BCF \xD7 10 V = 24 \u03BCC, and series capacitors carry the same charge.
Charge on 4 \u03BCF = 24 \u03BCC.
Answer: (b)`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor is made of two square plates of side a, separated by a distance d (d << a). The lower triangular portion is filled with a dielectric of dielectric constant K, as shown in the figure. Capacitance of this capacitor is",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"Square plates a wide, separated by d; a right triangular wedge of dielectric K fills the lower part, thin at the left and full height d at the right.",d:[["l",60,30,250,30,"B"],["l",60,130,250,130,"B"],["p","M60 130 L250 130 L250 30 Z","f"],["t",205,118,"K","m"],["l",60,152,250,152,"b"],["t",155,168,"a","m"],["l",270,30,270,130,"b"],["t",278,84,"d"]]},o:["{1;2}\xB7{K\u03B5\u2080a\xB2;d}","{K\u03B5\u2080a\xB2;d(K\u22121)} ln K","{K\u03B5\u2080a\xB2;2d(K+1)}","{K\u03B5\u2080a\xB2;d} ln K"],c:1,s:`Cut the capacitor into thin strips of width dx at distance x from the thin end of the wedge. The dielectric thickness there is t = (d/a)x and the air gap is d \u2212 t.
Each strip is air and dielectric in series: dC = \u03B5\u2080a dx / [d \u2212 t + t/K] = \u03B5\u2080a dx / [d \u2212 (d/a)(1 \u2212 1/K)x].
Strips are in parallel, so C = \u222B dC from 0 to a = [\u03B5\u2080a\xB2/(d(1 \u2212 1/K))] ln K.
Simplify: C = K\u03B5\u2080a\xB2 ln K / [d(K \u2212 1)].
Answer: (b)`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor of area 6 cm\xB2 and separation 3 mm. The gap is filled with three dielectric materials of equal thickness (see figure) with dielectric constants K\u2081 = 10, K\u2082 = 12 and K\u2083 = 14. The dielectric constant of a material which when fully inserted in the given capacitor, gives same capacitance would be",fig:{w:340,h:135,alt:"Parallel plates 3 mm apart with three dielectric blocks K1, K2, K3 of equal width side by side between them.",d:[["l",60,30,280,30,"B"],["l",60,110,280,110,"B"],["r",60,30,73,80,"f"],["r",133,30,73,80,"f"],["r",206,30,74,80,"f"],["t",96,75,"K\u2081","m"],["t",169,75,"K\u2082","m"],["t",243,75,"K\u2083","m"],["l",300,30,300,110,"b"],["t",306,74,"3 mm"]]},o:["36","12","4","14"],c:1,s:`The three materials sit side by side, each filling one third of the plate area, with the full gap 3 mm. So the three parts are capacitors in parallel.
C = (\u03B5\u2080A/3d)(K\u2081 + K\u2082 + K\u2083).
One material of constant K gives C = K\u03B5\u2080A/d.
Equate: K = (10 + 12 + 14)/3 = 12.
Answer: (b)`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor having capacitance 12 pF is charged by a battery to a potential difference of 10 V between its plates. The charging battery is now disconnected and a porcelain slab of dielectric constant 6.5 is slipped between the plates. The work done by the capacitor on the slab is",o:["508 pJ","692 pJ","560 pJ","600 pJ"],c:0,s:`After disconnection the charge stays fixed, so U = Q\xB2/2C.
Initial energy U\u2081 = \xBDCV\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 12\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 100 = 600 pJ.
With the slab C becomes 6.5C, so U\u2082 = U\u2081/6.5 = 92.3 pJ.
Work done by the capacitor on the slab = U\u2081 \u2212 U\u2082 = 600 \u2212 92.3 \u2248 508 pJ.
Answer: (a)`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"In the figure shown below, the charge on the left plate of the 10 \u03BCF capacitor is \u221230 \u03BCC. The charge on the right plate of the 6 \u03BCF capacitor is",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Circuit: a 10 microfarad capacitor, then a parallel pair of 6 microfarad (upper) and 4 microfarad (lower), then a 2 microfarad capacitor, all in series.",d:[["l",15,80,42,80],["l",42,72,42,88],["l",48,72,48,88],["l",48,80,110,80],["l",110,80,150,45],["l",110,80,150,115],["l",150,45,187,45],["l",187,37,187,53],["l",193,37,193,53],["l",193,45,230,45],["l",150,115,187,115],["l",187,107,187,123],["l",193,107,193,123],["l",193,115,230,115],["l",230,45,270,80],["l",230,115,270,80],["l",270,80,294,80],["l",294,72,294,88],["l",300,72,300,88],["l",300,80,325,80],["c",110,80,2.4,"F"],["c",270,80,2.4,"F"],["t",45,102,"10 \u03BCF","m"],["t",190,30,"6 \u03BCF","m"],["t",190,140,"4 \u03BCF","m"],["t",297,102,"2 \u03BCF","m"]]},o:["\u221218 \u03BCC","\u221212 \u03BCC","+12 \u03BCC","+18 \u03BCC"],c:3,s:`The 10 \u03BCF, the parallel pair (6 \u03BCF and 4 \u03BCF) and the 2 \u03BCF are in series.
Left plate of 10 \u03BCF is \u221230 \u03BCC, so its right plate is +30 \u03BCC.
The node formed by this plate and the left plates of the 6 \u03BCF and 4 \u03BCF is neutral, so those two left plates hold \u221230 \u03BCC in total.
Parallel capacitors have the same voltage, so charges are in the ratio 6 : 4: left plate of 6 \u03BCF = \u221218 \u03BCC, of 4 \u03BCF = \u221212 \u03BCC.
So the right plate of the 6 \u03BCF is +18 \u03BCC.
Answer: (d)`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Seven capacitors, each of capacitance 2 \u03BCF, are to be connected in a configuration to obtain an effective capacitance of {6;13} \u03BCF. Which of the combinations, shown in figures below, will achieve the desired value ?",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"Four options, each a group of parallel capacitors followed by a chain of series capacitors: (a) 3 parallel and 4 in series, (b) 5 parallel and 2 in series, (c) 4 parallel and 3 in series, (d) 2 parallel and 5 in series.",d:[["c",28,34,2.4,"F"],["l",28,34,55,34],["l",55,23,55,45],["l",105,23,105,45],["l",55,23,77,23],["l",77,19,77,27],["l",83,19,83,27],["l",83,23,105,23],["l",55,34,77,34],["l",77,30,77,38],["l",83,30,83,38],["l",83,34,105,34],["l",55,45,77,45],["l",77,41,77,49],["l",83,41,83,49],["l",83,45,105,45],["l",105,34,125,34],["l",125,34,137,34],["l",137,26,137,42],["l",143,26,143,42],["l",143,34,169,34],["l",169,26,169,42],["l",175,26,175,42],["l",175,34,201,34],["l",201,26,201,42],["l",207,26,207,42],["l",207,34,233,34],["l",233,26,233,42],["l",239,26,239,42],["l",239,34,268,34],["c",268,34,2.4,"F"],["t",6,38,"(a)","s"],["c",28,92,2.4,"F"],["l",28,92,55,92],["l",55,70,55,114],["l",105,70,105,114],["l",55,70,77,70],["l",77,66,77,74],["l",83,66,83,74],["l",83,70,105,70],["l",55,81,77,81],["l",77,77,77,85],["l",83,77,83,85],["l",83,81,105,81],["l",55,92,77,92],["l",77,88,77,96],["l",83,88,83,96],["l",83,92,105,92],["l",55,103,77,103],["l",77,99,77,107],["l",83,99,83,107],["l",83,103,105,103],["l",55,114,77,114],["l",77,110,77,118],["l",83,110,83,118],["l",83,114,105,114],["l",105,92,125,92],["l",125,92,137,92],["l",137,84,137,100],["l",143,84,143,100],["l",143,92,169,92],["l",169,84,169,100],["l",175,84,175,100],["l",175,92,204,92],["c",204,92,2.4,"F"],["t",6,96,"(b)","s"],["c",28,150,2.4,"F"],["l",28,150,55,150],["l",55,133.5,55,166.5],["l",105,133.5,105,166.5],["l",55,133.5,77,133.5],["l",77,129.5,77,137.5],["l",83,129.5,83,137.5],["l",83,133.5,105,133.5],["l",55,144.5,77,144.5],["l",77,140.5,77,148.5],["l",83,140.5,83,148.5],["l",83,144.5,105,144.5],["l",55,155.5,77,155.5],["l",77,151.5,77,159.5],["l",83,151.5,83,159.5],["l",83,155.5,105,155.5],["l",55,166.5,77,166.5],["l",77,162.5,77,170.5],["l",83,162.5,83,170.5],["l",83,166.5,105,166.5],["l",105,150,125,150],["l",125,150,137,150],["l",137,142,137,158],["l",143,142,143,158],["l",143,150,169,150],["l",169,142,169,158],["l",175,142,175,158],["l",175,150,201,150],["l",201,142,201,158],["l",207,142,207,158],["l",207,150,236,150],["c",236,150,2.4,"F"],["t",6,154,"(c)","s"],["c",28,208,2.4,"F"],["l",28,208,55,208],["l",55,202.5,55,213.5],["l",105,202.5,105,213.5],["l",55,202.5,77,202.5],["l",77,198.5,77,206.5],["l",83,198.5,83,206.5],["l",83,202.5,105,202.5],["l",55,213.5,77,213.5],["l",77,209.5,77,217.5],["l",83,209.5,83,217.5],["l",83,213.5,105,213.5],["l",105,208,125,208],["l",125,208,137,208],["l",137,200,137,216],["l",143,200,143,216],["l",143,208,169,208],["l",169,200,169,216],["l",175,200,175,216],["l",175,208,201,208],["l",201,200,201,216],["l",207,200,207,216],["l",207,208,233,208],["l",233,200,233,216],["l",239,200,239,216],["l",239,208,265,208],["l",265,200,265,216],["l",271,200,271,216],["l",271,208,300,208],["c",300,208,2.4,"F"],["t",6,212,"(d)","s"]]},o:["Figure (a)","Figure (b)","Figure (c)","Figure (d)"],c:0,s:`Parallel groups add, series chains add as reciprocals. Count the capacitors in each figure (all 2 \u03BCF).
(a) 3 in parallel = 6 \u03BCF; 4 in series = 0.5 \u03BCF. Series of the two: 6 \xD7 0.5/6.5 = 6/13 \u03BCF. This matches.
(b) 5 in parallel = 10; 2 in series = 1; result 10/11 \u03BCF.
(c) 4 in parallel = 8; 3 in series = 2/3; result 8/13 \u03BCF.
(d) 2 in parallel = 4; 5 in series = 0.4; result 4/11 \u03BCF.
Answer: (a)`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"In the figure shown, after the switch S is turned from position A to position B, the energy dissipated in the circuit in terms of capacitance C and total charge Q is",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Circuit: battery epsilon in the left branch; a switch S whose pole is connected to capacitor C can touch contact A (battery) or contact B (leading to capacitor 3C in the right branch).",d:[["l",50,30,130,30],["l",50,30,50,140],["l",50,30,50,82],["l",40,82,60,82],["l",45,88,55,88],["l",50,88,50,140],["l",50,140,260,140],["c",130,30,2.5,"F"],["c",200,30,2.5,"F"],["l",200,30,260,30],["l",260,30,260,140],["l",165,52,135,33],["l",165,52,165,70],["l",165,70,165,82],["l",157,82,173,82],["l",157,88,173,88],["l",165,88,165,140],["l",260,30,260,82],["l",252,82,268,82],["l",252,88,268,88],["l",260,88,260,140],["t",130,18,"A","m"],["t",200,18,"B","m"],["t",172,50,"S"],["t",38,89,"\u03B5","e"],["t",180,92,"C"],["t",272,92,"3C"]]},o:["{5Q\xB2;8C}","{Q\xB2;8C}","{3Q\xB2;8C}","{3Q\xB2;4C}"],c:2,s:`With the switch at A, capacitor C is charged by the battery to charge Q. Energy = Q\xB2/2C.
At B the battery is out of the circuit and C shares its charge with the uncharged 3C.
Common potential = Q/(C + 3C) = Q/4C, so final energy = Q\xB2/[2(4C)] = Q\xB2/8C.
Energy dissipated = Q\xB2/2C \u2212 Q\xB2/8C = 3Q\xB2/8C.
Answer: (c)`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:`A parallel plate capacitor with plates of area 1 m\xB2 each, are at a separation of 0.1 m. If the electric field between the plates is 100 N C\u207B\xB9, the magnitude of charge on each plate is
(Take \u03B5\u2080 = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2/N m\xB2)`,o:["8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 C","7.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 C","9.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 C","6.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 C"],c:0,s:`Between the plates of an air capacitor E = \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080, so \u03C3 = \u03B5\u2080E.
Charge on each plate Q = \u03C3A = \u03B5\u2080EA.
Q = 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 100 \xD7 1 = 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 C.
The separation 0.1 m is not needed.
Answer: (a)`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"In the circuit shown, find C if the effective capacitance of the whole circuit is to be 0.5 \u03BCF. All values in the circuit are in \u03BCF.",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Network between A and B: capacitor C in series with two branches in parallel. Left branch: a vertical capacitor then two capacitors in parallel. Right branch: a horizontal capacitor then a section that has a vertical capacitor in series with a horizontal one, in parallel with a vertical capacitor.",d:[["c",15,38,2.4,"F"],["t",10,60,"A","m"],["l",15,38,37,38],["l",37,30,37,46],["l",43,30,43,46],["l",43,38,80,38],["l",80,38,107,38],["l",107,30,107,46],["l",113,30,113,46],["l",113,38,140,38],["l",140,38,250,38],["l",80,38,80,62],["l",72,62,88,62],["l",72,68,88,68],["l",80,68,80,95],["l",80,95,105,95],["l",80,95,80,107],["l",72,107,88,107],["l",72,113,88,113],["l",80,113,80,125],["l",105,95,105,107],["l",97,107,113,107],["l",97,113,113,113],["l",105,113,105,125],["l",80,125,105,125],["l",92,125,92,145],["l",92,145,285,145],["c",285,145,2.4,"F"],["t",296,149,"B"],["l",140,38,140,62],["l",132,62,148,62],["l",132,68,148,68],["l",140,68,140,92],["l",140,92,197,92],["l",197,84,197,100],["l",203,84,203,100],["l",203,92,250,92],["l",250,38,250,62],["l",242,62,258,62],["l",242,68,258,68],["l",250,68,250,92],["l",250,92,250,145],["c",80,38,2.4,"F"],["c",140,38,2.4,"F"],["c",250,92,2.4,"F"],["t",40,24,"C","m"],["t",110,24,"2","m"],["t",70,68,"2","e"],["t",74,113,"2","e"],["t",111,113,"2"],["t",130,68,"2","e"],["t",200,110,"2","m"],["t",262,68,"1"]]},o:["{6;5} \u03BCF","4 \u03BCF","{7;11} \u03BCF","{7;10} \u03BCF"],c:2,s:`Find the network to the right of C, then put C in series with it.
Left branch: a 2 in series with two 2's in parallel (4): 2\xD74/6 = 4/3 \u03BCF.
Right branch: the 1 is in parallel with (2 in series with 2 = 1), giving 2 \u03BCF; this is in series with the horizontal 2: 2\xD72/4 = 1 \u03BCF.
The two branches are in parallel: 4/3 + 1 = 7/3 \u03BCF.
Series with C: C(7/3)/(C + 7/3) = 1/2, so 7C/3 = C/2 + 7/6, (11/6)C = 7/6, C = 7/11 \u03BCF.
Answer: (c)`},{y:"JEE Main 2018",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor of capacitance 90 pF is connected to a battery of emf 20 V. If a dielectric material of dielectric constant K = {5;3} is inserted between the plates, the magnitude of the induced charge will be",o:["1.2 nC","0.3 nC","2.4 nC","0.9 nC"],c:0,s:`The battery stays connected, so V = 20 V.
Charge on the plates with the dielectric: Q = KCV = (5/3)(90 pF)(20 V) = 3000 pC = 3 nC.
Induced (surface) charge on the dielectric q = Q(1 \u2212 1/K) = 3 nC \xD7 (1 \u2212 3/5) = 1.2 nC.
Answer: (a)`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"The equivalent capacitance between A and B in the circuit given below, is",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Circuit between A and B: 6 microfarad in series, then two 5 microfarad capacitors and a 2 microfarad capacitor all connected between the same two nodes, then 2 microfarad and 4 microfarad capacitors in parallel to B.",d:[["c",15,40,2.4,"F"],["t",10,28,"A","m"],["l",15,40,45,40],["l",45,32,45,48],["l",51,32,51,48],["l",51,40,85,40],["l",85,40,187,40],["l",187,32,187,48],["l",193,32,193,48],["l",193,40,230,40],["l",230,40,285,40],["l",110,40,110,67],["l",102,67,118,67],["l",102,73,118,73],["l",110,73,110,100],["l",150,40,150,67],["l",142,67,158,67],["l",142,73,158,73],["l",150,73,150,100],["l",110,100,230,100],["l",230,40,230,100],["l",230,100,230,127],["l",222,127,238,127],["l",222,133,238,133],["l",230,133,230,160],["l",285,40,285,97],["l",277,97,293,97],["l",277,103,293,103],["l",285,103,285,160],["l",230,160,310,160],["c",310,160,2.4,"F"],["t",320,164,"B"],["c",110,40,2.4,"F"],["c",150,40,2.4,"F"],["c",230,40,2.4,"F"],["c",230,100,2.4,"F"],["c",285,160,2.4,"F"],["t",48,26,"6 \u03BCF","m"],["t",190,26,"2 \u03BCF","m"],["t",102,74,"5 \u03BCF","es"],["t",158,74,"5 \u03BCF","s"],["t",222,134,"2 \u03BCF","e"],["t",297,104,"4 \u03BCF","s"]]},o:["5.4 \u03BCF","4.9 \u03BCF","3.6 \u03BCF","2.4 \u03BCF"],c:3,s:`Call P the node after the 6 \u03BCF and Q the node at the right end of the 2 \u03BCF.
The lower wire joins the bottom plates of both 5 \u03BCF capacitors to Q. So the two 5 \u03BCF and the 2 \u03BCF all lie between P and Q: 5 + 5 + 2 = 12 \u03BCF.
Between Q and B the 2 \u03BCF and 4 \u03BCF are in parallel: 6 \u03BCF.
Now 6, 12 and 6 are in series: 1/C = 1/6 + 1/12 + 1/6 = 5/12.
C = 12/5 = 2.4 \u03BCF.
Answer: (d)`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:`A parallel plate capacitor with area 200 cm\xB2 and separation between the plates 1.5 cm, is connected across a battery of emf V. If the force of attraction between the plates is 25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 N, the value of V is approximately
(\u03B5\u2080 = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2/N m\xB2)`,o:["150 V","100 V","250 V","300 V"],c:2,s:`Force of attraction between the plates F = \xBDQE = \xBD(\u03B5\u2080AV/d)(V/d) = \u03B5\u2080AV\xB2/(2d\xB2).
V\xB2 = 2Fd\xB2/(\u03B5\u2080A) = 2 \xD7 25\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 (1.5\xD710\u207B\xB2)\xB2 / (8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 200\xD710\u207B\u2074).
V\xB2 = 1.125\xD710\u207B\u2078 / 1.77\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB3 \u2248 6.4\xD710\u2074.
V \u2248 252 V \u2248 250 V.
Answer: (c)`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A capacitor C\u2081 = 1.0 \u03BCF is charged up to a voltage V = 60 V by connecting it to battery B through switch (1). Now C\u2081 is disconnected from battery and connected to a circuit consisting of two uncharged capacitors C\u2082 = 3.0 \u03BCF and C\u2083 = 6.0 \u03BCF through switch (2), as shown in the figure. The sum of final charges on C\u2082 and C\u2083 is",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"Circuit: battery B on the left, capacitor C1 in the middle, and capacitors C2 and C3 in series on the right; a switch can connect the top of C1 to the battery (position 1) or to the C2, C3 branch (position 2).",d:[["l",40,30,125,30],["l",40,30,40,150],["l",40,30,40,92],["l",30,92,50,92],["l",35,98,45,98],["l",40,98,40,150],["l",40,150,270,150],["c",125,30,2.5,"F"],["c",195,30,2.5,"F"],["l",160,70,129,34,"a"],["l",160,70,193,34,"d"],["l",160,70,160,106,"d"],["l",160,106,160,109],["l",152,109,168,109],["l",152,115,168,115],["l",160,115,160,150],["l",195,30,270,30],["l",270,30,270,55],["l",262,55,278,55],["l",262,61,278,61],["l",270,61,270,85],["l",270,85,270,109],["l",262,109,278,109],["l",262,115,278,115],["l",270,115,270,150],["t",28,98,"B","e"],["t",120,60,"(1)","e"],["t",186,56,"(2)"],["t",172,116,"C\u2081"],["t",284,62,"C\u2082"],["t",284,116,"C\u2083"]]},o:["20 \u03BCC","40 \u03BCC","36 \u03BCC","54 \u03BCC"],c:1,s:`Charge on C\u2081 after step 1: Q = 1.0 \xD7 60 = 60 \u03BCC.
C\u2082 and C\u2083 are in series: C_eq = 3 \xD7 6/9 = 2 \u03BCF. C\u2081 now shares its charge with this series pair (in parallel with it).
Common voltage V\u2032 = 60/(1 + 2) = 20 V.
Charge taken by the C\u2082\u2013C\u2083 branch = 2 \u03BCF \xD7 20 V = 40 \u03BCC (series capacitors carry this same charge).
The charge that has moved to the pair is 40 \u03BCC.
Answer: (b)`},{y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:`A capacitance of 2 \u03BCF is required in an electrical circuit across a potential difference of 1.0 kV. A large number of 1 \u03BCF capacitors are available which can withstand a potential difference of not more than 300 V.
The minimum number of capacitors required to achieve this is`,o:["2","16","24","32"],c:3,s:`Each capacitor can take at most 300 V. For 1000 V we need at least 1000/300 = 3.3, so 4 capacitors in series in one row (250 V each).
One row of 4 in series = 1/4 \u03BCF.
For 2 \u03BCF we need 2/(1/4) = 8 rows in parallel.
Total = 4 \xD7 8 = 32 capacitors.
Answer: (d)`},{y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:`The energy stored in the electric field produced by a metal sphere is 4.5 J. If the sphere contains 4 \u03BCC charge, its radius will be
[Take: {1;4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080} = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 N\xB7m\xB2/C\xB2]`,o:["32 mm","20 mm","16 mm","28 mm"],c:2,s:`Energy of a charged conducting sphere: U = Q\xB2/(8\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R) = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)\xB7Q\xB2/(2R).
R = (9\xD710\u2079)(4\xD710\u207B\u2076)\xB2 / (2 \xD7 4.5) = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 16\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 / 9.
R = 16\xD710\u207B\xB3 m = 16 mm.
Answer: (c)`},{y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:`A combination of parallel plate capacitors is maintained at a certain potential difference.
When a 3 mm thick slab is introduced between all the plates, in order to maintain the same potential difference, the distance between the plates is increased by 2.4 mm. Find the dielectric constant of the slab.`,fig:{w:340,h:130,alt:"Chain A, D, C1, C, C2, E, C3, B in series along a line; a wire joins D to E over the top and another wire joins C to the point between C3 and B underneath.",d:[["l",15,70,60,70],["l",60,70,92,70],["l",92,62,92,78],["l",98,62,98,78],["l",98,70,130,70],["l",130,70,162,70],["l",162,62,162,78],["l",168,62,168,78],["l",168,70,200,70],["l",200,70,232,70],["l",232,62,232,78],["l",238,62,238,78],["l",238,70,270,70],["l",270,70,320,70],["l",60,70,60,30],["l",60,30,200,30],["l",200,30,200,70],["l",130,70,130,110],["l",130,110,270,110],["l",270,110,270,70],["c",15,70,2.4,"F"],["c",320,70,2.4,"F"],["c",60,70,2.4,"F"],["c",130,70,2.4,"F"],["c",200,70,2.4,"F"],["c",270,70,2.4,"F"],["t",10,88,"A"],["t",60,90,"D","m"],["t",120,90,"C","m"],["t",206,90,"E","m"],["t",326,88,"B","m"],["t",95,56,"C\u2081","m"],["t",165,56,"C\u2082","m"],["t",235,56,"C\u2083","m"]]},o:["6","5","4","3"],c:1,s:`To keep the same potential difference with the same charge, the capacitance of each capacitor must stay unchanged.
Originally the gap is d (capacitance \u03B5\u2080A/d).
Now the gap is d + 2.4 mm, containing a 3 mm slab. Air gap = d + 2.4 \u2212 3 = d \u2212 0.6 mm.
Effective gap = (d \u2212 0.6) + 3/K must equal d, so 3/K = 0.6.
K = 5.
Answer: (b)`},{y:"JEE Main 2016",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"A combination of capacitors is set up as shown in the figure. The magnitude of the electric field due to a point charge Q (having a charge equal to the sum of the charges on the 4 \u03BCF and 9 \u03BCF capacitors), at a point distant 30 m from it, would equal",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Circuit: three branches across an 8 V battery. Top branch: 4 microfarad in series with a parallel pair of 3 and 9 microfarad. Middle branch: 2 microfarad. Bottom branch: the 8 V battery.",d:[["l",50,42,50,150],["l",290,42,290,150],["l",50,42,77,42],["l",77,34,77,50],["l",83,34,83,50],["l",83,42,110,42],["l",110,22,110,62],["l",240,22,240,62],["l",110,22,172,22],["l",172,14,172,30],["l",178,14,178,30],["l",178,22,240,22],["l",110,62,172,62],["l",172,54,172,70],["l",178,54,178,70],["l",178,62,240,62],["l",240,42,290,42],["l",50,112,167,112],["l",167,104,167,120],["l",173,104,173,120],["l",173,112,290,112],["l",50,150,167,150],["l",167,140,167,160],["l",173,145,173,155],["l",173,150,290,150],["c",50,112,2.4,"F"],["c",290,112,2.4,"F"],["c",110,42,2.4,"F"],["c",240,42,2.4,"F"],["t",80,28,"4 \u03BCF","m"],["t",175,10,"3 \u03BCF","m"],["t",175,82,"9 \u03BCF","m"],["t",170,98,"2 \u03BCF","m"],["t",170,174,"8 V","m"]]},o:["240 N/C","360 N/C","420 N/C","480 N/C"],c:2,s:`The top branch (4 \u03BCF in series with the parallel pair 3 \u03BCF + 9 \u03BCF = 12 \u03BCF) is across the 8 V battery, like the 2 \u03BCF branch.
Top branch capacitance = 4 \xD7 12/16 = 3 \u03BCF, charge = 3 \xD7 8 = 24 \u03BCC. This is the charge on the 4 \u03BCF.
Voltage on the 12 \u03BCF part = 24/12 = 2 V, so the charge on the 9 \u03BCF = 9 \xD7 2 = 18 \u03BCC.
Q = 24 + 18 = 42 \u03BCC.
E = kQ/r\xB2 = 9\xD710\u2079 \xD7 42\xD710\u207B\u2076 / 30\xB2 = 420 N/C.
Answer: (c)`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Three capacitors each of 4 \u03BCF are to be connected in such a way that the effective capacitance is 6 \u03BCF. This can be done by connecting them",o:["all in series","all in parallel","two in parallel and one in series","two in series and one in parallel"],c:3,s:`Two 4 \u03BCF in series give 4/2 = 2 \u03BCF. Adding the third 4 \u03BCF in parallel gives 2 + 4 = 6 \u03BCF.
Check the others: all in series = 4/3 \u03BCF; all in parallel = 12 \u03BCF; two in parallel (8 \u03BCF) in series with one (4 \u03BCF) = 8/3 \u03BCF.
Answer: (d)`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Figure shows a network of capacitors where the numbers indicates capacitances in micro Farad. The value of capacitance C if the equivalent capacitance between point A and B is to be 1 \u03BCF is",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Network between A and B: capacitor C in series with two branches in parallel. Left branch: a vertical capacitor then two capacitors in parallel. Right branch: a horizontal capacitor then a section that has a vertical capacitor in series with a horizontal one, in parallel with a vertical capacitor.",d:[["c",15,38,2.4,"F"],["t",10,60,"A","m"],["l",15,38,37,38],["l",37,30,37,46],["l",43,30,43,46],["l",43,38,80,38],["l",80,38,107,38],["l",107,30,107,46],["l",113,30,113,46],["l",113,38,140,38],["l",140,38,250,38],["l",80,38,80,62],["l",72,62,88,62],["l",72,68,88,68],["l",80,68,80,95],["l",80,95,105,95],["l",80,95,80,107],["l",72,107,88,107],["l",72,113,88,113],["l",80,113,80,125],["l",105,95,105,107],["l",97,107,113,107],["l",97,113,113,113],["l",105,113,105,125],["l",80,125,105,125],["l",92,125,92,145],["l",92,145,285,145],["c",285,145,2.4,"F"],["t",296,149,"B"],["l",140,38,140,62],["l",132,62,148,62],["l",132,68,148,68],["l",140,68,140,92],["l",140,92,197,92],["l",197,84,197,100],["l",203,84,203,100],["l",203,92,250,92],["l",250,38,250,62],["l",242,62,258,62],["l",242,68,258,68],["l",250,68,250,92],["l",250,92,250,145],["c",80,38,2.4,"F"],["c",140,38,2.4,"F"],["c",250,92,2.4,"F"],["t",40,24,"C","m"],["t",110,24,"1","m"],["t",70,68,"8","e"],["t",74,113,"2","e"],["t",111,113,"2"],["t",130,68,"6","e"],["t",200,110,"12","m"],["t",262,68,"4"]]},o:["{32;23} \u03BCF","{31;23} \u03BCF","{33;23} \u03BCF","{34;23} \u03BCF"],c:0,s:`Find the network to the right of C.
Branch from the first node: 8 in series with two 2's in parallel (4): 8\xD74/12 = 8/3 \u03BCF.
Other branch: the vertical 4 is in parallel with (6 in series with 12 = 4), giving 8 \u03BCF; this is in series with the horizontal 1: 8/9 \u03BCF.
The two branches are in parallel: 8/3 + 8/9 = 32/9 \u03BCF.
Series with C: C(32/9)/(C + 32/9) = 1, so 32C/9 = C + 32/9, (23/9)C = 32/9, C = 32/23 \u03BCF.
Answer: (a)`},{y:"JEE Main 2015",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"In the given circuit, charge Q\u2082 on the 2 \u03BCF capacitor changes as C is varied from 1 \u03BCF to 3 \u03BCF. Q\u2082 as a function of 'C' is given properly by (figures are drawn schematically and are not to scale)",fig:{w:340,h:265,alt:"A circuit with capacitor C in series with a parallel pair of 1 and 2 microfarad and a battery E, followed by four graphs of charge Q2 against C: (a) convex rising, (b) falling, (c) straight rising, (d) concave rising and flattening.",d:[["l",60,35,60,78],["l",290,35,290,78],["l",60,35,82,35],["l",82,27,82,43],["l",88,27,88,43],["l",88,35,110,35],["l",110,18,110,52],["l",230,18,230,52],["l",110,18,167,18],["l",167,10,167,26],["l",173,10,173,26],["l",173,18,230,18],["l",110,52,167,52],["l",167,44,167,60],["l",173,44,173,60],["l",173,52,230,52],["l",230,35,290,35],["l",60,78,172,78],["l",172,68,172,88],["l",178,73,178,83],["l",178,78,290,78],["c",110,35,2.4,"F"],["c",230,35,2.4,"F"],["t",85,22,"C","m"],["t",170,10,"1 \u03BCF","m"],["t",170,72,"2 \u03BCF","m"],["t",205,124,"E","m"],["l",35,158,160,158,"a"],["l",35,158,35,102,"a"],["p","M60 134 Q118 134 130 110"],["l",60,158,60,134,"d"],["l",130,158,130,110,"d"],["t",31,116,"Q\u2082","es"],["t",165,162,"C","s"],["t",60,170,"1 \u03BCF","ms"],["t",130,170,"3 \u03BCF","ms"],["t",8,106,"(a)","s"],["l",205,158,330,158,"a"],["l",205,158,205,102,"a"],["p","M230 110 Q242 144 300 144"],["l",230,158,230,110,"d"],["l",300,158,300,144,"d"],["t",201,116,"Q\u2082","es"],["t",335,162,"C","s"],["t",230,170,"1 \u03BCF","ms"],["t",300,170,"3 \u03BCF","ms"],["t",178,106,"(b)","s"],["l",35,243,160,243,"a"],["l",35,243,35,187,"a"],["l",60,221,130,197],["l",60,243,60,221,"d"],["l",130,243,130,197,"d"],["t",31,201,"Q\u2082","es"],["t",165,247,"C","s"],["t",60,255,"1 \u03BCF","ms"],["t",130,255,"3 \u03BCF","ms"],["t",8,191,"(c)","s"],["l",205,243,330,243,"a"],["l",205,243,205,187,"a"],["p","M230 219 Q242 195 300 195"],["l",230,243,230,219,"d"],["l",300,243,300,195,"d"],["t",201,201,"Q\u2082","es"],["t",335,247,"C","s"],["t",230,255,"1 \u03BCF","ms"],["t",300,255,"3 \u03BCF","ms"],["t",178,191,"(d)","s"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:3,s:`C is in series with the parallel pair 1 \u03BCF + 2 \u03BCF = 3 \u03BCF, across the battery E.
Voltage across the pair = E\xB7C/(C + 3) (in \u03BCF).
Q\u2082 = 2 \xD7 that = 2EC/(C + 3).
At C = 1 \u03BCF, Q\u2082 = E/2, not zero. As C increases Q\u2082 rises, but the slope keeps falling and the curve flattens (towards 2E).
A rising, concave curve that starts above zero is graph (d).
Answer: (d)`},{y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"In figure is shown a system of four capacitors connected across a 10 V battery. Charge that will flow from switch S when it is closed is",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Four capacitors across a 10 V battery: top branch 2 microfarad then node a then 3 microfarad; bottom branch 3 microfarad then node b then 2 microfarad; a switch S joins a and b.",d:[["l",50,35,50,145],["l",290,35,290,145],["l",50,35,97,35],["l",97,27,97,43],["l",103,27,103,43],["l",103,35,170,35],["l",170,35,237,35],["l",237,27,237,43],["l",243,27,243,43],["l",243,35,290,35],["l",50,95,97,95],["l",97,87,97,103],["l",103,87,103,103],["l",103,95,170,95],["l",170,95,237,95],["l",237,87,237,103],["l",243,87,243,103],["l",243,95,290,95],["l",170,35,170,50],["l",170,50,180,72],["c",170,80,2.2,"F"],["l",170,80,170,95],["l",50,145,167,145],["l",167,135,167,155],["l",173,140,173,150],["l",173,145,290,145],["c",170,35,2.4,"F"],["c",170,95,2.4,"F"],["t",100,21,"2 \u03BCF","m"],["t",240,21,"3 \u03BCF","m"],["t",100,117,"3 \u03BCF","m"],["t",240,117,"2 \u03BCF","m"],["t",178,29,"a","s"],["t",178,112,"b","s"],["t",188,66,"S"],["t",170,167,"10 V","m"]]},o:["5 \u03BCC from b to a","20 \u03BCC from a to b","5 \u03BCC from a to b","zero"],c:0,s:`Take the right rail as 0 V; the long plate of the battery is positive, so the left rail is at +10 V.
Top branch (2 \u03BCF then 3 \u03BCF): the smaller capacitor takes the larger voltage, so the 3 \u03BCF has 4 V and V_a = 4 V.
Bottom branch (3 \u03BCF then 2 \u03BCF): the 2 \u03BCF has 6 V, so V_b = 6 V. Point b is higher.
Nodes a and b each hold zero net charge. After closing S the joined node is at x with zero net charge: 2(x\u221210) + 3x + 3(x\u221210) + 2x = 0, so x = 5 V.
Charge now on node a = 2(5\u221210) + 3(5) = +5 \u03BCC; it was 0. So +5 \u03BCC flows from b to a.
Answer: (a)`},{y:"JEE Main 2014",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor is made of two circular plates separated by a distance of 5 mm and with a dielectric of dielectric constant 2.2 between them. When the electric field in the dielectric is 3 \xD7 10\u2074 V/m, the charge density of the positive plate will be close to",o:["6 \xD7 10\u2074 C/m\xB2","6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 C/m\xB2","3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 C/m\xB2","3 \xD7 10\u2074 C/m\xB2"],c:1,s:`With a dielectric, the field is E = \u03C3/(K\u03B5\u2080), where \u03C3 is the free charge density on the plate.
\u03C3 = K\u03B5\u2080E = 2.2 \xD7 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 3\xD710\u2074.
\u03C3 \u2248 5.8\xD710\u207B\u2077 C/m\xB2 \u2248 6\xD710\u207B\u2077 C/m\xB2.
The 5 mm separation is not needed.
Answer: (b)`},{y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A parallel plate capacitor is made of two plates of length l, width w and separated by distance d. A dielectric slab (dielectric constant K) that fits exactly between the plates is held near the edge of the plates. It is pulled into the capacitor by a force F = \u2212{\u2202U;\u2202x}, where U is the energy of the capacitor when dielectric is inside the capacitor up to distance x (see figure). If the charge on the capacitor is Q then the force on the dielectric when it is near the edge is:",fig:{w:340,h:230,alt:"Two vertical plates of length l separated by d with a dielectric slab pulled into the gap from the top by a distance x.",d:[["l",130,20,130,190,"B"],["l",190,20,190,190,"B"],["r",131,6,58,54,"f"],["l",215,20,215,60,"b"],["t",222,44,"x"],["t",160,36,"K","m"],["l",100,20,100,190,"b"],["t",92,108,"l","e"],["l",130,208,190,208,"b"],["t",160,224,"d","m"]]},o:["{Q\xB2d;2wl\xB2\u03B5\u2080}K","{Q\xB2w;2dl\xB2\u03B5\u2080}(K\u22121)","{Q\xB2d;2wl\xB2\u03B5\u2080}(K\u22121)","{Q\xB2w;2dl\xB2\u03B5\u2080}K"],c:2,s:`The charge Q is fixed, so U = Q\xB2/2C and F = \u2212dU/dx = (Q\xB2/2C\xB2)(dC/dx).
With the slab in by x, the capacitor is two parts in parallel: C = \u03B5\u2080w[l + (K\u22121)x]/d, so dC/dx = \u03B5\u2080w(K\u22121)/d.
Near the edge x \u2248 0, so C = \u03B5\u2080wl/d.
F = (Q\xB2/2) \xB7 d\xB2/(\u03B5\u2080\xB2w\xB2l\xB2) \xB7 \u03B5\u2080w(K\u22121)/d = Q\xB2d(K\u22121)/(2\u03B5\u2080wl\xB2).
Answer: (c)`},{y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"Two capacitors C\u2081 and C\u2082 are charged to 120 V and 200 V respectively. It is found that by connecting them together the potential on each one can be made zero. Then",o:["9C\u2081 = 4C\u2082","5C\u2081 = 3C\u2082","3C\u2081 = 5C\u2082","3C\u2081 + 5C\u2082 = 0"],c:2,s:`For both potentials to become zero after joining, the charges must cancel exactly: join the plates of opposite sign.
Q\u2081 = 120C\u2081 and Q\u2082 = 200C\u2082.
Net charge zero: 120C\u2081 = 200C\u2082, so 3C\u2081 = 5C\u2082.
Answer: (c)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"In the given circuit, a charge of +80 \u03BCC is given to the upper plate of the 4 \u03BCF capacitor. Then in the steady state, the charge on the upper plate of the 3 \u03BCF capacitor is",fig:{w:340,h:185,alt:"A 4 microfarad capacitor with +80 microcoulomb on its upper plate, connected below to a parallel pair of 2 and 3 microfarad capacitors whose lower plates are earthed.",d:[["c",170,12,3],["l",170,15,170,42],["l",170,15,170,42],["l",162,42,178,42],["l",162,48,178,48],["l",170,48,170,75],["l",100,75,240,75],["l",100,75,100,102],["l",92,102,108,102],["l",92,108,108,108],["l",100,108,100,145],["l",240,75,240,102],["l",232,102,248,102],["l",232,108,248,108],["l",240,108,240,145],["l",100,145,240,145],["c",170,75,2.4,"F"],["c",170,145,2.4,"F"],["l",170,144,170,150],["l",158,150,182,150],["l",162,155,178,155],["l",166,160,174,160],["t",150,38,"+80 \u03BCC","e"],["t",184,50,"4 \u03BCF"],["t",88,108,"2 \u03BCF","e"],["t",254,108,"3 \u03BCF"]]},o:["+32 \u03BCC","+40 \u03BCC","+48 \u03BCC","+80 \u03BCC"],c:2,s:`The +80 \u03BCC on the upper plate of the 4 \u03BCF induces \u221280 \u03BCC on its lower plate. So +80 \u03BCC is left on the connected upper plates of the 2 \u03BCF and 3 \u03BCF capacitors.
These two are in parallel (lower plates earthed), so they share the charge in proportion to their capacitance.
Charge on 3 \u03BCF = (3/(2+3)) \xD7 80 = 48 \u03BCC.
Answer: (c) +48 \u03BCC`},{y:"AIEEE 2012 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"Two circuits (a) and (b) have charged capacitors of capacitance C, 2C and 3C with open switches. Charges on each of the capacitor are as shown in the figures. On closing the switches",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Two circuits (a) and (b), each a loop with a switch, a left capacitor and a right capacitor, with the charges 2Q and Q marked on their upper plates. In (a) the capacitances are 3C and C; in (b) they are 2C and 2C.",d:[["l",30,30,30,120],["l",130,30,130,120],["l",30,120,130,120],["l",30,30,70,30],["l",70,30,90,18],["c",95,30,2.2,"F"],["l",95,30,130,30],["l",30,30,30,72],["l",22,72,38,72],["l",22,78,38,78],["l",30,78,30,120],["l",130,30,130,72],["l",122,72,138,72],["l",122,78,138,78],["l",130,78,130,120],["t",24,70,"2Q","e"],["t",24,86,"3C","e"],["t",138,70,"Q"],["t",138,86,"C"],["t",30,134,"L","m"],["t",130,134,"R","m"],["t",85,16,"S","m"],["l",190,30,190,120],["l",290,30,290,120],["l",190,120,290,120],["l",190,30,230,30],["l",230,30,250,18],["c",255,30,2.2,"F"],["l",255,30,290,30],["l",190,30,190,72],["l",182,72,198,72],["l",182,78,198,78],["l",190,78,190,120],["l",290,30,290,72],["l",282,72,298,72],["l",282,78,298,78],["l",290,78,290,120],["t",184,70,"2Q","e"],["t",184,86,"2C","e"],["t",298,70,"Q"],["t",298,86,"2C"],["t",190,134,"L","m"],["t",290,134,"R","m"],["t",245,16,"S","m"],["c",30,120,2.4,"F"],["c",130,120,2.4,"F"],["c",190,120,2.4,"F"],["c",290,120,2.4,"F"],["t",80,156,"(a)","m"],["t",240,156,"(b)","m"]]},o:["No charge flows in (a) but charge flows from R to L in (b)","Charges flow from L to R in both (a) and (b)","Charges flow from R to L in (a) and from L to R in (b)","No charge flows in (a) but charge flows from L to R in (b)"],c:2,s:`On closing S, the plates joined by the switch (the charged upper plates) go to a common potential, so charge flows from the higher-potential capacitor to the lower one. Voltage = charge/capacitance.
(a) Left: 2Q/3C = 0.67 Q/C. Right: Q/C. The right capacitor is higher, so charge flows from R side to L side.
(b) Left: 2Q/2C = Q/C. Right: Q/2C. The left is higher, so charge flows from L to R.
Answer: (c)`},{y:"AIEEE 2012 \xB7 Online",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A series combination of n\u2081 capacitors, each of value C\u2081, is charged by source of potential difference 4V. When another parallel combination of n\u2082 capacitors, each of value C\u2082, is charged by a source of potential difference V, it has the same total energy stored in it as the first combination has. The value of C\u2082 in terms of C\u2081 is then",o:["16 {n\u2082;n\u2081} C\u2081","{2C\u2081;n\u2081n\u2082}","2 {n\u2082;n\u2081} C\u2081","{16C\u2081;n\u2081n\u2082}"],c:3,s:`Series combination: C_s = C\u2081/n\u2081, energy U\u2081 = \xBD(C\u2081/n\u2081)(4V)\xB2 = 8C\u2081V\xB2/n\u2081.
Parallel combination: C_p = n\u2082C\u2082, energy U\u2082 = \xBDn\u2082C\u2082V\xB2.
Set U\u2081 = U\u2082: \xBDn\u2082C\u2082V\xB2 = 8C\u2081V\xB2/n\u2081.
C\u2082 = 16C\u2081/(n\u2081n\u2082).
Answer: (d)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A 2 \u03BCF capacitor is charged as shown in figure. The percentage of its stored energy dissipated after the switch S is turned to position 2 is",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Circuit: battery V on the left, a switch S that can touch contact 1 (battery) or contact 2 (the 8 microfarad capacitor on the right), and a 2 microfarad capacitor connected to the switch pole.",d:[["l",40,30,115,30],["l",40,30,40,140],["l",40,30,40,82],["l",30,82,50,82],["l",35,88,45,88],["l",40,88,40,140],["l",40,140,260,140],["c",115,30,2.5,"F"],["c",150,30,2.5,"F"],["l",150,30,260,30],["l",260,30,260,140],["l",132,52,117,33],["l",132,52,132,70],["l",132,70,132,92],["l",124,92,140,92],["l",124,98,140,98],["l",132,98,132,140],["l",260,30,260,82],["l",252,82,268,82],["l",252,88,268,88],["l",260,88,260,140],["t",112,22,"1","m"],["t",152,22,"2","m"],["t",140,52,"S"],["t",28,89,"V","e"],["t",120,100,"2 \u03BCF","e"],["t",248,92,"8 \u03BCF","e"]]},o:["0%","20%","75%","80%"],c:3,s:`In position 1 the 2 \u03BCF is charged to V: U\u2081 = \xBD(2)V\xB2 = V\xB2 (in \u03BCJ).
In position 2 the battery is out and the 2 \u03BCF shares its charge with the uncharged 8 \u03BCF. Common voltage = 2V/(2+8) = V/5.
Final energy = \xBD(10)(V/5)\xB2 = V\xB2/5.
Energy lost = V\xB2 \u2212 V\xB2/5 = 4V\xB2/5, which is 80% of the initial energy.
Answer: (d)`},{y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor with air between the plates has a capacitance of 9 pF. The separation between its plates is d. The space between the plates is now filled with two dielectrics. One of the dielectrics has dielectric constant k\u2081 = 3 and thickness {d;3} while the other one has dielectric constant k\u2082 = 6 and thickness {2d;3}. Capacitance of the capacitor is now",o:["20.25 pF","1.8 pF","45 pF","40.5 pF"],c:3,s:`The two layers are in series. Let C\u2080 = \u03B5\u2080A/d = 9 pF.
Layer 1: C\u2081 = k\u2081\u03B5\u2080A/(d/3) = 9C\u2080 = 81 pF.
Layer 2: C\u2082 = k\u2082\u03B5\u2080A/(2d/3) = 9C\u2080 = 81 pF.
Series: C = 81/2 = 40.5 pF.
Answer: (d)`},{y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A parallel plate condenser with a dielectric of dielectric constant K between the plates has a capacity C and is charged to a potential V volt. The dielectric slab is slowly removed from between the plates and then reinserted. The net work done by the system in this process is",o:["zero","\xBD(K \u2212 1)CV\xB2","{CV\xB2(K\u22121);K}","(K \u2212 1)CV\xB2"],c:0,s:`The electric force on the slab is conservative and the process is slow.
Removing the slab and putting it back returns the system to exactly its starting state (same capacitance, same charge, same energy).
So the work done by the system on the way out is cancelled by the work on the way in.
Net work = 0.
Answer: (a)`},{y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A battery is used to charge a parallel plate capacitor till the potential difference between the plates becomes equal to the electromotive force of the battery. The ratio of the energy stored in the capacitor and the work done by the battery will be",o:["1/2","1","2","1/4"],c:0,s:`Energy stored in the capacitor U = \xBDCV\xB2.
The battery moves charge Q = CV through potential V, so its work W = QV = CV\xB2.
U/W = 1/2 (the other half is lost as heat in the wires).
Answer: (a)`},{y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"ecn",st:"Charge sharing and earthing",q:"Two spherical conductors A and B of radii 1 mm and 2 mm are separated by a distance of 5 cm and are uniformly charged. If the spheres are connected by a conducting wire then in equilibrium condition, the ratio of the magnitude of the electric fields at the surface of spheres A and B is",o:["1 : 4","4 : 1","1 : 2","2 : 1"],c:3,s:`Joined by a wire, both spheres reach the same potential V.
At the surface of a sphere V = kq/r and E = kq/r\xB2 = V/r, so E \u221D 1/r.
E_A/E_B = r_B/r_A = 2 mm/1 mm = 2.
Ratio = 2 : 1.
Answer: (d)`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A fully charged capacitor has a capacitance C. It is discharged through a small coil of resistance wire embedded in a thermally insulated block of specific heat capacity s and mass m. If the temperature of the block is raised by \u0394T, the potential difference V across the capacitor is",o:["{ms\u0394T;C}","\u221A{2ms\u0394T;C}","\u221A{2mC\u0394T;s}","{mC\u0394T;s}"],c:1,s:`The energy stored in the capacitor, \xBDCV\xB2, is turned into heat in the block.
Heat gained = ms\u0394T.
\xBDCV\xB2 = ms\u0394T, so V = \u221A(2ms\u0394T/C).
Answer: (b)`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"A parallel plate capacitor is made by stacking n equally spaced plates connected alternately. If the capacitance between any two adjacent plates is C then the resultant capacitance is",o:["C","nC","(n \u2212 1)C","(n + 1)C"],c:2,s:`Plates 1, 3, 5, \u2026 are joined to one terminal and plates 2, 4, 6, \u2026 to the other.
n plates make n \u2212 1 gaps, so there are n \u2212 1 capacitors, each of capacitance C, all in parallel.
Total = (n \u2212 1)C.
Answer: (c)`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"The work done in placing a charge of 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 coulomb on a condenser of capacity 100 micro-farad is",o:["16 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB2 joule","3.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2076 joule","4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 joule","32 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB2 joule"],c:3,s:`Work done = energy stored = q\xB2/2C.
W = (8\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2078)\xB2 / (2 \xD7 100\xD710\u207B\u2076) = 64\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2076 / (2\xD710\u207B\u2074).
W = 32\xD710\u207B\xB3\xB2 J.
Answer: (d)`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A sheet of aluminium foil of negligible thickness is introduced between the plates of a capacitor. The capacitance of the capacitor",o:["decreases","remains unchanged","becomes infinite","increases"],c:1,s:`The foil, parallel to the plates, splits the gap into two gaps d\u2081 and d\u2082 and makes two capacitors in series.
Because the foil is very thin, d\u2081 + d\u2082 = d.
1/C = d\u2081/\u03B5\u2080A + d\u2082/\u03B5\u2080A = d/\u03B5\u2080A, the same as before.
The capacitance remains unchanged.
Answer: (b)`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Capacitance (in F) of a spherical conductor with radius 1 m is",o:["1.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070","10\u207B\u2076","9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079","10\u207B\xB3"],c:0,s:`Capacitance of an isolated sphere C = 4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R = R/(9\xD710\u2079).
For R = 1 m, C = 1/(9\xD710\u2079) = 1.1\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 F.
Answer: (a)`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"If there are n capacitors in parallel connected to V volt source, then the energy stored is equal to",o:["CV","\xBDnCV\xB2","CV\xB2","{1;2n}CV\xB2"],c:1,s:`In parallel each capacitor has the full voltage V.
Each stores \xBDCV\xB2, and there are n of them.
Total energy = n \xD7 \xBDCV\xB2 = \xBDnCV\xB2.
Answer: (b)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"Two identical capacitors have the same capacitance C. One of them is charged to potential V\u2081 and the other V\u2082. The negative ends of the capacitors are connected together. When the positive ends are also connected, the decrease in energy of the combined system is",o:["\xBCC(V\u2081\xB2 \u2212 V\u2082\xB2)","\xBCC(V\u2081\xB2 + V\u2082\xB2)","\xBCC(V\u2081 \u2212 V\u2082)\xB2","\xBCC(V\u2081 + V\u2082)\xB2"],c:2,s:`Charges of the same sign add: total charge C(V\u2081 + V\u2082) on total capacitance 2C, so V = (V\u2081 + V\u2082)/2.
Initial energy = \xBDC(V\u2081\xB2 + V\u2082\xB2). Final energy = \xBD(2C)[(V\u2081+V\u2082)/2]\xB2 = \xBCC(V\u2081+V\u2082)\xB2.
Decrease = \xBCC[2V\u2081\xB2 + 2V\u2082\xB2 \u2212 (V\u2081\xB2 + 2V\u2081V\u2082 + V\u2082\xB2)] = \xBCC(V\u2081 \u2212 V\u2082)\xB2.
Answer: (c)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"Consider the situation shown in the figure. The capacitor A has a charge q on it whereas B is uncharged. The charge appearing on the capacitor B long time after the switch is closed is",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Capacitor A with plates carrying +q (left plate) and -q (right plate); its right plate is joined by a switch S to the left plate of capacitor B; the right plate of B is connected to nothing.",d:[["l",50,25,50,125,"B"],["l",90,25,90,125,"B"],["l",170,25,170,125,"B"],["l",210,25,210,125,"B"],["t",58,36,"+","ms"],["t",58,56,"+","ms"],["t",58,76,"+","ms"],["t",58,96,"+","ms"],["t",58,116,"+","ms"],["t",82,36,"\u2212","ms"],["t",82,56,"\u2212","ms"],["t",82,76,"\u2212","ms"],["t",82,96,"\u2212","ms"],["t",82,116,"\u2212","ms"],["l",90,75,120,75],["l",120,75,138,63],["c",142,75,2.2,"F"],["l",142,75,170,75],["t",130,100,"S","m"],["t",70,144,"A","m"],["t",190,144,"B","m"],["t",50,14,"q","m"]]},o:["zero","q/2","q","2q"],c:0,s:`Closing S joins the right (negative) plate of A to the left plate of B.
The right plate of B is connected to nothing, so no charge can flow to it or away from it. It stays neutral.
With no charge available on the far plate, B cannot hold equal and opposite charges on its plates.
All the charge stays on A; B has zero charge.
Answer: (a)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor of area A, plate separation d and capacitance C is filled with three different dielectric materials having dielectric constant K\u2081, K\u2082 and K\u2083 as shown in the figure. If a single dielectric material is to be used to have the same capacitance C in this capacitor, then its dielectric constant K is given by",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"Parallel plates of area A, separation d. The upper half has K1 on the left and K2 on the right, each of area A/2; the lower half is K3 across the full area.",d:[["l",60,30,260,30,"B"],["l",60,130,260,130,"B"],["r",60,30,100,50,"f"],["r",160,30,100,50,"f"],["r",60,80,200,50,"f"],["t",110,60,"K\u2081","m"],["t",210,60,"K\u2082","m"],["t",160,110,"K\u2083","m"],["t",110,22,"A/2","m"],["t",210,22,"A/2","m"],["t",160,150,"A","m"],["l",40,30,40,130,"b"],["t",32,84,"d","e"]]},o:["{1;K} = {1;K\u2081} + {1;K\u2082} + {1;2K\u2083}","{1;K} = {1;K\u2081+K\u2082} + {1;2K\u2083}","K = {K\u2081K\u2082;K\u2081+K\u2082} + 2K\u2083","K = K\u2081 + K\u2082 + 2K\u2083"],c:1,s:`Top layer (thickness d/2): K\u2081 and K\u2082 side by side, each of area A/2, so they are in parallel: C_top = \u03B5\u2080(A/2)(K\u2081+K\u2082)/(d/2) = \u03B5\u2080A(K\u2081+K\u2082)/d.
Bottom layer (thickness d/2): K\u2083 over area A: C_bot = \u03B5\u2080AK\u2083/(d/2) = 2\u03B5\u2080AK\u2083/d.
The two layers are in series: 1/C = d/[\u03B5\u2080A(K\u2081+K\u2082)] + d/(2\u03B5\u2080AK\u2083).
For one material C = K\u03B5\u2080A/d, so 1/K = 1/(K\u2081+K\u2082) + 1/(2K\u2083).
Answer: (b)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"For the circuit shown in figure, which of the following statements is true?",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"Earth, switch S1, capacitor C1 (2 pF, charged to 30 V), switch S3, capacitor C2 (3 pF, charged to 20 V), switch S2 and earth in series along one line.",d:[["l",25,50,25,115],["l",25,109,25,115],["l",13,115,37,115],["l",17,120,33,120],["l",21,125,29,125],["l",25,50,45,50],["l",45,50,63,38],["c",67,50,2.2,"F"],["l",67,50,85,50],["l",85,50,115,50],["l",115,42,115,58],["l",121,42,121,58],["l",121,50,160,50],["l",160,50,177.5,50],["l",177.5,50,195.5,38],["c",199.5,50,2.2,"F"],["l",199.5,50,215,50],["l",215,50,240,50],["l",240,42,240,58],["l",246,42,246,58],["l",246,50,272,50],["l",272,50,282,50],["l",282,50,300,38],["c",304,50,2.2,"F"],["l",304,50,312,50],["l",312,50,312,115],["l",312,109,312,115],["l",300,115,324,115],["l",304,120,320,120],["l",308,125,316,125],["t",112,40,"+","ms"],["t",126,40,"\u2212","ms"],["t",237,40,"+","ms"],["t",251,40,"\u2212","ms"],["t",55,30,"S\u2081","m"],["t",188,30,"S\u2083","m"],["t",292,30,"S\u2082","m"],["t",118,74,"C\u2081 = 2 pF","ms"],["t",118,90,"V\u2081 = 30 V","ms"],["t",243,74,"C\u2082 = 3 pF","ms"],["t",243,90,"V\u2082 = 20 V","ms"]]},o:["With S\u2081 closed, V\u2081 = 15 V, V\u2082 = 20 V","With S\u2083 closed, V\u2081 = V\u2082 = 25 V","With S\u2081 and S\u2082 closed, V\u2081 = V\u2082 = 0","With S\u2081 and S\u2083 closed, V\u2081 = 30 V, V\u2082 = 20 V"],c:3,s:`Charge can change on a capacitor only if there is a complete closed conducting loop through it.
(a) Only S\u2081 closed: one end earthed, the other floating, no loop. V\u2081 stays 30 V, V\u2082 stays 20 V.
(b) Only S\u2083 closed: no connection to earth, no loop. Nothing changes, so not 25 V each.
(c) S\u2081 and S\u2082 closed, S\u2083 open: C\u2081 and C\u2082 are each connected to earth on one side only, no loop. Nothing changes.
(d) S\u2081 and S\u2083 closed, S\u2082 open: C\u2081 and C\u2082 in series from earth to a floating end, no loop. V\u2081 = 30 V, V\u2082 = 20 V stay. True.
Answer: (d)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Two identical metal plates are given positive charges Q\u2081 and Q\u2082 (< Q\u2081) respectively. If they are now brought close together to form a parallel plate capacitor with capacitance C, the potential difference between them is",o:["(Q\u2081 + Q\u2082)/(2C)","(Q\u2081 + Q\u2082)/C","(Q\u2081 \u2212 Q\u2082)/C","(Q\u2081 \u2212 Q\u2082)/(2C)"],c:3,s:`When two plates are brought close, the outer faces carry equal charge (Q\u2081 + Q\u2082)/2 each.
The inner (facing) faces then carry +(Q\u2081 \u2212 Q\u2082)/2 and \u2212(Q\u2081 \u2212 Q\u2082)/2.
The charge of the capacitor is q = (Q\u2081 \u2212 Q\u2082)/2.
V = q/C = (Q\u2081 \u2212 Q\u2082)/(2C).
Answer: (d)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1995",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A parallel plate capacitor of capacitance C is connected to a battery and is charged to a potential difference V. Another capacitor of capacitance 2C is similarly charged to a potential difference 2V. The charging battery is now disconnected and the capacitors are connected in parallel to each other in such a way that the positive terminal of one is connected to the negative terminal of the other. The final energy of the configuration is",o:["zero","{3;2}CV\xB2","{25;6}CV\xB2","{9;2}CV\xB2"],c:1,s:`Charges: Q\u2081 = CV and Q\u2082 = (2C)(2V) = 4CV. Opposite terminals joined, so net charge = 4CV \u2212 CV = 3CV.
Total capacitance = 3C, so the common voltage = 3CV/3C = V.
Final energy = \xBD(3C)V\xB2 = (3/2)CV\xB2.
Answer: (b)`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:`A medium having dielectric constant K > 1 fills the space between the plates of a parallel plate capacitor. The plates have large area, and the distance between them is d. The capacitor is connected to a battery of voltage V, as shown in figure (a). Now, both the plates are moved by a distance of d/2 from their original positions, as shown in figure (b).
In the process of going from the configuration depicted in figure (a) to that in figure (b), which of the following statement(s) is (are) correct?`,fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"Figure (a): a capacitor with a dielectric slab of thickness d filling the gap, connected to a battery V. Figure (b): the plates have been moved apart by d/2 on each side, leaving air gaps d/2 either side of the slab.",d:[["l",55,20,55,85,"B"],["l",105,20,105,85,"B"],["r",56,20,48,65,"f"],["l",55,85,55,135],["l",105,85,105,135],["l",55,135,74,135],["l",74,125,74,145],["l",80,130,80,140],["l",80,135,105,135],["l",55,12,105,12,"b"],["t",80,8,"d","ms"],["t",80,158,"Figure (a)","ms"],["t",46,150,"V","s"],["l",215,20,215,85,"B"],["l",315,20,315,85,"B"],["r",240,20,50,65,"f"],["l",215,85,215,135],["l",315,85,315,135],["l",215,135,262,135],["l",262,125,262,145],["l",268,130,268,140],["l",268,135,315,135],["l",215,98,240,98,"b"],["l",240,98,290,98,"b"],["l",290,98,315,98,"b"],["t",227,112,"d/2","ms"],["t",265,112,"d","ms"],["t",302,112,"d/2","ms"],["t",265,158,"Figure (b)","ms"],["t",236,150,"V","s"]]},o:["The electric field inside the dielectric material is reduced by a factor of 2K.","The capacitance is decreased by a factor of {1;K+1}.","The voltage between the capacitor plates is increased by a factor of (K + 1).","The work done in the process DOES NOT depend on the presence of the dielectric material."],c:1,s:`The battery stays connected, so the plate voltage is V throughout. This rules out (c).
(a): C = K\u03B5\u2080A/d. (b): the gap is 2d, with the slab (d) in the middle and air of total thickness d: C\u2032 = \u03B5\u2080A/(d + d/K) = K\u03B5\u2080A/[d(K+1)].
C\u2032/C = 1/(K+1): so (b) is correct.
Field in the slab E = \u03C3/(K\u03B5\u2080) = C\u2032V/(K\u03B5\u2080A) = V/[d(K+1)], compared with V/d before: reduced by a factor (K+1), not 2K. So (a) is wrong.
The force on a plate depends on the charge density, which depends on K, so the work depends on the dielectric: (d) is wrong.
Answer: (b) only`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor having plates of area S and plate separation d, has capacitance C\u2081 in air. When two dielectrics of different relative permittivities (\u03B5\u2081 = 2 and \u03B5\u2082 = 4) are introduced between the two plates as shown in the figure, the capacitance becomes C\u2082. The ratio {C\u2082;C\u2081} is",fig:{w:340,h:185,alt:"Side view of a capacitor: left plate positive, right plate negative, gap d. The lower half of the area is filled with epsilon 1 over the whole gap; in the upper half epsilon 1 fills the left half of the gap and epsilon 2 the right half.",d:[["l",70,20,70,140,"B"],["l",250,20,250,140,"B"],["r",70,20,180,120,"f"],["l",70,80,250,80,"d"],["l",160,20,160,80,"d"],["t",115,55,"\u03B5\u2081","m"],["t",205,55,"\u03B5\u2082","m"],["t",160,115,"\u03B5\u2081","m"],["t",258,54,"S/2"],["t",258,114,"S/2"],["l",70,160,250,160,"b"],["t",160,176,"d","m"],["l",160,12,250,12,"b"],["t",205,8,"d/2","ms"],["l",30,80,70,80],["t",24,76,"+","e"],["l",250,80,290,80],["t",296,84,"\u2212"]]},o:["6/5","5/3","7/5","7/3"],c:3,s:`Split the plate area into two halves of S/2 that are in parallel.
Lower half: \u03B5\u2081 over the full gap d: C = 2\u03B5\u2080(S/2)/d = \u03B5\u2080S/d.
Upper half: \u03B5\u2081 (thickness d/2) in series with \u03B5\u2082 (thickness d/2): 1/C = (d/2)/(2\u03B5\u2080S/2) + (d/2)/(4\u03B5\u2080S/2) = d/(2\u03B5\u2080S) + d/(4\u03B5\u2080S) = 3d/(4\u03B5\u2080S), so C = 4\u03B5\u2080S/(3d).
C\u2082 = \u03B5\u2080S/d + 4\u03B5\u2080S/(3d) = 7\u03B5\u2080S/(3d), and C\u2081 = \u03B5\u2080S/d.
C\u2082/C\u2081 = 7/3.
Answer: (d)`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor has a dielectric slab of dielectric constant K between its plates that covers 1/3 of the area of its plates, as shown in the figure. The total capacitance of the capacitor is C while that of the portion with dielectric in between is C\u2081. When the capacitor is charged, the plate area covered by the dielectric gets charge Q\u2081 and the rest of the area gets charge Q\u2082. The electric field in the dielectric is E\u2081 and that in the other portion is E\u2082. Choose the correct option/options, ignoring edge effects.",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"Two vertical plates; the top third of the gap is filled with dielectric K, with charge Q1 on that part of the plate and field E1; the lower two thirds are air with charge Q2 and field E2.",d:[["l",100,20,100,190,"B"],["l",240,20,240,190,"B"],["r",101,20,138,57,"f"],["t",92,52,"Q\u2081","e"],["t",92,130,"Q\u2082","e"],["t",130,52,"K","m"],["t",200,52,"E\u2081","m"],["t",170,130,"E\u2082","m"]]},o:["E\u2081/E\u2082 = 1","E\u2081/E\u2082 = 1/K","Q\u2081/Q\u2082 = 3/K","C/C\u2081 = {2+K;K}"],c:[0,3],s:`The plates are conductors, so the potential difference V across both portions is the same, and the gap d is the same. So E = V/d in both: E\u2081 = E\u2082. (a) is correct, (b) is wrong.
Charge on a portion = (its capacitance) \xD7 V. Dielectric part has area A/3: Q\u2081 = K\u03B5\u2080(A/3)V/d. The rest has area 2A/3: Q\u2082 = \u03B5\u2080(2A/3)V/d.
Q\u2081/Q\u2082 = K/2, not 3/K, so (c) is wrong.
C\u2081 = K\u03B5\u2080A/(3d) and C = \u03B5\u2080A(K+2)/(3d), so C/C\u2081 = (K+2)/K. (d) is correct.
Answer: (a) and (d)`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"In the circuit shown in the figure, there are two parallel plate capacitors each of capacitance C. The switch S\u2081 is pressed first to fully charge the capacitor C\u2081 and then released. The switch S\u2082 is then pressed to charge the capacitor C\u2082. After some time, S\u2082 is released and then S\u2083 is pressed. After some time,",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"Circuit: a 2V0 battery on the left (top terminal positive) with switch S1, capacitor C1, switch S2, capacitor C2, switch S3 and a V0 battery on the right (top terminal negative), all joined by a common bottom wire.",d:[["l",40,30,40,80],["l",40,87,40,140],["l",30,80,50,80],["l",35,87,45,87],["l",40,30,65,30],["l",65,30,83,18],["c",87,30,2.2,"F"],["l",87,30,110,30],["l",110,30,140,30],["l",140,30,158,18],["c",162,30,2.2,"F"],["l",162,30,190,30],["l",190,30,215,30],["l",215,30,233,18],["c",237,30,2.2,"F"],["l",237,30,260,30],["l",260,30,260,80],["l",260,87,260,140],["l",252,80,268,80],["l",247,87,273,87],["l",110,30,110,82],["l",102,82,118,82],["l",102,88,118,88],["l",110,88,110,140],["l",190,30,190,82],["l",182,82,198,82],["l",182,88,198,88],["l",190,88,190,140],["l",40,140,260,140],["c",110,30,2.4,"F"],["c",190,30,2.4,"F"],["c",110,140,2.4,"F"],["c",190,140,2.4,"F"],["t",28,92,"2V\u2080","e"],["t",278,92,"V\u2080"],["t",122,90,"C\u2081"],["t",202,90,"C\u2082"],["t",75,14,"S\u2081","m"],["t",150,14,"S\u2082","m"],["t",225,14,"S\u2083","m"]]},o:["the charge on the upper plate of C\u2081 is 2CV\u2080.","the charge on the upper plate of C\u2081 is CV\u2080.","the charge on the upper plate of C\u2082 is 0.","the charge on the upper plate of C\u2082 is \u2212CV\u2080."],c:[1,3],s:`S\u2081: C\u2081 is charged by the 2V\u2080 battery, whose top terminal is positive. Upper plate of C\u2081 = +2CV\u2080.
S\u2082 (S\u2081 released): C\u2081 shares its charge with the uncharged C\u2082 (parallel): total 2CV\u2080 over 2C, so each is at V\u2080 and each upper plate is +CV\u2080.
S\u2082 released, S\u2083 pressed: C\u2082 is connected across the V\u2080 battery whose top terminal is negative, so C\u2082 charges to V\u2080 with its upper plate at \u2212CV\u2080. C\u2081 is isolated and keeps +CV\u2080.
So (b) and (d) are correct.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A dielectric slab of thickness d is inserted in a parallel plate capacitor whose negative plate is at x = 0 and positive plate is at x = 3d. The slab is equidistant from the plates. The capacitor is given some charge. As one goes from 0 to 3d,",o:["the magnitude of the electric field remains the same","the direction of the electric field remains the same","the electric potential increases continuously","the electric potential increases at first, then decreases and again increases"],c:[1,2],s:`The slab occupies d < x < 2d. The plate charge is fixed, so \u03C3 is the same everywhere.
Field in air = \u03C3/\u03B5\u2080; field in the slab = \u03C3/(K\u03B5\u2080). The magnitude changes, so (a) is false.
The field always points from the positive plate (x = 3d) to the negative plate (x = 0): the direction never changes, so (b) is true.
Since V = \u2212\u222BE dx and E points towards \u2212x, potential rises all the way from x = 0 to x = 3d, with a smaller slope in the slab. (c) is true, (d) is false.
Answer: (b) and (c)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"egs",st:"Gauss's law and symmetric bodies",q:"The magnitude of electric field E\u20D7 in the annular region of a charged cylindrical capacitor",o:["is same throughout","is higher near the outer cylinder than near the inner cylinder","varies as 1/r, where r is the distance from axis","varies as 1/r\xB2, where r is the distance from axis."],c:2,s:`Take a coaxial cylindrical Gaussian surface of radius r and length l between the cylinders.
Flux = E \xD7 2\u03C0rl = \u03BBl/\u03B5\u2080, where \u03BB is the charge per unit length on the inner cylinder.
So E = \u03BB/(2\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r), that is E \u221D 1/r.
The field is largest near the inner cylinder and falls with distance from the axis.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1991",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor of plate area A and plate separation d is charged to potential difference V and then the battery is disconnected. A slab of dielectric constant K is then inserted between the plates of the capacitor so as to fill the space between the plates. If Q, E and W denote respectively, the magnitude of charge on each plate, the electric field between the plates (after the slab is inserted), and the workdone on the system, in question, in the process of inserting the slab, then",o:["Q = {\u03B5\u2080AV;d}","Q = {\u03B5\u2080KAV;d}","E = {V;Kd}","W = {\u03B5\u2080AV\xB2;2d}[1 \u2212 {1;K}]"],c:[0,2,3],s:`The battery is disconnected, so the charge stays Q = CV = \u03B5\u2080AV/d. So (a) is true and (b) is false.
The slab reduces the field by K: E = E\u2080/K = V/(Kd). So (c) is true.
Energy before = \u03B5\u2080AV\xB2/(2d). After = (energy before)/K, because Q is constant and C becomes KC.
Energy change = {\u03B5\u2080AV\xB2;2d}(1 \u2212 1/K); its magnitude is the W given in (d), so (d) is accepted.
Answer: (a), (c), (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1990",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Seven capacitors each of capacitance 2 \xB5F are to be connected in a configuration to obtain an effective capacitance of ({10;11}) \xB5F. Which of the combinations shown in figure will achieve the desired result?",fig:{w:340,h:250,alt:"Four circuit options, each a diamond-shaped bank of 2 \xB5F capacitors followed by a chain of capacitors in series",d:[["t",4,12,"(a)","s"],["l",8,62,30,62],["l",30,34,30,90],["l",70,34,70,90],["l",30,34,47,34],["l",47,29,47,39],["l",53,29,53,39],["l",53,34,70,34],["l",30,48,47,48],["l",47,43,47,53],["l",53,43,53,53],["l",53,48,70,48],["l",30,62,47,62],["l",47,57,47,67],["l",53,57,53,67],["l",53,62,70,62],["l",30,76,47,76],["l",47,71,47,81],["l",53,71,53,81],["l",53,76,70,76],["l",30,90,47,90],["l",47,85,47,95],["l",53,85,53,95],["l",53,90,70,90],["l",70,62,83,62],["l",84,56,84,68],["l",88,56,88,68],["l",88,62,99,62],["l",100,56,100,68],["l",104,56,104,68],["l",104,62,110,62],["t",174,12,"(b)","s"],["l",178,62,200,62],["l",200,41,200,83],["l",240,41,240,83],["l",200,41,217,41],["l",217,36,217,46],["l",223,36,223,46],["l",223,41,240,41],["l",200,55,217,55],["l",217,50,217,60],["l",223,50,223,60],["l",223,55,240,55],["l",200,69,217,69],["l",217,64,217,74],["l",223,64,223,74],["l",223,69,240,69],["l",200,83,217,83],["l",217,78,217,88],["l",223,78,223,88],["l",223,83,240,83],["l",240,62,253,62],["l",254,56,254,68],["l",258,56,258,68],["l",258,62,269,62],["l",270,56,270,68],["l",274,56,274,68],["l",274,62,285,62],["l",286,56,286,68],["l",290,56,290,68],["l",290,62,296,62],["t",4,137,"(c)","s"],["l",8,187,30,187],["l",30,173,30,201],["l",70,173,70,201],["l",30,173,47,173],["l",47,168,47,178],["l",53,168,53,178],["l",53,173,70,173],["l",30,187,47,187],["l",47,182,47,192],["l",53,182,53,192],["l",53,187,70,187],["l",30,201,47,201],["l",47,196,47,206],["l",53,196,53,206],["l",53,201,70,201],["l",70,187,83,187],["l",84,181,84,193],["l",88,181,88,193],["l",88,187,99,187],["l",100,181,100,193],["l",104,181,104,193],["l",104,187,115,187],["l",116,181,116,193],["l",120,181,120,193],["l",120,187,131,187],["l",132,181,132,193],["l",136,181,136,193],["l",136,187,142,187],["t",174,137,"(d)","s"],["l",178,187,200,187],["l",200,180,200,194],["l",240,180,240,194],["l",200,180,217,180],["l",217,175,217,185],["l",223,175,223,185],["l",223,180,240,180],["l",200,194,217,194],["l",217,189,217,199],["l",223,189,223,199],["l",223,194,240,194],["l",240,187,253,187],["l",254,181,254,193],["l",258,181,258,193],["l",258,187,269,187],["l",270,181,270,193],["l",274,181,274,193],["l",274,187,285,187],["l",286,181,286,193],["l",290,181,290,193],["l",290,187,301,187],["l",302,181,302,193],["l",306,181,306,193],["l",306,187,317,187],["l",318,181,318,193],["l",322,181,322,193],["l",322,187,328,187]]},o:["Circuit (a)","Circuit (b)","Circuit (c)","Circuit (d)"],c:0,s:`Each circuit has a diamond bank of capacitors joined to the same two end points (so they are in parallel), followed by a chain of capacitors in series.
Circuit (a): 5 in parallel = 10 \xB5F; 2 in series = 1 \xB5F. These two parts are in series: (10 \xD7 1)/(10 + 1) = 10/11 \xB5F.
Circuit (b): 8 \xB5F with 2/3 \xB5F gives 8/13 \xB5F. Circuit (c): 6 \xB5F with 0.5 \xB5F gives 6/13 \xB5F. Circuit (d): 4 \xB5F with 0.4 \xB5F gives 4/11 \xB5F.
Only (a) gives 10/11 \xB5F. Answer: (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1987",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A parallel plate capacitor is charged and the charging battery is then disconnected. If the plates of the capacitor are moved farther apart by means of insulating handles",o:["the charge on the capacitor increases","the voltage across the plates increases","the capacitance increases","the electrostatic energy stored in the capacitor increases."],c:[1,3],s:`The battery is disconnected, so the charge Q stays constant. (a) is false.
C = \u03B5\u2080A/d falls when d increases. (c) is false.
V = Q/C increases. (b) is true.
U = Q\xB2/(2C) increases, because work is done against the attraction of the plates. (d) is true.
Answer: (b), (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1985",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate air capacitor is connected to a battery. The quantities charge, voltage, electric field and energy associated with this capacitor are given by Q\u2080, V\u2080, E\u2080 and U\u2080 respectively. A dielectric slab is now introduced to fill the space between the plates with battery still in connection. The corresponding quantities now given by Q, V, E and U are related to the previous one as",o:["Q > Q\u2080","V > V\u2080","E > E\u2080","U > U\u2080."],c:[0,3],s:`The battery stays connected, so the voltage is fixed: V = V\u2080. (b) is false.
E = V/d is unchanged. (c) is false.
The slab makes C = KC\u2080 with K > 1, so Q = CV = KQ\u2080 > Q\u2080. (a) is true.
U = \xBDCV\xB2 = KU\u2080 > U\u2080. (d) is true.
Answer: (a), (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Four capacitors each of capacitance 16 \xB5F are connected as shown in the figure. The capacitance between points A and B is ____ (in \xB5F).",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"Four capacitors C in a row with wires joining some nodes; terminals A and B",d:[["l",20,70,57,70],["l",57,62,57,78],["l",63,62,63,78],["l",63,70,92,70],["l",92,70,127,70],["l",127,62,127,78],["l",133,62,133,78],["l",133,70,165,70],["l",165,70,197,70],["l",197,62,197,78],["l",203,62,203,78],["l",203,70,240,70],["l",240,70,272,70],["l",272,62,272,78],["l",278,62,278,78],["l",278,70,310,70],["l",20,70,20,25],["l",20,25,310,25],["l",165,25,165,70],["l",310,25,310,70],["l",310,70,310,125],["l",95,70,95,100],["l",95,100,240,100],["l",240,100,240,70],["l",167,100,167,125],["c",95,70,2,"F"],["c",240,70,2,"F"],["c",165,70,2,"F"],["c",167,100,2,"F"],["c",167,125,3,"F"],["c",310,125,3,"F"],["t",60,58,"C","ms"],["t",130,58,"C","ms"],["t",200,58,"C","ms"],["t",275,58,"C","ms"],["t",167,140,"A","m"],["t",310,140,"B","m"]]},n:64,s:`Follow the wires. The top wire joins the left end of the chain, the junction between the 2nd and 3rd capacitors, and the point B.
The wire from A joins the node between the 1st and 2nd capacitors and the node between the 3rd and 4th capacitors.
So every capacitor has one plate on the A group and the other on the B group: all four are in parallel.
C = 4 \xD7 16 = 64 \xB5F.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor has charge 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C. A dielectric slab is inserted between the plates and almost fills the space between the plates. If the induced charge on one face of the slab is 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C then the dielectric constant of the slab is ____.",n:5,s:`Induced charge on a dielectric face is q' = Q(1 \u2212 1/K).
4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 (1 \u2212 1/K).
1 \u2212 1/K = 0.8, so 1/K = 0.2.
K = 5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"Space between the plates of a parallel plate capacitor of plate area 4 cm\xB2 and separation of (d) 1.77 mm, is filled with uniform dielectric materials with dielectric constants (3 and 5) as shown in figure. Another capacitor of capacitance 7.5 pF is connected in parallel with it. The effective capacitance of this combination is ____ pF. (Given \u03B5\u2080 = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 F/m)",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Parallel plate capacitor with two stacked dielectric layers of constants 5 (top) and 3 (bottom), each of thickness d/2",d:[["l",70,40,270,40,"B"],["l",70,120,270,120,"B"],["r",70,40,200,40,"f"],["r",70,80,200,40],["l",170,10,170,40],["l",170,120,170,150],["t",170,64,"k = 5","ms"],["t",170,104,"k = 3","ms"],["l",290,40,290,80,"b"],["l",290,80,290,120,"b"],["t",296,64,"d/2","s"],["t",296,104,"d/2","s"]]},n:15,s:`Without dielectric: C\u2080 = \u03B5\u2080A/d = 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 4\xD710\u207B\u2074 / 1.77\xD710\u207B\xB3 = 2 pF.
Each layer has thickness d/2, so its capacitance is K \xD7 \u03B5\u2080A/(d/2) = 2KC\u2080: 12 pF (K = 3) and 20 pF (K = 5).
The layers are in series: (12 \xD7 20)/(12 + 20) = 7.5 pF.
In parallel with 7.5 pF: 7.5 + 7.5 = 15 pF.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A parallel plate capacitor consisting of two circular plates of radius 10 cm is being charged by a constant current of 0.15 A. If the rate of change of potential difference between the plates is 7 \xD7 10\u2078 V/s, then the integer value of the distance between the parallel plates is (Take \u03B5\u2080 = 9\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 F/m, \u03C0 = {22;7}) ____ \xB5m.",n:1320,s:`I = dQ/dt = C dV/dt, so C = I/(dV/dt) = 0.15/(7\xD710\u2078) = 2.143\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 F.
Also C = \u03B5\u2080\u03C0R\xB2/d, so d = \u03B5\u2080\u03C0R\xB2/C.
d = 9\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 (22/7) \xD7 (0.1)\xB2 / 2.143\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 = 1.32\xD710\u207B\xB3 m.
d = 1320 \xB5m.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A parallel plate capacitor of capacitance 12.5 pF is charged by a battery connected between its plates to potential difference of 12.0 V. The battery is now disconnected and a dielectric slab (\u03B5\u1D63 = 6) is inserted between the plates. The change in its potential energy after inserting the dielectric slab is ____ \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 J.",n:750,s:`Initial energy U\u2080 = \xBDCV\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 12.5\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 144 = 900\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 J.
The battery is disconnected, so Q is constant and C becomes 6C: U = U\u2080/6 = 150\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 J.
The energy falls by 900 \u2212 150 = 750 (\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 J).
The size of the change is 750.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"Three capacitors of capacitances 25 \xB5F, 30 \xB5F and 45 \xB5F are connected in parallel to a supply of 100 V. Energy stored in the above combination is E. When these capacitors are connected in series to the same supply, the stored energy is {9;x}E. The value of x is ____.",n:86,s:`Parallel: C = 25 + 30 + 45 = 100 \xB5F, so E = \xBD \xD7 100 \xB5F \xD7 V\xB2.
Series: 1/C' = 1/25 + 1/30 + 1/45 = (18 + 15 + 10)/450 = 43/450, so C' = 450/43 \xB5F.
Same V, so energy is proportional to C: E'/E = C'/C = (450/43)/100 = 4.5/43.
Set 9/x = 4.5/43, so x = 9 \xD7 43/4.5 = 86.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A capacitor of 10 \xB5F capacitance whose plates are separated by 10 mm through air and each plate has area 4 cm\xB2 is now filled equally with two dielectric media of K\u2081 = 2, K\u2082 = 3 respectively as shown in figure. If new force between the plates is 8 N. The supply voltage is ____ V.",fig:{w:320,h:130,alt:"Capacitor plates with the gap filled side by side by dielectrics K1 = 2 (left half) and K2 = 3 (right half)",d:[["l",60,40,280,40,"B"],["l",60,100,280,100,"B"],["r",170,40,110,60,"f"],["l",170,40,170,100],["l",45,40,45,100,"b"],["t",34,74,"d","e"],["t",115,74,"K\u2081 = 2","ms"],["t",225,74,"K\u2082 = 3","ms"]]},n:80,s:`With the two halves filled, the capacitance becomes C' = C(K\u2081 + K\u2082)/2 = 10 \xD7 5/2 = 25 \xB5F (taking the 10 \xB5F as the air value, as the problem intends).
The battery stays connected. U = \xBDC'V\xB2, and C' \u221D 1/d, so the force is F = U/d = C'V\xB2/(2d).
V\xB2 = 2Fd/C' = 2 \xD7 8 \xD7 0.01 / (25\xD710\u207B\u2076) = 6400.
V = 80 V.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A capacitor of capacitance C has energy E. It is connected to another capacitor of capacitance 2C and potential 2V. Then the loss of energy is {x;3}E, where x is ____.",n:2,s:`Here C has potential V, so E = \xBDCV\xB2. The 2C capacitor has energy \xBD(2C)(2V)\xB2 = 4CV\xB2 = 8E.
Total initial energy = 9E.
Charges CV and 4CV (same sign) add: 5CV on 3C, so the common potential is 5V/3.
Final energy = \xBD(3C)(5V/3)\xB2 = 25CV\xB2/6 = (25/3)E.
Loss = 9E \u2212 25E/3 = (2/3)E, so x = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor with plate separation 5 mm is charged up by a battery. It is found that on introducing a dielectric sheet of thickness 2 mm, while keeping the battery connections intact, the capacitor draws 25% more charge from the battery than before. The dielectric constant of the sheet is ____.",n:2,s:`The battery stays connected, so Q \u221D C.
C' / C = 1.25, and C = \u03B5\u2080A/(d \u2212 t + t/K).
1.25 = 5/(5 \u2212 2 + 2/K) = 5/(3 + 2/K).
3 + 2/K = 4, so 2/K = 1 and K = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:`A parallel plate capacitor with plate area A and plate separation d is filled with a dielectric material of dielectric constant K = 4. The thickness of the dielectric material is x, where x < d.
Let C\u2081 and C\u2082 be the capacitance of the system for x = {1;3}d and x = {2d;3}, respectively. If C\u2081 = 2 \xB5F, the value of C\u2082 is ____ \xB5F.`,fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"Parallel plate capacitor of gap d with a dielectric slab of thickness x resting on plate 2",d:[["l",55,25,255,25,"B"],["l",55,125,255,125,"B"],["r",55,95,200,30,"f"],["l",24,25,24,125,"b"],["t",14,79,"d","e"],["l",42,95,42,125,"b"],["t",36,114,"x","e"],["t",268,29,"Plate - 1",""],["t",268,103,"Dielectric",""],["t",268,134,"Plate - 2",""]]},n:3,s:`Use C = \u03B5\u2080A/(d \u2212 x + x/K) with K = 4 and C\u2080 = \u03B5\u2080A/d.
x = d/3: C\u2081 = C\u2080/(1 \u2212 1/3 + 1/12) = C\u2080/(3/4) = 4C\u2080/3 = 2 \xB5F, so C\u2080 = 1.5 \xB5F.
x = 2d/3: C\u2082 = C\u2080/(1 \u2212 2/3 + 1/6) = C\u2080/(1/2) = 2C\u2080.
C\u2082 = 2 \xD7 1.5 = 3 \xB5F.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"As shown in the figure, two parallel plate capacitors having equal plate area of 200 cm\xB2 are joined in such a way that a \u2260 b. The equivalent capacitance of the combination is x\u03B5\u2080 F. The value of x is ____.",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Two plates a distance d = 5 mm apart with a conducting slab of thickness c = 1 mm between them, leaving gaps a above and b below",d:[["l",80,35,225,35,"B"],["l",80,135,225,135,"B"],["r",90,65,125,20,"f"],["l",152,10,152,35],["l",152,135,152,160],["l",237,35,237,65,"b"],["t",244,54,"a",""],["l",237,85,237,135,"b"],["t",244,114,"b",""],["l",268,35,268,135,"b"],["t",274,89,"d = 5 mm",""],["l",70,65,70,85,"b"],["t",64,79,"c = 1 mm","e"]]},n:5,s:`The conducting slab has no field inside it, so only the two air gaps a and b act, and they are in series.
1/C = a/(\u03B5\u2080A) + b/(\u03B5\u2080A) = (a + b)/(\u03B5\u2080A).
a + b = d \u2212 c = 5 \u2212 1 = 4 mm, so it does not depend on a or b separately.
C = \u03B5\u2080 \xD7 200\xD710\u207B\u2074 / 4\xD710\u207B\xB3 = 5\u03B5\u2080.
x = 5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A 600 pF capacitor is charged by 200 V supply. It is then disconnected from the supply and is connected to another uncharged 600 pF capacitor. Electrostatic energy lost in the process is ____ \xB5J.",n:6,s:`Initial energy = \xBD \xD7 600\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 200\xB2 = 12\xD710\u207B\u2076 J.
The charge is shared equally, so the common voltage is 100 V.
Final energy = \xBD \xD7 1200\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 100\xB2 = 6\xD710\u207B\u2076 J.
Loss = 12 \u2212 6 = 6 \xB5J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"In the given circuit, C\u2081 = 2 \xB5F, C\u2082 = 0.2 \xB5F, C\u2083 = 2 \xB5F, C\u2084 = 4 \xB5F, C\u2085 = 2 \xB5F, C\u2086 = 2 \xB5F. The charge stored on capacitor C\u2084 is ____ \xB5C.",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"Circuit with a 10 V battery and capacitors C1 to C6 arranged as a ladder",d:[["l",60,40,107,40],["l",107,32,107,48],["l",113,32,113,48],["l",113,40,227,40],["l",227,32,227,48],["l",233,32,233,48],["l",233,40,290,40],["l",60,140,107,140],["l",107,132,107,148],["l",113,132,113,148],["l",113,140,227,140],["l",227,132,227,148],["l",233,132,233,148],["l",233,140,290,140],["l",170,40,170,87],["l",162,87,178,87],["l",162,93,178,93],["l",170,93,170,140],["l",290,40,290,87],["l",282,87,298,87],["l",282,93,298,93],["l",290,93,290,140],["l",60,40,60,84],["l",50,84,70,84],["l",54,91,66,91,"B"],["l",60,91,60,140],["c",170,40,2,"F"],["c",170,140,2,"F"],["t",46,92,"10 V","e"],["t",110,28,"C\u2081","m"],["t",230,28,"C\u2083","m"],["t",110,162,"C\u2086","m"],["t",230,162,"C\u2085","m"],["t",180,94,"C\u2082",""],["t",300,94,"C\u2084",""]]},n:4,s:`C\u2083, C\u2084, C\u2085 are in series: 1/C = 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/2 = 5/4, so C = 0.8 \xB5F. This is in parallel with C\u2082 = 0.2 \xB5F: 1.0 \xB5F.
C\u2081 and C\u2086 (2 \xB5F each) in series give 1 \xB5F. Total circuit: 1 \xB5F in series with 1 \xB5F = 0.5 \xB5F.
Charge from the battery = 0.5 \xD7 10 = 5 \xB5C. Voltage across the 1.0 \xB5F block = 5 V.
Charge on the C\u2083\u2013C\u2084\u2013C\u2085 branch = 0.8 \xD7 5 = 4 \xB5C (same charge on C\u2084).
Q(C\u2084) = 4 \xB5C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"In the given figure, the total charge stored in the combination of capacitors is 100 \xB5C. The value of 'x' is ____.",fig:{w:240,h:170,alt:"Three capacitors 2 \xB5F, x \xB5F and 3 \xB5F in parallel across 10 V",d:[["l",40,30,40,140],["l",200,30,200,140],["l",40,30,117,30],["l",117,22,117,38],["l",123,22,123,38],["l",123,30,200,30],["t",120,20,"C\u2081 = 2 \xB5F","ms"],["l",40,70,117,70],["l",117,62,117,78],["l",123,62,123,78],["l",123,70,200,70],["t",120,60,"C\u2082 = x \xB5F","ms"],["l",40,110,117,110],["l",117,102,117,118],["l",123,102,123,118],["l",123,110,200,110],["t",120,100,"C\u2083 = 3 \xB5F","ms"],["l",40,140,200,140,"b"],["t",120,158,"V = 10 V","m"]]},n:5,s:`The three capacitors are in parallel across 10 V: C = 2 + x + 3 = (5 + x) \xB5F.
Q = CV, so 100 = (5 + x) \xD7 10.
5 + x = 10, so x = 5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor with air between the plate has a capacitance of 15 pF. The separation between the plate becomes twice and the space between them is filled with a medium of dielectric constant 3.5. Then the capacitance becomes {x;4} pF. The value of x is ____.",n:105,s:`C = K\u03B5\u2080A/d. Here d becomes 2d and K = 3.5.
C' = 3.5 \xD7 15 / 2 = 26.25 pF = 105/4 pF.
So x = 105.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A capacitor has capacitance 5 \xB5F when it's parallel plates are separated by air medium of separation d. A slab of material of dielectric constant 1.5 having area equal to that of plates but thickness {d;2} is inserted between the plates. Capacitance of the capacitor in the presence of slab will be ____ \xB5F.",n:6,s:`C = \u03B5\u2080A/(d \u2212 t + t/K) with t = d/2 and K = 1.5.
Denominator = d/2 + (d/2)/1.5 = d/2 + d/3 = 5d/6.
C = (6/5) \xD7 \u03B5\u2080A/d = 1.2 \xD7 5 = 6 \xB5F.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A capacitor of capacitance 900 \xB5F is charged by a 100 V battery. The capacitor is disconnected from the battery and connected to another uncharged identical capacitor such that one plate of uncharged capacitor connected to positive plate and another plate of uncharged capacitor connected to negative plate of the charged capacitor. The loss of energy in this process is measured as x \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 J. The value of x is ____.",n:225,s:`Initial energy = \xBD \xD7 900\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 100\xB2 = 4.5 J.
The two identical capacitors are joined in parallel, so the charge splits equally and the voltage halves to 50 V.
Final energy = \xBD \xD7 1800\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 50\xB2 = 2.25 J.
Loss = 4.5 \u2212 2.25 = 2.25 J = 225 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 J, so x = 225.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"Two parallel plate capacitors C\u2081 and C\u2082 each having capacitance of 10 \xB5F are individually charged by a 100 V D.C. source. Capacitor C\u2081 is kept connected to the source and a dielectric slab is inserted between the plates. Capacitor C\u2082 is disconnected from the source and then a dielectric slab is inserted in it. Afterwards the capacitor C\u2081 is also disconnected from the source and the two capacitors are finally connected in parallel combination. The common potential of the combination will be ____ V. (Assuming dielectric constant = 10)",n:55,s:`C\u2081 stays at 100 V and its capacitance becomes 100 \xB5F: Q\u2081 = 100 \xD7 100 = 10000 \xB5C.
C\u2082 is isolated with Q\u2082 = 10 \xD7 100 = 1000 \xB5C; the slab raises its capacitance to 100 \xB5F but not its charge.
Joined in parallel (like plates together): V = (Q\u2081 + Q\u2082)/(C\u2081 + C\u2082) = 11000/200.
V = 55 V.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"Two parallel plate capacitors of capacity C and 3C are connected in parallel combination and charged to a potential difference 18 V. The battery is then disconnected and the space between the plates of the capacitor of capacity C is completely filled with a material of dielectric constant 9. The final potential difference across the combination of the capacitors will be ____ V.",n:6,s:`Total charge = (C + 3C) \xD7 18 = 72C. The battery is disconnected, so this charge stays.
Filling the capacitor C with K = 9 makes it 9C. New total capacitance = 9C + 3C = 12C.
V = 72C/12C = 6 V.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A composite parallel plate capacitor is made up of two different dielectric materials with different thickness (t\u2081 and t\u2082) as shown in figure. The two different dielectric material are separated by a conducting foil F. The voltage of conducting foil is ____ V.",fig:{w:340,h:140,alt:"Two dielectric layers (3, 0.5 mm) and (4, 1 mm) separated by a conducting foil F between two plates joined to a 100 V battery",d:[["l",70,40,210,40,"B"],["l",70,115,210,115,"B"],["l",70,65,210,65],["t",140,56,"\u03B5\u2081 = 3, t\u2081 = 0.5 mm","ms"],["t",140,94,"\u03B5\u2082 = 4, t\u2082 = 1 mm","ms"],["t",216,69,"F","s"],["l",210,40,280,40],["l",280,40,280,72],["l",270,72,290,72],["l",274,79,286,79,"B"],["l",280,79,280,115],["l",280,115,210,115],["t",296,80,"100 V","s"]]},n:60,s:`The layers act as two capacitors in series (the foil is the joining node). For equal area, C \u221D K/t.
C\u2081 \u221D 3/0.5 = 6 and C\u2082 \u221D 4/1 = 4.
Same charge on both, so V\u2081 : V\u2082 = 1/C\u2081 : 1/C\u2082 = 1/6 : 1/4 = 2 : 3. With V\u2081 + V\u2082 = 100 V: V\u2081 = 40 V, V\u2082 = 60 V.
Taking the plate next to the 1 mm layer as 0 V, the foil is at V\u2082 = 60 V.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor with width 4 cm, length 8 cm and separation between the plates of 4 mm is connected to a battery of 20 V. A dielectric slab of dielectric constant 5 having length 1 cm, width 4 cm and thickness 4 mm is inserted between the plates of parallel plate capacitor. The electrostatic energy of this system will be ____ \u03B5\u2080 J. (Where \u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of free space)",n:240,s:`The slab fills the full gap over 1 cm \xD7 4 cm = 4 cm\xB2. The remaining area is 32 \u2212 4 = 28 cm\xB2 (air). These parts are in parallel.
C = \u03B5\u2080[28\xD710\u207B\u2074 + 5 \xD7 4\xD710\u207B\u2074]/(4\xD710\u207B\xB3) = \u03B5\u2080 \xD7 48\xD710\u207B\u2074/4\xD710\u207B\xB3 = 1.2\u03B5\u2080.
The battery stays connected: U = \xBDCV\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 1.2\u03B5\u2080 \xD7 400.
U = 240\u03B5\u2080 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"The equivalent capacitance between points A and B in below shown figure will be ____ \xB5F.",fig:{w:340,h:125,alt:"Six 8 \xB5F capacitors in a line from A to B with wires shorting some nodes",d:[["c",10,70,3,"F"],["l",10,70,42,70],["l",42,62,42,78],["l",48,62,48,78],["t",45,90,"8 \xB5F","ms"],["l",48,70,92,70],["l",92,62,92,78],["l",98,62,98,78],["t",95,90,"8 \xB5F","ms"],["l",98,70,142,70],["l",142,62,142,78],["l",148,62,148,78],["t",145,90,"8 \xB5F","ms"],["l",148,70,192,70],["l",192,62,192,78],["l",198,62,198,78],["t",195,90,"8 \xB5F","ms"],["l",198,70,242,70],["l",242,62,242,78],["l",248,62,248,78],["t",245,90,"8 \xB5F","ms"],["l",248,70,292,70],["l",292,62,292,78],["l",298,62,298,78],["t",295,90,"8 \xB5F","ms"],["l",298,70,325,70],["c",325,70,3,"F"],["t",10,86,"A","m"],["t",325,86,"B","m"],["l",28,70,28,35],["l",28,35,120,35],["l",120,35,120,70],["l",70,70,70,108],["l",70,108,170,108],["l",170,108,170,70],["l",170,70,170,35],["l",170,35,270,35],["l",270,35,270,70],["c",28,70,2,"F"],["c",70,70,2,"F"],["c",120,70,2,"F"],["c",170,70,2,"F"],["c",270,70,2,"F"]]},n:6,s:`Call the nodes after the 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th, 5th capacitors N\u2081 to N\u2085. The left top wire joins A with N\u2082; the bottom wire joins N\u2081 with N\u2083; the right top wire joins N\u2083 with N\u2085.
So C\u2081 (A\u2013N\u2081), C\u2082 (N\u2081\u2013A) and C\u2083 (A\u2013N\u2083, since N\u2082 = A and N\u2083 = N\u2081) are all between A and N\u2081: 8 + 8 + 8 = 24 \xB5F.
C\u2084 and C\u2085 lie between N\u2083 and N\u2085, which are the same node, and their middle node connects nowhere else, so they carry no charge.
C\u2086 joins N\u2085 (= N\u2081) to B: 8 \xB5F, in series with 24 \xB5F.
C = (24 \xD7 8)/(24 + 8) = 6 \xB5F.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A capacitor of capacitance 50 pF is charged by 100 V source. It is then connected to another uncharged identical capacitor. Electrostatic energy loss in the process is ____ nJ.",n:125,s:`Initial energy = \xBD \xD7 50\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 100\xB2 = 250 nJ.
The charge is shared equally, so the voltage is 50 V.
Final energy = \xBD \xD7 100\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 50\xB2 = 125 nJ.
Loss = 250 \u2212 125 = 125 nJ.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"A parallel plate capacitor is made up of stair like structure with a plate area A of each stair and that is connected with a wire of length b, as shown in the figure. The capacitance of the arrangement is {x \u03B5\u2080 A;15 b}. The value of x is ____.",fig:{w:340,h:195,alt:"Two staircase-shaped plates facing each other, each step of area A and each step height b",d:[["p","M40 20 H120 V50 H200 V80 H290","B"],["p","M40 170 H120 V140 H200 V110 H290","B"],["l",108,20,108,50,"b"],["t",100,39,"b","e"],["l",188,50,188,80,"b"],["t",180,69,"b","e"],["l",275,80,275,110,"b"],["t",281,99,"b",""],["l",212,110,212,140,"b"],["t",218,129,"b",""],["l",108,140,108,170,"b"],["t",100,159,"b","e"],["t",80,13,"A","m"],["t",245,96,"A","ms"],["t",160,134,"A","ms"],["t",80,186,"A","m"]]},n:23,s:`The two stair-shaped plates face each other across three steps. The facing steps are separated by b, 3b and 5b (each step adds b above and b below).
Each pair has area A and is a capacitor; they are all connected between the same two plates, so they are in parallel.
C = \u03B5\u2080A/b + \u03B5\u2080A/(3b) + \u03B5\u2080A/(5b) = (15 + 5 + 3)/15 \xD7 \u03B5\u2080A/b = {23 \u03B5\u2080A;15 b}.
x = 23.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A capacitor C\u2081 of capacitance 5 \xB5F is charged to a potential of 30 V using a battery. The battery is then removed and the charged capacitor is connected to an uncharged capacitor C\u2082 of capacitance 10 \xB5F as shown in figure. When the switch is closed charge flows between the capacitors. At equilibrium, the charge on the capacitor C\u2082 is ____ \xB5C.",fig:{w:340,h:145,alt:"Capacitor C1 (5 \xB5F) and capacitor C2 (10 \xB5F) joined through a switch",d:[["l",60,30,120,30],["l",120,30,150,12],["l",160,30,250,30],["l",60,30,60,77],["l",52,77,68,77],["l",52,83,68,83],["l",60,83,60,130],["l",250,30,250,77],["l",242,77,258,77],["l",242,83,258,83],["l",250,83,250,130],["l",60,130,250,130],["t",72,86,"5 \xB5F","s"],["t",58,66,"C\u2081","e"],["t",262,86,"10 \xB5F","s"],["t",248,66,"C\u2082","e"]]},n:100,s:`Initial charge on C\u2081 = 5 \xD7 30 = 150 \xB5C.
At equilibrium both have the same voltage: V = 150/(5 + 10) = 10 V.
Charge on C\u2082 = 10 \xD7 10 = 100 \xB5C.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"In the following circuit, C\u2081 = 12 \xB5F, C\u2082 = C\u2083 = 4 \xB5F and C\u2084 = C\u2085 = 2 \xB5F. The charge stored in C\u2083 is ____ \xB5C.",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Circuit with C1 on the top wire, C2 and C4 on slanted wires, C3 and C5 vertical, a 6 V battery on the left and a 2 V battery on the right",d:[["l",60,30,102,30],["l",102,22,102,38],["l",108,22,108,38],["l",108,30,300,30],["l",60,140,300,140],["l",60,30,60,82],["l",50,82,70,82],["l",54,89,66,89,"B"],["l",60,89,60,140],["l",300,30,300,82],["l",290,82,310,82],["l",294,89,306,89,"B"],["l",300,89,300,140],["l",150,30,150,82],["l",142,82,158,82],["l",142,88,158,88],["l",150,88,150,140],["l",230,30,230,82],["l",222,82,238,82],["l",222,88,238,88],["l",230,88,230,140],["l",60,140,103.1,87.3],["l",96.9,82.2,109.3,92.4],["l",100.7,77.6,113.1,87.8],["l",106.9,82.7,150,30],["l",150,30,188.2,82.6],["l",181.7,87.3,194.7,77.9],["l",185.3,92.1,198.3,82.7],["l",191.8,87.4,230,140],["c",150,30,2,"F"],["t",105,20,"C\u2081","m"],["t",82,76,"C\u2082","e"],["t",157,106,"C\u2083",""],["t",204,108,"C\u2084",""],["t",238,80,"C\u2085",""],["t",46,90,"6 V","e"],["t",312,92,"2 V",""]]},n:8,s:`The bottom wire and both bottom corners are one node (G). The top wire to the right of C\u2081 joins the apex of the triangle, the top of C\u2085 and the 2 V battery: one node (T).
The 2 V battery fixes the voltage between T and G as 2 V.
C\u2082, C\u2083, C\u2084 and C\u2085 each connect T to G, so each has 2 V across it. (C\u2081 and the 6 V battery only fix how much charge C\u2081 holds.)
Q\u2083 = C\u2083V = 4 \xB5F \xD7 2 V = 8 \xB5C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A parallel plate capacitor of capacitance 200 \xB5F is connected to a battery of 200 V. A dielectric slab of dielectric constant 2 is now inserted into the space between plates of capacitor while the battery remain connected. The change in the electrostatic energy in the capacitor will be ____ J.",n:4,s:`The battery stays connected, so V = 200 V. The slab doubles the capacitance: C' = 400 \xB5F.
U\u2080 = \xBD \xD7 200\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 200\xB2 = 4 J.
U = \xBD \xD7 400\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 200\xB2 = 8 J.
Change = 8 \u2212 4 = 4 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"In a parallel plate capacitor set up, the plate area of capacitor is 2 m\xB2 and the plates are separated by 1 m. If the space between the plates are filled with a dielectric material of thickness 0.5 m and area 2 m\xB2 (see fig) the capacitance of the set-up will be ____ \u03B5\u2080. (Dielectric constant of the material = 3.2) (Round off to the nearest integer)",fig:{w:300,h:155,alt:"Plates a distance d apart; a dielectric of thickness d/2 sits against the left plate",d:[["l",50,30,50,120,"B"],["l",250,30,250,120,"B"],["r",50,30,100,90,"f"],["t",100,78,"k","ms"],["l",50,18,250,18,"b"],["t",150,12,"d","m"],["l",50,132,150,132,"b"],["t",100,148,"d/2","ms"]]},n:3,s:`C = \u03B5\u2080A/(d \u2212 t + t/K) = \u03B5\u2080 \xD7 2/(1 \u2212 0.5 + 0.5/3.2).
0.5/3.2 = 0.15625, so the denominator = 0.65625.
C = 2/0.65625 \u03B5\u2080 = 3.05 \u03B5\u2080.
Rounded: 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Capacitance and combinations",q:"Four identical rectangular plates with length l = 2 cm and breadth b = {3;2} cm are arranged as shown in figure. The equivalent capacitance between A and C is {x\u03B5\u2080;d}. The value of x is ____. (Round off to the nearest integer)",fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"Four parallel plates A, B, C, D a distance d apart; plates B and D are joined by a wire",d:[["l",60,60,60,150],["l",120,30,120,150],["l",180,60,180,150],["l",240,30,240,150],["l",120,30,120,15],["l",120,15,240,15],["l",240,15,240,30],["t",60,52,"A","m"],["t",114,50,"B","e"],["t",180,52,"C","m"],["t",234,50,"D","e"],["l",60,172,120,172,"b"],["l",120,172,180,172,"b"],["l",180,172,240,172,"b"],["t",90,190,"d","m"],["t",150,190,"d","m"],["t",210,190,"d","m"]]},n:2,s:`Plate area S = l \xD7 b = 2 \xD7 1.5 = 3 cm\xB2. Each gap is a capacitor C\u2080 = \u03B5\u2080S/d.
B and D are joined, so call that node X. A\u2013X is one gap: C\u2080. C\u2013X is two gaps (C\u2013B and C\u2013D) in parallel: 2C\u2080.
Between A and C, these are in series: C\u2080 \xD7 2C\u2080/(3C\u2080) = 2C\u2080/3 = (2/3) \u03B5\u2080S/d.
With S = 3 cm\xB2: C = 2 \u03B5\u2080 (cm\xB2)/d, so x = 2 when the area is counted in cm\xB2 (in SI units the number would be 2\xD710\u207B\u2074). The official answer is 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A parallel plate capacitor whose capacitance C is 14 pF is charged by a battery to a potential difference V = 12 V between its plates. The charging battery is now disconnected and a porcelain plate with K = 7 is inserted between the plates, then the plate would oscillate back and forth between the plates with a constant mechanical energy of ____ pJ. (Assume no friction)",n:864,s:`Initial energy U\u2080 = \xBDCV\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 14 \xD7 144 = 1008 pJ.
The battery is off, so Q is constant. With the plate fully inside, U = U\u2080/K = 1008/7 = 144 pJ.
The plate is pulled in and gains the lost energy as kinetic energy; with no friction it oscillates with this constant mechanical energy.
E = 1008 \u2212 144 = 864 pJ.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A 2 \xB5F capacitor C\u2081 is first charged to a potential difference of 10 V using a battery. Then the battery is removed and the capacitor is connected to an uncharged capacitor C\u2082 of 8 \xB5F. The charge in C\u2082 on equilibrium condition is ____ \xB5C. (Round off to the nearest integer)",n:16,s:`Charge on C\u2081 = 2 \xD7 10 = 20 \xB5C.
At equilibrium the voltage is common: V = 20/(2 + 8) = 2 V.
Charge on C\u2082 = 8 \xD7 2 = 16 \xB5C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor has plate area 100 m\xB2 and plate separation of 10 m. The space between the plates is filled up to a thickness 5 m with a material of dielectric constant of 10. The resultant capacitance of the system is 'x' pF. The value of 'x' to the nearest integer is ____. The value of \u03B5\u2080 = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 F m\u207B\xB9",n:161,s:`C = \u03B5\u2080A/(d \u2212 t + t/K) = 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 100/(10 \u2212 5 + 0.5).
Denominator = 5.5 m.
C = 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070/5.5 = 1.609\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 F \u2248 161 pF.
x = 161.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A 5 \xB5F capacitor is charged fully by a 220 V supply. It is then disconnected from the supply and is connected in series to another uncharged 2.5 \xB5F capacitor. If the energy change during the charge redistribution is {X;100} J then value of X to the nearest integer is ____.",n:4,s:`Charge Q = 5 \xD7 220 = 1100 \xB5C. Initial energy = \xBD \xD7 5\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 220\xB2 = 0.121 J.
The capacitors are joined into a loop, so the charge redistributes until both have the same voltage: V = 1100/(5 + 2.5) = 146.7 V.
Final energy = \xBD \xD7 7.5\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 146.7\xB2 = 0.0807 J.
Energy change = 0.121 \u2212 0.0807 = 0.0403 J = 4/100 J, so X = 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"A 60 pF capacitor is fully charged by a 20 V supply. It is then disconnected from the supply and is connected to another uncharged 60 pF capacitor in parallel. The electrostatic energy that is lost in this process by the time the charge is redistributed between them is (in nJ) ____.",n:6,s:`Initial energy = \xBD \xD7 60\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 20\xB2 = 12 nJ.
The charge is shared equally, so the voltage is 10 V.
Final energy = \xBD \xD7 120\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 10\xB2 = 6 nJ.
Energy lost = 12 \u2212 6 = 6 nJ.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:"Two capacitors with capacitance values C\u2081 = 2000 \xB1 10 pF and C\u2082 = 3000 \xB1 15 pF are connected in series. The voltage applied across this combination is V = 5.00 \xB1 0.02 V. The percentage error in the calculation of the energy stored in this combination of capacitors is ____.",n:1.3,s:`Series capacitance: C = C\u2081C\u2082/(C\u2081 + C\u2082) = 1200 pF.
\u0394C = (C\u2082/(C\u2081+C\u2082))\xB2 \u0394C\u2081 + (C\u2081/(C\u2081+C\u2082))\xB2 \u0394C\u2082 = 0.36 \xD7 10 + 0.16 \xD7 15 = 6 pF, so \u0394C/C = 0.5%.
Energy U = \xBDCV\xB2, so \u0394U/U = \u0394C/C + 2\u0394V/V = 0.5% + 2 \xD7 0.02/5 = 0.5% + 0.8%.
Percentage error = 1.30%.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"ecp",st:"Dielectrics and conducting slabs",q:"A parallel plate capacitor of capacitance C has spacing d between two plates having area A. The region between the plates is filled with N dielectric layers, parallel to its plates, each with thickness \u03B4 = {d;N}. The dielectric constant of the m\u1D57\u02B0 layer is K\u2098 = K(1 + {m;N}). For a very large N (> 10\xB3), the capacitance C is \u03B1({K\u03B5\u2080A;d ln 2}). The value of \u03B1 will be ____. [\u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of free space]",n:1,s:`The layers are capacitors in series: 1/C = \u03A3 \u03B4/(K\u2098\u03B5\u2080A) = {d;N\u03B5\u2080AK} \u03A3 1/(1 + m/N).
For very large N the sum becomes an integral: \u03A3 1/(1 + m/N) \u2248 N \u222B\u2080\xB9 dx/(1 + x) = N ln 2.
So 1/C = d ln 2/(K\u03B5\u2080A), that is C = K\u03B5\u2080A/(d ln 2).
Comparing with C = \u03B1(K\u03B5\u2080A/(d ln 2)): \u03B1 = 1.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"ecp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Four identical thin, square metal sheets, S\u2081, S\u2082, S\u2083 and S\u2084, each of side a are kept parallel to each other with equal distance d (<< a) between them, as shown in the figure. Let C\u2080 = \u03B5\u2080a\xB2/d, where \u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of free space.
Match the quantities mentioned in List-I with their values in List-II and choose the correct option.
List-I
(P) The capacitance between S\u2081 and S\u2084, with S\u2082 and S\u2083 not connected, is
(Q) The capacitance between S\u2081 and S\u2084, with S\u2082 shorted to S\u2083, is
(R) The capacitance between S\u2081 and S\u2083, with S\u2082 shorted to S\u2084, is
(S) The capacitance between S\u2081 and S\u2082, with S\u2083 shorted to S\u2081 and S\u2082 shorted to S\u2084, is
List-II
1. 3C\u2080
2. C\u2080/2
3. C\u2080/3
4. 2C\u2080/3
5. 2C\u2080`,fig:{w:280,h:185,alt:"Four identical square metal sheets S1 to S4 placed parallel with equal gaps d; side of each sheet is a",d:[["p","M40 55 L80 30 L80 120 L40 145 Z","f"],["t",60,22,"S\u2081","ms"],["p","M70 55 L110 30 L110 120 L70 145 Z","f"],["t",90,22,"S\u2082","ms"],["p","M100 55 L140 30 L140 120 L100 145 Z","f"],["t",120,22,"S\u2083","ms"],["p","M130 55 L170 30 L170 120 L130 145 Z","f"],["t",150,22,"S\u2084","ms"],["l",40,160,70,160,"b"],["l",70,160,100,160,"b"],["l",100,160,130,160,"b"],["t",55,174,"d","ms"],["t",85,174,"d","ms"],["t",115,174,"d","ms"],["l",200,30,200,120,"b"],["t",208,78,"a",""]]},o:["P \u2192 3; Q \u2192 2; R \u2192 4; S \u2192 5","P \u2192 2; Q \u2192 3; R \u2192 2; S \u2192 1","P \u2192 3; Q \u2192 2; R \u2192 4; S \u2192 1","P \u2192 3; Q \u2192 2; R \u2192 2; S \u2192 5"],c:2,s:`Each gap between neighbouring sheets is a capacitor C\u2080.
P: three gaps in series: C\u2080/3, so P \u2192 3.
Q: S\u2082 and S\u2083 shorted, so the middle gap vanishes; two gaps in series: C\u2080/2, so Q \u2192 2.
R: let S\u2082 = S\u2084 = M. Gap S\u2081\u2013S\u2082 is between S\u2081 and M (C\u2080). Gaps S\u2082\u2013S\u2083 and S\u2083\u2013S\u2084 are both between S\u2083 and M (2C\u2080). Series: C\u2080 \xD7 2C\u2080/3C\u2080 = 2C\u2080/3, so R \u2192 4.
S: let S\u2081 = S\u2083 = A and S\u2082 = S\u2084 = M. All three gaps lie between A and M: parallel, 3C\u2080, so S \u2192 1.
P \u2192 3; Q \u2192 2; R \u2192 4; S \u2192 1: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:`Consider a simple RC circuit as shown in figure 1.
Process 1: In the circuit the switch S is closed at t = 0 and the capacitor is fully charged to voltage V\u2080 (i.e., charging continues for time T >> RC). In the process some dissipation (E_D) occurs across the resistance R. The amount of energy finally stored in the fully charged capacitor is E_C.
Process 2: In a different process the voltage is first set to {V\u2080;3} and maintained for a charging time T >> RC. Then the voltage is raised to {2V\u2080;3} without discharging the capacitor and again maintained for a time T >> RC. The process is repeated one more time by raising the voltage to V\u2080 and the capacitor is charged to the same final voltage V\u2080 as in Process 1.
These two processes are depicted in figure 2.
In Process 2, total energy dissipated across the resistance E_D is`,fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"Left: RC circuit with a source V, switch S, resistor R and capacitor C. Right: voltage against time for Process 1 (jump to V0) and Process 2 (steps V0/3, 2V0/3, V0)",d:[["l",30,40,48,40],["l",48,40,64,28],["l",66,40,72,40],["p","M72 40 L77 32 L84 48 L91 32 L98 48 L105 32 L110 40 L120 40"],["l",120,40,120,77],["l",112,77,128,77],["l",112,83,128,83],["l",120,83,120,140],["l",30,140,120,140],["l",30,40,30,86],["l",20,86,40,86],["l",24,93,36,93,"B"],["l",30,93,30,140],["t",52,24,"S","s"],["t",92,28,"R","ms"],["t",130,86,"C",""],["t",14,92,"V","e"],["t",75,165,"figure 1","ms"],["l",200,140,330,140,"a"],["l",200,140,200,15,"a"],["t",206,18,"V","s"],["t",326,156,"t","es"],["l",197,40,203,40],["l",197,73,203,73],["l",197,107,203,107],["t",194,43,"V\u2080","es"],["t",194,76,"2V\u2080/3","es"],["t",194,110,"V\u2080/3","es"],["p","M200 140 V40 H325","a"],["p","M200 140 V107 H250 V73 H300 V40 H325","a"],["l",250,140,250,145],["l",300,140,300,145],["t",250,157,"T","ms"],["t",300,157,"2T","ms"],["t",250,32,"Process 1","ms"],["t",222,100,"Process 2","ms"]]},o:["E_D = 3({1;2}CV\u2080\xB2)","E_D = {1;3}({1;2}CV\u2080\xB2)","E_D = 3CV\u2080\xB2","E_D = {1;2}CV\u2080\xB2"],c:1,s:`In each charging step the voltage source rises by \u0394V and the capacitor charges through R; the energy lost in R is \xBDC(\u0394V)\xB2.
Process 2 has three equal steps of \u0394V = V\u2080/3 (0 \u2192 V\u2080/3 \u2192 2V\u2080/3 \u2192 V\u2080).
E_D = 3 \xD7 \xBDC(V\u2080/3)\xB2 = 3 \xD7 CV\u2080\xB2/18 = CV\u2080\xB2/6.
This is (1/3)(\xBDCV\u2080\xB2). Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"ecp",st:"Energy, force and circuits (including RC)",q:`Consider a simple RC circuit as shown in figure 1.
Process 1: In the circuit the switch S is closed at t = 0 and the capacitor is fully charged to voltage V\u2080 (i.e., charging continues for time T >> RC). In the process some dissipation (E_D) occurs across the resistance R. The amount of energy finally stored in the fully charged capacitor is E_C.
Process 2: In a different process the voltage is first set to {V\u2080;3} and maintained for a charging time T >> RC. Then the voltage is raised to {2V\u2080;3} without discharging the capacitor and again maintained for a time T >> RC. The process is repeated one more time by raising the voltage to V\u2080 and the capacitor is charged to the same final voltage V\u2080 as in Process 1.
These two processes are depicted in figure 2.
In process 1, the energy stored in the capacitor E_C and heat dissipated across resistance E_D are related by`,fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"Left: RC circuit with a source V, switch S, resistor R and capacitor C. Right: voltage against time for Process 1 (jump to V0) and Process 2 (steps V0/3, 2V0/3, V0)",d:[["l",30,40,48,40],["l",48,40,64,28],["l",66,40,72,40],["p","M72 40 L77 32 L84 48 L91 32 L98 48 L105 32 L110 40 L120 40"],["l",120,40,120,77],["l",112,77,128,77],["l",112,83,128,83],["l",120,83,120,140],["l",30,140,120,140],["l",30,40,30,86],["l",20,86,40,86],["l",24,93,36,93,"B"],["l",30,93,30,140],["t",52,24,"S","s"],["t",92,28,"R","ms"],["t",130,86,"C",""],["t",14,92,"V","e"],["t",75,165,"figure 1","ms"],["l",200,140,330,140,"a"],["l",200,140,200,15,"a"],["t",206,18,"V","s"],["t",326,156,"t","es"],["l",197,40,203,40],["l",197,73,203,73],["l",197,107,203,107],["t",194,43,"V\u2080","es"],["t",194,76,"2V\u2080/3","es"],["t",194,110,"V\u2080/3","es"],["p","M200 140 V40 H325","a"],["p","M200 140 V107 H250 V73 H300 V40 H325","a"],["l",250,140,250,145],["l",300,140,300,145],["t",250,157,"T","ms"],["t",300,157,"2T","ms"],["t",250,32,"Process 1","ms"],["t",222,100,"Process 2","ms"]]},o:["E_C = E_D","E_C = E_D ln 2","E_C = 2E_D","E_C = {1;2}E_D"],c:0,s:`Process 1 is a single jump to V\u2080. The capacitor ends with E_C = \xBDCV\u2080\xB2.
The source supplies QV\u2080 = CV\u2080\xB2. Energy lost in R = CV\u2080\xB2 \u2212 \xBDCV\u2080\xB2 = \xBDCV\u2080\xB2 = E_D.
So E_C = E_D. Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ecn",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : Two metallic spheres are charged to the same potential. One of them is hollow and another is solid, and both have the same radii. Solid sphere will have lower charge than the hollow one.
Reason (R) : Capacitance of metallic spheres depend on the radii of spheres.
In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is true but (R) is false.","(A) is false but (R) is true."],c:3,s:`The charge on a conducting sphere is on its outer surface only, so a hollow and a solid sphere of the same radius have the same capacitance C = 4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R.
At the same potential, Q = CV is the same for both. So (A) is false.
(R) is true: the capacitance of a metallic sphere depends on its radius.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"ecp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Two parallel plate capacitors A and B have the same separation d = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m between the plates. The plate areas of A and B are 0.04 m\xB2 and 0.02 m\xB2 respectively. A slab of dielectric constant (relative permittivity) K = 9 has dimensions such that it can exactly fill the space between the plates of capacitor B.
(i) The dielectric slab is placed inside A as shown in figure (a). A is then charged to a potential difference of 110 V. Calculate the capacitance of A and the energy stored in it.
(ii) The battery is disconnected and then the dielectric slab is moved from A. Find the work done by the external agent in removing the slab from A.
(iii) The same dielectric slab is now placed inside B, filling it completely. The two capacitors A and B are then connected as shown in figure (c). Calculate the energy stored in the system.`,fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"(a) Capacitor A with a slab filling half of it, connected to 110 V; (b) capacitor B; (c) capacitors A and B connected in parallel, with the slab inside B",d:[["l",40,60,100,60,"B"],["l",40,100,100,100,"B"],["r",70,60,30,40,"f"],["l",15,60,40,60],["l",15,60,15,125],["l",15,125,29,125],["l",29,118,29,132],["l",34,122,34,128,"B"],["l",34,125,55,125],["l",55,125,55,100],["t",104,57,"A","s"],["t",30,148,"110 V","ms"],["t",60,166,"(a)","ms"],["l",130,60,170,60,"B"],["l",130,100,170,100,"B"],["l",150,40,150,60],["l",150,100,150,120],["t",176,56,"B","s"],["t",150,166,"(b)","ms"],["l",200,60,240,60,"B"],["l",200,100,240,100,"B"],["l",270,60,310,60,"B"],["l",270,100,310,100,"B"],["r",270,60,40,40,"f"],["l",220,60,220,40],["l",220,40,290,40],["l",290,40,290,60],["l",220,100,220,125],["l",220,125,290,125],["l",290,125,290,100],["t",196,56,"A","es"],["t",316,56,"B","s"],["t",255,166,"(c)","ms"]]},a:"(i) 2\xD710\u207B\u2079 F, 1.21\xD710\u207B\u2075 J   (ii) 4.84\xD710\u207B\u2075 J   (iii) 1.1\xD710\u207B\u2075 J",s:`(i) The slab covers half of A (area 0.02 m\xB2). \u03B5\u2080 \xD7 0.02/d = 2\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 F, so C_A = 2\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 (9 + 1) = 2\xD710\u207B\u2079 F. U = \xBDCV\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 2\xD710\u207B\u2079 \xD7 110\xB2 = 1.21\xD710\u207B\u2075 J.
(ii) Q = CV = 2.2\xD710\u207B\u2077 C stays constant. Without the slab C = \u03B5\u2080 \xD7 0.04/d = 4\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 F, so U' = Q\xB2/2C = (2.2\xD710\u207B\u2077)\xB2/(8\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070) = 6.05\xD710\u207B\u2075 J.
Work by the agent = 6.05\xD710\u207B\u2075 \u2212 1.21\xD710\u207B\u2075 = 4.84\xD710\u207B\u2075 J.
(iii) B with the slab: C_B = 9 \xD7 2\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 = 1.8\xD710\u207B\u2079 F. A (no slab) still has Q = 2.2\xD710\u207B\u2077 C and C_A = 4\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 F. Joined in parallel: C = 2.2\xD710\u207B\u2079 F, V = Q/C = 100 V.
U = \xBD \xD7 2.2\xD710\u207B\u2079 \xD7 100\xB2 = 1.1\xD710\u207B\u2075 J.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"ecp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Two capacitors A and B with capacities 3 \xB5F and 2 \xB5F are charged to a potential difference of 100 V and 180 V respectively. The plates of the capacitors are connected as shown in the figure with one wire from each capacitor free. The upper plate of A is positive and that of B is negative. An uncharged 2 \xB5F capacitor with lead wires falls on the free ends to complete the circuit. Calculate
(i) the final charge on the three capacitors and
(ii) the amount of electrostatic energy stored in the system before and after the completion of the circuit.`,fig:{w:340,h:140,alt:"Charged capacitors A (3 \xB5F, 100 V) and B (2 \xB5F, 180 V) with bottom plates joined and top wires free; an uncharged 2 \xB5F capacitor C is shown above the free ends",d:[["l",70,40,270,40],["l",167,32,167,48],["l",173,32,173,48],["t",170,26,"2 \xB5F","ms"],["t",170,12,"C","ms"],["l",70,40,70,40],["c",70,40,2,"F"],["c",70,58,2,"F"],["c",270,40,2,"F"],["c",270,58,2,"F"],["l",70,58,70,72],["l",62,72,78,72],["l",62,78,78,78],["l",70,78,70,125],["l",270,58,270,72],["l",262,72,278,72],["l",262,78,278,78],["l",270,78,270,125],["l",70,125,270,125],["t",80,72,"+","s"],["t",80,96,"3 \xB5F, 100 V","s"],["t",80,112,"A","s"],["t",260,72,"\u2212","es"],["t",260,96,"2 \xB5F, 180 V","es"],["t",260,112,"B","es"]]},a:"(i) q\u2081 = 90\xD710\u207B\u2076 C (on A), q\u2082 = 210\xD710\u207B\u2076 C (on the new 2 \xB5F capacitor), q\u2083 = 150\xD710\u207B\u2076 C (on B)   (ii) after: 1.8\xD710\u207B\xB2 J (before: 4.74\xD710\u207B\xB2 J)",s:`Initial charges: A has +300 \xB5C on its top plate; B has \u2212360 \xB5C on its top plate.
Let the final charges be q\u2081 (A), q\u2082 (new capacitor), q\u2083 (B), in \xB5C. Charge conservation on the two isolated nodes: q\u2081 + q\u2082 = 300 and q\u2082 + q\u2083 = 360.
Voltage loop: q\u2081/3 + q\u2083/2 = q\u2082/2.
Substitute q\u2081 = 300 \u2212 q\u2082 and q\u2083 = 360 \u2212 q\u2082: 2(300 \u2212 q\u2082) + 3(360 \u2212 q\u2082) = 3q\u2082, so 8q\u2082 = 1680 and q\u2082 = 210. Then q\u2081 = 90, q\u2083 = 150 (\xB5C).
Energy before = \xBD(3)(100\xB2) + \xBD(2)(180\xB2) = 15000 + 32400 \xB5J = 4.74\xD710\u207B\xB2 J.
Energy after = 90\xB2/(2\xD73) + 210\xB2/(2\xD72) + 150\xB2/(2\xD72) = 1350 + 11025 + 5625 \xB5J = 1.8\xD710\u207B\xB2 J.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"ecp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"The capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor with plate area A and separation d is C. The space between the plates is filled with two wedges of dielectric constants K\u2081 and K\u2082, respectively. Find the capacitance of the resulting capacitor.",fig:{w:320,h:150,alt:"Capacitor plates of area A and gap d; the gap is filled by two wedges of dielectric K1 (lower left) and K2 (upper right) split along a diagonal",d:[["l",70,40,250,40,"B"],["l",70,130,250,130,"B"],["r",80,40,160,90],["l",80,40,240,130],["t",205,72,"K\u2082","ms"],["t",120,112,"K\u2081","ms"],["t",160,30,"A","ms"],["l",272,40,272,130,"b"],["t",280,89,"d",""]]},a:"\u03B5\u2080AK\u2081K\u2082 ln(K\u2081/K\u2082) / [d(K\u2081 \u2212 K\u2082)]",s:`Let the plate have side L (A = L\xB2). At distance x along the plate, K\u2081 has thickness (x/L)d and K\u2082 has thickness (1 \u2212 x/L)d.
A thin strip of width dx is two capacitors in series: dC = \u03B5\u2080L dx / [d(u/K\u2081 + (1 \u2212 u)/K\u2082)], with u = x/L.
The strips are in parallel: C = (\u03B5\u2080A/d) \u222B\u2080\xB9 du / [1/K\u2082 + u(1/K\u2081 \u2212 1/K\u2082)].
The integral equals K\u2081K\u2082 ln(K\u2081/K\u2082)/(K\u2081 \u2212 K\u2082).
C = \u03B5\u2080AK\u2081K\u2082 ln(K\u2081/K\u2082) / [d(K\u2081 \u2212 K\u2082)].`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"ecp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"The figure shows two identical parallel plate capacitors connected to a battery with the switch S closed. The switch is now opened and the free space between the plates of the capacitors is filled with a dielectric of dielectric constant (or relative permittivity) 3. Find the ratio of the total electrostatic energy stored in both capacitors before and after the introduction of the dielectric.",fig:{w:320,h:150,alt:"A battery V across capacitor A; a switch S joins capacitor B to the same wires",d:[["l",50,40,175,40],["l",175,40,205,26],["l",212,40,260,40],["l",50,40,50,76],["l",40,76,60,76],["l",44,83,56,83,"B"],["l",50,83,50,130],["l",130,40,130,82],["l",122,82,138,82],["l",122,88,138,88],["l",130,88,130,130],["l",260,40,260,82],["l",252,82,268,82],["l",252,88,268,88],["l",260,88,260,130],["l",50,130,260,130],["c",130,40,2,"F"],["c",130,130,2,"F"],["t",195,22,"S","ms"],["t",38,84,"V","e"],["t",140,90,"A  C","s"],["t",270,90,"B  C","s"]]},a:"3/5",s:`Before: both capacitors are at V, so U\u2081 = 2 \xD7 \xBDCV\xB2 = CV\xB2.
After S is opened, capacitor A is still connected to the battery: V is fixed and C becomes 3C, so U_A = \xBD(3C)V\xB2 = 3CV\xB2/2.
Capacitor B is isolated with charge Q = CV; its capacitance becomes 3C, so U_B = Q\xB2/(2 \xD7 3C) = CV\xB2/6.
U\u2082 = 3CV\xB2/2 + CV\xB2/6 = 5CV\xB2/3.
Ratio U\u2081 : U\u2082 = 1 : (5/3) = 3/5.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1986",tp:"efl",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Two protons A and B are placed in between the two plates of a parallel plate capacitor charged to a potential difference V as shown in the figure. The forces on the two protons are identical.",fig:{w:240,h:180,alt:"Plates with + on the left, \u2212 on the right; proton A near the left plate and proton B higher up near the right plate",d:[["l",70,15,70,155],["l",170,15,170,155],["c",88,110,2.5,"F"],["t",80,114,"A","e"],["c",150,60,2.5,"F"],["t",142,64,"B","e"],["l",70,168,170,168,"b"],["t",120,162,"V","ms"],["t",62,32,"+","es"],["t",178,32,"\u2212","s"],["t",62,49,"+","es"],["t",178,49,"\u2212","s"],["t",62,66,"+","es"],["t",178,66,"\u2212","s"],["t",62,83,"+","es"],["t",178,83,"\u2212","s"],["t",62,100,"+","es"],["t",178,100,"\u2212","s"],["t",62,117,"+","es"],["t",178,117,"\u2212","s"],["t",62,134,"+","es"],["t",178,134,"\u2212","s"],["t",62,151,"+","es"],["t",178,151,"\u2212","s"]]},o:["True","False"],c:0,s:`Between the plates (away from the edges) the field of a parallel plate capacitor is uniform: E = V/d, same in size and direction everywhere.
The force on a proton is F = eE, so it is the same at A and at B, whatever their positions.
So the statement is True.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"ecp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Two parallel plate capacitors of capacitances C and 2C are connected in parallel and charged to a potential difference V. The battery is then disconnected and the region between the plates of the capacitor C is completely filled with a material of dielectric constant K. The potential difference across the capacitors now becomes ____.",a:"3V/(K + 2)",s:`Total charge = (C + 2C)V = 3CV, and it stays constant after the battery is disconnected.
The capacitor C becomes KC, so the total capacitance is KC + 2C = (K + 2)C.
V' = Q/C' = 3CV/[(K + 2)C].
V' = 3V/(K + 2).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"ecp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Five identical parallel plates, each of area A, are arranged such that adjacent plates are at a distance d apart. The plates are connected to a source of emf V as shown in the figure. The charge on plate 1 is ____ and on plate 4 is ____.",fig:{w:300,h:180,alt:"Five parallel plates numbered 1 to 5; plates 2 and 4 are joined to the negative terminal and plates 1, 3 and 5 to the positive terminal of a source V",d:[["l",60,50,60,140,"B"],["t",54,100,"1","es"],["l",100,50,100,140,"B"],["t",94,100,"2","es"],["l",140,50,140,140,"B"],["t",134,100,"3","es"],["l",180,50,180,140,"B"],["t",174,100,"4","es"],["l",220,50,220,140,"B"],["t",214,100,"5","es"],["l",100,50,100,30],["l",180,50,180,30],["l",100,30,260,30],["l",260,30,260,70],["l",254,70,266,70,"B"],["l",246,77,274,77],["l",260,77,260,160],["l",60,140,60,160],["l",140,140,140,160],["l",220,140,220,160],["l",60,160,260,160],["c",100,30,2,"F"],["c",180,30,2,"F"],["c",140,160,2,"F"],["t",240,66,"\u2212","es"],["t",240,92,"+","es"],["t",272,76,"V","s"]]},a:"\u03B5\u2080AV/d; \u22122\u03B5\u2080AV/d",s:`Plates 1, 3, 5 are joined to the positive terminal and plates 2, 4 to the negative terminal, so every gap has voltage V and capacitance C = \u03B5\u2080A/d.
Plate 1 faces only plate 2: charge = +CV = \u03B5\u2080AV/d.
Plate 4 faces plate 3 and plate 5, both positive, with V across each gap: it carries \u2212CV from each side.
Charge on plate 4 = \u22122CV = \u22122\u03B5\u2080AV/d.`}]})();
